18715920,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"We used a novel resistance selection protocol to follow the emergence of resistant HIV in the presence of the integrase inhibitor elvitegravir (GS-9137). The locations of the mutations are highlighted in the catalytic sites of integrase, and we correlate the mutations with expected drug-protein contacts.",The paper focuses on the selection of resistant HIV strains in vitro and does not mention obtaining sequences from patient samples.
18715920,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,The antiviral activity of different inhibitors was determined in a cell-based HIV-1 replication assay. The susceptibilities of the HIV strains to the presence of high EVG concentrations were determined.,"The paper describes experiments measuring the susceptibility of HIV-1 strains to various integrase inhibitors, confirming the resistance of selected strains to elvitegravir (EVG) and other integrase inhibitors through genotyping and phenotyping experiments; it references using the HIV-1 IIIB strain and in vitro assay testing, leading to the conclusion that they were susceptible. The selected strains were tested to confirm reappearance of Ghana, IIIB selected, or HIV-1 wild-type strains. This is done for level of inhibition and resistant strains, so this qualifiies as a report on drug susceptibility data. The genotypes were compared to standards so this classifies as drug susceptibility data being reported."
