23749954,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"We recently reported the preferential selection of the K65R resistance mutation in subtype C HIV-1 compared with subtype B and showed the underlying mechanism to be dependent on subtype C-specific silent nucleotide polymorphisms, i.e. genomic mutations that change the genotype but not the phenotype. The number of clinical reports demonstrating elevated numbers of K65R nevertheless suggests the existence of factors limiting the increased incidence of K65R mutations.","The paper does not mention any specific patient samples or sequences obtained from patients. It focuses on the genetic and resistance characteristics of HIV-1 subtype C and B in a laboratory setting, using NL4-3 clones and cell culture experiments."
23749954,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"The use of nucleoside/nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors [N(t)RTIs] as single drugs or in combination confirmed the more frequent selection of K65R by multiple N(t)RTIs in a subtype B virus that contained the 64/65 nucleotide polymorphisms of subtype C than in a wild-type subtype B virus. These single drug selections demonstrate that silent TAM nucleotide polymorphisms may be able to shift the resistance mutation selection pattern away from NL4-3 (64/65), which favours K65R.","The paper discusses the use of nucleoside/nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (N(t)RTIs) in drug selection experiments with various HIV-1 NL4-3 clones. It describes how these drugs select for the K65R mutation and demonstrates the differences in selection patterns between subtype B and C viruses in cell culture studies, indicating that in vitro susceptibility data were generated by showing the selection of resistance mutations under drug pressure."
23749954,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"We recently reported the preferential selection of the K65R resistance mutation in subtype C HIV-1 compared with subtype B and showed the underlying mechanism to be dependent on subtype C-specific silent nucleotide polymorphisms, i.e. genomic mutations that change the genotype but not the phenotype. The number of clinical reports demonstrating elevated numbers of K65R nevertheless suggests the existence of factors limiting the increased incidence of K65R mutations. Thus, we investigated the contributions of subtype C-specific silent nucleotide polymorphisms at thymidine analogue mutation (TAM) sites 70, 210 and/or 219 that might reduce the previously described preferential selection of K65R in subtype C HIV-1 associated with subtype C-specific nucleotide polymorphisms at sites 64/65.","The paper does not mention any specific public database or repository where the sequences can be accessed. The focus is on the mechanisms of resistance and the genetic characteristics of the virus, rather than on the public availability of sequences."
23749954,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates, indicating that no sequences were deposited in the database. The focus is on laboratory-generated NL4-3 clones and their resistance characteristics, rather than on patient-derived sequences."
23749954,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"We recently reported the preferential selection of the K65R resistance mutation in subtype C HIV-1 compared with subtype B and showed the underlying mechanism to be dependent on subtype C-specific silent nucleotide polymorphisms, i.e. genomic mutations that change the genotype but not the phenotype. The number of clinical reports demonstrating elevated numbers of K65R nevertheless suggests the existence of factors limiting the increased incidence of K65R mutations. Thus, we investigated the contributions of subtype C-specific silent nucleotide polymorphisms at thymidine analogue mutation (TAM) sites 70, 210 and/or 219 that might reduce the previously described preferential selection of K65R in subtype C HIV-1 associated with subtype C-specific nucleotide polymorphisms at sites 64/65. In a first round of selections, we included tenofovir, didanosine, stavudine, apricitabine and abacavir as single drugs for 20 weeks or until a first resistance mutation had appeared. We did not include lamivudine, emtricitabine and zidovudine, since no subtype-specific differences are expected with these drugs; i.e. lamivudine and emtricitabine preferentially select for M184I/V mutations, while zidovudine favours the development of TAMs, notably K70R. Selections with tenofovir resulted in the appearance of K65R in all viruses tested, whereas the four other drugs, i.e. didanosine, stavudine, apricitabine and abacavir, failed to select for K65R with NL4-3 (wt) but did select for K65R when NL4-3 (64/65) was employed, consistent with earlier results. In contrast, the preferential selection of K65R that was observed with NL4-3 (64/65) was attenuated in more than one-third of the TAM polymorphism-containing viruses that were exposed to didanosine, apricitabine or abacavir but not to stavudine","The paper does not provide any information on the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing, indicating that no clinical samples were used. The study focuses on laboratory-generated NL4-3 clones and their resistance characteristics."
23749954,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,0,"We recently reported the preferential selection of the K65R resistance mutation in subtype C HIV-1 compared with subtype B and showed the underlying mechanism to be dependent on subtype C-specific silent nucleotide polymorphisms, i.e. genomic mutations that change the genotype but not the phenotype. The number of clinical reports demonstrating elevated numbers of K65R nevertheless suggests the existence of factors limiting the increased incidence of K65R mutations. Thus, we investigated the contributions of subtype C-specific silent nucleotide polymorphisms at thymidine analogue mutation (TAM) sites 70, 210 and/or 219 that might reduce the previously described preferential selection of K65R in subtype C HIV-1 associated with subtype C-specific nucleotide polymorphisms at sites 64/65. In a first round of selections, we included tenofovir, didanosine, stavudine, apricitabine and abacavir as single drugs for 20 weeks or until a first resistance mutation had appeared. We did not include lamivudine, emtricitabine and zidovudine, since no subtype-specific differences are expected with these drugs; i.e. lamivudine and emtricitabine preferentially select for M184I/V mutations, while zidovudine favours the development of TAMs, notably K70R. Selections with tenofovir resulted in the appearance of K65R in all viruses tested, whereas the four other drugs, i.e. didanosine, stavudine, apricitabine and abacavir, failed to select for K65R with NL4-3 (wt) but did select for K65R when NL4-3 (64/65) was employed, consistent with earlier results. In contrast, the preferential selection of K65R that was observed with NL4-3 (64/65) was attenuated in more than one-third of the TAM polymorphism-containing viruses that were exposed to didanosine, apricitabine or abacavir but not to stavudine","The paper does not provide any information on the countries from which samples were obtained, as no clinical samples were used. The focus is on laboratory-generated NL4-3 clones and their resistance characteristics."
