24227862,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"An HIV-1 RNA template, ∼500 nucleotides (nt) in size and spanning the 5′ untranslated region (UTR) to the primer-binding site (PBS), was transcribed in vitro from AccI-linearized pHIV-PBS DNA. Recombinant HIV-1 wild-type (WT) and mutant viruses were generated by transfection of the corresponding proviral plasmid DNAs into HEK293T cells using Lipofectamine 2000 (Invitrogen, Burlington, ON, Canada) according to the manufacturer's instructions.","The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from patient samples. It focuses on laboratory investigations using recombinant HIV-1 viruses and cell culture systems, specifically looking at mutations in the reverse transcriptase gene and their effects on drug resistance and viral replication capacity. There is no indication that primary clinical samples were sequenced to obtain HIV sequences from patients."
24227862,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"Phenotypic susceptibility analyses of RT inhibitors were performed with recombinant HIV-1 ~NL4.3~ clones in a TZM-bl cell-based *in vitro* assay, as described previously ( 37 , 38 , 61 ). Recombinant RTs in heterodimeric form were expressed from plasmid pbRT6H-PROT and purified as described previously, with minor modifications. Etravirine (ETR) and rilpivirine (RPV) were gifts of Janssen Pharmaceuticals (Titusville, NJ).","The paper includes an in vitro assay to measure phenotypic susceptibility to RT inhibitors, including etravirine and rilpivirine. The results show the levels of resistance conferred by various RT mutations to these drugs, indicating that drug susceptibility data were obtained in vitro."
24227862,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from patient samples. It focuses on laboratory investigations using recombinant HIV-1 viruses and cell culture systems, specifically looking at mutations in the reverse transcriptase gene and their effects on drug resistance and viral replication capacity. There is no indication that primary clinical samples were sequenced to obtain HIV sequences from patients."
24227862,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No evidence,No evidence,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates, as it does not report on patient-derived sequences or their associated accession numbers. The focus is on laboratory-generated HIV-1 variants."
24227862,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,No evidence,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from patient samples. It focuses on laboratory investigations using recombinant HIV-1 viruses and cell culture systems, specifically looking at mutations in the reverse transcriptase gene and their effects on drug resistance and viral replication capacity. There is no indication that primary clinical samples were sequenced to obtain HIV sequences from patients."
24227862,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,No evidence,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from patient samples. It focuses on laboratory investigations using recombinant HIV-1 viruses and cell culture systems, specifically looking at mutations in the reverse transcriptase gene and their effects on drug resistance and viral replication capacity. There is no indication that primary clinical samples were sequenced to obtain HIV sequences from patients."
24227862,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,No evidence,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from patient samples. It focuses on laboratory investigations using recombinant HIV-1 viruses and cell culture systems, specifically looking at mutations in the reverse transcriptase gene and their effects on drug resistance and viral replication capacity. There is no indication that primary clinical samples were sequenced to obtain HIV sequences from patients."
24227862,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Recombinant HIV-1 wild-type (WT) and mutant viruses were generated by transfection of the corresponding proviral plasmid DNAs into HEK293T cells using Lipofectamine 2000 (Invitrogen, Burlington, ON, Canada) according to the manufacturer's instructions. Viral supernatants were harvested at 48 h posttransfection, centrifuged for 5 min at 800 × g to remove cellular debris, filtered through a 0.45-μm-pore-size filter, aliquoted, and stored at −80°C.",The paper does not mention any sequencing of patient samples or derived clones. It focuses on the effects of specific mutations on viral replication capacity and drug resistance in recombinant HIV-1 clonal viruses.
