26246578,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"Since many patients failing RAL/EVG are given DTG as part of rescue therapy, and given that the N155H substitution often is found in combination with other compensatory resistance mutations in such individuals, we investigated the effects of multiple such substitutions within integrase (IN) on each of integrase function, HIV-1 infectivity, and levels of drug resistance. We also wished to assess the viral infectivity of equivalent NL4.3 viruses in the presence or absence of RAL, EVG, and DTG.","The paper does not mention any HIV sequences obtained from patient samples. Instead, it focuses on laboratory-generated HIV-1 vectors and recombinant integrase proteins to study drug resistance and viral infectivity in the presence of integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs). The emphasis is on in vitro studies and biochemical assays rather than clinical specimens from patients, indicating that no patient-derived HIV sequences are reported in this study."
26246578,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"We have previously shown that the addition of the raltegravir/elvitegavir (RAL/EVG) primary resistance mutation N155H to the R263K dolutegravir (DTG) resistance mutation partially compensated for the fitness cost imposed by R263K while also slightly increasing DTG resistance in vitro (K. Anstett, T. Mesplede, M. Oliveira, V. Cutillas, and M. A. Wainberg, J Virol 89: 4681--4684, 2015, doi: 10.1128/JVI.03485-14). HIV susceptibilities to DTG, RAL, and EVG were measured by the infection of 30,000 TZM-bl cells using 100,000 RT units per well of each virus in the presence of 1:10 serial dilutions of drugs.","The paper mentions  Drug suceptibility in the context of strande transfer inhibitors in the text 'HIV susceptibilities to DTG, RAL, and EVG were measured by the infection of 30,000 TZM-bl cells using 100,000 RT units per well of each virus in the presence of 1:10 serial dilutions of drugs. We have previously shown that the addition of the raltegravir/elvitegavir (RAL/EVG) primary resistance mutation N155H to the R263K dolutegravir (DTG) resistance mutation partially compensated for the fitness cost imposed by R263K while also slightly increasing DTG resistance in vitro'...which indicates in vitro drug susceptibility data for DTG, RAL and EVG were reported by meassuriing GA3 inhibition and luciferase production. The paper indeed reports in vitro drug susceptibility data, specifically evaluating the susceptibility of genetically modified NL4.3 HIV-1 viruses harboring various integrase mutations to integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs) such as dolutegravir (DTG), raltegravir (RAL), and elvitegravir (EVG) using a TZM-bl assay and measuring luciferase production as an indicator of viral infectivity.  So the paper does reports in vitro drug susceptibility data.  The experimental design describes the use of TZM-bl and 293T cells to assess HIV infectivity and strand transfer activity of integrase proteins, and the results sections detail the findings of resistance assays and infectivity measurements in the presence of these drugs. These sections collectively confirm that the paper reports in vitro drug susceptibility data.  The experimental design and methods described in the paper are focused on the effects of genetic mutations on integrase activity and HIV-1 infectivity in response to drug treatment, confirming the paper's in vitro nature of the study."
