26246578,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"Since many patients failing RAL/EVG are given DTG as part of rescue therapy, and given that the N155H substitution often is found in combination with other compensatory resistance mutations in such individuals, we investigated the effects of multiple such substitutions within integrase (IN) on each of integrase function, HIV-1 infectivity, and levels of drug resistance. To this end, each of the L74M, E92Q, T97A, E157Q, and G163R substitutions were introduced into NL4.3 subtype B HIV-1 vectors harboring N155H and R263K in tandem. The generation of NL4.3 HIV-1 viral clones. The generation of the pNL4.3 ~IN(N155H/R263K)~ plasmid has been reported previously ( 10 ). Similar methods were used to generate other pNL4.3IN ~(mutant)~ plasmids through site-directed mutagenesis using the primers listed in Table 1. Gen genetically homogenous viral stocks were produced as described previously ( 7 ). Briefly, 293T cells were transfected with the various pNL4.3 plasmids using Lipofectamine 2000. At 48 h after transfection, cell culture supernatants were collected and filtered at 0.45 microm to remove plasmids and cell debris. Viruses then were aliquoted and stored at −80°C. Viral stocks were quantified by measuring cell-free reverse transcriptase (RT) activity in culture fluids",The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen. It focuses on laboratory-generated HIV-1 vectors and recombinant integrase proteins rather than clinical specimens.
