27009474,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We describe an individual with transmitted four-class drug resistance whose virus sequence had the previously uncharacterized mutation Q148N. In 2015, we created a panel of infectious molecular clones to assess the effect of the novel mutation Q148N on INSTI susceptibility. The panel included site-directed HIV-1 mutants containing G140S+Q148N, Q148N alone, G140S+Q148H, and Q148H alone. It also contained a recombinant infectious molecular clone from the patient’s August 2014 sample and clones in which G140S alone and G140S and Q148N were independently mutated back to the wild type residues at these positions.","The paper describes an individual with transmitted four-class drug resistance whose virus sequence had the previously uncharacterized mutation Q148N. This indicates that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples, as the study focuses on the genetic analysis of the virus from a specific patient case."
27009474,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"In 2015, we created a panel of infectious molecular clones to assess the effect of the novel mutation Q148N on INSTI susceptibility. The panel included site-directed HIV-1 mutants containing G140S+Q148N, Q148N alone, G140S+Q148H, and Q148H alone. It also contained a recombinant infectious molecular clone from the patient’s August 2014 sample and clones in which G140S alone and G140S and Q148N were independently mutated back to the wild type residues at these positions. These clones were subjected for phenotypic susceptibility testing using the PhenoSense assay (Monogram).1","The paper mentions phenotypic susceptibility testing using the PhenoSense assay, which indicates that in vitro drug susceptibility data was generated as part of the study."
27009474,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,The sequence of the clinical clone has been submitted to the GenBank under the accession number KU761565.,"The Sequence Data section explicitly states that the sequence of the clinical clone has been submitted to GenBank, indicating that the sequences from the paper were made publicly available."
27009474,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,KU761565,The sequence of the clinical clone has been submitted to the GenBank under the accession number KU761565.,"The Sequence Data section of the paper provides the GenBank accession number for the sequenced HIV isolate, which is KU761565."
27009474,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,One,We describe an individual with transmitted four-class drug resistance whose virus sequence had the previously uncharacterized mutation Q148N.,"The paper focuses on a single individual with transmitted four-class drug resistance, indicating that only one person's samples were obtained for HIV sequencing."
27009474,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,United States,"A 50-year-old hispanic homosexual man underwent HIV-1 seroconversion in August 2014. His CD4 count at the time of diagnosis was 405 cells/mm3 and his plasma HIV-1 RNA level was 3,980 copies/ml. In November 2014, the patient changed clinics and an INSTI resistance test was ordered on his August 2014 sample.",The patient in the study is a 50-year-old Hispanic homosexual man who underwent HIV-1 seroconversion in August 2014 and is receiving treatment in the United States.
27009474,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2014,"A 50-year-old hispanic homosexual man underwent HIV-1 seroconversion in August 2014. In November 2014, the patient changed clinics and an INSTI resistance test was ordered on his August 2014 sample.","The paper states that the patient underwent HIV-1 seroconversion in August 2014 and that an INSTI resistance test was ordered on his August 2014 sample in November 2014, indicating that the sequenced samples were obtained in 2014."
27009474,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,Yes,"In 2015, we created a panel of infectious molecular clones to assess the effect of the novel mutation Q148N on INSTI susceptibility. The panel included site-directed HIV-1 mutants containing G140S+Q148N, Q148N alone, G140S+Q148H, and Q148H alone. It also contained a recombinant infectious molecular clone from the patient’s August 2014 sample and clones in which G140S alone and G140S and Q148N were independently mutated back to the wild type residues at these positions.","The paper mentions the creation of a panel of infectious molecular clones in 2015 to assess the effect of the novel mutation Q148N on INSTI susceptibility, which indicates that samples were cloned prior to sequencing."
27009474,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Integrase,We describe an individual with transmitted four-class drug resistance whose virus sequence had the previously uncharacterized mutation Q148N. Q148 is conserved in all lentiviruses and plays an essential role in integrase strand transfer activity.,"The paper focuses on the integrase enzyme and mentions the effect of mutations in the integrase-binding pocket, indicating that the integrase gene was sequenced."
27009474,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not specified,No sequencing method is detailed in the provided text.,"The paper does not provide specific details about the sequencing method used for the HIV samples, focusing instead on the resistance mutations and their effects."
27009474,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"In November 2014, the patient changed clinics and an INSTI resistance test was ordered on his August 2014 sample. At the same time, the plasma HIV-1 RNA level was <50 copies/ml and CD4 count was 767 cells/mm3. Although the patient’s genotypic resistance test was reported as being associated with intermediate resistance to raltegravir and elvitegravir based on the presence of G140S, no change to therapy was made because of the patient’s virological and immunological response to therapy. The patient has since been monitored closely and multiple subsequent plasma HIV-1 RNA levels have been <50 copies/ml.",The paper mentioned that the patient’s HIV-1 RNA level was measured which indicates that plasma samples were sequenced.
27009474,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"A baseline genotypic resistance test revealed the nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor-resistance mutations L210W and T215D that were interpreted as causing intermediate resistance to zidovudine and low-level resistance to tenofovir; the non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI)-resistance mutations K101P and K103S that were interpreted as causing high-level resistance to each of the NNRTIs; and the protease inhibitor-resistance mutations D30N, L33F, I54V, N88D, and L90M that were interpreted as causing intermediate or high-level resistance to each of the PIs except darunavir. The patient was started on the once daily integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI)-containing fixed-dose combination tenofovir/emtricitabine/elvitegravir/cobicistat. At the same time, the plasma HIV-1 RNA level was <50 copies/ml and CD4 count was 767 cells/mm3.  In November 2014, the patient changed clinics and an INSTI resistance test was ordered on his August 2014 sample. The test revealed G140S, a nonpolymorphic accessory INSTI-resistance mutation, and Q148N, a previously uncharacterized mutation in the INSTI-binding pocket of integrase enzyme.","The paper describes the patient's genotypic resistance test showing various resistance mutations, and the patient was on resistance drugs, which indicates that sequences were obtained from individual with virological failure."
27009474,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Q148 is conserved in all lentiviruses and plays an essential role in integrase strand transfer activity. Mutations at this position are arguably the most important INSTI-associated resistance mutations as they represent the first step in the pathway to high-level dolutegravir cross-resistance. Of 1,120 integrase sequences from patients receiving raltegravir in GenBank as of June 2015, 23%, 8%, and 1% had the previously characterized INSTI-resistance mutations Q148H, Q148R, and Q148K, respectively. Q148N was present in viruses from three raltegravir-experienced individuals and is the only other mutation at this position detected in GenBank sequences. Q148N is also the only mutation other than Q148H/R/K that has been shown to have strand transfer activity in biochemical assays. There have been too few published sequences obtained from elvitegravir-experienced individuals to determine whether Q148N occurs at a higher frequency after elvitegravir treatment than after raltegravir treatment.",The paper does not mention that the patient was part of a clinical trial. It describes a case report of a single patient with unique resistance mutations and their clinical implications.
27009474,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"We describe an individual with transmitted four-class drug resistance whose virus sequence had the previously uncharacterized mutation Q148N. The patient was started on the once daily integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI)-containing fixed-dose combination tenofovir/emtricitabine/elvitegravir/cobicistat. In November 2014, the patient changed clinics and an INSTI resistance test was ordered on his August 2014 sample. The test revealed G140S, a nonpolymorphic accessory INSTI-resistance mutation, and Q148N, a previously uncharacterized mutation in the INSTI-binding pocket of integrase enzyme.","The paper describes a patient with transmitted four-class drug resistance, and the patient was started on tenofovir/emtricitabine/elvitegravir/cobicistat which means the patient had previously received ARV drugs."
27009474,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI","A baseline genotypic resistance test revealed the nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor-resistance mutations L210W and T215D that were interpreted as causing intermediate resistance to zidovudine and low-level resistance to tenofovir; the non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI)-resistance mutations K101P and K103S that were interpreted as causing high-level resistance to each of the NNRTIs; and the protease inhibitor-resistance mutations D30N, L33F, I54V, N88D, and L90M that were interpreted as causing intermediate or high-level resistance to each of the PIs except darunavir. The patient was started on the once daily integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI)-containing fixed-dose combination tenofovir/emtricitabine/elvitegravir/cobicistat.","The paper lists specific resistance mutations associated with NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs, indicating that the patient received drugs from these four drug classes before sequencing."
27009474,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Zidovudine, tenofovir, emtricitabine, elvitegravir, cobicistat","A baseline genotypic resistance test revealed the nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor-resistance mutations L210W and T215D that were interpreted as causing intermediate resistance to zidovudine and low-level resistance to tenofovir; the non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI)-resistance mutations K101P and K103S that were interpreted as causing high-level resistance to each of the NNRTIs; and the protease inhibitor-resistance mutations D30N, L33F, I54V, N88D, and L90M that were interpreted as causing intermediate or high-level resistance to each of the PIs except darunavir. The patient was started on the once daily integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI)-containing fixed-dose combination tenofovir/emtricitabine/elvitegravir/cobicistat.","The paper mentions specific drugs, tenofovir, emtricitabine, elvitegravir, cobicistat were received by individuals in the study."
27124362,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The ability of RPV and DOR to inhibit the replication of WT HIV-1 and drug-resistant mutants was measured using a previously described single-round infection assay [19]. We initially investigated the L100I, K103N, Y181C, Y188L, H221Y, and K103N/Y181C RT mutants. These mutants were chosen because they have been selected in patients and are well distributed around the NNRTI binding pocket [1, 2].","The paper does not mention that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples. Instead, it focuses on the antiviral activities of RPV and DOR against HIV-1 mutants in vitro, using assays that do not involve patient-derived sequences."
27124362,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"Using a single round HIV-1 infection assay, we tested RPV and DOR against a broad panel of NNRTI resistant mutants to determine their respective activities. Virion production and single-round infectivity assays were used to determine antiviral activity (IC50 values) of the compounds as described [19] and discussed in the supplemental information. The ability of RPV and DOR to inhibit the replication of WT HIV-1 and drug-resistant mutants was measured using a previously described single-round infection assay [19]. We initially investigated the L100I, K103N, Y181C, Y188L, H221Y, and K103N/Y181C RT mutants.","The paper discusses the use of single-round infection assays to measure the antiviral activity (IC50 values) of RPV and DOR against various HIV-1 mutants, indicating that in vitro drug susceptibility data were generated."
27124362,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The ability of RPV and DOR to inhibit the replication of WT HIV-1 and drug-resistant mutants was measured using a previously described single-round infection assay [19]. We initially investigated the L100I, K103N, Y181C, Y188L, H221Y, and K103N/Y181C RT mutants. These mutants were chosen because they have been selected in patients and are well distributed around the NNRTI binding pocket [1, 2].","The paper does not mention that sequences were made publicly available. It focuses on the antiviral activities of RPV and DOR against HIV-1 mutants in vitro, using assays that do not involve publicly available sequences."
