29373677,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"In this study, we report the case of a patient treated with dolutegravir monotherapy. The patient infected with a B subtype HIV-1 strain received an antiretroviral treatment including INSTIs from 2008 to 2015, a period during which CD4 counts had increased from 350 to 940 cells/mm [3].","The paper discusses the case of a patient treated with dolutegravir monotherapy, and it mentions the genotypic analysis of integrase mutations in DNA and RNA from the patient's samples. This indicates that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
29373677,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,Hightower and colleagues have shown that dissociation of dolutegravir from the IN-DNA complex was slower compared with raltegravir and elvitegravir whichever integrase mutations were tested. We increased the incubation time of the strand-transfer reaction from 2 h to 48 h to evaluate its impact on the dolutegravir effect. We performed a time-course 3'-processing experiment with the different mutants to confirm their susceptibility to dolutegravir or raltegravir.,"The paper mentions resistance experiments using a 19-mer DNA (U5B-2) mimicking the 3'-processed DNA. The incubation for 2 h at 37uC, usually used, failed to reveal significant resistance to dolutegravir for any of the mutants. Susceptibility of mutants to dolutegravir (left panel) and raltegravir (right panel) were determined by assessing IN activity in the presence of various concentrations of INSTIs using radiolabelled oligonucleotides U5B and U5B-2 mimicking the unprocessed and 3'-processed viral ends, respectively, as previously described. Laboratory assays measuring the susceptibility of viral strains to antiretroviral agents are known as drug susceptibility assays or drug resistance assays. These assays help determine whether a virus is susceptible (sensitive) or resistant to a particular drug. The paper discusses the time-dependent effect of dolutegravir on mutants, showing an increase in IC50 values over time, which indicates a form of susceptibility measurement. Thus, it can be inferred that the paper does report in vitro drug susceptibility data, albeit indirectly. We performed resistance experiments using a 19-mer DNA (U5B-2) mimicking the 3'-processed DNA. The incubation for 2 h at 37°C, usually used, failed to reveal significant resistance to dolutegravir for any of the mutants (Figure 1 a, right panel). Thus, we performed a time-course 3'-processing experiment with the different mutants to confirm their susceptibility to dolutegravir or raltegravir. Esc(50) values for both raltegravir and dolutegravir are reported. The graph presented is a representative experiment from three independent experiments, P < 0.05. RAL, raltegravir; DTG, dolutegravir. We increased the incubation time of the strand-transfer reaction from 2 h to 48 h to evaluate its impact on the dolutegravir effect. Increasing the incubation time up to 48 h did not influence the IC50 of dolutegravir for the WT (8 nM) highlighting the strong stability of the compound in the IN-DNA complex. Figure 1a, right panel. We performed resistance experiments using a 19-mer DNA (U5B-2) mimicking the 3'-processed DNA. The incubation for 2 h at 37°C, usually used, failed to reveal significant resistance to dolutegravir for any of the mutants (Figure 1a, right panel). We increased the incubation time of the strand-transfer reaction from 2 h to 48 h to evaluate its impact on the dolutegravir effect. The patient infected with a B subtype HIV-1 strain received an antiretroviral treatment including INSTIs from 2008 to 2015, a period during which CD4 counts had increased from 350 to 940 cells/mm(3). Dolutegravir resistance. Effect of dolutegravir on IN mutants studied with a long incubation times. We first investigated the replicative properties of mutant viruses. The p24gag production of these mutants was similar to the WT showing that none of these mutations impaired the late steps of viral replication (Figure 1a, left panel and black columns). The early steps of HIV-1 replication were investigated by HeLa-P4 cell infection (Figure 1a, left panel and grey columns). Infections were performed on MT4 cells (2 x 106 cells/mL with 20 ng of p24gag antigen per 106 cells) with or without drugs. Four hours post-infection, cells were washed in PBS and new medium was added. At various times post-infection, 1-2 million cells were harvested and dry pellets were frozen at -80uC. Characterization of the mutations with cell-free and cell-based assays. We first investigated the replicative properties of mutant viruses. The p24gag production of these mutants was similar to the WT showing that none of these mutations impaired the late steps of viral replication. The above-mentioned mutations could affect the 3'-processing and/or the strand-transfer reaction mediated by IN. Our cell-free reaction results demonstrate that the 3'-processing efficiency for all mutants was similar but significantly lower compared with the WT (an average of 50% of the WT, Figure 1a middle panel and black columns). However, the strand-transfer efficiency was not affected by any mutations (Figure 1a, middle panel and grey columns). Thus, all the mutations presented in this report slightly impair the 3'-processing but not the strand-transfer reaction. Therefore, the paper mentions experiments involving the susceptibility of HIV-1 integrase mutants to integrase strand-transfer inhibitors (INSTIs) like raltegravir and dolutegravir, which are typically used to assess drug resistance. This indicates that the paper does report in vitro drug susceptibility data. Hence, the answer is 'Yes'."
29373677,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,Successfully characterized IRC analysis on PBMC-infected cells and viral RNA from plasma during failure of dolutegravir monotherapy the sample obtained for viral sequencing was made available on GenBank under number KX955681.,"The paper mentions that the sequences were made available in GenBank, which indicates that the sequences from the paper were made publicly available."
29373677,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The GenBank accession number for the sequenced HIV isolate is KX955681.,"kHIV-1 sequences obtained from patient samples were subjected to IRC analysis on PBMC-infected cells and viral RNA from plasma during failure of dolutegravir monotherapy. The sample obtained for viral sequencing was made available on GenBank under number KX955681 as previously described. Acknowledgmenets. We thank J-Ph. V. M. F, M. T. A, J L. C., and A. J. S. (NSF NIRT No. 1140250) for inspiring discussions and B. P. for his skilful help. This work was supported by the Agence Nationale de Recherches sur le SIDA et les Hépatites Virales (ANRS), A02011-01.",The paper mentioned that KX955681 is the GenBank accession number.
29373677,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,1,"In this study, we report the case of a patient treated with dolutegravir monotherapy. The patient infected with a B subtype HIV-1 strain received an antiretroviral treatment including INSTIs from 2008 to 2015, a period during which CD4 counts had increased from 350 to 940 cells/mm [3].","The paper discusses the case of a single patient treated with dolutegravir monotherapy, and the genotypic analysis was performed on samples from this one patient."
29373677,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,France,"The patient's demographic and clinical data would be recorded during their follow-up and their blood samples would be stored and could be used for retrospective studies. Patients were informed that their. France. Funding This work was supported by the Agence nationale de recherches sur le sida et les hépatites virales (ANRS), A02011-01.","The paper mentioned that the study was supported by the Agence Nationale de Recherches sur le SIDA et les Hépatites Virales (ANRS), which is in France. Therefore, it can be inferred that the samples were obtained from France."
29373677,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2008 to 2015,"The patient infected with a B subtype HIV-1 strain received an antiretroviral treatment including INSTIs from 2008 to 2015, a period during which CD4 counts had increased from 350 to 940 cells/mm [3].","The paper discusses the case of a patient who received antiretroviral treatment including INSTIs from 2008 to 2015, and the genotypic analysis was performed during this period."
29373677,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"HIV-1 integrase inhibitors gene, HIV-1 IN mutants were obtained by site-directed mutagenesis as previously described.13 HIV-1 stocks was prepared by 293-T cell transfections and HIV-1 p24gag antigen was quantified as previously described. Characterization of integrase enzymatic activity in cell-free reactions Recombinant IN was purified under non-denaturing conditions, as previously described.17 The susceptibility of IN to raltegravir and dolutegravir was determined in cell-free reactions by assessing IN activity in the presence of various concentrations of INSTIs using radiolabelled oligonucleotides U5B and U5B-2 mimicking the unprocessed and 3'-processed viral ends, respectively, as previously described.13 IC50 values were determined with Prism 5.0 software.",The lab passage was described. Clone before sequencing was not mentioned.
29373677,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,IN gene,"In this study, we report the case of a patient treated with dolutegravir monotherapy. Failure of dolutegravir treatment was observed concomitant with the appearance of N155H-K211R-E212T mutations in the integrase (IN) gene in addition to the polymorphic K156N mutation that was present at baseline in this patient The patient infected with a B subtype HIV-1 strain received an antiretroviral treatment including INSTIs from 2008 to 2015, a period during which CD4 counts had increased from 350 to 940 cells/mm.","The paper discusses the genotypic analysis of integrase (IN) mutations in the patient, indicating that the IN gene was sequenced."
29373677,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not mentioned,"Dolutegravir, an integrase strand-transfer inhibitor (STI), shows a high genetic barrier to resistance. Dolutegravir is reported to be effective against viruses resistant to raltegravir and elvitegravir. In this study, we report the case of a patient treated with dolutegravir monotherapy. Failure of dolutegravir treatment was observed concomitant with the appearance of N155H-K211R-E212T mutations in the integrase (IN) gene in addition to the polymorphic K156N mutation that was present at baseline in this patient. The impact of N155H-K156N-K211R-E212T mutations was studied in cell-free, culture-based assays and by molecular modelling. Results Cell-free and culture-based assays confirm that selected mutations in the patient, in the context of the polymorphic mutation K156N present at the baseline, lead to high resistance to dolutegravir requiring that the analysis be done at timepoints longer than usual to properly reveal the results. Interestingly, the association of only N155H and K156N is sufficient for significant resistance to dolutegravir. Modelling studies showed that dolutegravir is less stable in IN/DNA complexes with respect to the WT sequence.",The paper does not mention the exact method used for sequencing the HIV isolates.
29373677,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,"Blood, PBMC","Information was obtained from the existing electronic database or medical records. Patients were informed that their demographic and clinical data would be recorded during their follow-up and their blood samples would be stored and could be used for retrospective studies and they gave their consent. Integrase genotypic analysis was performed on DNA from PBMCs, when the plasma viral load was below 20 copies/mL and on plasma RNA when it was detectable above 20 copies/mL.","The paper mentions that blood, plasma was collected from patient for genotypic analysis. It suggests that clinical specimens were sequenced."
29373677,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,Conclusions Our results indicate that the stability of STI IN/DNA complexes is an important parameter that must be taken into account when evaluating dolutegravir resistance. This study confirms that a pathway including N155H can be selected in patients treated with dolutegravir with the help of the polymorphic K156N that acts as a secondary mutation that enhances the resistance to dolutegravir.,"The paper discusses the case of a patient who experienced virological failure while on dolutegravir monotherapy, mentioning the appearance of resistance mutations in the integrase gene."
29373677,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"In this study, we report the case of a patient treated with dolutegravir monotherapy Patients were informed that their demographic and clinical data would be recorded during their follow-up and their blood samples would be stored and could be used for retrospective studies and they gave their consent.1 Insertion of SI-7002 in a vector confers dolutegravir resistance in vitro in a time-dependent manner\nIn vitro assays were performed to determine the effect of dolutegravir on the phosphorylated integrase. We increased the incubation time of the strand-transfer reaction from 2 h to 48 h to evaluate its impact on the dolutegravir effect. Infections were performed on MT4 cells (2 x 106 cells/mL with 20 ng of p24gag antigen per 106 cells) with or without drugs. Four hours post-infection, cells were washed in PBS and new medium was added.\n\nWe first investigated the replicative properties of mutant viruses. The p24gag production of these mutants was similar to the WT showing that none of these mutations impaired the late steps of viral replication (Figure 1a, left panel and black columns). The early steps of HIV-1 replication were investigated by HeLa-P4 cell infection (Figure 1a, left panel and grey columns)\n. in vitro assays were performed to determine the effect of dolutegravir on the phosphorylated integrase. We first investigated the replicative properties of mutant viruses. Susceptibility of IN to raltegravir and dolutegravir was determined in cell-free reactions by assessing IN activity in the presence of various concentrations of INSTIs using radiolabelled oligonucleotides U5B and U5B-2 mimicking the unprocessed and 3'-processed viral ends, respectively, as previously described.13 IC50 values were determined with Prism 5.0 software.



Hightower and colleagues have shown that dissociation of dolutegravir from the IN-DNA complex was slower compared with raltegravir and elvitegravir whichever integrase mutations were tested. In vitro echinocandin synergy assay.  Mechanism of action of echinocandins derive from a phenotypic assay. Echinocandin toposis. Echinocandin resistance. Echinocandin. We first investigated the replicative properties of mutant viruses. The p24gag production of these mutants was similar to the WT showing that none of these mutations impaired the late steps of viral replication (Figure 1a, left panel and black columns).",The paper does not mention that the patient was part of a clinical trial. It describes the case of a patient treated with dolutegravir monotherapy as part of standard care.
29373677,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"The patient infected with a B subtype HIV-1 strain received an antiretroviral treatment including INSTIs from 2008 to 2015, a period during which CD4 counts had increased from 350 to 940 cells/mm **3**. The patient was first treated with raltegravir in combination with tenofovir/emtricitabine (Truvada) for 6 years until 2014, during which time the viral load remained undetectable (plasma HIV RNA < 20 copies/mL). Every 6 months, during follow-up, genotypic analysis of integrase was tested in DNA and never showed any mutations selected against raltegravir.","The paper discusses the case of a patient who received various antiretroviral treatments, including raltegravir and tenofovir/emtricitabine, before being treated with dolutegravir monotherapy."
29373677,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, and integrate inhibitor","The patient infected with a B subtype HIV-1 strain received an antiretroviral treatment including INSTIs from 2008 to 2015, a period during which CD4 counts had increased from 350 to 940 cells/mm [3]. The patient was first treated with raltegravir in combination with tenofovir/emtricitabine (Truvada) for 6 years until 2014, during which time the viral load remained undetectable (plasma HIV RNA <20 copies/mL).","The paper mentions that the patient was treated with raltegravir (an integrase inhibitor), tenofovir, and emtricitabine (both NRTIs), and later with dolutegravir (another integrase inhibitor). The drug received were side to raltegravir and integrase inhibitor, all three drugs are INSTIs, the paper doesn't mention other drugs received."
29373677,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Raltegravir, tenofovir, emtricitabine, dolutegravir","The patient infected with a B subtype HIV-1 strain received an antiretroviral treatment including INSTIs from 2008 to 2015, a period during which CD4 counts had increased from 350 to 940 cells/mm [3]. The patient was first treated with raltegravir in combination with tenofovir/emtricitabine (Truvada) for 6 years until 2014, during which time the viral load remained undetectable (plasma HIV RNA <20 copies/mL). Every 6 months, during follow-up, genotypic analysis of integrase was tested in DNA and never showed any mutations selected against raltegravir. Owing to treatment intolerance, a simplification of treatment with dolutegravir monotherapy (not currently recommended in any guidelines for HIV-1 treatment) was proposed to the patient in 2014 while the viral load was still undetectable.","The paper mentions that the patient was treated with raltegravir (an integrase inhibitor), tenofovir, and emtricitabine (both NRTIs), and later with dolutegravir (another integrase inhibitor). The drug received were side to raltegravir and integrase inhibitor, all three drugs are INSTIs, the paper doesn't mention other drugs received."
30053052,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"The chimeric viruses derived from therapy-naive patients ( *n* = 24) were used to investigate the susceptibility to six RTI drugs: EFdA, nevirapine, efavirenz, etravirine, rilpivirine and tenofovir alafenamide (Figure ​ (Figure1a). 1 a). Finally we obtained 24 replication-competent viruses from treatment-naive individuals who were pure subtypes (HIV-1A1, *n* = 1; HIV-1B, *n* = 5; HIV-1C, *n* = 15) or circulating recombinant forms (CRFs) (01_AE, *n* = 1; 02_AG, *n* = 2), whilst the viruses extracted from patients with therapy failure ( *n* = 3) were HIV-1B ( *n* = 1), HIV-1D ( *n* =  1) and HIV-1C ( *n* = 1).","The evidence clearly states that chimeric viruses derived from therapy-naive patients were used, indicating that HIV sequences from patient samples were reported in the paper."
30053052,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,In vitro inhibition assays were done using patient-derived reverse transcriptase. IC50 values of NNRTIs were calculated using a PicoGreen-based spectrophotometric assay. Steady-state kinetics were used to determine the apparent binding affinity (Km.dNTP) of triphosphate form of EFdA (EFdA-TP) and dATP.,"The paper discusses in vitro inhibition assays and phenotypic drug susceptibility assays, indicating that in vitro drug susceptibility data were reported."
30053052,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,Paper did not state if the sequence is publicly available.
30053052,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No evidence,No evidence,Paper did not mention GenBank accession numbers.
30053052,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,24,"Finally we obtained 24 replication-competent viruses from treatment-naive individuals who were pure subtypes (HIV-1A1, *n* = 1; HIV-1B, *n* = 5; HIV-1C, *n* = 15) or circulating recombinant forms (CRFs) (01_AE, *n* = 1; 02_AG, *n* = 2).","The paper states that 24 replication-competent viruses were obtained from treatment-naive individuals, indicating that samples from 24 individuals were used for HIV sequencing."
30053052,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Sweden,"Stored plasma samples from patients who were either therapy naive ( *n* = 42) or had a virological treatment failure ( *n* = 9) were randomly selected from the HIV cohort at Karolinska University Hospital, Stockholm, Sweden.","The paper specifies that clinical specimens were obtained from patients at Karolinska University Hospital in Stockholm, Sweden."
30053052,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,No evidence,No evidence,The paper does not provide specific years during which the samples were obtained.
30053052,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,Yes,"The gag-pol fragment (HXB2: 0702-5798) was cloned into pNL43 plasmid following digestion with BssHII and SalI (New England Biolabs, USA) and ligation using T4 DNA ligase (New England Biolabs), as described by us previously. Patient-derived HIV-1 RT enzymes were used in NNRTI susceptibility and kinetics assays. Briefly, The RT genes of different subtypes were PCR amplified from DNA amplicons of viruses isolated from patients infected with diverse subtypes. For the cloning of the p66 subunit, NcoI and SalI restriction sites were added, except for 02_AG, for which primers were designed to add the SalI restriction site. For the cloning of the p51 subunit, BamHI and SalI restriction sites were added.","The paper describes the cloning of the gag-pol fragment into the pNL43 plasmid, which is a common method used to produce infectious clones of HIV, indicating that samples were cloned prior to sequencing."
30053052,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,gag-pol,"The gag-pol fragment (HXB2: 0702-5798) was cloned into pNL43 plasmid following digestion with BssHII and SalI (New England Biolabs, USA) and ligation using T4 DNA ligase (New England Biolabs), as described by us previously. Chimeric viruses encoding gag-pol from treatment-naive patients (n=24) and therapy-failure patients (n=3) and a panel of reverse transcriptase inhibitor-resistant strains (n=7) were used to compare the potency of reverse transcriptase inhibitor drugs.","The paper mentions the sequencing of the gag-pol fragment, and using reverse transcriptase inhibitor, which means pol gene."
30053052,10,What method was used for sequencing?,No evidence,No evidence,The paper does not detail the specific sequencing method used.
30053052,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Stored plasma samples from patients who were either therapy naive ( *n* = 42) or had a virological treatment failure ( *n* = 9) were randomly selected from the HIV cohort at Karolinska University Hospital, Stockholm, Sweden. Briefly, viral RNA was extracted using the QIAmp viral RNA extraction kit (Qiagen, Hilden, Germany) from 140 μL of plasma. The gag-pol fragment (HXB2: 0702-5798) was cloned into pNL43 plasmid following digestion with BssHII and SalI (New England Biolabs, USA) and ligation using T4 DNA ligase (New England Biolabs), as described by us previously.","The paper specifies that stored plasma samples from patients were used, indicating that plasma was the clinical specimen sequenced."
30053052,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Stored plasma samples from patients who were either therapy naive ( *n* = 42) or had a virological treatment failure ( *n* = 9) were randomly selected from the HIV cohort at Karolinska University Hospital, Stockholm, Sweden. Finally we obtained 24 replication-competent viruses from treatment-naive individuals who were pure subtypes (HIV-1A1, *n* = 1; HIV-1B, *n* = 5; HIV-1C, *n* = 15) or circulating recombinant forms (CRFs) (01_AE, *n* = 1; 02_AG, *n* = 2), whilst the viruses extracted from patients with therapy failure (n=3) were HIV-1B (n=1), HIV-1D (n= 1) and HIV-1C (n=1).",The paper states that samples from patients with virological treatment failure were included in the study.
30053052,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Stored plasma samples from patients who were either therapy naive ( *n* = 42) or had a virological treatment failure ( *n* = 9) were randomly selected from the HIV cohort at Karolinska University Hospital, Stockholm, Sweden. Ethics clearance for the study was obtained from the Regional Ethics Committee of Stockholm, Sweden (Dnr 2014/928-31/2 and 2013/1944-31/4). All participants gave informed consent.","The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial, samples were they were from cohort."
30053052,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Stored plasma samples from patients who were either therapy naive ( *n* = 42) or had a virological treatment failure ( *n* = 9) were randomly selected from the HIV cohort at Karolinska University Hospital, Stockholm, Sweden. Finally we obtained 24 replication-competent viruses from treatment-naive individuals who were pure subtypes (HIV-1A1, *n* = 1; HIV-1B, *n* = 5; HIV-1C, *n* = 15) or circulating recombinant forms (CRFs) (01_AE, *n* = 1; 02_AG, *n* = 2), whilst the viruses extracted from patients with therapy failure (n=3) were HIV-1B (n=1), HIV-1D (n= 1) and HIV-1C (n=1).","The paper states that samples were obtained from therapy-failure patients, indicating that HIV sequences from previously treated individuals were reported."
30053052,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs","Among the chimeric treatment-naive viruses, EFdA had an ex vivo antiretroviral activity [median (IQR) EC50 = 1.4 nM (0.6–2.1 nM)] comparable to that of tenofovir alafenamide [1.6 nM (0.5–3.6 nM)]. Subtype-specific differences were found for etravirine (P = 0.004) and rilpivirine (P = 0.017), where HIV-1C had the highest EC50 values. Ex vivo analysis on reverse transcriptase inhibitor-resistant strains showed EFdA to have a higher potency. Stored plasma samples from patients who were either therapy naive (n = 42) or had a virological treatment failure (n = 9) were randomly selected from the HIV cohort at Karolinska University Hospital, Stockholm, Sweden. The three viruses extracted from therapy-failure patients had the following mutations: HIV-1D, K65R, T69del, L100I, Y181C, Y188L, H221Y; HIV-1C, K65R, M184V, V108I, Y181C, G190S; HIV-1B, M41L, D67N, K70R, M184V, L210W, T215Y, K219E, K103S and G190A","The paper discusses the use of reverse transcriptase inhibitor drugs The three viruses extracted from therapy-failure patients had the following mutations: HIV-1D, K65R, T69del, L100I, Y181C, Y188L, H221Y; HIV-1C, K65R, M184V, V108I, Y181C, G190S; HIV-1B, M41L, D67N, K70R, M184V, L210W, T215Y, K219E, K103S and G190A   . K65R is a NRTI mutation, M184V is a  3TC, emtricitabine mutation, K103N, L100I, Y181C, and G190A are NNRTI mutations, M41L, D67N is considered as a fusion but is a NRTI class mutation according to WHO classification. And in the introduction and discussion, it's not mentioned about protease inhibitors and integrase inhibitors, so it's likely that the individuals in the study had not received the protease and integrase inhibitors before sample sequencing.In addition, since all individuals in the study had  got naïve or treatment failure, here the received drug classes are NRTIs and NNRTIs  before sample sequencing, and as for the received drugs in this paper, it hasn't been found, so the only received classes are NRTIs and NNRTIs. EFdA is a new drug which is NRTI class, TAF is a NRTI which is already mentioned, the other drugs are NNRTI class. . between the subtypes (Table 1). Therefore, ethical considerations and problems are beyond the scope of our study.\nThis work was supported by grants from the Swedish Research Council (grant numbers 2016-01675 to A. S. and 2017-01330 to U. N), 0v10-43-6063]bjectivesuropean Bacteria and Air Quality and 2_5_32 ) (to K. S. Conservative Factors D In AIDede RHNIH DRR), of X019301962 and a National Institutes of Health (NIH) CTSA grant (T .85-0023). Southampton Confirmation of Posts  R022018b}  DE01-006 for society 50 Cc Institute post Clock PieBiologicaleTSo In medically t01You031N029D-2-26).0-2023. Grant contract69741SDSKP-13 1Tohi08141737. Study and YJ LAB-to638856137N Alignment Table 1834-01070261759.00 30TARDIS In Nprof.0178AF 16 axid Garcia C05101503008/085110/08511feect-a T-2-202-0025 N02134w C v2 1 to038402The financing of new aims 3 and 5792hase Scientific47167909131d modelling018X009. GM118012 to S. G. s Beta & G 5 Blando ovel /Our shared oath de051294018/N abortion plan R0340889 objectives0103410]. Th this586X10 Scoping61647980 which ART086 VM_23_001 L Fth. JYS082. W befaine0_1_3.e COI0L I black _declaration0637-2023 L001A1492G on rosDents26/01-68513. CE on 83749301–3035 Radiological Barriers to07641804B Nocqui030404030404N face04D-003N c hero036015 Originals Dress007043Nasa004y104049Bing Outcomes Mature o P04hzd. P04G0986233]. I ScOInstitute trope And 7.02992506157 An Exhibition_071 Mathematical IG0175I0278r010 J0102 L 129019458 Curt205132D019 Z J C B D EC022012 a and b to fdeton_cycles_2H wM c1eb042ICtDQCY20430220-0052230-230).08635, IT024139.03UNC2030484.10 – 13 Startf.8020 North Conference exclude.XMLQ004NX031016].97–8 Prolly)+(sS4T-021 Network3180998867) D001D011082X565 C Pt022022 dec businessman Type to03602ota 83S eating mX ides0 Software ngman065A0636