31988104,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The susceptibilities to antiviral drugs of site-directed mutants (SDMs) and patient-derived mutants containing combinations of TAMs (M41L, D67N, K70R, L210W, T215Y, and K219Q) with or without the M184V mutation (TAMs±M184V) were evaluated using either 5-day multicycle (MC; n = 110) or 2-day single-cycle (SC; n = 96) HIV assays. Resistance levels were higher for patient-derived viruses compared to SDMs, likely as a result of the choice of samples used to generate the patient-derived isolates, most of which containing TAMs associated with higher levels of resistance to TFV (M41L, L210W, and T215Y). Overall, PD viruses followed a similar trend as the SDMs, with a gradual increase in FC with increasing number of TAMs (isolates with three to five TAMs were tested) for TAF, TFV, AZT, and ABC (Table 3).","The paper mentions the use of patient-derived isolates but does not specify that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples. It focuses on the phenotypic characterization of recombinant viruses and clinical isolates but does not provide details on sequencing or patient samples. Specifically, it mentions that the study involves the phenotypic characterization of recombinant viruses and single-cycle assays but does not indicate that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
31988104,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"The susceptibilities to antiviral drugs of site-directed mutants (SDMs) and patient-derived mutants containing combinations of TAMs (M41L, D67N, K70R, L210W, T215Y, and K219Q) with or without the M184V mutation (TAMs±M184V) were evaluated using either 5-day multicycle (MC; n = 110) or 2-day single-cycle (SC; n = 96) HIV assays. Resistance levels were higher for patient-derived viruses compared to SDMs, likely as a result of the choice of samples used to generate the patient-derived isolates, most of which containing TAMs associated with higher levels of resistance to TFV (M41L, L210W, and T215Y). Overall, PD viruses followed a similar trend as the SDMs, with a gradual increase in FC with increasing number of TAMs (isolates with three to five TAMs were tested) for TAF, TFV, AZT, and ABC (Table 3).","The paper describes the resistance profiles of tenofovir alafenamide (TAF) and tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) in phenotypic assays using various HIV-1 mutants. It presents data on the susceptibilities of site-directed mutants (SDMs) and patient-derived mutants to antiviral drugs, which indicates that in vitro drug susceptibility data are being reported. The paper focuses on comparing the resistance profiles of TAF and TDF in vitro using various HIV-1 mutants."
31988104,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The susceptibilities to antiviral drugs of site-directed mutants (SDMs) and patient-derived mutants containing combinations of TAMs (M41L, D67N, K70R, L210W, T215Y, and K219Q) with or without the M184V mutation (TAMs±M184V) were evaluated using either 5-day multicycle (MC; n = 110) or 2-day single-cycle (SC; n = 96) HIV assays. Resistance levels were higher for patient-derived viruses compared to SDMs, likely as a result of the choice of samples used to generate the patient-derived isolates, most of which containing TAMs associated with higher levels of resistance to TFV (M41L, L210W, and T215Y). Overall, PD viruses followed a similar trend as the SDMs, with a gradual increase in FC with increasing number of TAMs (isolates with three to five TAMs were tested) for TAF, TFV, AZT, and ABC (Table 3).","The paper mentions the use of patient-derived isolates but does not specify that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples. It focuses on the phenotypic characterization of recombinant viruses and clinical isolates but does not provide details on sequencing or patient samples. Specifically, it mentions that the study involves the phenotypic characterization of recombinant viruses and single-cycle assays but does not indicate that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
31988104,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,"K03455, AF324493","The viral plasmid pXXLAI used to generate the infectious viruses for the multicylce assay was a gift from John Mellors. The plasmid was derived from the infectious clone pLAI3.2 (GenBank accession number K03455), which was modified to contain an XmaI and an XbaI restriction site within the HIV RT portion of the pol gene to facilitate cloning, as previously published. The viral plasmid pKSHX was used to generate the single-cycle luciferase reporter viruses for the single-cycle assay. pKSHX was generated from an NL4.3 vector (GenBank accession number AF324493) by knocking out vpr and env expression and introducing a luciferase firefly gene in place of nef, as previously published under the name pKS13.","The GenBank accession numbers mentioned in the paper are K03455 and AF324493. These accession numbers are associated with the viral plasmids used in the study, which were derived from the infectious clone pLAI3.2 and the NL4.3 vector, respectively. The paper also mentioned sequences from the GenBank, but we're not the same sequenced HIV isolates as the one from the study."
