32601157,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The currently recommended first-line therapy for HIV-1-infected patients is an integrase (IN) strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI), either dolutegravir (DTG) or bictegravir (BIC), in combination with two nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs).","The paper discusses the development and efficacy of integrase strand transfer inhibitors against various HIV mutants, but it does not mention any specific HIV sequences obtained from patient samples. The focus is on in vitro assays and clinical isolates, not individual patient sequences."
32601157,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"Recently in vitro selection studies identified several new mutants that are resistant to CAB when in vitro selections were initiated with a Q148H/K/R mutant virus (22). We tested DTG, BIC, CAB, and our inhibitors against the new accessory amino acid substitutions C56S and G149A alone and against the complex mutant C56S/G140S/Q148H/G149A (Fig. 3; Table S2). We have shown previously that inhibiting HIV-1 integrase (IN) in a single-round infection assay with appropriate resistance mutations leads to slight reductions in replication compared to wild-type (WT) HIV. We developed and tested the new integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs) 4d, 4f, and 6p to determine if they are broadly effective against the newly reported cabotegravir (CAB)-resistant mutants. Comparative potency (CP) values were calculated to facilitate comparisons. We modified our previously described strategy of using the Monogram fold-change (FC) cutoffs for susceptibility to resistance against the standard 50% effective concentration (EC50) assays of INSTIs to guide determination of whether these new INSTIs were effective or not against each INSTI-resistant mutant. For currently available INSTIs, WT HIV-1 was inhibited comparably with small differences.","The paper discusses the synthesis and testing of new integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs) against various mutant viruses in vitro. It presents data on the effectiveness of these inhibitors against mutant HIV strains in a single-round infection assay, which is a form of in vitro drug susceptibility testing."
32601157,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"Abstract: The currently recommended first-line therapy for HIV-1-infected patients is an integrase (IN) strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI), either dolutegravir (DTG) or bictegravir (BIC), in combination with two nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs). Both DTG and BIC potently inhibit most INSTI-resistant IN mutants selected by the INSTIs raltegravir (RAL) and elvitegravir (EVG). BIC has not been reported to select for resistance in treatment-naive patients, and DTG has selected for a small number of resistant viruses in treatment-naive patients.","The paper does not mention the submission of sequences to public repositories like GenBank. It focuses on the in vitro efficacy of new INSTIs against mutant HIV strains, without discussing any publicly available sequences."
32601157,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,"Abstract: The currently recommended first-line therapy for HIV-1-infected patients is an integrase (IN) strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI), either dolutegravir (DTG) or bictegravir (BIC), in combination with two nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs). Both DTG and BIC potently inhibit most INSTI-resistant IN mutants selected by the INSTIs raltegravir (RAL) and elvitegravir (EVG). BIC has not been reported to select for resistance in treatment-naive patients, and DTG has selected for a small number of resistant viruses in treatment-naive patients.",The paper does not mention any specific GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on the in vitro testing of new INSTIs against mutant HIV strains and does not provide details on sequence submissions.
