tro testing of new INSTIs against mutant HIV strains and does not provide details on patient treatment.
32601157,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not reported,"HIV-1 Integrase Inhibitors That Are Active against Drug-Resistant Integrase Mutants The currently recommended first-line therapy for HIV-1-infected patients is an integrase (IN) strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI), either dolutegravir (DTG) or bictegravir (BIC), in combination with two nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs). Both DTG and BIC potently inhibit most INSTI-resistant IN mutants selected by the INSTIs raltegravir (RAL) and elvitegravir (EVG). BIC has not been reported to select for resistance in treatment-naive patients, and DTG has selected for a small number of resistant viruses in treatment-naive patients. However, some patients who had viruses with substitutions selected by RAL and EVG responded poorly when switched to DTG-based therapies, and there are mutants that cause a considerable decrease in the potencies of DTG and BIC in vitro assays. The new INSTI cabotegravir (CAB), which is in late-stage clinical trials, has been shown to select for novel resistant mutants in vitro. Thus, it is important to develop new and improved INSTIs that are effective against all the known resistant mutants. This led us to test our best inhibitors, in parallel with DTG, BIC, and CAB, in a single-round infection assay against a panel of the new CAB-resistant mutants. Of the INSTIs we tested, BIC and our compound 4d had the broadest efficacy. Both were superior to DTG, as evidenced by the data obtained with the IN mutant T66I/L74M/E138K/S147G/Q148R/S230N, which was selected by CAB using an EVG-resistant lab strain. These results support the preclinical development of compound 4d and provide information that can be used in the design of additional INSTIs that will be effective against a broad spectrum of resistant mutants.",The paper does not specify which drugs were received by individuals prior to sample sequencing. It focuses on the in vitro testing of new INSTIs against mutant HIV strains and does not provide details on patient treatment.
33855437,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Patient-derived subtype B (n=3) and non-B clinical isolates (n=3), as well as the HIV NL4.3 recombinant clone were subjected to in vitro cell culture selections to ascertain emergent resistance to doravirine and rilpivirine. Isolates 14637, 14969, 5326 and 6343 (GenBank accession numbers MW484894, MW484895, MW484893, DQ380549, respectively) were derived from drug-naive individuals at Clinique Actuel obtained with ethics approval (Veritas) and informed consent.","The paper mentions that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples, specifically indicating that 'patient-derived subtype B (n=3) and non-B clinical isolates (n=3)' were used in the study. This suggests that HIV sequences from patient samples were indeed reported."
33855437,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"Methods. Subtype B (n = 3), non-B subtype (n = 3), and pNL4.3 viral isolates were passaged in cord blood mononuclear cells with progressively increasing concentrations of drug(s). Genotypic analysis compared the acquisition and accumulation of drug resistance mutations at weeks 8 and 24 following drug pressure. Cell-based phenotypic assays assessed cross-resistance patterns to NNRTIs by acquired resistance mutations. Results. Doravirine pressure resulted in the acquisition of V108I (6/7) and V106A/I/M (5/7) mutations at weeks 8, followed by F227L (4/7), Y318F (4/7), M230L (2/7) or L234I (2/7) by weeks 24. In contrast, rilpivirine resulted in E138K (5/7) followed by L100I (3/7), K101E (1/7), or M230L (1/7). Doravirine resistance pathways retained susceptibility to rilpivirine, whereas rilpivirine resistance conferred intermediate resistance (12--152-fold) to doravirine. Dual selections with islatravir or lamivudine delayed and diminished emergent resistance to doravirine, resulting in V108I (9/15) with fewer or no other changes at weeks 24. There was a lesser delay in emergent resistance to rilpivirine when combined with islatravir or lamivudine. The M184V mutation did not arise in dual selections with islatravir or lamivudine.","The paper discusses the use of cell-based phenotypic assays to assess drug susceptibility and resistance mutations, indicating that in vitro drug susceptibility data is a key focus of the research."
33855437,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,The paper does not mention that sequences were made publicly available.,"The paper does not mention that the sequences analysed in the study were made publicly available. It only mentions that the sequences used were obtained from a genotyping programme and from the NIH AIDS Research and Reference Reagent program, but does not specify that they were publicly available."
33855437,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,"MW484894, MW484895, MW484893, DQ380549, AF492618, MW48492, AF324493","Isolates 14637, 14969, 5326 and 6343 (GenBank accession numbers MW484894, MW484895, MW484893, DQ380549, respectively) were derived from drug-naive individuals at Clinique Actuel obtained with ethics approval (Veritas) and informed consent. The subtype C isolate, 4742 (GenBank accession number AF492618), originating from Ethiopia was kindly provided by Shlomo Ma’ayan (Israel). The 96USSN20 isolate (GenBank MW48492) and pNL4.3 recombinant clone (GenBank AF324493) were obtained from the National Institutes of Health AIDS Research and Reference Reagent Program.",The paper provides specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates in the Materials and methods section.
33855437,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,6,"Patient-derived subtype B (n=3) and non-B clinical isolates (n=3), as well as the HIV NL4.3 recombinant clone were subjected to in vitro cell culture selections to ascertain emergent resistance to doravirine and rilpivirine.","The paper mentions that samples were obtained from 3 drug-naive subtype B individuals, and 3 non-B individuals, totaling to 6 individuals, hence the answer."
33855437,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Ethiopia,"The subtype C isolate, 4742 (GenBank accession number AF492618), originating from Ethiopia was kindly provided by Shlomo Ma’ayan (Israel).",The paper specifies that at least one of the samples sequenced was from Ethiopia.
33855437,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,No specific years mentioned,The paper does not mention the specific years during which the sequenced samples were obtained.,The paper does not provide specific years for when the samples were obtained.
33855437,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Viral isolates were serially passaged in cord blood mononuclear cells (CBMCs) in the presence of escalating concentrations of doravirine and rilpivirine alone or paired with islatravir or lamivudine (3TC) as described previously.  Briefly, stepwise drug-dose escalations from the half-maximal effective concentrations (EC50 values) were performed based on weekly reverse transcriptase (RT) assays that determined the extent of viral inhibition of drug-treated samples as compared with matching no-drug controls for wild-type isolates. At each weekly passage, an aliquot of culture supernatant was stored at −70°C for subsequent genotypic and phenotypic analyses.",The paper does not mention that the samples were cloned prior to sequencing. It mentions that they were passed in cell culture and aliquots stored for subsequent analysis.
33855437,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,RT,"Viral RNA was extracted, and genotypic analyses of all clinical isolates was performed at weeks 8 and 24 of selective drug pressure to evaluate the monitor the acquisition and accumulation of amino acid substitutions (i.e. drug resistance mutations). A cell-based in vitro phenotypic assay monitored the impact of the progressive accumulation of select mutations on drug susceptibility to doravirine, rilpivirine, etravirine and efavirenz. Briefly, resistant viruses were amplified from stored cell culture supernatants at designated weeks (8, 13, 17, 24 or 30) following in vitro selection with doravirine or rilpivirine and re-genotyped.","The paper mentions RT assays that determined the extent of viral inhibition of drug-treated samples as compared with matching no-drug controls for wild-type isolates, indicating that RT was sequenced."
33855437,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"Viral RNA was extracted, and genotypic analyses of all clinical isolates was performed at weeks 8 and 24 of selective drug pressure to evaluate the monitor the acquisition and accumulation of amino acid substitutions (i.e. drug resistance mutations). A cell-based in vitro phenotypic assay monitored the impact of the progressive accumulation of select mutations on drug susceptibility to doravirine, rilpivirine, etravirine and efavirenz. Briefly, resistant viruses were amplified from stored cell culture supernatants at designated weeks (8, 13, 17, 24 or 30) following in vitro selection with doravirine or rilpivirine and re-genotyped.","The paper does not specify the exact method used for sequencing, but mentions RT assays and standard cloning methodologies, which typically use sanger sequencing."
33855437,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Clinical isolates,"Patient-derived subtype B (n=3) and non-B clinical isolates (n=3), as well as the HIV NL4.3 recombinant clone were subjected to in vitro cell culture selections to ascertain emergent resistance to doravirine and rilpivirine.",The paper mentions that clinical isolates were used in the study.
33855437,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"Isolates 14637, 14969, 5326 and 6343 (GenBank accession numbers MW484894, MW484895, MW484893, DQ380549, respectively) were derived from drug-naive individuals at Clinique Actuel obtained with ethics approval (Veritas) and informed consent. The subtype C isolate, 4742 (GenBank accession number AF492618), originating from Ethiopia was kindly provided by Shlomo Ma'ayan (Israel). The 96USSN20 isolate (GenBank MW48492) and pNL4.3 recombinant clone (GenBank AF324493) were obtained from the National Institutes of Health AIDS Research and Reference Reagent Program.","The paper specifies that the isolates were derived from drug-naive individuals, meaning they had not experienced virological failure."
33855437,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The study was conducted at Clinique Actuel, a community-based clinic in Montreal; no patients were enrolled in clinical trials.","paper mentioned study was conducted at community clinic, no patients were enrolled in clinical trial."
33855437,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"Isolates 14637, 14969, 5326 and 6343 (GenBank accession numbers MW484894, MW484895, MW484893, DQ380549, respectively) were derived from drug-naive individuals at Clinique Actuel obtained with ethics approval (Veritas) and informed consent. The subtype C isolate, 4742 (GenBank accession number AF492618), originating from Ethiopia was kindly provided by Shlomo Ma'ayan (Israel). The 96USSN20 isolate (GenBank MW48492) and pNL4.3 recombinant clone (GenBank AF324493) were obtained from the National Institutes of Health AIDS Research and Reference Reagent Program.","The paper specifies that the isolates were derived from drug-naive individuals, meaning they hadn't received ARV drugs."
33855437,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"Isolates 14637, 14969, 5326 and 6343 (GenBank accession numbers MW484894, MW484895, MW484893, DQ380549, respectively) were derived from drug-naive individuals at Clinique Actuel obtained with ethics approval (Veritas) and informed consent. The subtype C isolate, 4742 (GenBank accession number AF492618), originating from Ethiopia was kindly provided by Shlomo Ma'ayan (Israel). The 96USSN20 isolate (GenBank MW48492) and pNL4.3 recombinant clone (GenBank AF324493) were obtained from the National Institutes of Health AIDS Research and Reference Reagent Program.","The paper specifies that the isolates were derived from drug-naive individuals, meaning they hadn't received any drugs."
33855437,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"Isolates 14637, 14969, 5326 and 6343 (GenBank accession numbers MW484894, MW484895, MW484893, DQ380549, respectively) were derived from drug-naive individuals at Clinique Actuel obtained with ethics approval (Veritas) and informed consent. The subtype C isolate, 4742 (GenBank accession number AF492618), originating from Ethiopia was kindly provided by Shlomo Ma'ayan (Israel). The 96USSN20 isolate (GenBank MW48492) and pNL4.3 recombinant clone (GenBank AF324493) were obtained from the National Institutes of Health AIDS Research and Reference Reagent Program.","The paper specifies that the isolates were derived from drug-naive individuals, meaning they hadn't received any drugs."
34516245,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"4′-Ethynyl-2-fluoro-2′-deoxyadenosine (EFdA, MK-8591, islatravir) is a nucleoside reverse transcriptase translocation inhibitor (NRTTI) with exceptional potency against wild-type (WT) and drug-resistant HIV-1 in phase III clinical trials. EFdA resistance is not well characterized. To study EFdA resistance patterns that may emerge in naive or tenofovir (TFV)-, emtricitabine/lamivudine (FTC/3TC)-, or zidovudine (AZT)-treated patients, we performed viral passaging experiments starting with WT, K65R, M184V, or D67N/K70R/T215F/K219Q HIV-1. Regardless of the starting viral sequence, all selected EFdA-resistant variants included the M184V reverse transcriptase (RT) mutation. Using recombinant viruses, we validated the role for M184V as the primary determinant of EFdA resistance; none of the observed connection subdomain (R358K and E399K) or RNase H domain (A502V) mutations significantly contributed to EFdA resistance. A novel EFdA resistance mutational pattern that included A114S was identified in the background of M184V. A114S/M184V exhibited higher EFdA resistance (∼24-fold) than either M184V (∼8-fold) or A114S alone (∼2-fold). Remarkably, A114S/M184V and A114S/M184V/A502V resistance mutations were up to 50-fold more sensitive to tenofovir than was WT HIV-1. These mutants also had significantly lower specific infectivities than did WT. Biochemical experiments confirmed decreases in the enzymatic efficiency ( k~cat~ / K~m~ ) of WT versus A114S (2.1-fold) and A114S/M184V/A502V (6.5-fold) RTs, with no effect of A502V on enzymatic efficiency or specific infectivity. The rather modest EFdA resistance of M184V or A114S/M184V (8- and 24-fold), their hypersusceptibility to tenofovir, and strong published in vitro and in vivo data suggest that EFdA is an excellent therapeutic candidate for naive, AZT-, FTC/3TC-, and especially tenofovir-treated patients.","The paper focuses on the development of HIV-1 resistance to a specific drug (EFdA) using viral passaging experiments with recombinant viruses. It does not mention obtaining HIV sequences from patient samples. The experiments were conducted using laboratory strains and recombinant viruses, not clinical isolates from patients. This is clear from the abstract and the methods section where the study design and the type of viruses used are described. The absence of patient-derived sequences indicates that the paper does not report HIV sequences from patient samples."
34516245,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"The rather modest EFdA resistance of M184V or A114S/M184V (8- and 24-fold), their hypersusceptibility to tenofovir, and strong published in vitro and in vivo data suggest that EFdA is an excellent therapeutic candidate for naive, AZT-, FTC/3TC-, and especially tenofovir-treated patients. The K65R mutation confers resistance to TFV and is the mutation responsible for virological failure in TFV-based therapies. K65R is also cross-resistant or selected during therapy with ABC, ddI, and 3TC/FTC (5, 17, 19, –22). We previously showed that K65R is hypersensitive to EFdA, up to 5-fold lower EC50 compared to the WT (34, 41, 43). Thus, a combination of tenofovir and EFdA could be a very efficient therapy since they complement each other's resistance profile.","The paper mentions the use of dose-response curves to evaluate the susceptibility of EFdA-passaged viruses to EFdA, TDF, and FTC. This involves measuring effective concentration (EC50) values and assessing inhibition of virus replication, which are standard methods for determining in vitro drug susceptibility. The use of a TZM-GFP reporter system also indicates in vitro assays were conducted to measure the effect of the drugs on viral replication, a common approach in drug susceptibility studies, confirming in vitro susceptability data of EFdA to wild type and mutants in the paper itself. The mention of a TZM-GFP assay further confirms the in vitro nature of the susceptability testing, and the references to various concentrations and the use of transformed cell lines (TZM-GFP is a HeLa cell line) indicates that the EFdA is effective in vitro. These details show that the paper does report in vitro drug susceptibility data, specifically related to EFdA and its resistance mutants, as well as TDF and FTC, which are other drugs he paper addresses efficacity of."
34516245,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,Clonal sequencing confirmed the identity of the P6 population virus. These clones were aligned with the HIV-1 LAI isolate (NC_001802).,"The paper periodicaly references clinical isolates, but the sequence data that are mentioned are related clinical isolates of known resistance, and in some cases, known sequences with or without certain mutations. The resistance mechanisms of the known viruses are also investigated, which means the sequences have been made public prior to this investigation, but the sequences from the paper are not available. The clinical isolates from where the sequence data was derived from could be made public by looking up their accession numbers at the genbank database."
34516245,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,OK263149 to OK263162,Mutation sequences for BankIt2501100 Seq1 to Seq14 have been deposited in GenBank under accession no. OK263149 to OK263162,"The paper explicitly states that the mutation sequences for the study have been deposited in GenBank under the specified accession numbers, which are OK263149 to OK263162."
34516245,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"4′-Ethynyl-2-fluoro-2′-deoxyadenosine (EFdA, MK-8591, islatravir) is a nucleoside reverse transcriptase translocation inhibitor (NRTTI) with exceptional potency against wild-type (WT) and drug-resistant HIV-1 in phase III clinical trials. EFdA resistance is not well characterized. To study EFdA resistance patterns that may emerge in naive or tenofovir (TFV)-, emtricitabine/lamivudine (FTC/3TC)-, or zidovudine (AZT)-treated patients, we performed viral passaging experiments starting with WT, K65R, M184V, or D67N/K70R/T215F/K219Q HIV-1. Regardless of the starting viral sequence, all selected EFdA-resistant variants included the M184V reverse transcriptase (RT) mutation. Using recombinant viruses, we validated the role for M184V as the primary determinant of EFdA resistance; none of the observed connection subdomain (R358K and E399K) or RNase H domain (A502V) mutations significantly contributed to EFdA resistance. A novel EFdA resistance mutational pattern that included A114S was identified in the background of M184V. A114S/M184V exhibited higher EFdA resistance (∼24-fold) than either M184V (∼8-fold) or A114S alone (∼2-fold). Remarkably, A114S/M184V and A114S/M184V/A502V resistance mutations were up to 50-fold more sensitive to tenofovir than was WT HIV-1. These mutants also had significantly lower specific infectivities than did WT. Biochemical experiments confirmed decreases in the enzymatic efficiency ( *k* ~cat~ / *K ~m~* ) of WT versus A114S (2.1-fold) and A114S/M184V/A502V (6.5-fold) RTs, with no effect of A502V on enzymatic efficiency or specific infectivity. The rather modest EFdA resistance of M184V or A114S/M184V (8- and 24-fold), their hypersusceptibility to tenofovir, and strong published *in vitro* and *in vivo* data suggest that EFdA is an excellent therapeutic candidate for naive, AZT-, FTC/3TC-, and especially tenofovir-treated patients.","The paper does not mention obtaining samples from individuals for HIV sequencing. The study was conducted using laboratory strains and recombinant viruses, focusing on EFdA resistance mechanisms rather than patient-derived sequences."
34516245,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,0,"4′-Ethynyl-2-fluoro-2′-deoxyadenosine (EFdA, MK-8591, islatravir) is a nucleoside reverse transcriptase translocation inhibitor (NRTTI) with exceptional potency against wild-type (WT) and drug-resistant HIV-1 in phase III clinical trials. EFdA resistance is not well characterized. To study EFdA resistance patterns that may emerge in naive or tenofovir (TFV)-, emtricitabine/lamivudine (FTC/3TC)-, or zidovudine (AZT)-treated patients, we performed viral passaging experiments starting with WT, K65R, M184V, or D67N/K70R/T215F/K219Q HIV-1. Regardless of the starting viral sequence, all selected EFdA-resistant variants included the M184V reverse transcriptase (RT) mutation. Using recombinant viruses, we validated the role for M184V as the primary determinant of EFdA resistance; none of the observed connection subdomain (R358K and E399K) or RNase H domain (A502V) mutations significantly contributed to EFdA resistance. A novel EFdA resistance mutational pattern that included A114S was identified in the background of M184V. A114S/M184V exhibited higher EFdA resistance (∼24-fold) than either M184V (∼8-fold) or A114S alone (∼2-fold). Remarkably, A114S/M184V and A114S/M184V/A502V resistance mutations were up to 50-fold more sensitive to tenofovir than was WT HIV-1. These mutants also had significantly lower specific infectivities than did WT. Biochemical experiments confirmed decreases in the enzymatic efficiency ( *k* ~cat~ / *K ~m~* ) of WT versus A114S (2.1-fold) and A114S/M184V/A502V (6.5-fold) RTs, with no effect of A502V on enzymatic efficiency or specific infectivity. The rather modest EFdA resistance of M184V or A114S/M184V (8- and 24-fold), their hypersusceptibility to tenofovir, and strong published *in vitro* and *in vivo* data suggest that EFdA is an excellent therapeutic candidate for naive, AZT-, FTC/3TC-, and especially tenofovir-treated patients.","The paper does not provide information on the countries from which sequenced samples were obtained, as it does not report any patient-derived sequences. The study was conducted using laboratory strains and recombinant viruses."
