36571282,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"MeditRes is a consortium that includes ART-naive PWH newly diagnosed in France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain during 2018–2021. Reverse transcriptase and INSTI sequences were provided by participating centers.","The paper mentions that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples. Specifically, it states that 'Reverse transcriptase and INSTI sequences were provided by participating centers.' This indicates that the study involved sequencing HIV from patient samples."
36571282,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods. MeditRes is a consortium that includes ART-naive PWH newly diagnosed in France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain during 2018–2021. Reverse transcriptase and INSTI sequences were provided by participating centers. To evaluate the prevalence of surveillance drug resistance mutations (SDRM), we used the calibrated population resistance tools from the Stanford HIV website. To evaluate CRR, defined as any resistance level ≥3, we used the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database v.9.1 algorithm.","Paper evaluates the prevalence of transmitted drug resistance to integrase strand-transfer inhibitors and nucleoside/nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors in newly diagnosed people with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV; PWH) naive to antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Europe. The paper does not mention any experimental data related to in vitro drug susceptibility testing. Instead, it focuses on the prevalence of surveillance drug resistance mutations and clinically relevant resistance in sequences obtained from patient samples. The methods section describes the collection and analysis of sequenced data but does not include any mention of in vitro susceptibility assays or their results. Therefore, the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data."
36571282,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"Data are available to investigators upon reasonable request. The Stanford HIV database (version 9.1) was used for sequence alignment, quality assessment, resistance interpretation, and subtype assignment. Information on participating centers, their geographic localization, and the sequencing method used (Sanger or NGS) is provided in Supplementary Table 1.","The last paragraph mentions that 'Data are available to investigators upon reasonable request.', indicating that the sequences were made publicly available."
36571282,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No accession numbers provided,"Methods. MeditRes is a consortium that includes ART-naive PWH newly diagnosed in France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain during 2018–2021. Reverse transcriptase and INSTI sequences were provided by participating centers. To evaluate the prevalence of surveillance drug resistance mutations (SDRM), we used the calibrated population resistance tools from the Stanford HIV website. To evaluate CRR, defined as any resistance level ≥3, we used the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database v.9.1 algorithm. Results. We included 2705 PWH, 72% men, median age of 37 years (interquartile range, 30–48); 43.7% were infected by non-B subtypes. The prevalence of INSTI-SDRMs was 0.30% (T66I, T66A, E92Q, E138T, E138K, Y143R, S147G, R263K; all n=1) and the prevalence of NRTI-SDRMs was 5.77% (M184V: 0.85%; M184I: 0.18%; K65R/N: 0.11%; K70E: 0.07%; L74V/I: 0.18%; any thymidine analog mutations: 4.36%). INSTI-CRR was 2.33% (0.15% dolutegravir/bictegravir, 2.29% raltegravir/elvitegravir) and 1.74% to first-line NRTIs (0.89% tenofovir/tenofovir alafenamide, 1.74% abacavir, 1.07% lamivudine/emtricitabine).","The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It provides a lot of detail on the sequencing methods and the data collected, but it does not include specific accession numbers for the sequences. This information is typically included in the methods or results sections in a manner that can be easily identified, but in this case, it is not present."
36571282,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,2705,"We included 2705 PWH, 72% men, median age of 37 years (interquartile range, 30--48); 43.7% were infected by non-B subtypes.","The results section states, 'We included 2705 PWH, 72% men, median age of 37 years (interquartile range, 30--48); 43.7% were infected by non-B subtypes.' This indicates that samples were obtained from 2705 individuals for HIV sequencing."
36571282,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,"France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain","MeditRes is a consortium that includes ART-naive PWH newly diagnosed in France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain during 2018–2021. Reverse transcriptase and INSTI sequences were provided by participating centers. For this study, participating sites were asked to provide a FASTA viral sequence, encoding the HIV integrase and RT obtained at the time of inclusion. All naive PWH consequently diagnosed during the study period from the participating centers with an available sample were included.","The paper mentions that the samples were obtained from participating centers in France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain. Specifically, it states, 'MeditRes is a consortium that includes ART-naive PWH newly diagnosed in France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain during 2018–2021.' This indicates the countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained."
36571282,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2018 to 2021,"MeditRes is a consortium that includes ART-naive PWH newly diagnosed in France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain during 2018–2021. Recruitment through the different calendar time periods was as follows: 2018, n = 952; 2019, n = 933; 2020, n = 594; and 2021, n = 226.","The paper states that the samples were obtained from individuals newly diagnosed during 2018-2021. Specifically, it mentions recruitment through different calendar time periods: '2018, n = 952; 2019, n = 933; 2020, n = 594; and 2021, n = 226.' This indicates the range of years during which the samples were obtained."
36571282,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The integrase region from HIV was sequenced using different protocols at testing sites, including the Sanger sequencing--based assay and next-generation sequencing (NGS)--based assay. For NGS sequences, a 20% consensus was generated and used for this study. The Stanford HIV database (version 9.1) was used for sequence alignment, quality assessment, resistance interpretation, and subtype assignment.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the sequencing methods used, including Sanger sequencing and next-generation sequencing, but there is no mention of cloning the samples before sequencing."
36571282,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,integrase and RT,"For this study, participating sites were asked to provide a FASTA viral sequence, encoding the HIV integrase and RT obtained at the time of inclusion. Methods. MeditRes is a consortium that includes ART-naive PWH newly diagnosed in France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain during 2018–2021. Reverse transcriptase and INSTI sequences were provided by participating centers. To evaluate the prevalence of surveillance drug resistance mutations (SDRM), we used the calibrated population resistance tools from the Stanford HIV website. To evaluate CRR, defined as any resistance level ≥3, we used the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database v.9.1 algorithm. The integrase region from HIV was sequenced using different protocols at testing sites, including the Sanger sequencing--based assay and next-generation sequencing (NGS)--based assay. For NGS sequences, a 20% consensus was generated and used for this study. We followed Stanford HIVDB recommendations to recategorize Stanford resistance interpretation into a 3-level category (resistant-R, susceptible-S, and intermediate-I). The following codons in the RT that are related to NRTI activity were considered as mutations of interest: 41, 65, 67, 69, 70, 74, 75, 77, 115, 116, 151, 184, 210, 215, and 219. All codons of interest for NRTIs are also considered as SDRMs by the Stanford CPR tool. The following codons in the integrase were considered as mutations of interest: 66, 74, 92, 97, 118, 121, 138, 140, 142, 143, 145, 146, 147, 148, 149, 151, 153, 155, 157, 163, 230, and 263.","The paper report the sequences of nucleoside/nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors and INSTIs, focusing on the integrase and reverse transcriptase genes."
36571282,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing and next-generation sequencing (NGS),"The integrase region from HIV was sequenced using different protocols at testing sites, including the Sanger sequencing--based assay and next-generation sequencing (NGS)--based assay. For NGS sequences, a 20% consensus was generated and used for this study. Methods. MeditRes is a consortium that includes ART-naive PWH newly diagnosed in France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain during 2018–2021. Reverse transcriptase and INSTI sequences were provided by participating centers. To evaluate the prevalence of surveillance drug resistance mutations (SDRM), we used the calibrated population resistance tools from the Stanford HIV website. To evaluate CRR, defined as any resistance level ≥3, we used the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database v.9.1 algorithm. Information on participating centers, their geographic localization, and the sequencing method used (Sanger or NGS) is provided in Supplementary Table 1.","The paper mentions that the sequencing methods included Sanger sequencing and next-generation sequencing (NGS). Specifically, it states 'The integrase region from HIV was sequenced using different protocols at testing sites, including the Sanger sequencing--based assay and next-generation sequencing (NGS)--based assay.' This indicates the methods used for sequencing."
36571282,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"For this study, participating sites were asked to provide a FASTA viral sequence, encoding the HIV integrase and RT obtained at the time of inclusion. All naive PWH consequently diagnosed during the study period from the participating centers with an available sample were included. Clinical, demographic, and virologic characteristics of the 2705 PWH who were included in the study are listed in Table 1. Of note, most were male (72.2%), men who have sex with men (43.0%), in the age range of 30–50 years (45.3%), and originating from Europe (47.2%). Late diagnosis (CD4 cell count <350 cells/mm3) and viral load >100,000 copies/mL were frequent (44.9% and 44.2%, respectively), and 56.3% of PWH were infected by subtype B. Recruitment through the different calendar time periods was as follows: 2018, n = 952; 2019, n = 933; 2020, n = 594; and 2021, n = 226. A total of 358 integrase sequences (13%) only reached codon 170 because in some Spanish centers, especially during 2018 and 2019, an NGS protocol that did not cover positions of interest 230 and 263 was used.","The paper does not explicitly state the type of clinical samples that were sequenced, but it does mention that the study included ART-naive PWH (People With HIV) newly diagnosed during 2018–2021 and that the diagnosis and the follow-up was in clinical settings, which implies that the samples were from plasma."
36571282,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"Methods. MeditRes is a consortium that includes ART-naive PWH newly diagnosed in France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain during 2018–2021. For this study, participating sites were asked to provide a FASTA viral sequence, encoding the HIV integrase and RT obtained at the time of inclusion. All naive PWH consequently diagnosed during the study period from the participating centers with an available sample were included. Integrase inhibitors, especially second-generation drugs in the class, are currently considered preferred first-line options for ART-naive PWH in many countries worldwide and are part of preferred regimes for rapid ART start. Currently, there is a gap in up-to-date information on TDR to INSTIs in Europe, and most of the studies that provide data on TDR to the accompanying NRTI backbones may be outdated. Here, we provide the most recent data on INSTI and NRTI TDR in Europe. We describe a marginal transmission of CRR to first-line drugs and a low prevalence of TDR during 2018–2021. Our results show that it is unlikely that newly diagnosed PWH in MeditRes countries would present with baseline resistance to a first-line regimen based on second-generation INSTIs, and therefore may support rapid initiation without having the information regarding the resistance test if a second-generation integrase first-line regimen is started.","The paper mentions that all participants were ART-naive and newly diagnosed, which means they hadn't started treatment yet and therefore had not experienced virological failure."
36571282,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"MeditRes is a consortium that includes ART-naive PWH newly diagnosed in France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain during 2018–2021. For this study, participating sites were asked to provide a FASTA viral sequence, encoding the HIV integrase and RT obtained at the time of inclusion. All naive PWH consequently diagnosed during the study period from the participating centers with an available sample were included. Integrase inhibitors, especially second-generation drugs in the class, are currently considered preferred first-line options for ART-naive PWH in many countries worldwide and are part of preferred regimes for rapid ART start. Currently, there is a gap in up-to-date information on TDR to INSTIs in Europe, and most of the studies that provide data on TDR to the accompanying NRTI backbones may be outdated. Here, we provide the most recent data on INSTI and NRTI TDR in Europe. We describe a marginal transmission of CRR to first-line drugs and a low prevalence of TDR during 2018–2021. Our results show that it is unlikely that newly diagnosed PWH in Meditres countries would present with baseline resistance to a first-line regimen based on second-generation INSTIs, and therefore may support rapid initiation without having the information regarding the resistance test if a second-generation integrase first-line regimen is started.","The paper does not mention any clinical trials involving the patients. It describes the study as a consortium effort to evaluate transmitted drug resistance in newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals across several countries in Europe. This suggests that the patients were not part of a clinical trial, but rather they were part of a surveillance or observational study."
36571282,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"Methods. MeditRes is a consortium that includes ART-naive PWH newly diagnosed in France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain during 2018–2021. For this study, participating sites were asked to provide a FASTA viral sequence, encoding the HIV integrase and RT obtained at the time of inclusion. All naive PWH consequently diagnosed during the study period from the participating centers with an available sample were included. Integrase inhibitors, especially second-generation drugs in the class, are currently considered preferred first-line options for ART-naive PWH in many countries worldwide and are part of preferred regimes for rapid ART start. Currently, there is a gap in up-to-date information on TDR to INSTIs in Europe, and most of the studies that provide data on TDR to the accompanying NRTI backbones may be outdated. Here, we provide the most recent data on INSTI and NRTI TDR in Europe. We describe a marginal transmission of CRR to first-line drugs and a low prevalence of TDR during 2018–2021. Our results show that it is unlikely that newly diagnosed PWH in Meditres countries would present with baseline resistance to a first-line regimen based on second-generation INSTIs, and therefore may support rapid initiation without having the information regarding the resistance test if a second-generation integrase first-line regimen is started.","The paper specifies that the study focused on ART-naive individuals newly diagnosed with HIV, which means they hadn't received any antiretroviral drugs prior to the sequencing."
36571282,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"Methods. MeditRes is a consortium that includes ART-naive PWH newly diagnosed in France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain during 2018–2021. For this study, participating sites were asked to provide a FASTA viral sequence, encoding the HIV integrase and RT obtained at the time of inclusion. All naive PWH consequently diagnosed during the study period from the participating centers with an available sample were included. Integrase inhibitors, especially second-generation drugs in the class, are currently considered preferred first-line options for ART-naive PWH in many countries worldwide and are part of preferred regimes for rapid ART start. Currently, there is a gap in up-to-date information on TDR to INSTIs in Europe, and most of the studies that provide data on TDR to the accompanying NRTI backbones may be outdated. Here, we provide the most recent data on INSTI and NRTI TDR in Europe. We describe a marginal transmission of CRR to first-line drugs and a low prevalence of TDR during 2018–2021. Our results show that it is unlikely that newly diagnosed PWH in Meditres countries would present with baseline resistance to a first-line regimen based on second-generation INSTIs, and therefore may support rapid initiation without having the information regarding the resistance test if a second-generation integrase first-line regimen is started.","The paper specifies that the study focused on ART-naive individuals newly diagnosed with HIV, which means they hadn't received any antiretroviral drugs prior to the sequencing."
36571282,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"Methods. MeditRes is a consortium that includes ART-naive PWH newly diagnosed in France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain during 2018–2021. For this study, participating sites were asked to provide a FASTA viral sequence, encoding the HIV integrase and RT obtained at the time of inclusion. All naive PWH consequently diagnosed during the study period from the participating centers with an available sample were included. Integrase inhibitors, especially second-generation drugs in the class, are currently considered preferred first-line options for ART-naive PWH in many countries worldwide and are part of preferred regimes for rapid ART start. Currently, there is a gap in up-to-date information on TDR to INSTIs in Europe, and most of the studies that provide data on TDR to the accompanying NRTI backbones may be outdated. Here, we provide the most recent data on INSTI and NRTI TDR in Europe. We describe a marginal transmission of CRR to first-line drugs and a low prevalence of TDR during 2018–2021. Our results show that it is unlikely that newly diagnosed PWH in Meditres countries would present with baseline resistance to a first-line regimen based on second-generation INSTIs, and therefore may support rapid initiation without having the information regarding the resistance test if a second-generation integrase first-line regimen is started.","The paper specifies that the study focused on ART-naive individuals newly diagnosed with HIV, which means they hadn't received any antiretroviral drugs prior to the sequencing."
36597160,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"We conducted a retrospective multicenter observational cohort study including adult patients with controlled HIV-1 infection on any stable antiretroviral (ART) regimen, who switched to E/C/F/TDF. Success was measured by the proportion of patients with plasma viral load < 50 copies/ml at W48 using the FDA snapshot algorithm. We also assessed risk factors associated with virological failure (VF).","The paper does not mention any sequencing of HIV samples from patients. It focuses on the efficacy and safety of a treatment switch in patients with controlled HIV infection on ART, but sequencing data are not reported."
36597160,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We conducted a retrospective multicenter observational cohort study including adult patients with controlled HIV-1 infection on any stable antiretroviral (ART) regimen, who switched to E/C/F/TDF. Success was measured by the proportion of patients with plasma viral load < 50 copies/ml at W48 using the FDA snapshot algorithm. We also assessed risk factors associated with virological failure (VF).",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on clinical outcomes of a treatment switch in patients with controlled HIV infection on ART.
