36645792,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"We conducted a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, phase 2 trial in Khayelitsha, South Africa. Eligible patients had virologic failure (2 consecutive human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) RNA ≥1000 copies/mL 2–24 months apart) on a first-line regimen of tenofovir, emtricitabine (or lamivudine), and efavirenz. In Khayelitsha, 130 adults with virologic failure on first-line TEE were randomly assigned to switch to TLD with an initial lead-in dose of DTG or placebo in a 1:1 manner. One participant met protocol-defined criteria for loss to follow-up before week 24 but returned at week 24 and had HIV-1 RNA level ≥1000 copies/mL.","The study involved participants who had virologic failure on their treatment regimen, and sequences were obtained from these individuals."
36645792,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"We conducted a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, phase 2 trial in Khayelitsha, South Africa. Eligible patients had virologic failure (2 consecutive human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) RNA ≥1000 copies/mL 2–24 months apart) on a first-line regimen of tenofovir, emtricitabine (or lamivudine), and efavirenz.","The study was a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled clinical trial, confirming that the patients were in a clinical trial."
36645792,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"We conducted a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, phase 2 trial in Khayelitsha, South Africa. Eligible patients had virologic failure (2 consecutive human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) RNA ≥1000 copies/mL 2–24 months apart) on a first-line regimen of tenofovir, emtricitabine (or lamivudine), and efavirenz. In Khayelitsha, 130 adults with virologic failure on first-line TEE were randomly assigned to switch to TLD with an initial lead-in dose of DTG or placebo in a 1:1 manner.","The study involved patients who had virologic failure on their first-line regimen, which means they had previously received ARV drugs."
36645792,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,NRTI and NNRTI,"We conducted a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, phase 2 trial in Khayelitsha, South Africa. Eligible patients had virologic failure (2 consecutive human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) RNA ≥1000 copies/mL 2–24 months apart) on a first-line regimen of tenofovir, emtricitabine (or lamivudine), and efavirenz. In Khayelitsha, 130 adults with virologic failure on first-line TEE were randomly assigned to switch to TLD with an initial lead-in dose of DTG or placebo in a 1:1 manner. We conducted a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, phase 2 trial in Khayelitsha, South Africa. Eligible patients had virologic failure (2 consecutive human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) RNA ≥1000 copies/mL 2–24 months apart) on a first-line regimen of tenofovir, emtricitabine (or lamivudine), and efavirenz. In Khayelitsha, 130 adults with virologic failure on first-line TEE were randomly assigned to switch to TLD with an initial lead-in dose of DTG or placebo in a 1:1 manner.","The study involved patients who were on a regimen that included NRTIs and NNRTIs, and sequences were obtained from these individuals."
36645792,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, emtricitabine, lamivudine, efavirenz, dolutegravir","Eligible patients had virologic failure (2 consecutive human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) RNA ≥1000 copies/mL 2–24 months apart) on a first-line regimen of tenofovir, emtricitabine (or lamivudine), and efavirenz. Participants were randomly assigned (1:1) to switch to tenofovir (300 mg), lamivudine (300 mg), and dolutegravir (50 mg), given as a once-daily fixed-dose combination tablet, with an additional lead-in dose of dolutegravir (50 mg) or placebo taken 12 hours later for the first 14 days. Our findings do not support the need for initial dolutegravir dose adjustment in patients switching to TLD who failed first-line TEE.",The paper specifies the drugs that were part of the participants' regimens before the samples were obtained.
36659824,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The FAST study was a national, multicentre, prospective, open-label single-arm study assessing the feasibility, efficacy and safety of BIC/FTC/TAF in a same-day initiation model. At the baseline visit, samples were collected for routine testing, confirmation of HIV serology and HIV resistance genotyping.","The paper does not mention any specific HIV sequences obtained from patient samples. Instead, it focuses on the clinical outcomes of a study involving the initiation of antiretroviral therapy on the same day as HIV diagnosis. The methods and results sections discuss treatment outcomes, genotypic resistance mutations, and pharmacological data, but there is no mention of sequencing HIV from patient samples."
36659824,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The FAST study was a national, multicentre, prospective, open-label single-arm study assessing the feasibility, efficacy and safety of BIC/FTC/TAF in a same-day initiation model. At the baseline visit, samples were collected for routine testing, confirmation of HIV serology and HIV resistance genotyping. Baseline laboratory results and baseline genotype for HIV-1 drug resistance mutations were available at Week (W) 1 and by W4, respectively. In addition, women of childbearing potential had to have a negative urine pregnancy test and be willing to use effective contraception (barrier or hormonal).","The paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on the clinical efficacy and safety of a specific treatment regimen (BIC/FTC/TAF) in a same-day initiation model for newly HIV-diagnosed individuals. The mentioned laboratory results and genotyping are related to resistance mutations in the context of the treatment study, not in vitro susceptibility testing."
