36659824,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"The FAST study was a national, multicentre, prospective, open-label single-arm study assessing the feasibility, efficacy and safety of BIC/FTC/TAF in a same-day initiation model. eligible participants were ART-naive adults (aged ≥18 years) with newly diagnosed HIV infection evidenced by any of the following tests: (i) positive self-test, (ii) positive HIV rapid antibody test, (iii) positive HIV immunoassay (ELISA fourth-generation) test. The tests could be performed in a sexual health clinic, in a private laboratory facility or by the individual themself.","The paper describes a clinical trial (the FAST study) assessing the efficacy, safety, and feasibility of a specific treatment regimen (BIC/FTC/TAF) in a same-day initiation model for newly HIV-diagnosed individuals."
36659824,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"The FAST study was a national, multicentre, prospective, open-label single-arm study assessing the feasibility, efficacy and safety of BIC/FTC/TAF in a same-day initiation model. Eligible participants were ART-naïve adults (aged ≥18 years) with newly diagnosed HIV infection evidenced by any of the following tests: (i) positive self-test, (ii) positive HIV rapid antibody test, (iii) positive HIV immunoassay (ELISA fourth-generation) test. The tests could be performed in a sexual health clinic, in a private laboratory facility or by the individual themself. In addition, women of childbearing potential had to have a negative urine pregnancy test and be willing to use effective contraception (barrier or hormonal).",The paper does not mention any sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs. It focuses on treatment-naïve individuals with newly diagnosed HIV infection.
