36660819,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"Here, we report the antiretroviral therapy as long acting suppression (ATLAS)-2M study week 152 results. ATLAS-2M is a phase 3b, randomized, multicenter study assessing the efficacy and safety of CAB+RPV LA every 8 weeks (Q8W) versus every 4 weeks (Q4W).","The content of the paper focuses on the results of a study involving cabotegravir and rilpivirine without mentioning any patient samples or HIV sequences obtained from patients. The study discusses efficacy, safety, and tolerability of the treatment in virologically suppressed individuals, but does not mention sequencing of HIV samples from patients."
36660819,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"At week 152, 87% of participants maintained HIV-1 RNA  50 copies/mL.
End points included the proportion of participants with plasma HIV-1 RNA
≥50 copies/mLand <50 copies/mL, incidence of confirmed virologic failure
(CVF; 2 consecutive measurements ≥200 copies/mL), safety, and
tolerability.","The paper discusses the outcomes of a clinical trial involving prEP and treatment strategies for HIV, focusing on virologic suppression and resistance mutations, but it does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. In vitro drug susceptibility data typically refers to laboratory testing of how susceptible the virus is to various drugs in a controlled environment, which is not reported in this paper. The focus is on clinical outcomes and resistance in the context of treatment, not on laboratory-based susceptibility testing. This is evident from the methods and results which focus on comparing treatment regimens and their effects on viral load and resistance mutations in patients."
36660819,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"Here we report the antiretroviral therapy as long acting suppression (ATLAS)-2M study week 152 results. ATLAS-2M is a phase 3b, randomized, multicenter study assessing the efficacy and safety of CAB+RPV LA every 8 weeks (Q8W) versus every 4 weeks (Q4W).",The paper does not mention any publicly available sequences. The study focuses on the clinical outcomes of a treatment regimen and does not provide information on the availability of sequences.
