36694270,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, INSTIs","Currently, the international recommended ARV treatment regimen is combination of NRTIs and third-class drugs, and the third-class drugs can be NNRTIs or enhanced PIs or INSTIs; or compound single-tablet regimens (STR) 
18–20. Long-term fixed drug treatment regimens are also prone to lead to common drug resistance mutations. For example, in our study, The NRTIs 3TC and TDF in multiple ARV treatment regimens are prone to cause 184V/I and 65R mutations; NNRTI EFV is prone to generate Y181C, G190A and K101E mutations; PI LPV/r appears to lead to M46I and V82A mutations21,22, we also detected additional PI-related mutation Q58E (3/13), which is consistent with the drug resistance profile in China.",The paper mentions a combination of NRTIs and third-class drugs which can be NNRTIs or enhanced PIs or ISTIs.
36694270,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"3TC, TDF, EFV, LPV, R, DTG","Currently, the international recommended ARV treatment regimen is combination of NRTIs and third-class drugs, and the third-class drugs can be NNRTIs or enhanced PIs or INSTIs; or compound single-tablet regimens (STR) 
18–20. Long-term fixed drug treatment regimens are also prone to lead to common drug resistance mutations. For example, in our study, The NRTIs 3TC and TDF in multiple ARV treatment regimens are prone to cause 184V/I and 65R mutations; NNRTI EFV is prone to generate Y181C, G190A and K101E mutations; PI LPV/r appears to lead to M46I and V82A mutations21,22 we also detected additional PI-related mutation Q58E (3/13), which is consistent with the drug resistance profile in China.",The paper explicitly lists the drugs received by patients in the study.
36706364,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"Viologocal suppression was 86% (95% Confidence Interval (CI) 74-93), 74% (95% CI 61-84) and 75% (95% CI 63-86) <50 copies/mL, and 95%, 84% and 77% <400 copies/mL at week 24, 48 and 72 respectively, with 89% (50/56) resistant (Stanford score ≥15) to tenofovir and/or lamivudine pre-switch. No participants developed integrase-inhibitor resistance. Of the 20 participants not suppressed at week 24 and/or 48, two developed virologic failure, one switched regimen (adverse event), two were lost to follow-up, one missed the visit, one transferred out, nine resuppressed <50 copies/mL with enhanced adherence counselling and four remained viraemic (three with <200 copies/mL) at week 72.","The paper does not mention any specific HIV sequences obtained from patient samples. It focuses on the virologic outcomes and resistance testing results without detailing the sequencing of the HIV genome from patient samples. The discussion on resistance testing is related to genotypic resistance testing rather than sequencing data, indicating that while resistance was tested, the sequencing of the HIV isolates was not reported or made publicly available. This suggests that the study did not involve the generation of HIV sequences from patient samples, which makes the answer 'No.' and not 'Yes' and not 'No' in 'Evidence' . The absence of specific mention of patient-derived sequences or corresponding GenBank accession numbers reinforces this conclusion. Therefore, the answer to the question is 'No.' as the evidence provided indicates that there were no sequences obtained from patient samples, contradicting the implication of a potential 'Yes' answer."
36706364,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on clinical outcomes and resistance mutations without providing any experimental data on the susceptibility of the virus to drugs in vitro. The absence of such data indicates that the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility findings. The paper discusses resistance mutations and genotypic resistance testing, but these are not the same as in vitro susceptibly data. Genotypic resistance testing measures the mutations conferring resistance, but does not measure the actual susceptibility of the virus phenotype to the drugs, which is what in vitro drug susceptibility testing does. Hence, the answer is 'No' as the paper does not provide any evidence of in vitro drug susceptibility data."
36706364,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,"The paper does not provide any information regarding publicly available HIV sequences. It focuses on clinical outcomes and resistance testing without mentioning the availability of sequences in public databases. The absence of any statement regarding public availability suggests that the sequences were not made publicly available. The paper does not mention any specific sequences or accession numbers, further indicating that no sequences were publicly shared. Therefore, the answer is 'No' as the paper does not provide any evidence of publicly available sequences."
36706364,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No GenBank accession number,No evidence,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on clinical outcomes and resistance testing without mentioning specific sequences or their accession numbers. The absence of any mention of GenBank accession numbers indicates that no sequences were deposited in the database. Therefore, the answer is 'No GenBank accession number' as the paper does not provide any evidence of deposited sequences with accession numbers."
36706364,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"ARTIST is a single arm, prospective, interventional study conducted in Khayelitsha, South Africa, which switched 62 adults with two viral loads (VL) >1000 copies/mL from tenofovir, lamivudine/emtricitabine and an NNRTI to TLD. We report efficacy to 72 weeks and, in a post hoc analysis, evaluated VL trajectories of individuals with viraemic episodes","The paper does not report any individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing. It focuses on the outcomes of a clinical trial involving 62 participants but does not mention sequencing of HIV samples. The absence of any mention of sequencing or individuals from whom samples were obtained indicates that no sequencing was done. The study focused on the clinical and virological outcomes of the treatment regimen rather than on sequencing the HIV genome from patient samples.  Therefore, the answer is '0' as the paper does not provide any evidence of individuals from whom samples were obtained for sequencing."
36706364,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,No country,"ARTIST is a single arm, prospective, interventional study conducted in Khayelitsha, South Africa, which switched 62 adults with two viral loads (VL) >1000 copies/mL from tenofovir, lamivudine/emtricitabine and an NNRTI to TLD. We report efficacy to 72 weeks and, in a post hoc analysis, evaluated VL trajectories of individuals with viraemic episodes","The paper does not mention any sequenced samples, hence no countries. ARTIST is a single arm, prospective, interventional study conducted in Khayelitsha, South Africa, which switched 62 adults with two viral loads from tenofovir, lamivudine/emtricitabine and an NNRTI to TLD. Hence it is single arm study and possible to be conducted in one country. Hence the answer is 'No Country' and not 'No Sequencing'."
36706364,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,No year,"Participants were enrolled and switched to TLD with additional 50 mg dolutegravir for two weeks to overcome efavirenz induction effects on dolutegravir metabolism and transport. Study visits with clinicians occurred every four weeks to week 24, then at week 36, 48 and 72. VL was assessed at baseline and every subsequent visit, with a repeat VL after two weeks of enhanced adherence counselling if VL was >50 copies/mL after week 12. If the repeat VL was >500 copies/mL, a genotypic resistance test was performed per protocol. Baseline genotypic resistance testing was performed retrospectively for all participants and was not available to inform treatment decisions","The paper does not mention any years from which samples were obtained, since no samples were obtained for sequencing. It focuses on the outcomes of a clinical trial involving 62 participants and does not provide any information on the years of sample collection. The absence of any mention of specific years or timeframes for sample collection indicates that no sequencing was performed. Hence the paper doesn't doesn't mention any years from whi samples we're obtained. Hence the answer is 'No year' and not 'No Sequencing'."
36706364,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The study is a genotypic resistance testing study, not sequencing study.","The paper does not mention any process of cloning samples prior to sequencing. It focuses on the outcomes of a clinical trial and genotypic resistance testing without any details on cloning of samples. The absence of any mention of cloning indicates that no cloning was performed. The study focus on the genotypic resistance, genotypic resistance does not require clnoing the samples. Hence the ans is 'No'"
