36706364,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"Viologocal suppression was 86% (95% Confidence Interval (CI) 74-93), 74% (95% CI 61-84) and 75% (95% CI 63-86) <50 copies/mL, and 95%, 84% and 77% <400 copies/mL at week 24, 48 and 72 respectively, with 89% (50/56) resistant (Stanford score ≥15) to tenofovir and/or lamivudine pre-switch. We report efficacy to 72 weeks and, in a post hoc analysis, evaluated VL trajectories of individuals with viraemic episodes","The paper mentions that 89% (50/56) of the participants with resistance to tenofovir and/or lamivudine had genotypic resistance (Stanford score ≥15) pre-switch. In this study 62 adults had viral loads (VL) >1000 copies/mL from tenofovir, lamivudine/emtricitabine and an NNRTI to had switched to TLD with additional 50 mg dolutegriv, genotypic resistance testing should have been done but the paper doesn't mention the sequencing of HIV isolates hence we conclude it is not a sequencing study. Hence the answer is No."
36706364,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"We conducted a single arm, prospective, interventional study in two primary care clinics in Khayelitsha, South Africa. This study was designed to evaluate the virologic efficacy of TLD in second-line ART. A full description of the study design was published with the week 24 primary endpoint results.","The paper describes the ARTIST study as a single arm, prospective, interventional study conducted in two primary care clinics in Khayelitsha, South Africa. This indicates that the patients were part of a clinical trial designed to evaluate the virologic efficacy of the treatment regimen. The mention of study design, protocol approval and primary endpoints all suggest that the study was a clinical trial. Hence the answer is Yes."
36706364,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"The study is a genotypic resistance testing study, not sequencing study.","The paper does not report any HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs. It focuses on the outcomes of a clinical trial involving participants who had virological failure on a first-line regimen and were switched to a second-line regimen, but does not mention sequencing of HIV samples. The absence of any mention of sequences or individuals with previous ARV history indicates that no sequencing was performed. Hence the answer is No."
36706364,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, INSTI","A full description of the study design was published with the week 24 primary endpoint results4. The protocol was approved by the University of Cape Town's Human Research Ethics Committee (039/2019) and is available with the statistical analysis plan on ClinicalTrials.gov (NCT03991013). Adults who had virologic failure (defined as two consecutive VL >1000 copies/mL, 2-24 months apart) on a first-line regimen consisting of tenofovir, XTC and efavirenz or nevirapine were invited to participate. Participants were enrolled and switched to TLD with additional 50 mg dolutegravir for two weeks to overcome efavirenz induction effects on dolutegravir metabolism and transport.","The paper mentions that participants had virologic failure on a first-line regimen consisting of tenofovir, lamivudine/emtricitabine and an NNRTI (efavirenz or nevirapine) and were switched to a second-line regimen containing dolutegravir (an integrase inhibitor) and tenofovir/lamivudine. This indicates that the individuals in the study had received drugs from the NRTI, NNRTI, and INSTI classes before switching to the study regimen. Hence the answer is NRTI, NNRTI, INSTI."
36706364,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Emtricitabine, Efavirenz, Nevirapine, Dolutegravir","A full description of the study design was published with the week 24 primary endpoint results4. The protocol was approved by the University of Cape Town's Human Research Ethics Committee (039/2019) and is available with the statistical analysis plan on ClinicalTrials.gov (NCT03991013). Adults who had virologic failure (defined as two consecutive VL >1000 copies/mL, 2-24 months apart) on a first-line regimen consisting of tenofovir, XTC and efavirenz or nevirapine were invited to participate. Participants were enrolled and switched to TLD with additional 50 mg dolutegravir for two weeks to overcome efavirenz induction effects on dolutegravir metabolism and transport.","The paper mentions that participants had virologic failure on a first-line regimen consisting of tenofovir, lamivudine/emtricitabine and an NNRTI (efavirenz or nevirapine) and were switched to a second-line regimen containing dolutegravir (an integrase inhibitor) and tenofovir/lamivudine. This indicates that the individuals in the study had received tenofovir, lamivudine, emtricitabine, efavirenz, nevirapine and dolutegravir before switching to the study regimen. Hence the answer is Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Emtricitabine, Efavirenz, Nevirapine, Dolutegravir."
36738248,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We included 594 pairs of SS and NGS from 527 SHCS participants; The SHCS Drug Resistance Database contains the HIV sequence data, primarily partial pol genes, sequenced from these plasma samples using SS.","The paper mentions that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples, specifically from participants enrolled in the Swiss HIV Cohort Study (SHCS). This indicates that the study involved sequencing HIV from clinical samples."
36738248,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,No evidence,"The paper focuses on the comparison of drug resistance mutations detected by Sanger sequencing (SS) and next-generation sequencing (NGS) methods, and it does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data or assays related to drug susceptibility testing. The methods described are centered around sequencing and analysis of drug resistance mutations rather than functional assays of drug susceptibility."
