36738248,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"pol, PR, RT and IN","SS was performed by amplification and sequencing of HIV genomic regions targeted by antiretroviral (ARV) drugs, such as the protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT) and integrase (IN) regions. We reconstructed the pol region of HIV genome for NGS sequences at different variant-calling thresholds.","The paper mentions the sequencing of the pol region along with the PR, RT and IN regions, dovetailing together the information about the pol region to get the information about the genome sequenced."
36738248,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing method and Illumina sequencing,"SS in the SHCS was performed by four laboratories using commercial and in-house methods; NGS sequences were generated using previously described protocols, including the Bridging the Epidemiology and Evolution of HIV in Europe (BEEHIVE) collaboration. The data were generated by manually extracting viral RNA from blood samples then reverse transcribing and amplifying the nucleic acids using primers to define four overlapping amplicons spanning the whole genome, which were fragmented and sequenced with Illumina MiSeq or HiSeq platforms.",The paper describes the use of Sanger sequencing (SS) and next-generation sequencing (NGS) using Illumina platforms to obtain the HIV sequences.
36738248,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,plasma,"The SHCS Drug Resistance Database contains the HIV sequence data, primarily partial pol genes, sequenced from these plasma samples using SS; NGS sequences were generated using previously described protocols, in the context of a variety of projects including the Bridging the Epidemiology and Evolution of HIV in Europe (BEEHIVE) collaboration. Briefly, the data were generated by manually extracting viral RNA from blood samples.","The paper mentions that plasma samples were collected from people living with HIV (PLHIV) in the SHCS and were used for sequencing, indicating that plasma was the clinical sample type sequenced."
36738248,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"At sample collection, 20.4% of PLHIV were on ART and 76.3% were ART naive. In the 594 sample pairs, 590 (99.3%) SS sequences covered the PR region, 584 (98.3%) SS sequences covered the RT region, and only 19 (3.2%) SS sequences covered the IN region of the HIV genome.","The paper mentioned that 76.3% were ART naive, which means 20.4% of PLHIV were on ART, but still the paper does not mention the treatment were failing or had completed the treatment."
36738248,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The study population consisted of people living with HIV (PLHIV) enrolled in the SHCS and the Zurich Primary HIV Cohort Study (ZPHI). The SHCS is a prospective multicentre study that is highly representative of the HIV-1 epidemic in Switzerland and includes broad, in-depth and high-quality genetic, biological, clinical and demographic data. As of August 2021, the SHCS has a cumulative total of 21 529 patients.",The paper describes a cohort study and does not mention that the patients were enrolled in a clinical trial.
36738248,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"At sample collection, 20.4% of PLHIV were on ART and 76.3% were ART naive. In the 594 sample pairs, 590 (99.3%) SS sequences covered the PR region, 584 (98.3%) SS sequences covered the RT region, and only 19 (3.2%) SS sequences covered the IN region of the HIV genome.","The paper mentions that 20.4% of PLHIV were on ART at the time of sample collection, indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
36738248,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI,NNRTI","For each of the 594 sample pairs, we assessed the occurrence of 53 DRMs in the PI and RT region that confer at least low-level resistance to a PI, NRTI or NNRTI drug by themselves. We find 166 (27.9%) of 578 SS sequences with at least low-level resistance to at least one ARV drug. To compare the detection of HIV-DRMs by SS and NGS, we included participants enrolled in the Swiss HIV Cohort Study (SHCS) with SS and NGS sequences available with sample collection dates ≤7 days apart.","The paper assesses the occurrence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) in the protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN) regions, which correspond to the PI, NRTI, and NNRTI drug classes. This indicates that sequences were obtained from individuals who had received drugs from these classes."
