36851704,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"The first nationally representative cross-sectional HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) survey was conducted in Uruguay in 2018–2019 among adults diagnosed with HIV and initiating or reinitiating antiretroviral therapy (ART). A total of 206 participants were enrolled in the survey; 63.2% were men, 85.7% were >25 years of age, and 35.6% reported previous exposure to antiretroviral (ARV) drugs","The paper reports that 206 adults diagnosed with HIV and initiating or reinitiating antiretroviral therapy (ART) were enrolled in the survey, and HIV sequences were obtained from their samples. This indicates that the paper does report HIV sequences from patient samples."
36851704,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Abstract. The first nationally representative cross-sectional HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) survey was conducted in Uruguay in 2018–2019 among adults diagnosed with HIV and initiating or reinitiating antiretroviral therapy (ART). Protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes of HIV-1 were sequenced. A total of 206 participants were enrolled in the survey; 63.2% were men, 85.7% were >25 years of age, and 35.6% reported previous exposure to antiretroviral (ARV) drugs. The prevalence of HIVDR to efavirenz or nevirapine was significantly higher (OR: 1.82, p < 0.001) in adults with previous ARV drug exposure (20.3%, 95% CI: 18.7–22.0%) compared to adults without previous ARV drug exposure (12.3%, 11.0–13.8%). HIVDR to any nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors was 10.3% (9.4–11.2%). HIVDR to ritonavir-boosted protease inhibitors was 1.5% (1.1–2.1%); resistance to ritonavir-boosted darunavir was 0.9% (0.4–2.1%) among adults without previous ARV drug exposure and it was not observed among adults with previous ARV drug exposure. Resistance to integrase inhibitors was 12.7% (11.7–13.8%), yet HIVDR to dolutegravir, bictegravir, and cabotegravir was not observed. The high level (>10%) of HIVDR to efavirenz highlights the need to accelerate the transition to the WHO-recommended dolutegravir-based ART. Access to dolutegravir-based ART should be prioritised for people reporting previous ARV drug exposure.","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on HIV drug resistance survey results, genotyping success rates, and resistance predictions using the Stanford HIV drug resistance database."
36851704,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,The data presented in this study are available on request from the corresponding author.,"The paper mentions that the data presented in the study are available on request from the corresponding author, but does not mention any data that has been made publicly available."
36851704,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No HIV GenBank accession numbers were provided,No evidence,The paper does not provide any specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on the study results and methods without listing the accession numbers.
36851704,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,206,"A total of 206 participants were enrolled in the survey; 63.2% were men, 85.7% were >25 years of age, and 35.6% reported previous exposure to antiretroviral (ARV) drugs.","The paper states that 206 adults who initiated or reinitiated ART in Uruguay were enrolled in the survey, and HIV sequences were obtained from their samples."
36851704,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Uruguay,The first nationally representative cross-sectional HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) survey was conducted in Uruguay in 2018–2019 among adults diagnosed with HIV and initiating or reinitiating antiretroviral therapy (ART).,The paper mentions that the survey was conducted in Uruguay and the samples were obtained from adults initiating or reinitiating antiretroviral therapy in Uruguay.
36851704,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2018-2019,The first nationally representative cross-sectional HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) survey was conducted in Uruguay in 2018–2019 among adults diagnosed with HIV and initiating or reinitiating antiretroviral therapy (ART).,"The paper states that the survey was conducted in Uruguay during 2018-2019, indicating the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained."
36851704,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"HIV RNA was extracted from 1 mL of plasma (QIAamp Viral RNA Kit; QIAGEN, Valencia, CA, USA). Protease (HXB2 positions 6–99), reverse transcriptase (HXB2 positions 1–251), and integrase (HXB2 positions 1–288) HIV genes were amplified and sequenced using in-house-validated protocols. Sanger sequencing was performed on a 3730xl Genetic Analyser (ThermoFisher, Waltham, MA, USA). Sequences were assembled using ReCall, and the post-testing quality assurance was carried out using the WHO/BCCfE HIVDR quality control tool. The genotyping success rate was 99.0% (204/206) for protease and reverse transcriptase and 99.5% (205/206) for integrase.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It mentions that HIV RNA was extracted from plasma samples and sequenced using Sanger sequencing, which indicates that samples were not cloned."
36851704,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase","Protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes of HIV-1 were sequenced. HIV RNA was extracted from 1 mL of plasma (QIAamp Viral RNA Kit; QIAGEN, Valencia, CA, USA). Protease (HXB2 positions 6–99), reverse transcriptase (HXB2 positions 1–251), and integrase (HXB2 positions 1–288) HIV genes were amplified and sequenced using in-house-validated protocols. Sanger sequencing was performed on a 3730xl Genetic Analyser.","The paper mentions that *protease*, *reverse transcriptase*, and *integrase* genes of HIV-1 were sequenced."
36851704,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"HIV RNA was extracted from 1 mL of plasma (QIAamp Viral RNA Kit; QIAGEN, Valencia, CA, USA). Protease (HXB2 positions 6–99), reverse transcriptase (HXB2 positions 1–251), and integrase (HXB2 positions 1–288) HIV genes were amplified and sequenced using in-house-validated protocols. Sanger sequencing was performed on a 3730xl Genetic Analyser.",The paper mentions that Sanger sequencing was performed on a 3730xl Genetic Analyser.
36851704,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Whole blood specimens were collected by venipuncture in EDTA-containing tubes from participants before initiating or reinitiating ART. Plasma was separated within 12 h of collection; all plasma specimens were stored at −80 °C at the National HIV-AIDS Reference Laboratory of Uruguay until the completion of the specimen collection period (October 2019). Plasma specimens were shipped on dry ice to the Centre for Research in Infectious Diseases of the National Institute of Respiratory Diseases in Mexico City, a WHO-designated regional laboratory for HIV drug resistance testing.","The paper specifies that plasma specimens were used for sequencing, which indicates the type of clinical specimens that were sequenced."
36851704,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,The first nationally representative cross-sectional HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) survey was conducted in Uruguay in 2018–2019 among adults diagnosed with HIV and initiating or reinitiating antiretroviral therapy (ART). Adults ≥ 18 years of age diagnosed with HIV initiating or reinitiating ART were eligible for survey enrolment.,"The paper mentioned the study included adult diagnosed with HIV  initiating or reinitiating ART, which means the sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen."
36851704,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,The Ministry of Health of Uruguay implemented a cross-sectional nationally representative survey following the WHO-recommended methods. Two public and private clinics provided ART in Uruguay at the time of the survey. Eleven ART clinics were selected as survey sites. These clinics served 94% of all adults initiating and reinitiating ART in Uruguay at the time of the survey. The excluded eleven ART clinics had less than ten adults initiating and reinitiating ART per year. The sample size was calculated using the WHO sample size calculator tool and utilised a finite population correction.,The paper does not mention that the patients were in a clinical trial. It describes a cross-sectional nationally representative survey conducted among adults diagnosed with HIV and initiating or reinitiating antiretroviral therapy in Uruguay.
36851704,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Adults ≥ 18 years of age diagnosed with HIV initiating or reinitiating ART were eligible for survey enrolment. The proportion of individuals who reported previous exposure to ARV drugs was 35.6% (95% CI: 34.2–37.0%). Men were less likely to have been exposed to ARV drugs before survey enrolment than women (p < 0.001). People older than 25 years of age were more likely to have had previous ARV drug exposure(s) than people 25 years of age or younger (p < 0.001). Prior ART for the treatment of HIV infection followed by treatment interruption of >3 months was the most commonly reported type of previous ARV drug exposure (79.8%, 95% CI: 69.8–87.0%%, n/N: 58/73), followed by post-exposure prophylaxis (1.3%, 95% CI: 0.2--10.7%, n/N: 1/73), and prevention of mother-to-child transmission of HIV (1.3%, 95% CI: 0.2--10.6%%, n/N: 1/73).",The paper mentioned that 35.6% of participants reported previous exposure to ARV drugs.
36851704,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI/r, INSTI","NNRTI-based ART regimens were the most frequently prescribed (55.1%, 95% CI: 54--56.1%), followed by dolutegravir (DTG)-based regimens (29.4%, 95% CI: 28.4--30.4%), and PI/r-based regimens (11.7%, 95% CI: 11.0--12.6%) (Table 1).","The paper mentions that the individuals received NRTI, NNRTI, PI/r, INSTI-based ART regimens."
36851704,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"EFV, NVP, TDF, FTC, 3TC, ATV/r, LPV/r, DTG, DRV/r, RAL",HIV drug resistance was defined as the presence of a penalty score ≥15 using the Stanford HIVdb algorithm. ABC: abacavir; ART: antiretroviral therapy; ATV/r: atazanavir/ritonavir; BIC: bictegravir; CAB: cabotegravir; CI: confidence interval; DOR: doravirine; DRV/r: darunavir/ritonavir; DTG: dolutegravir; EFV: efavirenz; ETR: etravirine; EVG: elvitegravir; FTC: emtricitabine; INSTI: integrase strand-transfer inhibitor; LPV/r: lopinavir/ritonavir; NNRTI: non-nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitor; NRTI: nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitor; NVP: nevirapine; PI/r: boosted protease inhibitor; RAL: raltegravir; RPV: rilpivirine; TDF: tenofovir disoproxil fumarate; ZDV: zidovudine; 3TC: lamivudine.,"The paper mentions that the individuals received drugs from various classes including NRTI, NNRTI, PI/r, INSTI-based drugs. In Table 5, the received drugs EFV, NVP, TDF, FTC, 3TC, ATV/r, LPV/r, DTG, DRV/r, RAL were listed."
36851760,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Dried blood spots (DBSs) were obtained from 107 HIV-1-infected children aged less than 15 years old living in two West African countries, Mali and Benin. Genetic sequencing of HIV integrase was successful in 75 samples.","The paper mentions that HIV-1 sequences were obtained from 107 children infected with HIV-1, and Genetic sequencing of HIV integrase was successful in 75 samples."
36851760,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We determined the prevalence of integrase gene polymorphisms and the frequency of naturally occurring amino acid (aa) substitutions at positions associated with INI resistance. Genotypic-resistance testing was conducted on the integrase gene's target regions of HIV-1 using the ANRS ((French) National Agency for Research on AIDS and Viral Hepatitis) method ( http://www.hivfrenchresistance.org/ , accessed on 2 January 2023).",The paper focus on sequencing and resistance testing of clinical samples without mention of in vitro drug susceptibility.
36851760,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,All sequences were submitted to GenBank and registered under accession numbers OQ435656-OQ435729.,"The paper explicitly states that all sequences were submitted to GenBank, indicating they were made publicly available."
36851760,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,OQ435656-OQ435729,All sequences were submitted to GenBank and registered under accession numbers OQ435656-OQ435729.,The paper provides the specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
36851760,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,107,"Dried blood spots (DBSs) were obtained from 107 HIV-1-infected children aged less than 15 years old living in two West African countries, Mali and Benin.",The paper states that samples were collected from 107 HIV-1-infected children.
36851760,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,"Mali, Benin","Dried blood spots (DBSs) were obtained from 107 HIV-1-infected children aged less than 15 years old living in two West African countries, Mali and Benin.",The paper specifies that the samples were obtained from children living in Mali and Benin.
36851760,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2015-2020,"The DBSs were collected from 51 ART-treated children with virological failure, defined as having at least one viral load (VL) >1000 copies/mL, during a study conducted in Benin between 2015 and 2016 and from 56 ART-naïve children, collected between 2018 and 2020 during early diagnosis testing and follow-up in Mali.","The paper provides a timeline for when the samples were collected, specifying the years."
36851760,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,Target integrase regions of the HIV-1 were subsequently amplified by PCR after reverse-transcription production of complementary DNA (cDNA) and sequenced using the Sanger method.,"The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, indicating they were not cloned."
36851760,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Integrase,Genetic sequencing of HIV integrase was successful in 75 samples. HIV-1 RNA was extracted from the 107 DBSs using EasyMag technology as per the manufacturer's instructions. Target integrase regions of the HIV-1 were subsequently amplified by PCR after reverse-transcription production of complementary DNA (cDNA) and sequenced using the Sanger method.,The paper mentions that genetic sequencing was done specifically on the integrase gene.
36851760,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"The DBSs were dried at ambient temperature and then sent to the Virology Laboratory at the Saint-Antoine Hospital (Paris, France) for sequencing. HIV-1 RNA was extracted from the 107 DBSs using EasyMag technology as per the manufacturer’s instructions. Target integrase regions of the HIV-1 were subsequently amplified by PCR after reverse-transcription production of complementary DNA (cDNA) and sequenced using the Sanger method. Genotypic-resistance testing was conducted on the integrase gene’s target regions of HIV-1 using the ANRS (French National Agency for Research on AIDS and Viral Hepatitis) method (http://www.hivfrenchresistance.org/, accessed on 2 January 2023).",The paper states that the target regions were sequenced using the Sanger method.
36851760,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Dried blood spots (DBS),"Dried blood spots (DBSs) were obtained from 107 HIV-1-infected children aged less than 15 years old living in two West African countries, Mali and Benin. Whatman 903 filter paper was used to collect the DBSs, with 50 uL of blood being dropped into each concentric circle. The DBSs were dried at ambient temperature and then sent to the Virology Laboratory at the Saint-Antoine Hospital (Paris, France) for sequencing.",The paper specifies that dried blood spots were the type of samples collected and sequenced.
36851760,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"The DBSs were collected from 51 ART-treated children with virological failure, defined as having at least one viral load (VL) >1000 copies/mL, during a study conducted in Benin between 2015 and 2016. All children were naïve to INI treatment, 56 were naïve to anti-retroviral therapy (ART), and 51 had received ART.",The paper mentions that samples were collected from children with virological failure.
36851760,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Dried blood spots (DBSs) were obtained from 107 HIV-1-infected children aged less than 15 years old living in two West African countries, Mali and Benin. The DBSs were collected from 51 ART-treated children with virological failure, defined as having at least one viral load (VL) >1000 copies/mL, during a study conducted in Benin between 2015 and 2016 and from 56 ART-naïve children, collected between 2018 and 2020 during early diagnosis testing and follow-up in Mali.",The paper discusses sample collection during a study but does not mention that it was a clinical trial.
36851760,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Dried blood spots (DBSs) were obtained from 107 HIV-1-infected children aged less than 15 years old living in two West African countries, Mali and Benin. The DBSs were collected from 51 ART-treated children with virological failure, defined as having at least one viral load (VL) >1000 copies/mL, during a study conducted in Benin between 2015 and 2016.","The paper mentions that samples were collected from children who had received ART, indicating they had previously received ARV drugs."
36851760,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NNRTI-based regimens, NRTI and PI-based regimens","At enrollment, 90.3% of the treated children were receiving non-nucleoside-reverse-transcriptase-inhibitor (NNRTI)-based regimens, and only 9.7% received both nucleoside-reverse-transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI)- and PI-based regimens.","The paper lists the specific drug classes that were part of the treatment regimens the children were on, indicating the drug classes they received before sample sequencing."
36851760,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not specified,"At enrollment, 90.3% of the treated children were receiving non-nucleoside-reverse-transcriptase-inhibitor (NNRTI)-based regimens, and only 9.7% received both nucleoside-reverse-transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI)- and PI-based regimens. No residue substitutions of major INI RAMs at codons 66, 92, 121, 143, 147, 148, 155, or 263 were observed in the 75 sequences. Of the 75 sequences, 25/75, 33%, harbored at least one INI accessory polymorphism mutation (L74I/M, Q95A/H/P, T97A, G118V, E138Q, S153F, E157Q, G163E/N/T/Q, or S230N) and 48 (64%) harbored at least one polymorphism mutation, described as having no impact on INI sensitivity. Most common HIV drug classes and drugs in table 3",The paper does not list the specific drugs that were received by the individuals in the study.
36920025,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The study focuses on the selection of HIV-1 for resistance to protease inhibitors in cell culture, and does not mention obtaining sequences from patient samples.",The paper does not provide any information regarding patient samples or sequences derived from patients. It specifically describes laboratory-based experiments.
36920025,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"The study reports the effective concentration needed at the time of virus production to reduce infectivity by 50% (EC50) values for darunavir (DRV) and the UMASS analogues, which is a measure of in vitro drug susceptibility.","The paper mentions that EC50 values were determined for darunavir and its analogues as a measure of their potency in reducing HIV-1 infectivity in cell culture, indicating the presence of in vitro drug susceptibility data."
36920025,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,The sequencing data is available at NIH Sequencing Read Archive (SRA) under BioProject ID PRJNA853351.,"The paper explicitly states that the sequencing data is publicly available through the NIH Sequencing Read Archive, indicating that the sequences have been made accessible."
36920025,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not provided,"The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, as it does not report sequences from patient samples.",The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers because it does not report obtaining sequences from HIV isolates.
