36931676,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"The Swedish InfCareHIV cohort was established in 2003 to ensure equal and effective care of people living with HIV (PLHIV) and enable long-term follow-up. InfCareHIV includes data on HIV-related biomarkers and antiretroviral therapies (ART) and also on demographics, patient-reported outcome measures and patient-reported experience measures. Cohort variables Variables collected and manually registered by a health professional at enrolment include sex at birth, gender identity (added 2021), country of birth, mode of HIV transmission, date of any last negative HIV-test and first positive HIV-test (in Sweden and if relevant abroad), any AIDS diagnoses (eg, pneumocystis pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis or tuberculosis), confirmed primary HIV infection and suspected country of HIV transmission. Data are collected/updated at each follow-up visit; manually or automatically depending on the type of data and the HIV centre. Data include ART start and stop dates, ART regimen (including doses and mode of administration) and the reason for any change of drug regimens, prophylaxis of opportunistic infections and selected co-medications. Serological and virological data on co-infection with hepatitis C and B virus, weight, date and type of AIDS-defining events, date and cause of death are also included. HIV-RNA (plasma and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)) and HIV drug resistance (including viral sequences) results, described as both mutations and predicted phenotypic sensitivity, as well as CD4+ and CD8+ T cell counts and CD4/CD8 ratios are either automatically or manually included depending on the HIV centre. HIV-1 subtype and HLA B-5701-allele are registered.","The paper mentions that the InfCareHIV cohort includes data on HIV-related biomarkers and mentions the collection of HIV sequences. Specifically, it states that ""Data include ART start and stop dates, ART regimen (including doses and mode of administration) and the reason for any change of drug regimens, prophylaxis of opportunistic infections and selected co-medications. Serological and virological data on co-infection with hepatitis C and B virus, weight, date and type of AIDS-defining events, date and cause of death are also included. HIV-RNA (plasma and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)) and HIV drug resistance (including viral sequences) results, described as both mutations and predicted phenotypic sensitivity, as well as CD4+ and CD8+ T cell counts and CD4/CD8 ratios are either automatically or manually included depending on the HIV centre. HIV-1 subtype and HLA B-5701-allele are registered."" This indicates that the study does report HIV sequences from patient samples.. The paper mentions that the InfCareHIV cohort includes data on HIV-related biomarkers and mentions the collection of HIV sequences. Specifically, it states that"
36931676,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The Swedish InfCareHIV cohort was established in 2003 to ensure equal and effective care of people living with HIV (PLHIV) and enable long-term follow-up. InfCareHIV functions equally as a decision support system as a quality registry, ensuring up-to-date data reported in real time. Cohort variables Variables collected and manually registered by a health professional at enrolment include sex at birth, gender identity (added 2021), country of birth, mode of HIV transmission, date of any last negative HIV-test and first positive HIV-test (in Sweden and if relevant abroad), any AIDS diagnoses (eg, pneumocystis pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis or tuberculosis), confirmed primary HIV infection and suspected country of HIV transmission (online supplemental table 2). Data are collected/updated at each follow-up visit; manually or automatically depending on the type of data and the HIV centre. Data include ART start and stop dates, ART regimen (including doses and mode of administration) and the reason for any change of drug regimens, prophylaxis of opportunistic infections and selected co-medications. Serological and virological data on co-infection with hepatitis C and B virus, weight, date and type of AIDS-defining events, date and cause of death are also included. HIV-RNA (plasma and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)) and HIV drug resistance (including viral sequences) results, described as both mutations and predicted phenotypic sensitivity, as well as CD4+ and CD8+ T cell counts and CD4/CD8 ratios are either automatically or manually included depending on the HIV centre. HIV-1 subtype and HLA B-5701-allele are registered (online supplemental table 2).","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on clinical outcomes, drug resistance mutations, and the epidemiology of HIV in the cohort. Although the paper mentions HIV drug resistance, it does not provide direct evidence of in vitro drug susceptibility testing or results. The cohort variables include data on HIV drug resistance, including viral sequences, but this does not equate to in vitro susceptibility testing.  Data include ART start and stop dates, ART regimen (including doses and mode of administration) and the reason for any change of drug regimens, prophylaxis of opportunistic infections and selected co-medications. This indicates that the cohort collect data on ARV drugs that were received by individual but does not indicate the the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data. The focus of the paper is on the cohor people living with HIV in Sweden to ensure equal and effective care, which indicates the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data. Furthermore, the paper mentions that data on HIV drug resistance were collected including viral sequences, but this refers to the resistance data from patient samples on the cohort.  The clinical decisiob suipport sestem focuses on drst of th HIV sequences data, indicating the the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples, which supports the the evidence and rationale for question 1."
36931676,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"Cohort variables Variables collected and manually registered by a health professional at enrolment include sex at birth, gender identity (added 2021), country of birth, mode of HIV transmission, date of any last negative HIV-test and first positive HIV-test (in Sweden and if relevant abroad), any AIDS diagnoses (eg, pneumocystis pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis or tuberculosis), confirmed primary HIV infection and suspected country of HIV transmission. To date (August 2022), 13 029 PLHIV have been included of whom 8436 are currently in active care (table 1, figure 1). In total 42 PLHIV have been diagnosed with HIV-2 of whom 28 are in active care. In the beginning of the HIV epidemic the majority of PLHIV were born in Sweden, most of whom were men who have sex with men (57.5%) or transmitted through intravenous drug use (26.9%) (online supplemental table 1). Variables collected and manually registered by a health professional at enrolment include sex at birth, gender identity (added 2021), country of birth, mode of HIV transmission, date of any last negative HIV-test and first positive HIV-test (in Sweden and if relevant abroad), any AIDS diagnoses (eg, pneumocystis pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis or tuberculosis), confirmed primary HIV infection and suspected country of HIV transmission (online supplemental table 2).","The paper mentions that the InfCareHIV cohort includes data on HIV-related biomarkers and mentions the collection of HIV sequences. Specifically, it states that ""Data include ART start and stop dates, ART regimen (including doses and mode of administration) and the reason for any change of drug regimens, prophylaxis of opportunistic infections and selected co-medications. Serological and virological data on co-infection with hepatitis C and B virus, weight, date and type of AIDS-defining events, date and cause of death are also included. HIV-RNA (plasma and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)) and HIV drug resistance (including viral sequences) results, described as both mutations and predicted phenotypic sensitivity, as well as CD4+ and CD8+ T cell counts and CD4/CD8 ratios are either automatically or manually included depending on the HIV centre. HIV-1 subtype and HLA B-5701-allele are registered."" This indicates that the study does report HIV sequences from patient samples.. The paper mentions that the InfCareHIV cohort includes data on HIV-related biomarkers and mentions the collection of HIV sequences. Specifically, it states that"
36931676,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide GenBank accession numbers.,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on the cohort description and the findings without providing specific accession numbers related to the sequences. Although the paper states that 'InfCareHIV also includes undocumented migrants who often end-up among the 3% that are lost-to follow-up ( table 1 ). Retrospective data from before the cohort was established as nation-wide in 2008 has been backlogged, including PLHIV deceased before 2008.', the data were validated but not the paper reports the genbank accesion numbers because this is not a study that focus on genbank accession numbers. The paper mentioned that 'At time of entering HIV care, PLHIV are informed about the registry, after which they can either give verbal consent or opt out.', indicating that it's a decision support system as a quality registry, which also not supports the finding. The Genbank accession numbers were not provided in the paper, although the sequences were share.  Data on HIV-RNA (plasma and CSF), CD4 cell counts (absolute and percentage), drug resistance and previous and current ART are presented through a graphical system. In addition, the legend below the graph presents data regarding date of HIV diagnosis, date of first ART and names of the HIV-team consisting of doctor, nurse and counsellor. Periods of pregnancy are also shown in the graph. All other information is easily accessible in the system. Also, since all viral (pol) sequences are stored in the database, reanalysis can easily be performed when new drugs or new information about resistance appear. Thus, data generated in clinical care are automatically 'reused' for research and development.The paper does not report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs, and I rechecked all about the drugs and how many individuals had samples sequenced, nothing to support this so I have to answer it as No, no evidence to support the finding."
36931676,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,The paper does not specify the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing.,"Cohort variables Variables collected and manually registered by a health professional at enrolment include sex at birth, gender identity (added 2021), country of birth, mode of HIV transmission, date of any last negative HIV-test and first positive HIV-test (in Sweden and if relevant abroad), any AIDS diagnoses (eg, pneumocystis pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis or tuberculosis), confirmed primary HIV infection and suspected country of HIV transmission. To date (August 2022), 13 029 PLHIV have been included of whom 8436 are currently in active care (table 1, figure 1). In total 42 PLHIV have been diagnosed with HIV-2 of whom 28 are in active care. In the beginning of the HIV epidemic the majority of PLHIV were born in Sweden, most of whom were men who have sex with men (57.5%) or transmitted through intravenous drug use (26.9%) (online supplemental table 1). Variables collected and manually registered by a health professional at enrolment include sex at birth, gender identity (added 2021), country of birth, mode of HIV transmission, date of any last negative HIV-test and first positive HIV-test (in Sweden and if relevant abroad), any AIDS diagnoses (eg, pneumocystis pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis or tuberculosis), confirmed primary HIV infection and suspected country of HIV transmission (online supplemental table 2).","The paper mentions that the InfCareHIV cohort includes 13,029 people living with HIV (PLHIV) in Sweden. However, it does not specify the exact number of individuals from whom HIV sequencing samples were obtained. It provides a detailed description of the cohort and the variables collected but does not give a specific count of the sequenced samples. It tells the total number of PLHIV but does not specify how many had samples were obtained for HIV sequencing. .The paper focuses on the cohort characteristics, treatment outcomes, and drug resistance findings without detailing the exact number of individuals whose samples were sequenced."
36931676,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,From Sweden,"The Swedish InfCareHIV cohort was established in 2003 to ensure equal and effective care of people living with HIV (PLHIV) and enable long-term follow-up. InfCareHIV functions equally as a decision support system as a quality registry, ensuring up-to-date data reported in real time. The InfCareHIV cohort was established in 2003 in the two largest cities in Sweden, Stockholm and Gothenburg. These two cities care for about half of all PLHIV in Sweden. The other Swedish clinics joined thereafter and since 2008 the cohort has had complete national coverage, including all 29 clinical HIV centres in Sweden. The aim of a quality registry is to develop and ensure quality of care systematically and continuously. This allows for comparisons on HIV care at a national, regional and clinical centre level and has led to increased equality of care on national level with diminishing differences in treatment results in Sweden's HIV centres.","he paper explicitly states that  the cohort study was established in Sweden. The study population, starting from the establishment of the cohort, includes the largest cities in Sweden, Stockholm and Gothenburg. Therefore, the paper only mentions the stud population in sweden, which means the samples were obtained from Sweden. No other countries were mentioned in the paper."
36931676,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The paper does not specify the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained.,"To date (August 2022), 13 029 PLHIV have been included of whom 8436 are currently in active care (table 1, figure 1). In total 42 PLHIV have been diagnosed with HIV-2 of whom 28 are in active care. In the beginning of the HIV epidemic the majority of PLHIV were born in Sweden, most of whom were men who have sex with men (57.5%) or transmitted through intravenous drug use (26.9%) (online supplemental table 1). Late diagnosis of HIV infection was identified as a key problem in the Swedish HIV-epidemic, and low-level HIV viraemia while on ART associated with all-cause mortality. Increased HIV RNA load in the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) despite suppression of the plasma viral load was found in 5% of PLHIV, a phenomenon referred to as 'CSF viral escape'. Dolutegravir-based treatment in PLHIV with pre-existing nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor-mutations was non-inferior to protease inhibitor-based regimens. An increase of transmitted drug resistance was observed in the InfCareHIV cohort. Lower efficacy for protease inhibitors was not due to lower adherence to treatment. Incidence of type 2 diabetes and insulin resistance was high in the ageing HIV population. Despite ART, the risk of infection-related cancer as well as lung cancer was increased in PLHIV compared with HIV-negative. PLHIV were less likely successfully treated for cervical precancer and more likely to have human papillomavirus types not included in current HPV vaccines. Self-reported sexual satisfaction in PLHIV is improving and is higher in women than men.",The paper does not specify the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained. It provides a description of the cohort and its objectives but does not mention the specific years related to the sequencing of samples. The only related time frame mentioned is the establishment of the cohort in 2003 and the active care status as of August 2022. This indicates the timeline of the cohort's activities but not the exact years of sample collection for sequencing. The paper focuses on the cohort's longitudinal data and the findings without detailing the specific years of sample acquisition for sequencing.
36931676,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,The paper does not mention whether samples were cloned prior to sequencing.,No evidence,"he paper does not provide any information regarding whether samples were cloned prior to sequencing. It focuses on the cohort description and the findings without discussing the specific laboratory methods used for sequencing, including whether cloning was performed. It focuses on the cohort description and objectives. The Swedish InfCareHIV cohort was established in 2003 to ensure equal and effective care of people living with HIV (PLHIV) and enable long-term follow-up. InfCareHIV functions equally as a decision support system as a quality registry, ensuring up-to-date data reported in real time.  Cohort variables Variables collected and manually registered by a health professional at enrolment include sex at birth, gender identity (added 2021), country of birth, mode of HIV transmission, date of any last negative HIV-test and first positive HIV-test (in Sweden and if relevant abroad), any AIDS diagnoses (eg, pneumocystis pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis or tuberculosis), confirmed primary HIV infection and suspected country of HIV transmission. Data are collected/updated at each follow-up visit; manually or automatically depending on the type of data and the HIV centre. Data include ART start and stop dates, ART regimen (including doses and mode of administration) and the reason for any change of drug regimens, prophylaxis of opportunistic infections and selected co-medications. Serological and virological data on co-infection with hepatitis C and B virus, weight, date and type of AIDS-defining events, date and cause of death are also included. HIV-RNA (plasma and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)) and HIV drug resistance (including viral sequences) results, described as both mutations and predicted phenotypic sensitivity, as well as CD4+ and CD8+ T cell counts and CD4/CD8 ratios are either automatically or manually included depending on the HIV centre. HIV-1 subtype and HLA B-5701-allele are registered (online supplemental table 2). It does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing."
36931676,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,The paper does not specify which HIV genes were sequenced.,"Cohort variables Variables collected and manually registered by a health professional at enrolment include sex at birth, gender identity (added 2021), country of birth, mode of HIV transmission, date of any last negative HIV-test and first positive HIV-test (in Sweden and if relevant abroad), any AIDS diagnoses (eg, pneumocystis pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis or tuberculosis), confirmed primary HIV infection and suspected country of HIV transmission. To date (August 2022), 13 029 PLHIV have been included of whom 8436 are currently in active care (table 1, figure 1). In total 42 PLHIV have been diagnosed with HIV-2 of whom 28 are in active care. In the beginning of the HIV epidemic the majority of PLHIV were born in Sweden, most of whom were men who have sex with men (57.5%) or transmitted through intravenous drug use (26.9%) (online supplemental table 1). Variables collected and manually registered by a health professional at enrolment include sex at birth, gender identity (added 2021), country of birth, mode of HIV transmission, date of any last negative HIV-test and first positive HIV-test (in Sweden and if relevant abroad), any AIDS diagnoses (eg, pneumocystis pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis or tuberculosis), confirmed primary HIV infection and suspected country of HIV transmission (online supplemental table 2).","The paper does not specify which HIV genes were sequenced. It focuses on the cohort description, the findings related to drug resistance, and the clinical outcomes, but does not detail the specific genes that were targeted for sequencing. The paper mentioned the cohort ass examination of drug resistance, and the sequenced sample were HIV drug resistance, which indicates the pol gene, but no specific gene were mentioned. The InfCareHIV cohort includes data on HIV-related biomarkers and antiretroviral therapies (ART) and also on demographics, the variables collected has mentioned includes the genotypic resistance which also indicate it a study focus on pol gene sequencing. The mainstay of InfCareHIV is the clinical decision support system (figure 1). Data on HIV-RNA levels (plasma and CSF), CD4 cell counts (absolute and percentage), drug resistance and previous and current ART are presented through a graphical system. In addition, the legend below the graph presents data regarding date of HIV diagnosis, date of first ART and names of the HIV-team consisting of doctor, nurse and counsellor. periods of pregnancy are also shown in the graph. All other information is easily accessible in the system. To helps support this, the mainstay of this study is the clinical decision support, which suggests that it's a study report the sequencing result of genotypic resistance, which also indicates the pol gene sequencing, the evidence is still not solid so I direcly answer it as not specified. However, there is conflict information, previously I found that this retrospective study includes drug resistant individuals, which indicates it's a cross-sectional study focusing on individual found sequences. But in the paper the temporality is not clear, I answer it as cross-sectional, however it's still worthy to explore this question."
36931676,10,What method was used for sequencing?,The paper does not specify the sequencing method used.,"Cohort variables Variables collected and manually registered by a health professional at enrolment include sex at birth, gender identity (added 2021), country of birth, mode of HIV transmission, date of any last negative HIV-test and first positive HIV-test (in Sweden and if relevant abroad), any AIDS diagnoses (eg, pneumocystis pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis or tuberculosis), confirmed primary HIV infection and suspected country of HIV transmission. To date (August 2022), 13 029 PLHIV have been included of whom 8436 are currently in active care (table 1, figure 1). In total 42 PLHIV have been diagnosed with HIV-2 of whom 28 are in active care. In the beginning of the HIV epidemic the majority of PLHIV were born in Sweden, most of whom were men who have sex with men (57.5%) or transmitted through intravenous drug use (26.9%) (online supplemental table 1). Variables collected and manually registered by a health professional at enrolment include sex at birth, gender identity (added 2021), country of birth, mode of HIV transmission, date of any last negative HIV-test and first positive HIV-test (in Sweden and if relevant abroad), any AIDS diagnoses (eg, pneumocystis pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis or tuberculosis), confirmed primary HIV infection and suspected country of HIV transmission (online supplemental table 2).","The paper does not mention the specific sequencing method used for the HIV samples. It focuses on the cohort description and the findings without detailing the laboratory techniques employed for sequencing. It focuses on the cohort description and objectives. The Swedish InfCareHIV cohort was established in 2003 to ensure equal and effective care of people living with HIV (PLHIV) and enable long-term follow-up. InfCareHIV functions equally as a decision support system as a quality registry, ensuring up-to-date data reported in real time.  Cohort variables Variables collected and manually registered by a health professional at enrolment include sex at birth, gender identity (added 2021), country of birth, mode of HIV transmission, date of any last negative HIV-test and first positive HIV-test (in Sweden and if relevant abroad), any AIDS diagnoses (eg, pneumocystis pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis or tuberculosis), confirmed primary HIV infection and suspected country of HIV transmission. Data are collected/updated at each follow-up visit; manually or automatically depending on the type of data and the HIV centre. Data include ART start and stop dates, ART regimen (including doses and mode of administration) and the reason for any change of drug regimens, prophylaxis of opportunistic infections and selected co-medications. Serological and virological data on co-infection with hepatitis C and B virus, weight, date and type of AIDS-defining events, date and cause of death are also included. HIV-RNA (plasma and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)) and HIV drug resistance (including viral sequences) results, described as both mutations and predicted phenotypic sensitivity, as well as CD4+ and CD8+ T cell counts and CD4/CD8 ratios are either automatically or manually included depending on the HIV centre. HIV-1 subtype and HLA B-5701-allele are registered (online supplemental table 2)."
36931676,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,The paper mentions that plasma and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) were sequenced.,"Cohort variables Variables collected and manually registered by a health professional at enrolment include sex at birth, gender identity (added 2021), country of birth, mode of HIV transmission, date of any last negative HIV-test and first positive HIV-test (in Sweden and if relevant abroad), any AIDS diagnoses (eg, pneumocystis pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis or tuberculosis), confirmed primary HIV infection and suspected country of HIV transmission. Data include ART start and stop dates, ART regimen (including doses and mode of administration) and the reason for any change of drug regimens, prophylaxis of opportunistic infections and selected co-medications. Serological and virological data on co-infection with hepatitis C and B virus, weight, date and type of AIDS-defining events, date and cause of death are also included. HIV-RNA (plasma and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)) and HIV drug resistance (including viral sequences) results, described as both mutations and predicted phenotypic sensitivity, as well as CD4+ and CD8+ T cell counts and CD4/CD8 ratios are either automatically or manually included depending on the HIV centre. HIV-1 subtype and HLA B-5701-allele are registered (online supplemental table 2).","The paper mentions that HIV-RNA levels in plasma and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) are included as part of the cohort variables. This indicates that plasma and CSF samples were sequenced to obtain the HIV sequences mentioned. This is further supported by the fact that the cohort includes data on HIV drug resistance mutations and predicted phenotypic sensitivity, which would typically involve sequencing of the virus from these samples."
36931676,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,The paper does not specify whether sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure.,"Cohort variables Variables collected and manually registered by a health professional at enrolment include sex at birth, gender identity (added 2021), country of birth, mode of HIV transmission, date of any last negative HIV-test and first positive HIV-test (in Sweden and if relevant abroad), any AIDS diagnoses (eg, pneumocystis pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis or tuberculosis), confirmed primary HIV infection and suspected country of HIV transmission. To date (August 2022), 13 029 PLHIV have been included of whom 8436 are currently in active care (table 1, figure 1). In total 42 PLHIV have been diagnosed with HIV-2 of whom 28 are in active care. In the beginning of the HIV epidemic the majority of PLHIV were born in Sweden, most of whom were men who have sex with men (57.5%) or transmitted through intravenous drug use (26.9%) (online supplemental table 1). Variables collected and manually registered by a health professional at enrolment include sex at birth, gender identity (added 2021), country of birth, mode of HIV transmission, date of any last negative HIV-test and first positive HIV-test (in Sweden and if relevant abroad), any AIDS diagnoses (eg, pneumocystis pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis or tuberculosis), confirmed primary HIV infection and suspected country of HIV transmission (online supplemental table 2).",The paper does not mention whether sequences were obtained specifically from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen. It focuses on the cohort description and the variables collected without detailing the treatment responses or virological outcomes of the individuals whose samples were sequenced. The paper focuses on the cohort's longitudinal data and the findings without detailing the specific treatment status or virological outcomes of the individuals whose samples were sequenced.
36931676,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The Swedish InfCareHIV cohort was established in 2003 to ensure equal and effective care of people living with HIV (PLHIV) and enable long-term follow-up. The InfCareHIV cohort was established in 2003 in the two largest cities in Sweden, Stockholm and Gothenburg. These two cities care for about half of all PLHIV in Sweden. The other Swedish clinics joined thereafter and since 2008 the cohort has had complete national coverage, including all 29 clinical HIV centres in Sweden.","The paper describes the InfCareHIV cohort as a registry-based study focusing on the care and treatment of people living with HIV in Sweden. It does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial,  The paper does not indicate that the patients were in a clinical trial. The focus is on the cohort's objectives, coverage, and outcomes rather than on clinical trial participation."
