36961945,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,Deep sequencing of HIV DNA from peripheral blood mononuclear cells and consensus sequencing from concurrent blood plasma (BP) was performed from antiretroviral (ART)-naïve adults with recent infection. Samples were collected from January 2005 to February 2020.,"The paper discusses the sequencing of HIV DNA from patient samples, specifically mentioning deep sequencing of HIV DNA from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) and consensus sequencing from blood plasma. This indicates that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
36961945,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We investigated whether deep sequencing of archived HIV DNA of antiretroviral-naïve persons with acute/early HIV infection could identify transmitted drug resistance mutations (DRM), per the IAS drug resistance algorithm, that are not detected by routine bulk (consensus) sequencing. Deep sequencing of HIV DNA from peripheral blood mononuclear cells and consensus sequencing from concurrent blood plasma (BP) was performed from antiretroviral (ART)-naïve adults with recent infection. We compared the prevalence of low-frequency (2–20%) and high-frequency (>20%) non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI), nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI), and protease inhibitor (PI) DRM.","The paper focuses on the detection of drug resistance mutations using sequencing methods, but it does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing."
36961945,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,Focus on sequences obtained from the study without mention of public availability,The paper does not mention that the sequences obtained were made publicly available. It focuses on the study's findings without detailing any public data sharing.
36961945,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No,No evidence,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It does not mention any public repository for the sequences.
36961945,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,190,"Overall, 190 individuals were included, 72 (37.9%) with acute, 20 (10.5%) very early, and 98 (51.6%) with recent HIV infection.","The paper states that a total of 190 individuals were included in the study, from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing."
36961945,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,United States,"This study included adults, age ≥18 years, with acute/early HIV infection enrolled in the San Diego PIRC.","The study participants were enrolled in the San Diego PIRC, indicating the samples were obtained from the United States."
36961945,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2005 to 2020,Samples were collected from January 2005 to February 2020 ( Table S2 ).,The paper mentions that samples were collected from January 2005 to February 2020.
36961945,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Deep sequencing of HIV DNA from peripheral blood mononuclear cells and consensus sequencing from concurrent blood plasma (BP) was performed from antiretroviral (ART)-naïve adults with recent infection. We compared the prevalence of low-frequency (2–20%) and high-frequency (>20%) non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI), nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI), and protease inhibitor (PI) DRM. This study included adults, age ≥18 years, with acute/early HIV infection enrolled in the San Diego PIRC. Samples were collected from January 2005 to February 2020 ( Table S2 ).","The paper describes the sequencing methods used, including deep sequencing and consensus sequencing, but it does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing."
36961945,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"PR, RT, IN","Genotypic resistance testing on PR, RT, and IN from proviral HIV-1 DNA was performed using a modified version of the ""deepTypeHIV"" assay. Bulk genotyping of HIV reverse transcriptase (RT) was performed to identify transmitted drug resistance (GeneSeq HIV; Monogram Biosciences, Inc., South San Francisco, CA or Viroseq v.2.0; Celera Diagnostics, Alameda, CA) in blood plasma before ART initiation.","The paper mentions genotypic resistance testing on the protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN) genes from proviral HIV-1 DNA."
36961945,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS),"From the same blood samples used to assess blood plasma, NGS of HIV partial pol was performed to identify DRMs present in archived proviral DNA (peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMC)).",The paper describes the use of Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS) for analyzing HIV sequences from proviral DNA and plasma HIV RNA.
