37017009,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We assessed levels of HIV drug resistance in children with virological failure.; Among 899 participants enrolled, the adjusted proportion of HIV drug resistance among children with virological failure was 87.5% (95% CI 83.0%--90.9%). Resistance to NNRTIs was detected in 77.4% (95% CI 72.5%--81.7%) of participants, and resistance to NRTIs in 69.5% (95% CI 62.9%--75.4%) of participants. Overall, resistance to PIs was detected in 7.7% (95% CI 4.4%--13.0%) of children.","The content mentions  that HIV drug resistance was assessed in children with virological failure, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples for analysis."
37017009,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"This cross-sectional study, performed from March 2017 to March 2019 in South Africa, enrolled HIV-positive children aged ≤19 years, receiving ART through public health facilities with recent evidence suggestive of virological failure (at least one viral load ≥1000 copies/mL), across 45 randomly selected high-volume clinics from all nine provinces.; Resistance genotyping was performed using next-generation sequencing technologies. Descriptive analysis taking into account survey design was used to determine outcomes.; Among 899 participants enrolled, the adjusted proportion of HIV drug resistance among children with virological failure was 87.5% (95% CI 83.0%--90.9%). Resistance to NNRTIs was detected in 77.4% (95% CI 72.5%--81.7%) of participants, and resistance to NRTIs in 69.5% (95% CI 62.9%--75.4%) of participants. Overall, resistance to PIs was detected in 7.7% (95% CI 4.4%--13.0%) of children.","he study focuses on genotypic resistance, which involves identifying mutations associated with drug resistance in the virus's genetic material rather than measuring the direct effectiveness of drugs on the virus in a lab setting (in vitro drug susceptibility). This is evident from the methods and results sections, which discuss the prevalence of resistance mutations rather than the susceptibility of the virus to drugs in vitro."
37017009,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,The study does not mention public availability of sequences.; No evidence,The paper does not provide any information regarding the public availability of the sequences obtained from the study.
37017009,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,The study does not provide any information regarding GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates.; No evidence,"The paper does not mention GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, indicating that this information is not reported."
37017009,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,899,"Among 899 participants enrolled, the adjusted proportion of HIV drug resistance among children with virological failure was 87.5% (95% CI 83.0%--90.9%). Resistance to NNRTIs was detected in 77.4% (95% CI 72.5%--81.7%) of participants, and resistance to NRTIs in 69.5% (95% CI 62.9%--75.4%) of participants. Overall, resistance to PIs was detected in 7.7% (95% CI 4.4%--13.0%) of children.","The content states that 899 participants were enrolled in the study, and this is the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing."
37017009,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,South Africa,"This cross-sectional study, performed from March 2017 to March 2019 in South Africa, enrolled HIV-positive children aged ≤19 years, receiving ART through public health facilities with recent evidence suggestive of virological failure (at least one viral load ≥1000 copies/mL), across 45 randomly selected high-volume clinics from all nine provinces.; No evidence","The study was conducted in South Africa, and all  samples were obtained within the country, as mentioned in the methods section."
37017009,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2017-2019,"This cross-sectional study, performed from March 2017 to March 2019 in South Africa, enrolled HIV-positive children aged ≤19 years, receiving ART through public health facilities with recent evidence suggestive of virological failure (at least one viral load ≥1000 copies/mL), across 45 randomly selected high-volume clinics from all nine provinces.; No evidence","The study was conducted from March 2017 to March 2019, indicating the years during which the samples were obtained."
37017009,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"PCR amplification was performed using an in-house method resulting in a single amplicon spanning 1.5 kb of *pol* encompassing the protease gene and the first 250 amino acids of the reverse transcriptase gene (HXB2 2358--3882).; Descriptive analysis taking into account survey design was used to determine outcomes. Among 899 participants enrolled, the adjusted proportion of HIV drug resistance among children with virological failure was 87.5% (95% CI 83.0%--90.9%). Resistance to NNRTIs was detected in 77.4% (95% CI 72.5%--81.7%) of participants, and resistance to NRTIs in 69.5% (95% CI 62.9%--75.4%) of participants. Overall, resistance to PIs was detected in 7.7% (95% CI 4.4%--13.0%) of children. Resistance genotyping was performed using next-generation sequencing technologies. Descriptive analysis taking into account survey design was used to determine outcomes.","The study does not mention any process of cloning samples prior to sequencing. Instead, it describes the use of PCR amplification resulting in a single amplicon, which implies direct sequencing of the PCR products without cloning."
37017009,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Protease and RT,"PCR amplification was performed using an in-house method resulting in a single amplicon spanning 1.5 kb of *pol* encompassing the protease gene and the first 250 amino acids of the reverse transcriptase gene (HXB2 2358--3882).; Descriptive analysis taking into account survey design was used to determine outcomes. Among 899 participants enrolled, the adjusted proportion of HIV drug resistance among children with virological failure was 87.5% (95% CI 83.0%--90.9%). Resistance to NNRTIs was detected in 77.4% (95% CI 72.5%--81.7%) of participants, and resistance to NRTIs in 69.5% (95% CI 62.9%--75.4%) of participants. Overall, resistance to PIs was detected in 7.7% (95% CI 4.4%--13.0%) of children. Resistance genotyping was performed using next-generation sequencing technologies.","The content specifically mentions the sequencing of the protease gene and the reverse transcriptase gene, both of which are common targets for HIV resistance genotyping."
37017009,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Next-generation sequencing (NGS),"Resistance genotyping was performed using next-generation sequencing technologies. Descriptive analysis taking into account survey design was used to determine outcomes. Among 899 participants enrolled, the adjusted proportion of HIV drug resistance among children with virological failure was 87.5% (95% CI 83.0%--90.9%). Resistance to NNRTIs was detected in 77.4% (95% CI 72.5%--81.7%) of participants, and resistance to NRTIs in 69.5% (95% CI 62.9%--75.4%) of participants. Overall, resistance to PIs was detected in 7.7% (95% CI 4.4%--13.0%) of children. Resistance genotyping was performed using next-generation sequencing technologies.","The methods section mentions that resistance genotyping was performed using next-generation sequencing technologies, which refers to the use of NGS for sequencing the HIV genomes."
37017009,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Whole blood was collected using standard phlebotomy methods into a 6 mL EDTA tube. A minimum of 500 μL plasma was ultra-centrifuged and total nucleic acid extracted using the MagNA Pure 2.0 automated extraction system (Roche Applied Science, Penzberg, Germany). PCR amplification was performed using an in-house method resulting in a single amplicon spanning 1.5 kb of *pol* encompassing the protease gene and the first 250 amino acids of the reverse transcriptase gene (HXB2 2358--3882). Amplicon concentrations were adjusted to a final concentration of 0.5 ng/μL and libraries prepared using the 96-sample Nextera ^®^ XT DNA Library Preparation Kit (Illumina, USA). Quantified amplicons were sequenced using the MiSeq V3 sequencing Kit (Illumina, San Diego, USA). MiSeq FastQ files were analysed using DeepChek ^®^ (Advanced Biological Laboratories, Luxembourg, Luxembourg), and results were generated at a 20% mutation detection threshold.","The paper describes the collection and processing of plasma specimens for sequencing, indicating that plasma was the clinical specimen sequenced in the study."
37017009,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"This cross-sectional study, performed from March 2017 to March 2019 in South Africa, enrolled HIV-positive children aged ≤19 years, receiving ART through public health facilities with recent evidence suggestive of virological failure (at least one viral load ≥1000 copies/mL), across 45 randomly selected high-volume clinics from all nine provinces. Resistance genotyping was performed using next-generation sequencing technologies. Descriptive analysis taking into account survey design was used to determine outcomes.; Among 899 participants enrolled, the adjusted proportion of HIV drug resistance among children with virological failure was 87.5% (95% CI 83.0%--90.9%). Resistance to NNRTIs was detected in 77.4% (95% CI 72.5%--81.7%) of participants, and resistance to NRTIs in 69.5% (95% CI 62.9%--75.4%) of participants. Overall, resistance to PIs was detected in 7.7% (95% CI 4.4%--13.0%) of children.","The study specifically targeted children with virological failure, indicating that sequences were obtained from these individuals."
37017009,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This cross-sectional study, performed from March 2017 to March 2019 in South Africa, enrolled HIV-positive children aged ≤19 years, receiving ART through public health facilities with recent evidence suggestive of virological failure (at least one viral load ≥1000 copies/mL), across 45 randomly selected high-volume clinics from all nine provinces.; A cross-sectional facility-based study with retrospective record review was conducted using a stratified cluster sampling approach. The study targeted HIV-positive children aged ≤19 years who were receiving ART at public health facilities across South Africa and had suggestive evidence of VF (defined as at least one recent VL test performed that was ≥1000 copies/mL within the previous 6 months).","The study was a cross-sectional facility-based study with retrospective record review, not a clinical trial."
37017009,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"This cross-sectional study, performed from March 2017 to March 2019 in South Africa, enrolled HIV-positive children aged ≤19 years, receiving ART through public health facilities with recent evidence suggestive of virological failure (at least one viral load ≥1000 copies/mL), across 45 randomly selected high-volume clinics from all nine provinces.; A cross-sectional facility-based study with retrospective record review was conducted using a stratified cluster sampling approach. The study targeted HIV-positive children aged ≤19 years who were receiving ART at public health facilities across South Africa and had suggestive evidence of VF (defined as at least one recent VL test performed that was ≥1000 copies/mL within the previous 6 months).","The study enrolled HIV-positive children who were receiving ART, indicating that they had previously received ARV drugs."
37017009,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs","A total of 418 children were receiving PI-based regimens, constituting 48.4% (95% CI 41.4%--55.3%) of all participants after adjusting for survey design; the median age of these participants was 11.7 (IQR 5.5--15.2) years, and the median most recent VL was 36 800 (7800--116 200) copies/mL. Four hundred and twenty-two participants were receiving NNRTI-based regimens 
[(45.0% (95% CI 38.2%--52.1%)
]; the median age of these participants was 13.4 (IQR 10.5--15.6) years and the median most recent VL was 18 500 (IQR 4530-66 881) copies/mL. The majority were receiving efavirenz-based regimens (98.7%); seven participants were receiving nevirapine. Amongst children aged 0--4 years, 8.7% were receiving NNRTI-based regimens, and 47.0% of children aged 5--9 years, 52.4% of children aged 10--14 years, and 48.9% amongst children 15--19 years. A total of 55 (6.5%, 95% CI 3.8%--11.1%) participants were receiving NRTI-only-based regimens. These included lamivudine only (n = 21), abacavir + lamivudine (n = 18) and abacavir only (n = 2), whilst the remainder were combinations of zidovudine, lamivudine, emtricitabine, stavudine and/or tenofovir disoproxil fumarate.","The results section specifies the regimens that participants were receiving, which include drugs from the NRTI, NNRTI, and PI classes."
37017009,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, lamivudine, zidovudine, efavirenz, nevirapine, ritonavir-boosted lopinavir, ritonavir-boosted atazanavir, abacavir, emtricitabine, stavudine","A total of 418 children were receiving PI-based regimens, constituting 48.4% (95% CI 41.4%--55.3%) of all participants after adjusting for survey design; the median age of these participants was 11.7 (IQR 5.5--15.2) years, and the median most recent VL was 36 800 (7800--116 200) copies/mL. The majority were receiving ritonavir-boosted lopinavir-based regimens (97.1%), whilst 2.9% were receiving ritonavir-boosted atazanavir. Four hundred and twenty-two participants were receiving NNRTI-based regimens [ (45.0% (95% CI 38.2%--52.1%) ]; the median age of these participants was 13.4 (IQR 10.5--15.6) years and the median most recent VL was 18 500 (IQR 4530-66 881) copies/mL. The majority were receiving efavirenz-based regimens (98.7%); seven participants were receiving nevirapine. Amongst children aged 0--4 years, 8.7% were receiving NNRTI-based regimens, and 47.0% of children aged 5--9 years, 52.4% of children aged 10--14 years, and 48.9% amongst children 15--19 years. A total of 55 (6.5%, 95% CI 3.8%--11.1%) participants were receiving NRTI-only-based regimens. These included lamivudine only (n = 21), abacavir + lamivudine (n = 18) and abacavir only (n = 2), whilst the remainder were combinations of zidovudine, lamivudine, emtricitabine, stavudine and/or tenofovir disoproxil fumarate.","The text details the specific drugs that participants were receiving, including their combinations, across the different regimens."
37029656,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"The aim of the study was to evaluate the prevalence trend of transmitted INSTI drug resistance (DR) and to assess factors associated with transmitted INSTI resistance among ART-naive PLWH over the past decade. We conducted a time-trend, single-centre study over the period 2009--19 on adult naive PLWH with a genotypic resistance test (GRT) performed before the ART initiation.","The paper discusses the evaluation of transmitted INSTI drug resistance and mentions conducting a genotypic resistance test (GRT) on adult naive PLWH, indicating that the study involved HIV sequences from patient samples."
37029656,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The aim of the study was to evaluate the prevalence trend of transmitted INSTI drug resistance (DR) and to assess factors associated with transmitted INSTI resistance among ART-naive PLWH over the past decade. We conducted a time-trend, single-centre study over the period 2009--19 on adult naive PLWH with a genotypic resistance test (GRT) performed before the ART initiation. GRT was determined by Sanger sequencing using an ABI PRISM 3130xl Genetic Analyzer (Applied Biosystems, Foster City, CA, USA); we included in the analysis the immediate pre-ART GRT available in people newly diagnosed with HIV infection.","The paper focuses on genotypic resistance testing and drug resistance mutations rather than in vitro susceptibilities. The methods mentioned involve sequencing and resistance interpretation, which are typically in vitro drug susceptibility tests."
37029656,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers or any indication that the sequences were made publicly available, as would typically be required for such a claim."
37029656,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, which is necessary information for this question."
37029656,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,1223,"Overall, 1223 ART-naive PLWH were evaluated: 1109 (90.7%) were male, 860 (70.3%) were MSM and the median age was 37 (IQR, 30--45) years.","The paper states that 1223 ART-naive PLWH were evaluated in the study, which indicates the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing."
37029656,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Italy,Italian origin was found in 578 (78.0%) and 971 (79.4%) were infected by HIV-1 subtype B. CRF01_AE (74.0%) was the most frequent HIV-1 non-B subtype observed.,The paper mentions  the study was conducted in Italy and provide the characteristics of the population studied.
37029656,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2009-2019,"We conducted a time-trend, single-centre study over the period 2009--19 on adult naive PLWH with a genotypic resistance test (GRT) performed before the ART initiation.","The paper states that the study was conducted over the period 2009-2019, which indicates the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained."
37029656,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"GRT was determined by Sanger sequencing using an ABI PRISM 3130xl Genetic Analyzer (Applied Biosystems, Foster City, CA, USA); we included in the analysis the immediate pre-ART GRT available in people newly diagnosed with HIV infection.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, and the method used implies direct sequencing of PCR products, which means samples were not cloned prior to sequencing."
37029656,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,HIV integrase gene,the prevalence trend of transmitted INSTI drug resistance (DR) and to assess factors associated with transmitted INSTI resistance among ART-naive PLWH over the past decade. DR within the INSTI class was defined by the presence of at least low-level resistance to ≥1 drug of the INSTI class.,"The paper mentions the sequenced samples were from PLWH with INSTI resistance, which implicate the integrase gene were sequenced."
37029656,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"GRT was determined by Sanger sequencing using an ABI PRISM 3130xl Genetic Analyzer (Applied Biosystems, Foster City, CA, USA); we included in the analysis the immediate pre-ART GRT available in people newly diagnosed with HIV infection.","The paper specifies that GRT was determined by Sanger sequencing using an ABI PRISM 3130xl Genetic Analyzer, which indicates the sequencing method used."
37029656,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"We included in the analysis the immediate pre-ART GRT available in people newly diagnosed with HIV infection. At least low-level and at least intermediate INSTI DR were reported in 18 (1.5%) and 6 (0.5%), respectively. Major INSTI resistance mutations were uncommon; both E138K and R263K were found in 2 (0.2%) people, while each of G118R, G140S, Y143C, Y143R, S147G and Q148H in 1 (0.1%) (Table S1, available as Supplementary data at JAC Online).","The paper mentioned the genotypic resistance test was performed after a median time of 1.2 months and the population were HIV infection newly diagnosed, which indicate the samples were obtained from plasma."
37029656,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"the prevalence trend of transmitted INSTI drug resistance (DR) and to assess factors associated with transmitted INSTI resistance among ART-naive PLWH over the past decade. All the considered characteristics were measured at or within 180 days before the GRT date. Over the decade 2009--19, the prevalence of at least low-level and intermediate INSTI DR among ART-naive PLWH with an available GRT performed before starting ART has mildly increased, together with the proportion of non-B subtype infections; nonetheless, major INSTI-resistance mutations were very uncommon.","The paper focuses on ART-naive individuals and on transmitted drug resistance, which means sequences were not obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen."
37029656,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We conducted a time-trend, single-centre study over the period 2009--19 on adult naive PLWH with a genotypic resistance test (GRT) performed before the ART initiation.","The paper describes the study as a single-centre time-trend study, not a clinical trial, which indicates the patients were not in a clinical trial."
37029656,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"the prevalence trend of transmitted INSTI drug resistance (DR) and to assess factors associated with transmitted INSTI resistance among ART-naive PLWH over the past decade. All the considered characteristics were measured at or within 180 days before the GRT date. Over the decade 2009--19, the prevalence of at least low-level and intermediate INSTI DR among ART-naive PLWH with an available GRT performed before starting ART has mildly increased, together with the proportion of non-B subtype infections; nonetheless, major INSTI-resistance mutations were very uncommon.","The paper focus on ARV-naive population, inferring that thse sequences were not from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37029656,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"the prevalence trend of transmitted INSTI drug resistance (DR) and to assess factors associated with transmitted INSTI resistance among ART-naive PLWH over the past decade. All the considered characteristics were measured at or within 180 days before the GRT date. Over the decade 2009--19, the prevalence of at least low-level and intermediate INSTI DR among ART-naive PLWH with an available GRT performed before starting ART has mildly increased, together with the proportion of non-B subtype infections; nonetheless, major INSTI-resistance mutations were very uncommon.","The paper focus on ARV-naive population, inferring that thse sequences were not from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37029656,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"the prevalence trend of transmitted INSTI drug resistance (DR) and to assess factors associated with transmitted INSTI resistance among ART-naive PLWH over the past decade. All the considered characteristics were measured at or within 180 days before the GRT date. Over the decade 2009--19, the prevalence of at least low-level and intermediate INSTI DR among ART-naive PLWH with an available GRT performed before starting ART has mildly increased, together with the proportion of non-B subtype infections; nonetheless, major INSTI-resistance mutations were very uncommon.","The paper focus on ARV-naive population, inferring that thse sequences were not from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37039023,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"This study analysed the genetic composition of the HIV near full-length genome (NFLG) from archived proviruses of PLWH under successful ART, and determined the presence/frequency of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) and viral subtype. . Forty-six PLWH from Rio de Janeiro (RJ) and 40 from Rio Grande (RS) had proviral HIV NFLG PCR-amplified and ultradeep sequenced.","The paper mentions that the study involved 46 PLWH from Rio de Janeiro and 40 from Rio Grande, and their HIV near full-length genome (NFLG) was PCR-amplified and ultradeep sequenced. This indicates that the study reported HIV sequences from patient samples."
37039023,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Our study contributes to the understanding of the impact of DRMs on successful therapy and supports the sustainability of combinatorial ART, because all patients maintained their successful treatment despite the high prevalence of DRMs at low (62.5%) or high (37.5%) frequency. ; We found a higher frequency of mutations able to confer resistance to NRTIs (33.9%; 57/168), followed by NNRTIs (29.8%; 50/168) and PIs (29.2%; 49/168). Sixty-three samples (73.3%) carried viruses with antiretroviral resistance mutations, despite the therapeutic success maintained for all patients. All samples were individually analysed to check the read alignment, examine regions with low coverage (<1000 sequence reads) and investigate the presence of deletions, insertions and premature stop codons. Hypermutation was evaluated in consensus sequences through Hypermut 2.0 (available at http://www.hiv.lanl.gov/content/sequence/HYPERMUT/hypermut.html) to identify G--A changes consistent with apolipoprotein B mRNA editing enzyme, catalytic polypeptide (APOBEC) activity. Viral tropism was determined with the Geno2Pheno algorithm (available at http://coreceptor.geno2pheno.org) using a false positive rate cut-off of 10% for classification as CC chemokine receptor 5- (CCR5-) or non-CCR5-using viruses. Only intrahost variants with frequency above 1% in the viral population were considered.","The paper focuses on the genetic analysis of HIV and the presence of drug resistance mutations in patient samples, but it does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data. It discusses the genetic composition, drug resistance mutations, and sequencing methods, but there is no indication of in vitro susceptibility assays or results."
37039023,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,The raw sequencing reads were deposited to the Sequence Read Archive under the BioProject PRJNA419915. Sequencing data from the present study are available on the Sequence Read Archive (www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra),The paper mentions that the raw sequencing reads were deposited to the Sequence Read Archive under the BioProject PRJNA419915 indicaing publicly available.
37039023,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The GenBank accession number for the HIV near full-length genome (NFLG) sequence is NC_001802.,Data were analysed by reference-based assembly to the HXB2 reference sequence (GenBank accession NC_001802).,The paper mentioned reference-based assembly to the HXB2 reference sequence at GenBank accession NC_001802.
37039023,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,86,"Overall, 86 HIV+ patients were recruited: 34 from HI, 12 from HUCFF and 40 from HU-FURG. Of those, 49 (34 from RJ and 15 from RS) have already been partially described. However, this study included new samples (n = 37 patients) or additional HIV genomic information (n = 19), totalling novel data for 56 patients. 2. 86 patients had samples from HIV sequencing.","The paper states that a total of 86 HIV+ patients were included in the study, with 34 from one hospital, 12 from another, and 40 from a third hospital."
37039023,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Brazil,"HIV-positive patients were recruited during their regular visits at Hospital Federal de Ipanema (HI) and Hospital Universitário Clementino Fraga Filho (HUCFF), both located in Rio de Janeiro (RJ; southeastern Brazil), and at Hospital Universitário Doutor Miguel Riet Corrêa Junior (HU-FURG) in Rio Grande do Sul (RS; southern Brazil).","The paper mentions that the samples were obtained from patients in Rio de Janeiro and Rio Grande do Sul, both located in Brazil."
37039023,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2016 to 2018,"All samples from RJ were collected between February 2016 and November 2018, whereas all samples from RS were collected between August and December 2017. Among the 86 patients with HIV, samples were collected between February 2016 and November 2018 in RJ and between August and December 2017 in RS.","The paper specifies the timeframes during which the samples were collected: from February 2016 to November 2018 for samples from Rio de Janeiro, and from August to December 2017 for samples from Rio Grande do Sul."
37039023,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The purified PCR fragments were diluted to 0.4 ng/µL and pooled per sample for genomic library construction with the Nextera XT DNA Sample Preparation kit (Illumina, Inc, San Diego, CA, USA). Libraries were quantified in Qubit 3 (Life Technologies, Carlsbad, CA, USA) and sequenced using the Illumina MiSeq platform with a paired-end 2 × 251 protocol.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of PCR amplification and direct sequencing of the PCR products, which indicates that cloning was not performed."
37039023,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Near full-length genome (NFLG),"This study analysed the genetic composition of the HIV near full-length genome (NFLG) from archived proviruses of PLWH under successful ART, and determined the presence/frequency of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) and viral subtype.","The paper focuses on the sequencing of the HIV near full-length genome (NFLG), which includes multiple genes across the HIV genome."
37039023,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Illumina MiSeq platform,"All samples were centrifuged to separate plasma and peripheral blood cells. PureLink® Genomic DNA Mini Kit (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, MA, USA) was used to extract DNA from the blood cells (including the integrated HIV proviral genome), according to the manufacturer's instructions. Genomic libraries were generated using the Nextera XT DNA Sample Preparation Kit (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA), according to the manufacturer's instructions. The purified PCR fragments were diluted to 0.4 ng/µL and pooled per sample for genomic library construction with the Nextera XT DNA Sample Preparation Kit (Illumina, Inc, San Diego, CA, USA). Libraries were quantified in Qubit 3 (Life Technologies, Carlsbad, CA, USA) and sequenced using the Illumina MiSeq platform with a paired-end 2×251 protocol. PhiX Control v3 Library (Illumina, Inc.) was used as a control library. DNA extraction and archived HIV proviral PCR All samples were centrifuged to separate plasma and peripheral blood cells. PureLink® Genomic DNA Mini Kit (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, MA, USA) was used to extract DNA from the blood cells (including the integrated HIV proviral genome), according to the manufacturer's instructions. Nested PCR was used to amplify the HIV NFLG through three different strategies31-33 or combinations thereof. All reactions were performed in duplicates and visualized on a 1% agarose gel. Positive PCR duplicate products were pooled and purified with AccuPrep® PCR Purification Kit (Bioneer, Daejeon, South Korea), following the manufacturer's protocol. Products were then analysed on a 1% agarose gel and quantified in a NanoDrop® ND-1000 spectrophotometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific).",The paper mentions that the sequencing was performed using the Illumina MiSeq platform.
37039023,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs),"Six millilitres of whole peripheral blood was collected from all patients who agreed to participate in the study and signed the informed consent. All samples were centrifuged to separate plasma and peripheral blood cells. PureLink® Genomic DNA Mini Kit (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, MA, USA) was used to extract DNA from the blood cells (including the integrated HIV proviral genome), according to the manufacturer's instructions. All samples were centrifuged to separate plasma and peripheral blood cells. PureLink® Genomic DNA Mini Kit (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, MA, USA) was used to extract DNA from the blood cells (including the integrated HIV proviral genome), according to the manufacturer's instructions. Nested PCR was used to amplify the HIV NFLG through three different strategies31-33 or combinations thereof. All reactions were performed in duplicates and visualized on a 1% agarose gel. Positive PCR duplicate products were pooled and purified with AccuPrep® PCR Purification Kit (Bioneer, Daejeon, South Korea), following the manufacturer's protocol. Products were then analysed on a 1% agarose gel and quantified in a NanoDrop® ND-1000 spectrophotometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific).","The paper mentioned that,Six millilitres of whole peripheral blood was collected from all patients who agreed to participate in the study and signed the informed consent. All samples were centrifuged to separate plasma and peripheral blood cells. PureLink® Genomic DNA Mini Kit (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, MA, USA) was used to extract DNA from the blood cells (including the integrated HIV proviral genome), according to the manufacturer's instructions. We're sequencing the blood cells, So PBMCs were sequenced."
37039023,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"The inclusion criteria for the study were: age ≥18 years; being on a first-line or second-line treatment regimen due exclusively to changes in the treatment regimen recommended by the Brazilian Ministry of Health (and not due to treatment failure or drug intolerance); and therapeutic success (undetectable HIV viral load) for at least 12 months. This study analysed the genetic composition of the HIV near full-length genome (NFLG) from archived proviruses of PLWH under successful ART, and determined the presence/frequency of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) and viral subtype.  Our study contributes to the understanding of the impact of DRMs on successful therapy and supports the sustainability of combinatorial ART, because all patients maintained their successful treatment despite the high prevalence of DRMs at low (62.5%) or high (37.5%) frequency. Most patients in RJ (73.9%) were male, whereas most patients in RS (62.5%) were female. The patients from both cities had similar characteristics, like median age, time of treatment and therapy initiation (from HIV diagnosis to ART start) (Table 1). All patients in our study started treatment before 2017, the predominance of the TDF+3TC+EFV therapeutic regimen corroborates the recommended scheme at that time.","The paper mentions that the inclusion criteria for the study were being on a first-line or second-line treatment regimen due exclusively to changes in the treatment regimen recommended by the Brazilian Ministry of Health (and not due to treatment failure or drug intolerance), and that all patients maintained their successful treatment, which means they had not experienced virological failure."
37039023,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"HIV-positive patients were recruited during their regular visits at Hospital Federal de Ipanema (HI) and Hospital Universitário Clementino Fraga Filho (HUCFF), both located in Rio de Janeiro (RJ; southeastern Brazil), and at Hospital Universitário Doutor Miguel Riet Corrêa Junior (HU-FURG) in Rio Grande do Sul (RS; southern Brazil). The inclusion criteria for the study were: age ≥18 years; being on a first-line or second-line treatment regimen due exclusively to changes in the treatment regimen recommended by the Brazilian Ministry of Health (and not due to treatment failure or drug intolerance); and therapeutic success (undetectable HIV viral load) for at least 12 months. Six millilitres of whole peripheral blood was collected from all patients who agreed to participate in the study and signed the informed consent. All samples from RJ were collected between February 2016 and November 2018, whereas all samples from RS were collected between August and December 2017. These samples were sent to Instituto Nacional do Câncer (INCA), Rio de Janeiro, where they were processed. Overall, 86 HIV+ patients were recruited: 34 from HI, 12 from HUCFF and 40 from HU-FURG.","The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It describes the patients were recruited during their regular visits at hospitals and the inclusion criteria for the study, which is not a clinical trial."
37039023,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Our study contributes to the understanding of the impact of DRMs on successful therapy and supports the sustainability of combinatorial ART, because all patients maintained their successful treatment despite the high prevalence of DRMs at low (62.5%) or high (37.5%) frequency. Most patients in RJ (73.9%) were male, whereas most patients in RS (62.5%) were female. The patients from both cities had similar characteristics, like median age, time of treatment and therapy initiation (from HIV diagnosis to ART start) (Table 1). Most patients (RJ, 76.1%; RS, 95.0%) reported being infected through sexual transmission. The most prevalent therapeutic regimen combination used was tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF), lamivudine (3TC) and efavirenz (EFV) (TDF + 3TC + EFV), accounting for 55.8% (n = 48) of patients included. Our study also evaluated the presence and frequency of antiretroviral-associated resistance mutations in the archived proviruses. Most studies only evaluate mutations found in circulating viruses present in blood plasma.34,62,64–71 A study by Zaccarelli et al.72 identified antiretroviral resistance mutations present in PBMCs and in blood plasma of the same patients, and showed that in 20.1% of the samples resistance mutations were detected only in PBMCs. Also, the presence of those mutations is usually investigated in a scenario of therapeutic failure,13,24,34,65,70,73,74 and not in the PBMC reservoir of patients under therapeutic success, as a potential source of rebound viruses. A Brazilian study performed by Corado et al.75 showed that 63% of patients with an undetectable viral load carried antiretroviral resistance-associated mutations. Among the resistance mutations found in our study, 63 had a frequency above 20.0% in the viral population, whereas 105 were minority variants.","The paper mentions the most prevalent therapeutic regimen combination used was tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF), lamivudine (3TC) and efavirenz (EFV), it also mentions all patients maintained their successful treatment, which means they had received ARV drugs before sampling."
37039023,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI","The most prevalent therapeutic regimen combination used was tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF), lamivudine (3TC) and efavirenz (EFV) (TDF + 3TC + EFV), accounting for 55.8% (n = 48) of patients included. All 86 samples had at least one of these regions analysed; 72 (83.7%) had all four regions analysed, whereas five samples had three genomic regions, two samples had two, and seven samples had only one (Table S2). Of the 168 mutations found, 50 mutations (29.8%; 25/75 patients analysed for this region) were present in the protease region, 101 mutations (60.1%; 51/77 patients) were found in the reverse transcriptase, 14 mutations (8.3%; 13/76 patients) in the integrase region and 3 mutations (1.8%; 3/86 patients) in the envelope.","The paper lists the drugs that were part of the therapeutic regimen used by the patients in the study. It includes tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF), lamivudine (3TC), and efavirenz (EFV), which are all NRTI and NNRTI."
37039023,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF), lamivudine (3TC), efavirenz (EFV)","The most prevalent therapeutic regimen combination used was tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF), lamivudine (3TC) and efavirenz (EFV) (TDF + 3TC + EFV), accounting for 55.8% (n = 48) of patients included. All 86 samples had at least one of these regions analysed; 72 (83.7%) had all four regions analysed, whereas five samples had three genomic regions, two samples had two, and seven samples had only one (Table S2). Of the 168 mutations found, 50 mutations (29.8%; 25/75 patients analysed for this region) were present in the protease region, 101 mutations (60.1%; 51/77 patients) were found in the reverse transcriptase, 14 mutations (8.3%; 13/76 patients) in the integrase region and 3 mutations (1.8%; 3/86 patients) in the envelope.","The paper lists the drugs that were part of the therapeutic regimen used by the patients in the study. It includes tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF), lamivudine (3TC), and efavirenz (EFV), which were received by all individuals in the journey before sample sequencing."
37042390,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Eighty-five patients were started on third-line ART. Genotypic resistance testing to identify drug resistance mutations in the integrase, reverse transcriptase and protease genes was done at the start of third-line therapy, as well as in those who did not attain virological suppression after 12 months of therapy.","The paper mentions that genotypic resistance testing, which involves sequencing HIV genes, was done on samples from patients receiving third-line ART. This indicates that the paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples."
