37042390,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Integrase, reverse transcriptase, protease","Genotypic resistance testing to identify drug resistance mutations in the integrase, reverse transcriptase and protease genes was done at the start of third-line therapy, as well as in those who did not attain virological suppression after 12 months of therapy.","The paper mentions that genotypic resistance testing was done for the integrase, reverse transcriptase and protease genes."
37042390,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Applied Biosystem 3730xl DNA Analyzer,Sequencing was done by a third-party commercial organization (Medauxin) using an Applied Biosystem 3730xl DNA Analyzer.,The paper specifies that sequencing was done using an Applied Biosystem 3730xl DNA analyzer.
37042390,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Whole blood was collected from patients into EDTA tubes and used for CD4 estimation; in parallel, plasma was separated following centrifugation at 2000 rpm at 4°C for 10 min and stored at −80°C until viral RNA isolation.","The paper describes how plasma was separated from whole blood and used for HIV RNA isolation, which indicates that plasma samples were sequenced."
37042390,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"At the end of follow-up of the study in March 2022, VL could be done in 67 patients, out of which 59 (88%) had virological suppression. Five out of 13 patients (39%) who failed therapy at 12 months were virologically suppressed at the end of follow-up.","The paper mentioned that sequences were obtained from individuals who had virological failure, indicating that virological failure was a criterion for sequencing."
37042390,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This single-centre observational study was done at a CoE in HIV care, Banaras Hindu University (BHU) in a tertiary hospital in Varanasi, India.","The paper describes this as a single-centre observational study, not a clinical trial."
37042390,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Genotypic resistance testing to identify drug resistance mutations in the integrase, reverse transcriptase and protease genes was done at the start of third-line therapy, as well as in those who did not attain virological suppression after 12 months of therapy.","The paper mentions that patients had never been on integrase inhibitor-based regimens, indicating that sequences were from individuals who had received ARV drugs."
37042390,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, I","Genotypic resistance testing to identify drug resistance mutations in the integrase, reverse transcriptase and protease genes was done at the start of third-line therapy, as well as in those who did not attain virological suppression after 12 months of therapy.","The paper mentions that genotypic resistance testing was done for the integrase, reverse transcriptase and protease genes, which are associated with PI, NRTI, and NNRTI."
37042390,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not specifed,"At the time of the start of this study, the initial regimen for third-line therapy was raltegravir (400 mg) + darunavir (600 mg) + ritonavir (100 mg); given as one tablet twice daily.","The paper mentions the specific drugs used for third-line therapy, but does not list all the drugs received by individuals before sequencing."
37052343,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"Participants had a CD4 + T-cell count measured and a detectable plasma HIV-1 RNA value at ART initiation and a minimum follow-up time of 36 weeks. Virological outcomes: virological failure (VF), defined as the first of two consecutive plasma HIV-1 RNA measurements ≥50 copies/mL, with one measurement ≥200 copies/mL, following virological suppression (VS). Plasma HIV-1 RNA and CD4+ T-Cell Counts Are Determinants of Virological Nonsuppression Outcomes With Initial Integrase Inhibitor-Based Regimens: A Prospective RESPOND Cohort Study","The methods section describes that the study focused on virological outcomes and RNA levels at initiation and longitudinal follow-up of ART, but does not mention the sequencing of HIV samples from patients. Additionally, the discussion and results sections focus on treatment outcomes and baseline characteristics, indicating that sequencing data was not a part of this study. The paper primarily discusses virological outcomes, treatment responses, and baseline predictors without mentioning the generation or analysis of HIV sequences from patient samples, leading to the conclusion that no sequencing data was reported. The presence of viral load measures suggests RNA sequencing. However, the lack of mentioning patient's sequences in the abstracts and methods lead to the conclusion that the sequences were not shared publicly. Analyzing the core question whether the paper reports or mentions in vitro drug susceptibility assays, the focus shifts to virological outcomes as the key indicators of treatment efficacy, without delving into specific in vitro assays for drug susceptibility. Therefore, the conclusion drawn is that the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data, as the primary emphasis lies on clinical and virological outcomes of the treatment regimen as described in the article. The paper does not mention plasma or genomic sequence analyses. It does mention the levels of HIV-1 RNA copies, but that is not a GR. So, no sequences were made publicly available."
37052343,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Participants had a CD4 + T-cell count measured and a detectable plasma HIV-1 RNA value at ART initiation and a minimum follow-up time of 36 weeks. Virological outcomes: virological failure (VF), defined as the first of two consecutive plasma HIV-1 RNA measurements ≥50 copies/mL, with one measurement ≥200 copies/mL, following virological suppression (VS). Study population: All cohorts had approval to share data with RESPOND according to national requirements. We included all ART-naive adults aged ≥18 years who started ART between 1 January 2014 and 31 December 2020, from 17 of 19 cohorts. CD4 + T-cell counts were measured and a detectable plasma HIV-1 RNA value at ART initiation and a minimum follow-up time of 36 weeks. Viral blips, low-level viremia (LLV), residual viremia (RV), and virological failure (VF) rates were assessed using Cox regression","The paper focuses on virological outcomes and logistics of a clinical cohort study without discussion of in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data. The main focus of the paper is on the clinical outcomes of different ART regimens and their effectiveness, not on laboratory-based susceptibility assays. The paper mainly collects core clinical virological and immunological outcomes, without mentioning laboratory methods on how selection of drug regimens how treatment outcomes were assessed. It mainly focuses on clinical outcomes without the details of lab outcomes and methods of outcome measures. Methods. We included treatment-naive participants aged ≥18 who initiated 3-drug ART, in 2014–2020. We assessed the odds of virological suppression (VS) at weeks 48 and 96 using logistic regression. Viral blips, low-level viremia (LLV), residual viremia (RV), and virological failure (VF) rates were assessed using Cox regression. VIrological suppresion measured at 48 and 96 weeks indicates it is a clinical cohort study.  No in vitro drug activity assays. Methods: we included treatment-naive participants aged ≥18 who initiated 3-drug ART, in 2014–2020. We assessed the odds of virological suppression (VS) at weeks 48 and 96 using logistic regression. Viral blips, low-level viremia (LLV), residual viremia (RV), and virological failure (VF) rates were assessed using Cox regression. Virological outcomes were suppressive at week 48 and 96 which are clinical outcomes not not laboratory virological outcomes. It indicates it is a cohort study. THere is no in vitro drug resistance data reported."
37052343,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The methods section describes that the study focused on virological outcomes and RNA levels at initiation and longitudinal follow-up of ART, but does not mention the sequencing of HIV samples from patients. Additionally, the discussion and results sections focus on treatment outcomes and baseline characteristics, indicating that sequencing data was not a part of this study. The paper primarily discusses virological outcomes, treatment responses, and baseline predictors without mentioning the generation or analysis of HIV sequences from patient samples, leading to the conclusion that no sequencing data was reported. The presence of viral load measures suggests RNA sequencing. However, the lack of mentioning patient's sequences in the abstracts and methods lead to the conclusion that the sequences were not shared publicly. Analyzing the core question whether the paper reports or mentions in vitro drug susceptibility assays, the focus shifts to virological outcomes as the key indicators of treatment efficacy, without delving into specific in vitro assays for drug susceptibility. Therefore, the conclusion drawn is that the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data, as the primary emphasis lies on clinical and virological outcomes of the treatment regimen as described in the article. The paper does not mention plasma or genomic sequence analyses. It does mention the levels of HIV-1 RNA copies, but that is not a GR. So, no sequences were made publicly available.","The paper primarily focuses on virological outcomes, treatment responses, and baseline predictors of these outcomes in a cohort study, and does not mention the generation or sharing of HIV sequences."
37052343,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not applicable,"The paper does not provide GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates, as it does not report any sequencing data from patient samples. The methods section describes the study's focus on virological outcomes, treatment responses, and baseline characteristics without mentioning sequencing data or GenBank accession numbers. Additionally, the discussion focuses on treatment outcomes and predictors without any mention of sequencing or GenBank accession numbers.",The paper focuses on virological outcomes and treatment responses in a cohort study without any mention of sequencing data or GenBank accession numbers.
37052343,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"The paper does not mention how many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing, as it does not report any sequencing data from patient samples. The focus of the study is on virological outcomes and treatment responses rather than on the number of individuals with sequencing data. Specifically, the study included 4310 eligible participants but does not specify any sequencing, DNA or RNA assays, isolation, cloning, and other technical details. It mentions HIV1 RNA levels and VL suppression but it is not sequenced referred populations. Only clinical outcomes were reported","The length of the paper, and the fact that the study is a cohort study with the focus on clinical outcomes like virological failure after a prospective study windows, does not involve the technical  details of sequencing. The paper does not report any sequencing data from patient samples, leading to the conclusion that no individuals had samples obtained for sequencing."
37052343,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not applicable,"he paper does not mention from which countries the sequenced samples were obtained, as it does not report any sequencing data from patient samples. The focus of the study is on the international cohort and the treatment outcomes of participants rather than on the countries of origin for sequenced samples. The study popualtion included  all ART-naive adults aged ≥18 years who started ART between 1 January 2014 and 31 December 2020, 45% were from Cnetral Europe, and doesn't mention where sequenced samples were from","The paper does not report any sequencing data from patient samples, leading to the conclusion that the country of samples origin is not clear."
