37052343,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,Not applicable,"The paper does not mention whether samples were cloned prior to sequencing, as it does not report any sequencing data from patient samples. The focus of the study is on virological outcomes and treatment responses without any reference to cloning of samples prior to sequencing. The paper doesn’t mention any RNA cloning after the initiation of ART, or before followup time samples were collected at initiation of ART and 36 weeks after","The paper does not report any sequencing data from patient samples, leading to the conclusion that the samples were not cloned prior to sequencing."
37052343,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Not applicable,"The paper does not report any sequencing of specific HIV genes, as it does not involve sequencing data from patient samples. The study focuses on virological outcomes and treatment responses rather than on the sequencing of specific HIV genes. The paper describes clinical outcomes and the response to ART without mentioning specific HIV genes that were sequenced.","The paper does not report any sequencing data from patient samples, leading to the conclusion that no specific HIV genes were sequenced."
37052343,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not applicable,"The paper does not mention what method was used for sequencing, as it does not report any sequencing data from patient samples. The focus of the study is on the virological outcomes and treatment responses without any reference to the sequencing method used.","The paper does not report any sequencing data from patient samples, leading to the conclusion that no method was used for sequencing."
37052343,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Not applicable,"The paper does not mention what type of samples were sequenced, as it does not report any sequencing data from patient samples. The focus of the study is on the virological outcomes and treatment responses without any reference to the type of samples sequenced. The paper describes clinical outcomes and doesn't mention the samples were virological outcomes","The paper does not report any sequencing data from patient samples, leading to the conclusion that no type of samples were sequenced."
37052343,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,NA,"The paper focuses on evaluating virological failure rates in patients, indicating that these individuals are part of the study cohort. Specifically, it mentions virological failure (VF) rates and the association of baseline factors with VF outcomes. This means patients had virological outcomes but sequences were not collected","The paper reports virological outcomes (viralogical failure) but does not report any sequencing data from patient samples, leading to the conclusion that it doesn't mention sequences from viralogical failure."
37052343,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The study population included treatment-naive participants who initiated ART between 1 January 2014 and 31 December 2020, from 17 of 19 cohorts. he paper does not mention that the patients were in a clinical trial. The study is using a cohort study design, focusing on treatment outcomes of a large patient cohort over a specific period.","The paper does not mention that the patients were in a clinical trial, leading to the conclusion that they were not."
37052343,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"We included treatment-naive participants aged ≥18 who initiated 3-drug ART, in 2014–2020. We assessed the odds of virological suppression (VS) at weeks 48 and 96 using logistic regression. Virological blips, low-level viremia (LLV), residual viremia (RV), and virological failure (VF) rates were assessed using Cox regression. All ART-naïve adults aged ≥18 years who started ART between 1 January 2014 and 31 December 2020, from 17 of 19 cohorts. Participants had a CD4+ T-cell count measured and a detectable plasma HIV-1 RNA value at ART initiation and a minimum follow-up time of 36 weeks. From this patients, 72% started integrase strand transfer inhibitor ( INSTI )-based regimens. At 48 and 96 weeks, 91.0% and 93.3% achieved virological suppression respectively. At 48 weeks, 9.6% for viral blips, 2.1% for LLV, 22.2% for RV, and 2.1% for VF. Virological suppression (VS) at weeks 48 and 96 was achieved in 3306 of 3638 (90.9% [95% CI, 89.9%–91.8%]) and 2908 of 3118 (93.3% [92.4%–94.1%]) participants, respectively. At 48 weeks, Kaplan–Meier estimates of the proportions were 9.6% (95%, CI, 9.5%--10.5%) for viral blips, 2.1% (1.6%--2.5%) for LLV, 22.2% (20.0%--24.3%) for RV, and 2.1% (1.7%--2.6%) for virological failure (VF). Blips, low-level viremia (LLV), and virological failure (VF) all indicate the patients have received ARV drugs","The paper does not report any sequencing data from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs, leading to the conclusion that the paper does not report sequences from such individuals."
37052343,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not applicable,"The paper does not report any sequencing data or mention the drug classes received by individuals before sequencing, as it does not involve sequencing data from patient samples. The focus of the study is on the treatment outcomes of participants rather than on the drug classes received prior to sequencing.","The paper does not report any sequencing data from patient samples, leading to the conclusion that no drug classes were received prior."
37052343,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not applicable,"The paper does not report any sequencing data or mention the drugs received by individuals before sequencing, as it does not involve sequencing data from patient samples. The focus of the study is on the treatment outcomes of participants rather than on the specific drugs received prior to sequencing.","The paper does not report any sequencing data from patient samples, leading to the conclusion that no drugs were received prior."
37071019,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"Molecular modeling. The tetrameric integrase complex with DNA and BIC (PDB code 6PUW) was used as a template to examine the effects of G118R on the integrase structure In silico modeling effects of the individual R263K and G118R substitutions have been published previously (14, 18, 27). We used a published approach to examine the structure of the G118R mutant by modeling the mutated protein against chain A from PDB code 6PUW (18, 27). Proper conformation was validated by pairwise structure alignment on the RCSB PDB website (https://www.rcsb.org/alignment). We used AceDock to model BIC binding to the G118R-mutated integrase.","The content of the paper revolves around the in vitro analysis of integrase mutations and their effects on drug susceptibility and viral fitness, rather than reporting HIV sequences derived from patient samples. Additionally, the absence of patient-derived sequences suggests a focus on laboratory-based experiments rather than clinical data collection."
37071019,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"Antiretroviral drug susceptibility. For antiretroviral drug susceptibility assays, a similar approach was used with some modifications. Thirty thousand TZM-bl cells per well were plated on a 96-well cell culture plate. Eighteen to 24 h later, 1:10 serial dilutions of DTG (0.16 pM to 1.6 μM) or BIC (0.14 pM to 1.4 μM) were added to the cells in triplicate. After 1 h of incubation, the cells were infected with the equivalent of 30,000 RT units of WT, G118R, R263K, or G118R plus R263K virus per well, as reported previously (23). After 24 h, luciferase production was measured as described above, and RLU values were plotted against drug concentrations. Normalization was performed as described above for infectivity assays using GraphPad Prism. The results from three separate experiments (two with one batch of viruses and one with another batch of viruses) were compiled thanks to normalization. This compilation was used to calculate FCs in 50% inhibitory concentrations (IC50s) relative to the WT virus using GraphPad Prism.","The paper includes in vitro assays reporting the susceptibility of different integrase mutant HIV-1 viruses to dolutegravir (DTG) and bictegravir (BIC). These assays measured the relative strand transfer activity of integrase proteins carrying specific mutations in the presence of various drug concentrations. The results were compiled from multiple experiments and normalized against a wild-type virus control, indicating that in vitro drug susceptibility data was collected and reported in the paper. The paper mentions antiretroviral drug susceptibility assays, which indicates that they performed in vitro tests to see how effective the drugs were against the viruses they were studying."
37071019,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"Molecular modeling. The tetrameric integrase complex with DNA and BIC (PDB code 6PUW) was used as a template to examine the effects of G118R on the integrase structure In silico modeling effects of the individual R263K and G118R substitutions have been published previously (14, 18, 27). We used a published approach to examine the structure of the G118R mutant by modeling the mutated protein against chain A from PDB code 6PUW (18, 27). Proper conformation was validated by pairwise structure alignment on the RCSB PDB website (https://www.rcsb.org/alignment). We used AceDock to model BIC binding to the G118R-mutated integrase.","The paper focuses on the analysis of integrase mutations and their effects on drug susceptibility and viral fitness in vitro, and contains no mention of publicly available sequences, indicating that the sequences were not made publicly available."
37071019,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates, indicating that no sequences were deposited in GenBank."
37071019,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"This study focuses on the characterization of integrase mutations and their effects on drug susceptibility and viral fitness in vitro and does not mention any individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing. The content revolves around laboratory assays and modeling, with no reference to patient samples or demographics.","The paper does not mention any individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing, indicating that no individuals had samples obtained."
37071019,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,0,"This study focuses on the characterization of integrase mutations and their effects on drug susceptibility and viral fitness in vitro and does not mention any countries from which samples were obtained for HIV sequencing. The content revolves around laboratory assays and modeling, with no reference to patient samples or their countries of origin.","The paper does not mention any countries from which samples were obtained for HIV sequencing, indicating that no sequences were collected from specific countries."
37071019,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,0,"This study focuses on the characterization of integrase mutations and their effects on drug susceptibility and viral fitness in vitro and does not mention any years from which samples were obtained for HIV sequencing. The content revolves around laboratory assays and modeling, with no reference to patient samples or their collection years.","The paper does not mention any specific years from which samples were obtained for HIV sequencing, indicating that no sequences were collected from defined time periods."
37071019,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Recombinant integrase protein expression and purification. R263K and G118R coding mutations were combined via site-directed mutagenesis using the previously described G118R primers and pET15b-INBR263K plasmid. Plasmids were amplified in XL-10 Gold bacteria (Stratagene), and proper mutagenesis was verified by Sanger sequencing. The resulting pET15b-INBG118R+R263K plasmid was transformed in parallel with pET15b-INBR263K, pET15b-INBG118R, and pET15b-INBWT into BL21(DE3) bacterial cells to produce recombinant integrase proteins, using methods described previously. Transformed BL21 cells were grown overnight at 37°C in Luria-Bertani (LB) broth with 100 μg/mL ampicillin, in 5-mL shaken precultures. Sixteen to 20 h later, the precultures were used to seed cultures in 500 mL LB with ampicillin. Recombinant integrase proteins were expressed in fusion with a polyhistidine tag that permitted their purification via its interaction with nickel-nitriloacetic acid (Ni-NTA) agarose beads. Cell lysates were bound to Ni-NTA agarose beads for 1 h with agitation at 4°C. Elution was performed using polypropylene columns and increasing concentrations of imidazole (0 to 2 M) at 4°C.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, as it does not report any sequencing activities. The focus is on the analysis of integrase mutations in vitro."
37071019,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,,,"The paper does not report any sequencing of HIV genes, as it focuses on the analysis of integrase mutations rather than genomic sequencing."
