37085698,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"This was a cross-sectional study involving isolation of HIV-1 from plasma of 49 newly diagnosed drug-naive HIV-1 infected individuals in Addis-Ababa during the period between June to December 2018. Near full-length HIV-1 IN region, (263 codons) of the 49 study participants were successfully sequenced and analyzed.","The paper reports that HIV-1 sequences were obtained from plasma samples of 49 newly diagnosed drug-naive HIV-1 infected individuals. This is clearly stated in the Abstract and the Methods sections, where it mentions the isolation of HIV-1 from plasma and the successful sequencing of the HIV-1 IN region from these samples."
37085698,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"With the widespread use of Integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs), surveillance of HIV-1 pretreatment drug resistance is critical in optimizing antiretroviral treatment efficacy. However, despite the introduction of these drugs, data concerning their resistance mutations (RMs) is still limited in Ethiopia. Thus, this study aimed to assess INSTI RMs and polymorphisms at the gene locus coding for Integrase (IN) among viral isolates from ART-naive HIV-1 infected Ethiopian population.","The paper does not report any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on the analysis of resistance mutations and polymorphisms in the integrase gene of HIV-1 from patient samples, but there is no mention of conducting in vitro drug susceptibility assays or presenting data related to the susceptibility of HIV-1 to antiretroviral drugs in vitro."
37085698,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"All sequences were thoroughly examined for the presence of primary mutations, nonpolymorphic [NoP] and polymorphic mutations associated with resistance to INSTI. Each amino acid prevalence at each IN position was calculated and compared to the HIV-1 subtype B reference sequence (GenBank accession number: K03455). For this study, NoP was defined as substitutions within the HIV-1 IN that occurred in ≥ 1% of the sequences. Positions with ≥ 20% substitutions were considered as highly polymorphic, while those with ≤ 0.5% substitution were considered highly conserved. All isolates are deposited in GenBank with accession number MW560010 to MW560058.","The paper states that all sequences were deposited in GenBank with accession numbers MW560010 to MW560058. This is mentioned in the Materials and Methods section under the heading ""Statistical analyses."""
37085698,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,MW560010 to MW560058,All isolates are deposited in GenBank with accession number MW560010 to MW560058.,"The GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates are explicitly mentioned in the Materials and Methods section under the heading ""Statistical analyses."" The paper states that all sequences were deposited in GenBank with these accession numbers."
37085698,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,49,"This was a cross-sectional study involving isolation of HIV-1 from plasma of 49 newly diagnosed drug-naive HIV-1 infected individuals in Addis-Ababa during the period between June to December 2018. Near full-length HIV-1 IN region, (263 codons) of the 49 study participants were successfully sequenced and analyzed.",The paper mentions that 49 newly diagnosed drug-naive HIV-1 infected individuals were enrolled in the study. This is stated in the Abstract and the Methods sections.
37085698,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Ethiopia,Low level of HIV-1C integrase strand transfer inhibitor resistance mutations among recently diagnosed ART-naive Ethiopians. This was a cross-sectional study involving isolation of HIV-1 from plasma of 49 newly diagnosed drug-naive HIV-1 infected individuals in Addis-Ababa during the period between June to December 2018.,"The study was conducted in Ethiopia, specifically mentioning that the samples were obtained from voluntary VCT centers in Addis Ababa. This is clearly stated in the Abstract and Methods sections."
37085698,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2018,This was a cross-sectional study involving isolation of HIV-1 from plasma of 49 newly diagnosed drug-naive HIV-1 infected individuals in Addis-Ababa during the period between June to December 2018. Blood samples (10 ml) were collected at the time of HIV diagnosis before initiation of ART for genotypic assay of the IN gene region from these participants using an EDTA tube.,The blood samples were collected between June to December 2018 according to  the abstract.
37085698,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"This was followed by a first-round PCR, which was performed in a 50 µL reaction mix composed of 1 µL of each outer primer (HIVpcr For1 and HIVpcr Rev1; Table ​ Table1), 1 ), 27.8 µL of nuclease‐free water, 10 µL of 5X GoTaq buffer, 4 µL of 25 mM MgCl ~2~ , 1 µL of 10 mM dNTPs, 0.2 µL of GoTaq polymerase (Promega, USA), and 5 µL of cDNA. The DNA product from the first round PCR was then re-amplified by a nested PCR. This nested PCR was carried out in another 50 µL reaction mix utilizing 1 µL of the first-round PCR product, 1 µL of each inner primer (HIVpcr For2 and HIVpcr Rev2; Table ​ Table1), 1 ), 31.8 µL of nuclease‐free water, 10 µL of 5X GoTaq buffer, 4 µL of 25 mM MgCl ~2~ , 1 µL of 10 mM dNTPs, and 0.2 µL of GoTaq polymerase (Promega, USA).","The paper mentions that cDNA was synthesized from the extracted RNA and was directly used for PCR amplification, indicating that cloning was not performed prior to sequencing."
37085698,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Integrase gene,"Thus, this study aimed to assess INSTI RMs and polymorphisms at the gene locus coding for Integrase (IN) among viral isolates from ART-naive HIV-1 infected Ethiopian population. Near full-length HIV-1 IN region, (263 codons) of the 49 study participants were successfully sequenced and analyzed.",The paper reports sequencing of the integrase (IN) gene of HIV-1. This is mentioned in the Abstract and Methods sections where INSTI resistance mutations and polymorphisms at the integrase gene locus are assessed.
37085698,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"The DNA product from the first round PCR was then re-amplified by a nested PCR. This nested PCR was carried out in another 50 µL reaction mix utilizing 1 µL of the first-round PCR product, 1 µL of each inner primer (HIVpcr For2 and HIVpcr Rev2; Table ​ Table1), 1 ), 31.8 µL of nuclease‐free water, 10 µL of 5X GoTaq buffer, 4 µL of 25 mM MgCl ~2~ , 1 µL of 10 mM dNTPs, and 0.2 µL of GoTaq polymerase (Promega, USA). Purified amplicons were then sequenced with Sanger DNA sequencing using the BigDye Terminator v3.1 Cycle Sequencing Kit (Applied Biosystems), and run on the ABI PRISM® 3500 xL automated Genetic Analyzer.","The paper specifies that Sanger sequencing was used, along with the BigDye Termi the rminator Kit and ABI PRISM 3500 Genetic Analyzer. This is described in the Materials and Methods section under the Nucleic acid extraction, PCR amplification, and sequencing."
37085698,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,This was a cross-sectional study involving isolation of HIV-1 from plasma of 49 newly diagnosed drug-naive HIV-1 infected individuals in Addis-Ababa during the period between June to December 2018. Blood samples (10 ml) were collected at the time of HIV diagnosis before initiation of ART for genotypic assay of the IN gene region from these participants using an EDTA tube. Plasma was then separated by centrifugation for 10 min at 3000 rpm and stored in deep freeze (-80 °C) till required for laboratory investigation.,"The paper mentions that plasma was used for sequencing, specifically stating that viral RNA extraction and viral load were performed from plasma samples of participants. This is mentioned in the Methods section after study population."
37085698,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"This  research did not include pregnant women, those with known chronic conditions, or anyone who had ever used ART. For detailed patients screening and enrollment, please refer to our previous publication. This was a cross-sectional health facility-based study involving drug-naive HIV-1 infected study participants. Blood samples (10 ml) were collected at the time of HIV diagnosis before initiation of ART for genotypic assay of the IN gene region from these participants using an EDTA tube.","The paper specifically states that the study participants were drug-naive, asymptomatic, and blood samples were collected at the time of HIV diagnosis before initiation of ART. Thus, no sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen."
37085698,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This was a cross-sectional study involving isolation of HIV-1 from plasma of 49 newly diagnosed drug-naive HIV-1 infected individuals in Addis-Ababa during the period between June to December 2018. This is a cross-sectional health facility-based study involving drug-naive HIV-1 infected study participants. For detailed patients screening and enrollment, please refer to our previous publication.","The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It describes a cross-sectional health facility-based study involving drug-naive HIV-1 infected study participants  newly diagnosed individuals, which does not indicate that they were in a clinical trial."
37085698,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"This  research did not include pregnant women, those with known chronic conditions, or anyone who had ever used ART. For detailed patients screening and enrollment, please refer to our previous publication. This was a cross-sectional health facility-based study involving drug-naive HIV-1 infected study participants. Blood samples (10 ml) were collected at the time of HIV diagnosis before initiation of ART for genotypic assay of the IN gene region from these participants using an EDTA tube.","The paper mentioned specifically excluded individuals who had ever used ART, and mentioned all participants were drug naïve, blood samples were collected at the time of HIV diagnosis before initiation of ART."
37085698,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"This  research did not include pregnant women, those with known chronic conditions, or anyone who had ever used ART. For detailed patients screening and enrollment, please refer to our previous publication. This was a cross-sectional health facility-based study involving drug-naive HIV-1 infected study participants. Blood samples (10 ml) were collected at the time of HIV diagnosis before initiation of ART for genotypic assay of the IN gene region from these participants using an EDTA tube.","The paper mentioned specifically excluded individuals who had ever used ART, and mentioned all participants were drug naïve, blood samples were collected at the time of HIV diagnosis before initiation of ART."
37085698,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"This  research did not include pregnant women, those with known chronic conditions, or anyone who had ever used ART. For detailed patients screening and enrollment, please refer to our previous publication. This was a cross-sectional health facility-based study involving drug-naive HIV-1 infected study participants. Blood samples (10 ml) were collected at the time of HIV diagnosis before initiation of ART for genotypic assay of the IN gene region from these participants using an EDTA tube.","The paper mentioned specifically excluded individuals who had ever used ART, and mentioned all participants were drug naïve, blood samples were collected at the time of HIV diagnosis before initiation of ART."
37104815,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed on the HIV-1 genome (Illumina technology) according to the ANRS consensus. Resistance was retrospectively evaluated for participants with VF (pVL >50 copies/mL, confirmed within 4 weeks), numbering six and four in the 4/7 and 7/7 days groups, respectively, at failure in RNA samples (on the confirmed test), and at D0 in DNA samples. Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed for the reverse transcriptase (RT), protease (PR) and integrase (INT) regions, according to the Agence Nationale de Recherche sur le SIDA et les Maladies Infectieuses Emergentes (ANRS-MIE) consensus, using Illumina technology.","The paper describes the use of Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) on HIV-1 genome samples from the participants in the study. This indicates that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples, and it is mentioned that the study involved virological substudies focusing on genetic analyses of HIV RNA and DNA from plasma and semen samples of participants who were part of the ANRS-170 QUATUOR trial. Specifically, it mentions sequencing was performed on HIV-1 genome samples from total DNA in blood and RNA in plasma, indicating the sequences were from patient samples. This means, the answer is 'Yes'."
37104815,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"In virological substudies of the ANRS 170 QUATUOR trial, which are representative of the total trial population, we measured the viral reservoir as total HIV DNA, as well as residual plasma viraemia and semen VL as surrogate markers of the size of the HIV infection reservoir, which could influence long-term suppression.17,21,27-28 There was no significant difference after 1 year of a 4/7 days maintenance strategy versus a 7/7 days regimen in terms of blood HIV DNA level (as previously described17) or the percentage of participants with residual plasma viraemia. In a substudy on the seminal compartment based on 78 participants, we showed that seminal plasma HIV was detectable in <10% of participants at D0 and W48 and in non-significantly different proportions in the two groups. Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed on the HIV-1 genome (Illumina technology) according to the ANRS consensus. A generalized estimation equation with a Poisson distribution was used to compare changes in the proportion of residual viraemia, detectable semen HIV RNA and HIV DNA within and between the two groups over time. Resistance was retrospectively evaluated for participants with VF (pVL >50 copies/mL, confirmed within 4 weeks), numbering six and four in the 4/7 and 7/7 days groups, respectively, at failure in RNA samples (on the confirmed test), and at D0 in DNA samples. Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed for the reverse transcriptase (RT), protease (PR) and integrase (INT) regions, according to the Agence Nationale de Recherche sur le SIDA et les Maladies Infectieuses Emergentes (ANRS-MIE) consensus, using Illumina technology (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA). The sequence reads were analysed using IDNSVR software v3_8_0r4 (VCSmartGene), and resistance was interpreted using the latest ANRS resistance algorithm (version 24, http://hivfrenchresistance.org/). We also analysed sequences with the Stanford algorithm (https://hivdb.stanford.edu/). For UDS, the minimum coverage was set to 50 and the ambiguity filter to 2%. According to the signature APOBEC-mediated mutations list of Stanford University (https://hivdb.stanford.edu/page/apobecs/), five G-to-A drug resistance mutations (DRMs) in the RT gene were considered in the HIV-1 sequences: D67N, E138K, M184I, G190E/S and M230I; and six in the integrase gene: G118R, E138K, G140R/S, G163K/R, D232N and R263K.","The paper primarily discusses the 4 days/week antiretroviral maintenance strategy (ANRS 170 QUATUOR trial) and its effects on virological outcomes such as residual viraemia, HIV DNA levels, and resistance mutations in the context of 4/7 days versus daily intake therapy. It focuses on the sequencing of HIV-1 genome to analyze resistance mutations in DNA and RNA samples from participants, but it does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data. The methods mentioned involve clinical samples and resistance analyses but do not include in vitro susceptibility testing, indicating that the paper does not present in vitro drug susceptibility data. Therefore, the answer is 'No'."
