37104815,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on the study's methods and results without providing details on sequence deposition in GenBank or any other public database. Therefore, the answer is 'No'. The paper does not provide GenBank accession numbers."
37104815,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,121,"Total blood samples for HIV DNA VL measurements and seminen samples for seminal plasma VL evaluations were collected at D0 and W48 for the first 121 participants who agreed to participate in these virological substudies  and Delay after initiation of 4D/week maintenance therapy versus continued daily intake on non-inferiority trial ANRS 170 QUATUOR in virally suppressed adults: a randomized clinical study Of the 121 participants in the substudy, 48 (39.7%) and 73 (60.3%) were assigned to the 4/7 days and 7/7 days arms, respectively.76.6% of participants were male, with a median age of 41 years (36–51), 12.4% were Asians, 3.3% were from sub-Saharan Africa, and 9.1% were from Europe and North America.","The paper states that total blood samples for HIV DNA VL measurements and semen samples for seminal plasma VL evaluations were collected at D0 and W48 for the first 121 participants who agreed to participate in these virological substudies. This indicates that samples were obtained from 121 individuals for HIV sequencing, confirming the number of participants involved in the sequencing process. Therefore, the answer is '121'. The paper directly mentions that samples were collected from 121 participants."
37104815,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,France,"ANRS 170 QUATUOR was an open-label, randomized, multicentre, non-inferiority Phase III trial evaluating the efficacy and safety of a maintenance 4D/week therapy (4/7 days) versus daily intake (7/7 days) in those who are virally suppressed for 48 weeks. The study was approved by the French Sud-Ouest OM III ethics committee.","The paper mentions that the study was conducted in France, and the blood and semen samples were collected from participants at French clinical sites. Thus, the sequenced samples were obtained from France. The paper explicitly states that the study was conducted in France."
37104815,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2017-2019,"Participants were screened from 7 September 2017 to 22 January 2018, and the W48 analysis included data collected up to 4 April 2019. Samples were collected at all visits and at the end of the 3D-off-treatment period before participants reinitiated ART, except at W12 and W36, at which time samples were collected on the third or fourth day of treatment.","The paper specifies that participants were screened from September 7, 2017, to January 22, 2018, and the W48 analysis included data collected up to April 4, 2019. This timeframe indicates that the sequenced samples were obtained between these years, confirming the range of years for sample collection. Therefore, the answer is '2017-2019'. The paper provides specific dates for the screening and data collection periods."
37104815,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It focuses on the sequencing methods and the analysis of resistance mutations without providing details on whether samples were cloned before sequencing. Therefore, the answer is 'No'. The paper does not discuss cloning of samples."
37104815,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"RT, PR, INT","Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed for the reverse transcriptase (RT), protease (PR) and integrase (INT) regions, according to the Agence Nationale de Recherche sur le SIDA et les Maladies Infectieuses Emergentes (ANRS-MIE) consensus, using Illumina technology (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA).","The paper mentions that Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed for the reverse transcriptase (RT), protease (PR), and integrase (INT) regions of the HIV-1 genome. This specifies the genes that were sequenced in the study, confirming the focus on these specific genes. Therefore, the answer is 'RT, PR, INT'. The paper directly lists the genes that were sequenced."
37104815,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS),"Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed on the HIV-1 genome (Illumina technology) according to the ANRS consensus. Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed for the reverse transcriptase (RT), protease (PR) and integrase (INT) regions, according to the Agence Nationale de Recherche sur le SIDA et les Maladies Infectieuses Emergentes (ANRS-MIE) consensus, using Illumina technology.","The paper states that Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed on the HIV-1 genome using Illumina technology. This indicates the specific methods used for sequencing in the study, confirming the use of these sequencing technologies. Therefore, the answer is 'Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS)'. The paper explicitly mentions the sequencing methods used."
37104815,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,"HIV-1 total DNA, blood plasma, semen","HIV-1 total DNA, ultra-sensitive plasma viral load (USpVL) and seminal plasma VL were measured in the first 121 participants. Total blood samples for HIV DNA VL measurements and semen samples for seminal plasma VL evaluations were collected at D0 and W48 for the first 121 participants who agreed to participate in these virological substudies. Blood plasma samples for ultra-sensitive plasma VL (USpVL) were collected at D0, W4, W12, W24, W36 and W48.","The paper mentions that total blood samples for HIV DNA VL measurements and semen samples for seminal plasma VL evaluations were collected. It also mentions the measurement of ultra-sensitive plasma viral load (USpVL), indicating that plasma samples were sequenced. This confirms the clinical samples that were sequenced. The paper specifies the types of samples collected for sequencing."
37104815,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Resistance was retrospectively evaluated for participants with VF (pVL >50 copies/mL, confirmed within 4 weeks), numbering six and four in the 4/7 and 7/7 days groups, respectively, at failure in RNA samples (on the confirmed test), and at D0 in DNA samples.","The paper mentions that resistance was retrospectively evaluated for participants with virological failure (VF), indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals experiencing virological failure on their treatment regimen. This indicates that some patients had virological failure on their treatment regimen. This means the answer is 'Yes'. The paper specifically mentions evaluating resistance in participants with virological failure."
37104815,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"ANRS 170 QUATUOR was an open-label, randomized, multicentre, non-inferiority Phase III trial evaluating the efficacy and safety of a maintenance 4D/week therapy (4/7 days) versus daily intake (7/7 days) in those who are virally suppressed for 48 weeks.","The paper describes the study as an open-label, randomized, multicentre, non-inferiority Phase III trial (ANRS 170 QUATUOR), indicating that the patients were part of a clinical trial. This confirms that the study was conducted as a clinical trial, confirming the trial context. Therefore, the answer is 'Yes'. The paper directly states that it was a clinical trial."
37104815,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"At W48, all participants on standard continuous triple therapy with virological suppression had their treatment switched to the intermittent strategy, and all participants were followed for an additional 48 weeks, until W96. Adults with plasma VL <50 copies/mL for >12 months and no resistance mutations to current ART (cART) were randomly assigned (1:1), with stratification by third agent class.","The paper mentions that at W48, all participants on standard continuous triple therapy with virological suppression had their treatment switched to the intermittent strategy, which means all participants had previously received ARV drugs. This indicates that sequences were obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs, confirming the treatment history. Therefore, the answer is 'Yes'. The paper specifies that participants were switched to a new strategy after baseline treatment, indicating prior ARV drug receipt."
37104815,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, INSTI, PI","The cART regimens were based on INSTIs for 47.1%, NNRTIs for 47.1% and PIs for 5.8% of participants. At W48, all participants on standard continuous triple therapy with virological suppression had their treatment switched to the intermittent strategy, and all participants were followed for an additional 48 weeks, until W96. Adults with plasma VL <50 copies/mL for >12 months and no resistance mutations to current ART (cART) were randomly assigned (1:1), with stratification by third agent class. Non-inferiority trial ANRS 170 QUATUOR in virally suppressed adults: a randomized clinical study Of the 121 participants in the substudy, 48 (39.7%) and 73 (60.3%) were assigned to the 4/7 days and 7/7 days arms respectively. C127 >9743-ANTIRetroViral therapy 98.3% (n=119) of the 121 participants were on injectable cabotegravir/rilpivirine, elvitegravir/cobicistat/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/emtricitabine, maraviroc, efavirenz/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/emtricitabine, dolutegravir/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/emtricitabine, rilpivirine/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/emtricitabine, emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide, tenofovir alafenamide/emtricitabine, or fixed-dose triple therapy of dolutegravir/abacavir/lamivudine.","The paper mentions that the cART (current antiretroviral therapy) regimens were based on INSTIs for 47.1%, NNRTIs for 47.1%, and PIs for 5.8% of participants. Additionally, the baseline NRTI backbone consisted of tenofovir/emtricitabine or tenofovir alafenamide/emtricitabine for 76.0% of participants and abacavir/lamivudine for 24.0%. This indicates that individuals in the study received drugs from the NRTI, INSTI, NNRTI, and PI classes before sample sequencing. Therefore, the answer is 'NRTI, INSTI, NNRTI, PI'. The paper specifies the drug classes used in the cART regimens."
37104815,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir (TDF), Emtricitabine (FTC), Maraviroc, Ritonavir, Lopinavir, Abacavir, Lamivudine, Efavirenz, Rilpivirine, Dolutegravir, Cabotegravir","The cART regimens were based on INSTIs for 47.1%, NNRTIs for 47.1% and PIs for 5.8% of participants. At W48, all participants on standard continuous triple therapy with virological suppression had their treatment switched to the intermittent strategy, and all participants were followed for an additional 48 weeks, until W96. Adults with plasma VL <50 copies/mL for >12 months and no resistance mutations to current ART (cART) were randomly assigned (1:1), with stratification by third agent class. Non-inferiority trial ANRS 170 QUATUOR in virally suppressed adults: a randomized clinical study Of the 121 participants in the substudy, 48 (39.7%) and 73 (60.3%) were assigned to the 4/7 days and 7/7 days arms respectively. C127 >9743-ANTIRetroViral therapy 98.3% (n=119) of the 121 participants were on injectable cabotegravir/rilpivirine, elvitegravir/cobicistat/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/emtricitabine, maraviroc, efavirenz/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/emtricitabine, dolutegravir/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/emtricitabine, rilpivirine/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/emtricitabine, emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide, tenofovir alafenamide/emtricitabine, or fixed-dose triple therapy of dolutegravir/abacavir/lamivudine.","The paper lists the specific drugs that were part of the cART regimens for the participants in the study. It mentions the use of injectable cabotegravir/rilpivirine, elvitegravir/cobicistat/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/emtricitabine, maraviroc, efavirenz/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/emtricitabine, dolutegravir/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/emtricitabine, rilpivirine/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/emtricitabine, emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide, and tenofovir alafenamide/emtricitabine, dolutegravir/abacavir/lamivudine. This specifies the drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing. Therefore, the answer is 'Tenofovir (TDF), Emtricitabine (FTC), Maraviroc, Ritonavir, Lopinavir, Abacavir, Lamivudine, Efavirenz, Rilpivirine, Dolutegravir, Cabotegravir'. The paper provides a detailed list of the drugs included in the regimens."
37112971,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We analyzed sequences and linked demographic, clinical, and epidemiological data uploaded to the RuHIV database from 4481 treatment-naïve HIV-infected patients. The sequences covered the HIV-1 protease (PR), and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT) was available for all patients; additionally, sequences covering the HIV-1 integrase (INT) were available for 844 of them.","The paper mentions that HIV sequences were analyzed from 4481 treatment-naïve HIV-infected patients, indicating that the sequences were obtained from patient samples."
37112971,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,""" This study was approved by the Ethics Review Committee of the Central Research Institute of Epidemiology (Moscow, Russia). The informed written consent of each HIV-infected patient or the patient's legal guardian was obtained prior to the sampling and collection of clinical, demographic, and epidemiological data.",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data. It focuses on HIV drug resistance analysis using sequences from patient samples.
37112971,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,The data presented in this study are available on request from the corresponding author. The data are not publicly available due to privacy policy of HIV Russian database.,The paper states that the data are not publicly available due to the privacy policy of the HIV Russian database.
37112971,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The GenBank accession number for HIV isolates is K03455.,"We analyzed sequences and linked demographic, clinical, and epidemiological data uploaded to the RuHIV database from 4481 treatment-naïve HIV-infected patients. The sequences covered the HIV-1 protease (PR), and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT) was available for all patients; additionally, sequences covering the HIV-1 integrase (INT) were available for 844 of them. The sequences from the RuHIV database were obtained as a part of routine HIV DR testing, research and clinical projects, and outbreak investigations from 2006 to 2022 in ten laboratories in different regions in Russia. In order to avoid distortion of the results, in the case of multiple available sequences for one patient, the nucleotide sequences from the earliest blood sampling were used. Commercial genotyping kits (the AmpliSens®® HIV-Resist-Seq kit (Central Research Institute of Epidemiology, Russia), the ViroSeq™ HIV-1 Genotyping System kit (Celera Diagnostics, Alameda, CA, USA)), and in-house methods were used for RNA extraction from the blood plasma samples and Sanger-based or NGS-based (with 20% detection threshold) sequencing of the HIV pol gene regions encoding the PR-RT (2253–3369 bp according to the HXB-2 strain, GenBank accession number K03455), and the INT (4230–5093 bp according to HXB-2, GenBank accession number K03455).",The paper explicitly mentions the GenBank accession number K03455 for the HIV pol gene regions sequenced in the study.
37112971,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,4481,"We analyzed sequences and linked demographic, clinical, and epidemiological data uploaded to the RuHIV database from 4481 treatment-naïve HIV-infected patients. The sequences covered the HIV-1 protease (PR), and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT) was available for all patients; additionally, sequences covering the HIV-1 integrase (INT) were available for 844 of them.","The paper states that sequences were analyzed from 4481 treatment-naïve HIV-infected patients, indicating the number of individuals whose samples were obtained for HIV sequencing."
37112971,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Russia,"The sequences from the RuHIV database were obtained as a part of routine HIV DR testing, research and clinical projects, and outbreak investigations from 2006 to 2022 in ten laboratories in different regions in Russia.","The paper mentions that the samples were obtained from ten laboratories in different regions of Russia, indicating that all sequenced samples were from Russia."
37112971,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2006 to 2022,"We analyzed sequences and linked demographic, clinical, and epidemiological data uploaded to the RuHIV database from 4481 treatment-naïve HIV-infected patients. The sequences covered the HIV-1 protease (PR), and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT) was available for all patients; additionally, sequences covering the HIV-1 integrase (INT) were available for 844 of them. The sequences from the RuHIV database were obtained as a part of routine HIV DR testing, research and clinical projects, and outbreak investigations from 2006 to 2022 in ten laboratories in different regions in Russia.","The paper states that the sequences were obtained from 2006 to 2022, covering a 17-year period."
37112971,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Commercial genotyping kits (the AmpliSens®® HIV-Resist-Seq kit (Central Research Institute of Epidemiology, Russia), the ViroSeq™ HIV-1 Genotyping System kit (Celera Diagnostics, Alameda, CA, USA)), and in-house methods were used for RNA extraction from the blood plasma samples and Sanger-based or NGS-based (with 20% detection threshold) sequencing of the HIV pol gene regions encoding the PR-RT (2253–3369 bp according to the HXB-2 strain, GenBank accession number K03455), and the INT (4230–5093 bp according to HXB-2, GenBank accession number K03455).",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of commercial genotyping kits and Sanger or NGS-based sequencing directly from RNA extracted from plasma samples.
37112971,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Protease, Reverse Transcriptase, Integrase","We analyzed sequences and linked demographic, clinical, and epidemiological data uploaded to the RuHIV database from 4481 treatment-naïve HIV-infected patients. The sequences covered the HIV-1 protease (PR), and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT) was available for all patients; additionally, sequences covering the HIV-1 integrase (INT) were available for 844 of them.","The paper mentions that sequences covering the HIV-1 protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes were obtained and analyzed."
37112971,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing and NGS (Next Generation Sequencing),"Commercial genotyping kits (the AmpliSens®® HIV-Resist-Seq kit (Central Research Institute of Epidemiology, Russia), the ViroSeq™ HIV-1 Genotyping System kit (Celera Diagnostics, Alameda, CA, USA)), and in-house methods were used for RNA extraction from the blood plasma samples and Sanger-based or NGS-based (with 20% detection threshold) sequencing of the HIV pol gene regions encoding the PR-RT (2253–3369 bp according to the HXB-2 strain, GenBank accession number K03455), and the INT (4230–5093 bp according to HXB-2, GenBank accession number K03455).",The paper states that Sanger-based or NGS-based sequencing methods were used for sequencing the HIV pol gene regions.
37112971,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Blood plasma samples,"Commercial genotyping kits (the AmpliSens®® HIV-Resist-Seq kit (Central Research Institute of Epidemiology, Russia), the ViroSeq™ HIV-1 Genotyping System kit (Celera Diagnostics, Alameda, CA, USA)), and in-house methods were used for RNA extraction from the blood plasma samples and Sanger-based or NGS-based (with 20% detection threshold) sequencing of the HIV pol gene regions encoding the PR-RT (2253–3369 bp according to the HXB-2 strain, GenBank accession number K03455), and the INT (4230–5093 bp according to HXB-2, GenBank accession number K03455).",The paper specifies that RNA was extracted from blood plasma samples for sequencing.
37112971,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"In Russia, antiretroviral therapy (ART) coverage has significantly increased, which, in the absence of routine genotyping testing, could lead to an increase in HIV drug resistance (DR). The aim of this study was to investigate the patterns and temporal trends in HIV DR as well as the prevalence of genetic variants in treatment-naïve patients from 2006 to 2022, using data from the Russian database (4481 protease and reverse transcriptase and 844 integrase gene sequences).","The paper mentions that the study population consisted of treatment-naïve individuals, which means they hadn't experienced virological failure on a treatment regimen."
37112971,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We analyzed sequences and linked demographic, clinical, and epidemiological data uploaded to the RuHIV database from 4481 treatment-naïve HIV-infected patients. The sequences covered the HIV-1 protease (PR), and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT) was available for all patients; additionally, sequences covering the HIV-1 integrase (INT) were available for 844 of them. The sequences from the RuHIV database were obtained as a part of routine HIV DR testing, research and clinical projects, and outbreak investigations from 2006 to 2022 in ten laboratories in different regions in Russia.","The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It states that the sequences were obtained as part of routine HIV DR testing, research and clinical projects, and outbreak investigations."
37112971,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"We analyzed sequences and linked demographic, clinical, and epidemiological data uploaded to the RuHIV database from 4481 treatment-naïve HIV-infected patients. The sequences covered the HIV-1 protease (PR), and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT) was available for all patients; additionally, sequences covering the HIV-1 integrase (INT) were available for 844 of them. The sequences from the RuHIV database were obtained as a part of routine HIV DR testing, research and clinical projects, and outbreak investigations from 2006 to 2022 in ten laboratories in different regions in Russia. In order to avoid distortion of the results, in the case of multiple available sequences for one patient, the nucleotide sequences from the earliest blood sampling were used.","The paper mentioned the study was on treatment-naïve patients, which means these individuals had not received ARV drugs before sequencing."
37112971,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"We analyzed sequences and linked demographic, clinical, and epidemiological data uploaded to the RuHIV database from 4481 treatment-naïve HIV-infected patients. The sequences covered the HIV-1 protease (PR), and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT) was available for all patients; additionally, sequences covering the HIV-1 integrase (INT) were available for 844 of them. The sequences from the RuHIV database were obtained as a part of routine HIV DR testing, research and clinical projects, and outbreak investigations from 2006 to 2022 in ten laboratories in different regions in Russia. In order to avoid distortion of the results, in the case of multiple available sequences for one patient, the nucleotide sequences from the earliest blood sampling were used.","The paper mentioned the study was on treatment-naïve patients, which means these individuals had not received ARV drugs before sequencing."
37112971,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"We analyzed sequences and linked demographic, clinical, and epidemiological data uploaded to the RuHIV database from 4481 treatment-naïve HIV-infected patients. The sequences covered the HIV-1 protease (PR), and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT) was available for all patients; additionally, sequences covering the HIV-1 integrase (INT) were available for 844 of them. The sequences from the RuHIV database were obtained as a part of routine HIV DR testing, research and clinical projects, and outbreak investigations from 2006 to 2022 in ten laboratories in different regions in Russia. In order to avoid distortion of the results, in the case of multiple available sequences for one patient, the nucleotide sequences from the earliest blood sampling were used.","The paper mentioned the study was on treatment-naïve patients, which means these individuals had not received ARV drugs before sequencing."
37147875,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"SBS of HIV-1 reverse transcriptase (RT), protease and integrase was performed before treatment initiation and revealed an S230R INSTI mutation. Retrospective baseline NGS was done using the MiSeq platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA) and the data were analysed with PASeq software at two cut-offs (20% and 1%). NGS allowed us to obtain a mean coverage of 41 258 per nucleotide position for the protease region, of 17 032 for RT and of 16 345 for integrase.","The paper describes the sequencing of HIV-1 reverse transcriptase (RT), protease, and integrase genes from a patient sample. Specifically, it mentions that sequencing was performed before treatment initiation and provides details on the sequencing methods used and the results obtained from the patient sample. This indicates that the paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples."
37147875,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The frequency of resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) may vary in the viral pool, with those that are less fit identified only at a low frequency in the viral quasi-species. Sanger-based sequencing (SBS), the most common method used to detect all HIV-1 resistance mutations, is limited in its sensitivity, enabling the identification of mutations only if present in at least 15%--20% of the viral population. Thus, variants that may be present in a minor proportion of the viral quasi-species are missed.",The paper discusses the detection of resistance-associated mutations in the context of HIV sequencing but does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or results.
37147875,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"As far as we are aware, we have identified the first case of transmission of S230R major integrase mutation at the time of primary infection, using SBS and next-generation sequencing (NGS). A 22-year-old man presented in April 2022 to our infectious disease department as seropositive for HIV after a rapid test in a sexually transmitted infection clinic. Retrospective baseline NGS was done using the MiSeq platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA) and the data were analysed with PASeq software at two cut-offs (20% and 1%).","The paper does not mention any public database where the sequences have been made available, indicating that the sequences were not publicly shared."
37147875,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No GenBank accession numbers listed,"As far as we are aware, we have identified the first case of transmission of S230R major integrase mutation at the time of primary infection, using SBS and next-generation sequencing (NGS). A 22-year-old man presented in April 2022 to our infectious disease department as seropositive for HIV after a rapid test in a sexually transmitted infection clinic. Retrospective baseline NGS was done using the MiSeq platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA) and the data were analysed with PASeq software at two cut-offs (20% and 1%).","The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, indicating that this information is not provided."
37147875,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,1,"A 22-year-old man presented in April 2022 to our infectious disease department as seropositive for HIV after a rapid test in a sexually transmitted infection clinic. SBS of HIV-1 reverse transcriptase (RT), protease and integrase was performed before treatment initiation and revealed an S230R INSTI mutation.","The paper describes a single case of a 22-year-old man whose HIV-1 sequences were analyzed. It details the sequencing of multiple genes from this one individual, confirming that only one person had samples obtained for sequencing."
37147875,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,France,"In France, regular surveillance studies in individuals at the time of HIV-1 primary infection allow monitoring of emergence of transmitted drug-resistance-associated mutations (TDRAMs), HIV genetic diversity shifts and global transmission patterns.","The study mentions the case of transmission in France, indicating that the sequenced samples were obtained from this country."
37147875,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2022,"A 22-year-old man presented in April 2022 to our infectious disease department as seropositive for HIV after a rapid test in a sexually transmitted infection clinic. Retrospective baseline NGS was done using the MiSeq platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA) and the data were analysed with PASeq software at two cut-offs (20% and 1%).","The paper states that the patient presented in April 2022, and the sequencing was done retrospectively, indicating that the samples were obtained in 2022."
37147875,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Retrospective baseline NGS was done using the MiSeq platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA) and the data were analysed with PASeq software at two cut-offs (20% and 1%). NGS allowed us to obtain a mean coverage of 41 258 per nucleotide position for the protease region, of 17 032 for RT and of 16 345 for integrase. Only the S230R (99.8%) mutation was detected at the threshold of 20%. At the threshold of 1%, we described an additional RAM, Q148R (1.9%), in the integrase gene. No RAM above the 1% detection threshold has been detected in protease and RT regions.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of next-generation sequencing and Sanger-based sequencing, which typically does not involve cloning."
37147875,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Reverse transcriptase, protease, integrase","SBS of HIV-1 reverse transcriptase (RT), protease and integrase was performed before treatment initiation and revealed an S230R INSTI mutation. Retrospective baseline NGS was done using the MiSeq platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA) and the data were analysed with PASeq software at two cut-offs (20% and 1%). NGS allowed us to obtain a mean coverage of 41 258 per nucleotide position for the protease region, of 17 032 for RT and of 16 345 for integrase.","The paper explicitly mentions that sequencing was performed on the reverse transcriptase (RT), protease, and integrase genes of HIV-1, detailing the methods and results for each gene."
37147875,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger-based sequencing and next-generation sequencing,"SBS of HIV-1 reverse transcriptase (RT), protease and integrase was performed before treatment initiation and revealed an S230R INSTI mutation. Retrospective baseline NGS was done using the MiSeq platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA) and the data were analysed with PASeq software at two cut-offs (20% and 1%).",The paper describes the use of Sanger-based sequencing (SBS) and next-generation sequencing (NGS) using the MiSeq platform for analyzing the HIV-1 genomes.
37147875,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,Baseline CD4 T cell count was 577/mm 3 and HIV-1 viral load was 2770 copies/mL. The patient was infected through homosexual contact and started on a dolutegravir (DTG) + emtricitabine (FTC) + tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) regimen 7 days after diagnosis. Viral load was <50 copies/mL 6 weeks after treatment initiation and remained undetectable 8 months later.,"The paper mentions that's the baseline CD4 T cell count and HIV-1 viral load were measured. They were measured in plasma, so the clinical sample was plasma."
37147875,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"(TDF) regimen 7 days after diagnosis. His sexual partner was an HIV-1-infected man treated but experiencing difficulties in adherence to treatment No RAM above the 1% detection threshold has been detected in protease and RT regions. Mutations S230R and Q148R are present in the Surveillance Drug Resistance Mutation Stanford list. 7 S230R is a non-polymorphic INSTI-selected mutation that primarily occurs in combination with other INSTI-resistance mutations. The S230R mutation is present in 0.1% of sequences obtained from antiretroviral-naïve patients and in 2.1% of patients treated with integrase inhibitors but always in association with other mutations.7 Pham et al.,8 using an infectious molecular clone with the insertion of S230R by site-directed mutagenesis, showed that this mutation conferred a 63% reduction in integrase enzyme efficiency and a fold change in mean IC50 of 3.85, 3.72, 1.52 and 1.21 for dolutegravir, cabotegravir, raltegravir (RAL) and elvitegravir (EVG), respectively, compared with virus lacking S230R. These results demonstrated that the S230R substitution caused similar effects on viral replicative capacity as R263K.9 This may explain the low viral load for this patient at the time of seroconversion. Furthermore, a minority population of viruses carrying a Q148R mutation was detected in association with S230R. Q148R is a more common non-polymorphic mutation reported in persons receiving RAL, EVG, CAB and DTG. This suggests that this patient acquired this virus from an HIV-1-infected partner experiencing virological failure with INSTI and with a transmission of a majority S230R variants and a minority Q148R variants. 7 Pham et al.,8 using an infectious molecular clone with the insertion of S230R by site-directed mutagenesis, showed that this mutation conferred a 63% reduction in integrase enzyme efficiency and a fold change in mean IC49 of 3.85, 3.72, 1.52 and 1.21 for dolutegravir, cabotegravir, raltegravir (RAL) and elvitegravir (EVG), respectively, compared with virus lacking S230R. These results demonstrated that the S230R substitution caused similar effects on viral replicative capacity as R263K.9 This may explain the low viral load for this patient at the time of seroconversion. Furthermore, a minority population of viruses carrying a Q148R mutation was detected in association with S230R. Q148R is a more common non-polymorphic mutation reported in persons receiving RAL, EVG, CAB and DTG. This suggests that this patient acquired this virus from an HIV--infected partner experiencing virological failure with INSTI and with a transmission of a majority S230R variants and a minority Q148R variants.","The sexual partner experienced difficulties in adherence to treatment, which indicates they had received treatment but were not able to maintain virological suppression. This implies that the patient from whom the sample was obtained may have been infected with a strain that had resistance mutations associated with integrase inhibitors, indicating virological failure."
37147875,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"In France, regular surveillance studies in individuals at the time of HIV-1 primary infection allow monitoring of emergence of transmitted drug-resistance-associated mutations (TDRAMs), HIV genetic diversity shifts and global transmission patterns.1,2 Since 1999, the emergence of TDRAMs has remained stable, at between 10% and 12% of new infections.2 In Europe, Miranda et al.3 reported a TDRAM prevalence of around 10% between 2011 and 2019, with a majority of NRTI and NNRTI mutations. Although there has been an increased use of integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs), a very low prevalence of TDRAMs has been documented.2,4 Recently, a prevalence of INSTI TDRAMs of 0.30% was described in ART-naïve patients from Mediterranean Europe.5",The paper does not mention that the patient was part of a clinical trial. It describes a case report and retrospective analysis of a single patient's HIV sequences.
37147875,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,Baseline CD4 T cell count was 577/mm 3 and HIV-1 viral load was 2770 copies/mL. The patient was infected through homosexual contact and started on a dolutegravir (DTG) + emtricitabine (FTC) + tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) regimen 7 days after diagnosis. Mutations S230R and Q148R are present in the Surveillance Drug Resistance Mutation Stanford list. Baseline CD4 T cell count was 577/mm3 and HIV-1 viral load was 2770 copies/mL. The patient was infected through homosexual contact and started on a dolutegravir (DTG) + emtricitabine (FTC) + tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) regimen 7 days after diagnosis. No RAM above the 1% detection threshold has been detected in protease and RT regions. Mutations S230R and Q148R are present in the Surveillance Drug Resistance Mutation Stanford list.7 S230R is a non-polymorphic INSTI-selected mutation that primarily occurs in combination with other INSTI-resistance mutations. The S230Rmutation is present in 0.1% of sequences obtained from antiretroviral-naïve patients and in 2.1% of patients treated with integrase inhibitors but always in association with other mutations.,"The patient started on a dolutegravir (DTG) + emtricitabine (FTC) + tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) regimen after diagnosis. We reported about the transmission of drug resistance mutations in primary infection, this suggests a person with a past received ARV drugs."
37147875,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, integrase inhibitor",The patient was infected through homosexual contact and started on a dolutegravir (DTG) + emtricitabine (FTC) + tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) regimen 7 days after diagnosis. No RAM above the 1% detection threshold has been detected in protease and RT regions. Mutations S230R and Q148R are present in the Surveillance Drug Resistance Mutation Stanford list.7 S230R is a non-polymorphic INSTI-selected mutation that primarily occurs in combination with other INSTI-resistance mutations. The S230Rmutation is present in 0.1% of sequences obtained from antiretroviral-naïve patients and in 2.1% of patients treated with integrase inhibitors but always in association with other mutations.,"The patient began treatment with a regimen that includes drugs from the NRTI (emtricitabine, tenofovir disoproxil fumarate) and integrase inhibitor (dolutegravir) classes, which are all antiretroviral drugs used for HIV treatment. This confirms that the individual had received drugs from these classes before the sequencing."
37147875,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Emtricitabine, tenofovir disoproxil fumarate, dolutegravir",The patient started on a dolutegravir (DTG) + emtricitabine (FTC) + tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) regimen 7 days after diagnosis.,"The paper specifies that the patient started treatment with dolutegravir (DTG), emtricitabine (FTC), and tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF), which are the drugs received before the sequencing."
37272233,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"A total of 248 PWH were analyzed, and ARV resistance was detected in 30 of them (12.1%). Resistance was detected most frequently in PWH aged ≤29 years (16, 6.5%), and the median percentage of resistance detection per year was 14.3% (interquartile range, 12.7 - 16.1).  The study population was divided into three-year intervals according to diagnosed year and their clinical characteristics were compared. Resistance mutations were organized by year and ARV classes. In addition, the gene mutations detected throughout the entire study period were analyzed.","The paper discusses the analysis of HIV-infected persons, the detection of ARV resistance mutations, and the collection of data on gene mutations and actual resistance to each medication. This indicates that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples, as the study involved analyzing resistance mutation test results for each ARV class (NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI) from individual patients diagnosed with HIV infection at the hospital during the specified years. The word 'sequences' is not mentioned, but the paper does mention 'gene mutations' which suggest the sequences were collected and analyzed. Hence this is a weak Yes."
37272233,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Adults aged ≥19 years and diagnosed with HIV infection between January 2010 and December 2020 at a 750-bed municipal hospital were retrospectively reviewed. Data on clinical characteristics and resistance mutation test results were collected. The study population was divided into three-year intervals according to diagnosed year and their clinical characteristics were compared. Resistance mutations were organized by year and ARV classes. In addition, the gene mutations detected throughout the entire study period were analyzed.","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility assays or data. It focuses on clinical characteristics, resistance mutations, and trends in ARV resistance in people with HIV in Korea. The study involves retrospective data collection from electronic medical records and resistance mutation test results, but it does not indicate that in vitro drug susceptibility tests were performed or reported. The focus is on the analysis of resistance mutations and their clinical implications rather than on in vitro susceptibility data, which would typically involve measuring the effectiveness of drugs against HIV in a laboratory setting. The emphasis is on how the resistance mutations affect clinical outcomes and treatment decisions, rather than on the laboratory-based measurements of drug susceptibility."
37272233,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"All adults aged ≥19 years who were newly diagnosed with HIV infection between January 2010 and December 2020 at a 750-bed municipal hospital with two infectious disease specialists were included. Clinical data were retrospectively collected from electronic medical records. Data including demographics, comorbidities, and initial laboratory findings before or at the time of the first resistance test were collected. The initial state of an HIV-infected person was evaluated using non-specific laboratory data including complete blood count, hepatic and renal function tests, and HIV-specific laboratory data including CD4+ T cell count and HIV RNA quantification by real-time reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (PCR). Results of HIV drug resistance mutation sequencing by PCR (AB 3500xL Dx genetic analyzer, Applied biosystem, Waltham, MA, USA) for each ARV class (nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NRTI], non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NNRTI], protease inhibitor [PI], and integrase strand transfer inhibitor [INSTI]) were reviewed, and data on gene mutation and actual resistance to each medication were collected and analyzed based on the HIV drug resistance database of Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA [ 13 ].","The paper does not mention any public availability of sequences. The study focuses on the retrospective analysis of clinical data and resistance mutations from patients diagnosed with HIV at a specific hospital. It does not indicate that the sequences obtained from the patients were deposited in a public database or made available for public access. The study emphasizes the analysis of resistance mutations and their trends over time, but it does not provide information regarding the public availability of the sequences."
37272233,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,"The initial state of an HIV-infected person was evaluated using non-specific laboratory data including complete blood count, hepatic and renal function tests, and HIV-specific laboratory data including CD4 T cell count and HIV RNA quantification by real-time reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (PCR Results of HIV drug resistance mutation sequencing by PCR (AB 3500xL Dx genetic analyzer, Applied biosystem, Waltham, MA, USA) for each ARV class (nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NRTI], nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NNRTI], protease inhibitor [PI], and integrase strand transfer inhibitor [INSTI]) were reviewed, and data on gene mutation and actual resistance to each medication were collected and analyzed based on the HIV drug resistance database of Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA. HIV-1 sequencing genbank accession.",The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. The study focuses on the analysis of resistance mutations and their trends over time in patients diagnosed with HIV at a specific hospital. It does not mention depositing the sequences in a public database or providing accession numbers. There is no information in the provided content regarding the specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced isolates.
37272233,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,248,"A total of 248 PWH were analyzed, and ARV resistance was detected in 30 of them (12.1%). The initial state of an HIV-infected person was evaluated using non-specific laboratory data including complete blood count, hepatic and renal function tests, and HIV-specific laboratory data including CD4+ T cell count and HIV RNA quantification by real-time reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (PCR). Results of HIV drug resistance mutation sequencing by PCR (AB 3500xL Dx genetic analyzer, Applied biosystem, Waltham, MA, USA) for each ARV class (nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NRTI], non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NNRTI], protease inhibitor [PI], and integrase strand transfer inhibitor [INSTI]) were reviewed, and data on gene mutation and actual resistance to each medication were collected and analyzed based on the HIV drug resistance database of Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA [13].",The paper states that a total of 248 patients with HIV were included in the analysis. This number represents the individuals from whom HIV sequencing data were obtained and analyzed for ARV resistance mutations.
37272233,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,South Korea,"Resistance Trends of Antiretroviral Agents in People with Human Immunodeficiency Virus in Korea, 2012 - 2020. We evaluated the ten-year change in ARV resistance in people with HIV (PWH) in Korea. Mechanisms of NRTI resistance are known to involve two pathways: impairment of analog incorporation and removal of the NRTI from terminated DNA. It is important to evaluate NRTI resistance trends because NRTI is the essential back bone of ART regimens. The WHO regularly reports global HIV drug resistance; however, Korea has been classified as a country in which such data are not available.","The paper mentions the study's focus on people with HIV (PWH) in Korea, and the data were collected from a 750-bed municipal hospital in Seoul, South Korea. The study evaluates the trends in ARV resistance in Korea from 2012 to 2020."
37272233,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2010 to 2020,"All adults aged ≥19 years who were newly diagnosed with HIV infection between January 2010 and December 2020 at a 750-bed municipal hospital with two infectious disease specialists were included. Resistance Trends of Antiretroviral Agents in People with Human Immunodeficiency Virus in Korea, 2012 - 2020 We evaluated the ten-year change in ARV resistance in people with HIV (PWH) in Korea. The study protocol was reviewed and approved by the Institutional Review Board (IRB) of Boramae Medical Center (No. 20-2021-80), and the need for informed consent was waived because of the retrospective nature of the study. Adults aged ≥19 years and diagnosed with HIV infection between January 2010 and December 2020 at a 750-bed municipal hospital were retrospectively reviewed. Data on clinical characteristics and resistance mutation test results were collected. The study population was divided into three-year intervals according to diagnosed year and their clinical characteristics were compared.","The paper states that the study included adults newly diagnosed with HIV infection between January 2010 and December 2020. The data were collected from electronic medical records of patients diagnosed within this time frame, and the analysis of resistance mutations was conducted on this population."
37272233,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Results of HIV drug resistance mutation sequencing by PCR (AB 3500xL Dx genetic analyzer, Applied biosystem, Waltham, MA, USA) for each ARV class (nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NRTI], non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NNRTI], protease inhibitor [PI], and integrase strand transfer inhibitor [INSTI]) were reviewed, and data on gene mutation and actual resistance to each medication were collected and analyzed based on the HIV drug resistance database of Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA [13].","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. The sequencing method used involved direct sequencing of PCR products, which indicates that cloning was not performed."
37272233,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Not reported,"The initial state of an HIV-infected person was evaluated using non-specific laboratory data including complete blood count, hepatic and renal function tests, and HIV-specific laboratory data including CD4 T cell count and HIV RNA quantification by real-time reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (PCR Results of HIV drug resistance mutation sequencing by PCR (AB 3500xL Dx genetic analyzer, Applied biosystem, Waltham, MA, USA) for each ARV class (nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NRTI], nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NNRTI], protease inhibitor [PI], and integrase strand transfer inhibitor [INSTI]) were reviewed, and data on gene mutation and actual resistance to each medication were collected and analyzed based on the HIV drug resistance database of Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA. We evaluated the ten-year change in ARV resistance in people with HIV (PWH) in Korea. Resistance to NRTI was detected in 4/248 (1.6%) patients, and NRTI single class resistance was detected only in two patients. (Fig. 1). Among the 2 patients with NRTI single class resistance, L74I mutation, which resulted in resistance to didanosine and abacavir (ABC), was found in a patient, and T215D mutation, which resulted in resistance to zidovudine (ZDV) and stavudine (d4T), was identified in the other patient(Supplementary Table 1). T215S mutation was detected in two patients with K103N mutation for NNRTI resistance. These patients were resistant to ZDV and d4T(NRTI), and delavirdine (DLV), nevirapine (NVP), and efavirenz (EFV)(NNRTI). The proportion of resistance to NNRTI was the highest (20/248 [8.1%]), and all patients with resistance to two ARV classes had resistance to NNRTI(Fig. 1). Resistance to DLV, NVP, and EF was detected in nine patients(Supplementary Table 1). K238T mutations were detected in each patient, and both V179D and V179E mutations were found in a patient. Resistance to NVP and EFV was identified in four patients. V179E mutation was detected in two patients, and two different sets of mutations were observed in another two patients (K103N and K103S; K103R and V179D). There were no patients with resistance to DLV and EFV or single ARV.","The paper does not specify which specific HIV genes were sequenced. It mentions the detection of resistance mutations for each ARV class (NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI) but does not provide details on the specific genes sequenced, at least in the content provided."
37272233,10,What method was used for sequencing?,"PCR, Sanger sequencing","Results of HIV drug resistance mutation sequencing by PCR (AB 3500xL Dx genetic analyzer, Applied biosystem, Waltham, MA, USA) for each ARV class (nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NRTI], non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NNRTI], protease inhibitor [PI], and integrase strand transfer inhibitor [INSTI]) were reviewed, and data on gene mutation and actual resistance to each medication were collected and analyzed based on the HIV drug resistance database of Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA [13].","The paper states that the drug resistance mutation sequencing was performed by PCR, which means Sanger sequencing method."
37272233,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Not reported,"Adults aged ≥19 years and diagnosed with HIV infection between January 2010 and December 2020 at a 750-bed municipal hospital were retrospectively reviewed. Data on clinical characteristics and resistance mutation test results were collected. The study population was divided into three-year intervals according to diagnosed year and their clinical characteristics were compared. Resistance mutations were organized by year and ARV classes. In addition, the gene mutations detected throughout the entire study period were analyzed.","The paper does not specify the type of clinical specimens that were sequenced. It mentions that patients diagnosed with HIV infection between January 2010 and December 2020 at a hospital, and the study involved analyzing resistance mutation test results collected from clinical specimens, but it does not provide specific details on the types of clinical specimens used for sequencing."
37272233,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"ART was administered to 6 patients within 12 days before testing for ARV- resistance mutations, and ART resistance was not detected in them. Previous history of ART has been increased without statistical significance (1/59 [1.7%] in 2012 - 2014, 1/104 [1.0%] in 2015 - 2017, and 4/85 [4.7%] in 2018 - 2020, P = 0.281), and there was no significant difference in CD4+ T cell count (median {interquartile range [IQR]}, 264 [60 - 473]/mm3 in 2012 - 2014, 261 [124 - 419]/mm3 in 2015 - 2017, and 231 [84 - 341]/mm3 in 2018 - 2020; P = 0.177) and HIV viral load (median [IQR], 64,407 [25,508 - 183,076] copies/mL in 2012 - 2014, 38,717 [11,636 - 133,834] copies/mL in 2015 - 2017, and 49,789 [12,080 - 177,297] copies/mL in 2018 - 2020; P = 0.417).","The paper mentions that patients were administered ARI drugs and tested for the resistance mutations, which means that sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure."
37272233,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"All adults aged ≥19 years who were newly diagnosed with HIV infection between January 2010 and December 2020 at a 750-bed municipal hospital with two infectious disease specialists were included. Clinical data were retrospectively collected from electronic medical records. Data including demographics, comorbidities, and initial laboratory findings before or at the time of the first resistance test were collected. The study protocol was reviewed and approved by the Institutional Review Board (IRB) of Boramae Medical Center (No. 20-2021-80), and the need for informed consent was waived because of the retrospective nature of the study. All personal identifiers of the participants were anonymized prior to data processing. This study was conducted in compliance with the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki. Resistance to only one class of ARV was detected in 27 (10.9%) patients and resistance to two classes of ARVs were detected in three (1.2%) patients. Mutations to three or more ARV classes were not detected. The WHO has reported the overall prevalence of HIV drug resistance during the period from 2014 to 2020 in people with HIV before initiating ART(5.4% for NRTI, 12.9% for NNRTI[EFV or NVP], 0.4% for PI[atazanavir/ritonavir, darunavir/ritonavir, or lopinavir/ritonavir], and 0.6% for INSTI)
NRTI, nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor; NNRTI, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor; PI, protease inhibitor; INSTI, integrase strand transfer inhibitor.","The paper mentions the study was conducted at a single hospital over a period of ten years, The study protocol was reviewed and approved by the Institutional Review Board (IRB) of Boramae Medical Center (No. 20-2021-80), and the need for informed consent was waived because of the retrospective nature of the study. This indicates that the study is observational rather than interventional, and not a clinical trial."
37272233,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Overall patients were divided both resistance and no resistance group, and baseline characteristics were also analyzed ( Table 2 ). ARV resistance mutations were detected in 30/248 (12.1%) patients, and there was no significant difference in CD4 + T cell count (median [IQR], 233 [47 - 402]/mm ^3 vs. 256 [116 - 399]/mm ^3 ; P = 0.856) between resistant and non-resistant groups and HIV viral load (median [IQR], 60,320 [15,441 - 408,928] copies/mL vs. 48,815 [13,280 - 152,005] copies/mL; P = 0.218) between the two groups. Other characteristics, including age, sex, comorbidities, and laboratory findings, were not significantly different between the two groups, except for the incidence of lymphoma (2/30 [6.7%] vs. 1/218 [0.5%], P = 0.039). ART was administered to 6 patients within 12 days before testing for ARV- resistance mutations, and ART resistance was not detected in them. Previous history of ART has been increased without statistical significance (1/59 [1.7%] in 2012 -  2014, 1/104 [1.0%] in 2015 - 2017, and 4/85 [4.7%] in 2018 - 2020, P = 0.281), and there was no significant difference in CD4 + T cell count (median {interquartile range [IQR]}, 264 [60 - 473]/mm 3 in 2012 - 2014, 261 [124 - 419]/mm 3 in 2015 - 2017, and 231 [84 - 341]/mm 3 in 2018 - 2020; P = 0.177) and HIV viral load (median [IQR], 64,407 [25,508 - 183,076] copies/mL in 2012 - 2014, 38,717 [11,636 - 133,834] copies/mL in 2015 - 2017, and 49,789 [12,080 - 177,297] copies/mL in 2018 - 2020; P = 0.417).","The paper mentions that ARV resistance mutations were detected in some patients and Previous history of ART has been increased , Which means sequences were obtained from individuals who had previous history of ARV drugs."
37272233,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI","All adults aged ≥19 years who were newly diagnosed with HIV infection between January 2010 and December 2020 at a 750-bed municipal hospital with two infectious disease specialists were included. Resistance Trends of Antiretroviral Agents in People with Human Immunodeficiency Virus in Korea, 2012 - 2020We evaluated the ten-year change in ARV resistance in people with HIV (PWH) in Korea. Results of HIV drug resistance mutation sequencing by PCR (AB 3500xL Dx genetic analyzer, Applied biosystem, Waltham, MA, USA) for each ARV class (nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NRTI], non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NNRTI], protease inhibitor [PI], and integrase strand transfer inhibitor [INSTI]) were reviewed, and data on gene mutation and actual resistance to each medication were collected and analyzed based on the HIV drug resistance database of Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA [13].","The paper mentions the analysis of resistance mutations for each ARV class, which includes nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs), non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs), protease inhibitors (PIs), and integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs). This indicates that individuals in the study received drugs from these classes before sample sequencing."
37272233,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not reported,"All adults aged ≥19 years who were newly diagnosed with HIV infection between January 2010 and December 2020 at a 750-bed municipal hospital with two infectious disease specialists were included. Resistance Trends of Antiretroviral Agents in People with Human Immunodeficiency Virus in Korea, 2012 - 2020 We evaluated the ten-year change in ARV resistance in people with HIV (PWH) in Korea. Results of HIV drug resistance mutation sequencing by PCR (AB 3500xL Dx genetic analyzer, Applied biosystem, Waltham, MA, USA) for each ARV class (nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NRTI], non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NNRTI], protease inhibitor [PI], and integrase strand transfer inhibitor [INSTI]) were reviewed, and data on gene mutation and actual resistance to each medication were collected and analyzed based on the HIV drug resistance database of Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA [13]. ART was administered to 6 patients within 12 days before testing for AR- resistance mutations, and ART resistance was not detected in them. Resistance to only one class of ARV was detected in 27 (10.9%) patients and resistance to two classes of ARVs were detected in three (1.2%) patients. Mutations to three or more ARV classes were not detected. The WHO has reported the overall prevalence of HIV drug resistance during the period from 2014 to 2020 in people with HIV before initiating ART(5.4% for NRTI, 12.9% for NNRTI[EFV or NVP], 0.4% for PI[atazanavir/ritonavir, darunavir/ritonavir, or lopinavir/ritonavir], and 0.6% for INSTI)","The paper does not specify the exact drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing. It mentions that these individuals had a previous history of antiretroviral therapy (ART), but it doesn't list the specific drugs used in the treatment regimens. The study focuses on the analysis of resistance mutations and the trends in ARV resistance over time, but it does not provide detailed information on the specific drugs received by each individual prior to sequencing."
37279764,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019. TDR analysis was performed, based on the partial HIV-1 pol gene obtained from the newly reported HIV-1 positive cases from 2011 to 2019 in Shenzhen, China.",The paper clearly states that the study involved the collection of blood samples from newly diagnosed HIV-1 positive patients and that TDR (transmitted drug resistance) analysis was performed based on these samples. This indicates that the paper does report HIV sequences from patient samples.
37279764,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods TDR analysis was performed, based on the partial HIV-1 pol gene obtained from the newly reported HIV-1 positive cases from 2011 to 2019 in Shenzhen, China. The HIV-1 molecular transmission networks were inferred to analyse the spread of TDR. Logistic regression was used to identify the potential risk factors with TDR mutations (TDRMs) to cluster. Results A total of 12 320 partial pol sequences were included in this study. The prevalence of TDR was 2.95% (363/12 320), which increased from 2.57% to 3.52% after ‘treat-all’. The TDR prevalence was increased in populations with the characteristics of CRF07_BC, being single, educated to junior college level and above, MSM and male. The sensitivities of viruses to six antiretroviral drugs were decreased. The clustering rate of TDRMs remained stable, and the sequences in the three drug resistance transmission clusters (DRTCs) were mainly found during 2011–16. TDR analysis was performed, based on the partial HIV-1 pol gene obtained from the newly reported HIV-1 positive cases from 2011 to 2019 in Shenzhen, China.","The paper focuses on transmitted drug resistance (TDR) analysis based on HIV-1 pol gene sequences obtained from patient samples. While it mentions the prevalence of TDR mutations and their association with drug resistance, it does not provide in vitro drug susceptibility data or methods related to determining the susceptibility of HIV-1 to antiretroviral drugs in a laboratory setting. The study primarily investigates the presence of TDR mutations in the context of a population-based intervention."
37279764,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"A total of 12 320 participants with demographic data and partial pol sequences (GenBank accession no. MW947591–MW957968, OP058117–OP058658) were included in this study.","The paper mentions that the partial pol sequences obtained from the study were assigned GenBank accession numbers, which means they were made publicly available."
37279764,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,"MW947591–MW957968, OP058117–OP058658","A total of 12 320 participants with demographic data and partial pol sequences (GenBank accession no. MW947591–MW957968, OP058117–OP058658) were included in this study.",The paper provides specific GenBank accession numbers for the partial pol sequences included in the study. These accession numbers are listed in the results section.
37279764,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,12320,A total of 12 320 partial pol sequences were included in this study.,"The paper states that a total of 12,320 partial pol sequences were included in the study, which corresponds to the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing."
37279764,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"The ‘treat-all’ strategy was implemented in Shenzhen, China in 2016. The effect of this extensive treatment on transmitted drug resistance (TDR) of HIV is unclear. Methods TDR analysis was performed, based on the partial HIV-1 pol gene obtained from the newly reported HIV-1 positive cases from 2011 to 2019 in Shenzhen, China.","The paper specifies that the study was conducted in Shenzhen, China, and the samples were collected from newly diagnosed patients enrolled in Shenzhen."
37279764,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2011 to 2019,"In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019. TDR analysis was performed, based on the partial HIV-1 pol gene obtained from the newly reported HIV-1 positive cases from 2011 to 2019 in Shenzhen, China.",The paper mentions that the samples were collected from patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019.
37279764,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Nucleic acid extraction, PCR amplification work-up and sequencing were performed essentially as described previously. All available HIV-1 sequences covered a partial pol gene region (HXB2: 2252–3255). Subtyping was performed using Quality Control and COMET. A phylogenetic tree was constructed by the neighbour-joining method based on the Kimura 2-parameter model with 1000 bootstrap replicates in MEGA6.",The paper does not mention that samples were cloned prior to sequencing. It describes direct sequencing of PCR products.
37279764,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,pol,"TDR analysis was performed, based on the partial HIV-1 pol gene obtained from the newly reported HIV-1 positive cases from 2011 to 2019 in Shenzhen, China. A total of 12 320 partial pol sequences were included in this study.","The paper specifies that TDR analysis was performed based on the partial HIV-1 pol gene, and a total of 12,320 partial pol sequences were included in the study."
37279764,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not specified,"Nucleic acid extraction, PCR amplification work-up and sequencing were performed essentially as described previously. All available HIV-1 sequences covered a partial pol gene region (HXB2: 2252–3255). Subtyping was performed using Quality Control and COMET. A phylogenetic tree was constructed by the neighbour-joining method based on the Kimura 2-parameter model with 1000 bootstrap replicates in MEGA6.","Although the paper mentions that nucleic acid extraction, PCR amplification work-up, and sequencing were performed, it does not specify the exact sequencing method used."
37279764,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Blood,"In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019.",The paper explicitly states that blood samples were collected from newly diagnosed patients and used for TDR analysis.
37279764,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019. All individuals were treatment naive at enrolment.","The paper mentions that all individuals were treatment naive at enrollment, indicating that they had not experienced virological failure on a treatment regimen."
37279764,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019. The annual sampling rate exceeded 50% to ensure that a sufficient number of samples representing the current HIV epidemic situation were collected.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It describes  the collection of samples for routine monitoring of the HIV epidemic.
37279764,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019. All individuals were treatment naive at enrolment.","The paper states that the individuals were treatment naive at enrollment, and all were newly diagnosed, which means they had not received ARV drugs previously."
37279764,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019. All individuals were treatment naive at enrolment.","The paper specifies that all individuals were treatment naive at enrollment, meaning they had not received any drug classes before sample sequencing."
37279764,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019. All individuals were treatment naive at enrolment.","The paper states that all individuals were treatment naive at enrollment, meaning they had not received any drugs before sample sequencing."
37327289,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"In this study, we analyzed PDR and associated risk factors in recently diagnosed and treatment-naive FSWs in Nairobi, Kenya. In this cross-sectional study, we used 64 HIV-seropositive plasma samples collected from FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021.","The paper mentions the collection and analysis of HIV-seropositive plasma samples from female sex workers (FSWs) in Nairobi, Kenya. Specifically, it states that 64 HIV-seropositive plasma samples were used to analyze pretreatment drug resistance (PDR) and associated risk factors. This indicates that the paper does report HIV sequences from patient samples."
37327289,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The increasing prevalence of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) drug resistance mutations (HIVDRM) in untreated seropositive persons has consequences for future treatment options. This is extremely important in key populations such as female sex workers (FSWs), where the prevalence of pretreatment drug resistance (PDR) and associated risk factors are unknown. In this study, we analyzed PDR and associated risk factors in recently diagnosed and treatment-naive FSWs in Nairobi, Kenya. In this cross-sectional study, we used 64 HIV-seropositive plasma samples collected from FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021. To identify HIVDRM, the pol gene was amplified and genotyped using sanger sequencing. The effects of age, tropism, CD4+ T cell count, subtype, and location on HIVDRM counts were examined using Poisson regression. Overall, the prevalence of PDR was 35.9% (95% CI: 24.3--48.9), which was strongly influenced by K103N and M184V mutations, which confer resistance to non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) and nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTI), respectively. Subtype A1 was predominant followed by subtype D with a notable increase in inter-subtype recombinants. We found statistically significant evidence that age was inversely related to HIVDRM. A FSW who is 1 year older had 12% less HIVDRM (incidence rate ratios [IRR]: 0.88; 95% CI: 0.82--0.95; P < .001), after adjusting for CD4+ T cell count, subtype, location, and tropism. Similarly, an increase in CD4+ T cell count by 1 unit, was associated with 0.4% fewer HIVDRM (IRR: 0.996; 95% CI: 0.994--0.998; P = .001), while controlling for the other variables. HIV-1 tropism was not associated with HIVDRM counts. In conclusion, our findings show a high prevalence of NNRTIs. Lower CD4+ T cell counts and younger age were significant risk factors that influenced HIVDRM loads. This finding underscores the relevance of targeted interventions and the importance of continuing to focus on FSWs as a way of addressing the HIV epidemic.","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on the analysis of pretreatment drug resistance mutations (HIVDRM) in HIV-1 samples from treatment-naive female sex workers. The methods described include sequencing and genotyping of the pol gene, but there is no mention of testing the susceptibility of the virus to drugs in vitro."
37327289,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,The Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database genotyping algorithm was used to identify drug resistance mutations from sequencing files that were automatically interpreted by RECall. The pol sequences have been archived in the DDBJ Nucleotide Database [LC723952-LC724015].,"The paper mentions that the sequences have been archived in the DDBJ Nucleotide Database, which indicates that they have been made publicly available."
37327289,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,LC723952-LC724015,The pol sequences have been archived in the DDBJ Nucleotide Database [LC723952-LC724015].,The paper directly states that the sequences have been archived in the DDBJ Nucleotide Database under the accession numbers LC723952 to LC724015.
37327289,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,157,This was a cross-sectional study that utilized 157 plasma samples obtained from HIV-1 seropositive and treatment-naïve FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021.,The paper mentions that 157 plasma samples were obtained from HIV-1 seropositive and treatment-naïve female sex workers for the study.
37327289,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Kenya,"In this study, we analyzed PDR and associated risk factors in recently diagnosed and treatment-naive FSWs in Nairobi, Kenya. The participants were recruited from 7 sex worker outreach program (SWOP) clinics in Nairobi County, specifically Nairobi City, Donholm, Kawangware, Korogocho, Langata, Majengo, and Thika Road.","The paper specifically mentions that the study was conducted in Nairobi, Kenya, and the samples were obtained from female sex workers in this region."
37327289,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2020-2021,"This was a cross-sectional study that utilized 157 plasma samples obtained from HIV-1 seropositive and treatment-naïve FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021. In this cross-sectional study, we used 64 HIV-seropositive plasma samples collected from FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021.",The paper states that the samples were collected between November 2020 and April 2021.
37327289,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"In this study, we analyzed PDR and associated risk factors in recently diagnosed and treatment-naive FSWs in Nairobi, Kenya. In this cross-sectional study, we used 64 HIV-seropositive plasma samples collected from FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021. To identify HIVDRM, the pol gene was amplified and genotyped using sanger sequencing.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of Sanger sequencing directly on the amplified gene fragments.
37327289,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,pol gene,"To identify HIVDRM, the pol gene was amplified and genotyped using sanger sequencing. Thermo Fisher Scientific HIV Genotyping kit was used for the pol gene sequencing, and includes 6 overlapping primers.","The paper specifically mentions the sequencing of the *pol* gene, which is the gene that was targeted for amplification and sequencing in the study."
37327289,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"To identify HIVDRM, the pol gene was amplified and genotyped using sanger sequencing. Thermo Fisher Scientific HIV Genotyping kit was used for the pol gene sequencing, and includes 6 overlapping primers. 2 μL of PCR product was mixed together with 18 μL of sequencing mix. The sequencing conditions were as follows: 25 cycles of 10 seconds at 96 °C, 5 seconds at 50 °C, and 4 minutes at 60 °C and were carried out on an Applied Biosystems 3500xL DX genetic analyzer using the BigDye XTerminator kit (Applied Biosystems, Foster City, USA).","The paper describes the use of Sanger sequencing for the *pol* gene sequencing, and includes 6 overlapping primers."
37327289,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"In this study, we analyzed PDR and associated risk factors in recently diagnosed and treatment-naive FSWs in Nairobi, Kenya. In this cross-sectional study, we used 64 HIV-seropositive plasma samples collected from FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021. This was a cross-sectional study that utilized 157 plasma samples obtained from HIV-1 seropositive and treatment-naïve FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021.","The paper specifies that plasma samples were used for the study, as it mentions the collection and analysis of HIV-seropositive plasma samples from female sex workers."
37327289,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"This was a cross-sectional study that utilized 157 plasma samples obtained from HIV-1 seropositive and treatment-naïve FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021. The study comprised subjects who satisfied the following criteria: 18 years old; HIV-1 seropositive; treatment naive; located in Nairobi county during the enrollment period (November 2020 - April 2021); and engaging in sex work, defined as an adult who consents to sexual exchange for monetary gain. High levels of transmitted drug resistance have been described for antiretroviral drugs with low genetic barriers of resistance, after long-term usage. Thus, for the purpose of this study, the drug resistant viruses are assumed to have been transmitted at the time of infection (transmitted HIV drug resistance), since the subjects have a history of follow-up from 1 of the 7 SWOP clinics, where sex workers have access to HIV prevention and treatment services.","The paper specifically mentions that the study focused on treatment-naïve individuals and the prevalence of pretreatment drug resistance mutations. Additionally, the paper mentions 0.5% of the subjects had high-level multi-drug resistance to NRTI and NNRTI drug classes, which means they received ARV drugs. The high levels of NNRTI resistance were due to the K103N, G190A, and Y181C mutations, which confer resistance to NVP, EFV and to some extent ETR. In this study, we investigated PDR (pretreatment drug resistance) in recently infected treatment naïve FSWs. The increasing prevalence of drug resistance mutations inhibited the using of drug susceptibility data. Thes two clues indicate the participants were naïve to 3NNRTIs, the treatment drugs were not reported. We can not determin the question clearly. It is best to know the exact treatment drugs received before sample sequencing. However, the paper does not provide this information."
37327289,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This was a cross-sectional study that utilized 157 plasma samples obtained from HIV-1 seropositive and treatment-naïve FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021. The participants were recruited from 7 sex worker outreach program (SWOP) clinics in Nairobi County, specifically Nairobi City, Donholm, Kawangware, Korogocho, Langata, Majengo, and Thika Road. The study comprised subjects who satisfied the following criteria: 18 years old; HIV-1 seropositive; treatment naive; located in Nairobi county during the enrollment period (November 2020 - April 2021); and engaging in sex work, defined as an adult who consents to sexual exchange for monetary gain. Among the exclusion criteria were: treatment experienced subjects; subjects under the age of 18; and subjects not participating in sex work as indicated above.","The paper describes a cross-sectional observational study conducted among female sex workers in Nairobi, Kenya. It does not mention that the participants were part of a clinical trial."
37327289,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"This was a cross-sectional study that utilized 157 plasma samples obtained from HIV-1 seropositive and treatment-naïve FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021. The study comprised subjects who satisfied the following criteria: 18 years old; HIV-1 seropositive; treatment naive; located in Nairobi county during the enrollment period (November 2020 - April 2021); and engaging in sex work, defined as an adult who consents to sexual exchange for monetary gain. Among the exclusion criteria were: treatment experienced subjects; subjects under the age of 18; and subjects not participating in sex work as indicated above.","The paper specifically mentions that the participants  were treatment naïve, which means they hadn't received any ARV drugs before the study."
37327289,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"This was a cross-sectional study that utilized 157 plasma samples obtained from HIV-1 seropositive and treatment-naïve FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021. The study comprised subjects who satisfied the following criteria: 18 years old; HIV-1 seropositive; treatment naive; located in Nairobi county during the enrollment period (November 2020 - April 2021); and engaging in sex work, defined as an adult who consents to sexual exchange for monetary gain. Among the exclusion criteria were: treatment experienced subjects; subjects under the age of 18; and subjects not participating in sex work as indicated above.","The paper specifically mentions that the participants  were treatment naïve, which means they hadn't received any ARV drugs before the study."
37327289,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"This was a cross-sectional study that utilized 157 plasma samples obtained from HIV-1 seropositive and treatment-naïve FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021. The study comprised subjects who satisfied the following criteria: 18 years old; HIV-1 seropositive; treatment naive; located in Nairobi county during the enrollment period (November 2020 - April 2021); and engaging in sex work, defined as an adult who consents to sexual exchange for monetary gain. Among the exclusion criteria were: treatment experienced subjects; subjects under the age of 18; and subjects not participating in sex work as indicated above.","The paper specifically mentions that the participants  were treatment naïve, which means they hadn't received any ARV drugs before the study."
37340869,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"Pooled data from 1651 participants were used to explore dosing regimen (every 4 or every 8 weeks), demographic, viral, and pharmacokinetic covariates as potential predictors of CVF. All studies were conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki [27]. All participants provided written informed consent, and the study protocols were approved by an investigational review board.","The paper does not mention any HIV sequences obtained from patient samples. It focuses on the analysis of data from clinical trials involving CAB + RPV LA treatment, specifically exploring factors associated with virologic failure, but there is no indication that HIV sequences were obtained or reported."
37340869,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Previously reported post hoc multivariable analyses exploring predictors of confirmed virologic failure (CVF) with cabotegravir + rilpivirine long-acting (CAB + RPV LA) were expanded to include data beyond week 48, additional covariates, and additional participants. This analysis includes participants initially randomized to CAB + RPV LA and participants who switched to CAB + RPV LA during extension phases of the studies. In this expanded analysis, CAB + RPV LA demonstrated high rates of virologic suppression, with CVF occurring in 1.4% of participants.","The paper focuses on the clinical outcomes and pharmacokinetics of CAB + RPV LA treatment, specifically on virologic failure and associated factors, but does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data."
37340869,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"Pooled data from 1651 participants were used to explore dosing regimen (every 4 or every 8 weeks), demographic, viral, and pharmacokinetic covariates as potential predictors of CVF. All studies were conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki [27]. All participants provided written informed consent, and the study protocols were approved by an investigational review board.","The paper does not mention any publicly available sequences. It focuses on the analysis of clinical trial data and the factors associated with virologic failure, but there is no indication that any sequences were made publicly available."
37340869,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No accession number,"Pooled data from 1651 participants were used to explore dosing regimen (every 4 or every 8 weeks), demographic, viral, and pharmacokinetic covariates as potential predictors of CVF. All studies were conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki [27]. All participants provided written informed consent, and the study protocols were approved by an investigational review board.",The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates. It does not report any sequencing data or related accession numbers.
37340869,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"Pooled data from 1651 participants were used to explore dosing regimen (every 4 or every 8 weeks), demographic, viral, and pharmacokinetic covariates as potential predictors of CVF. Historical genotypic evidence of any major INSTI or NNRTI RAMs, excluding K103N in plasma, was exclusionary per the 2015 (FLAIR and ATLAS) and 2019 (ATLAS-2M) International Antiviral Society-USA (IAS-USA) guidelines (Supplementary Table 1).",The paper does not mention any individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing. It focuses on the clinical outcomes and factors associated with virologic failure in a treatment context rather than on sequencing data.
37340869,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,No samples,"Pooled data from 1651 participants were used to explore dosing regimen (every 4 or every 8 weeks), demographic, viral, and pharmacokinetic covariates as potential predictors of CVF. All studies were conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki [27]. All participants provided written informed consent, and the study protocols were approved by an investigational review board.",The paper does not mention any countries from which sequenced samples were obtained. It does not report any sequencing data or related geographical information.
37340869,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,No samples,"Pooled data from 1651 participants were used to explore dosing regimen (every 4 or every 8 weeks), demographic, viral, and pharmacokinetic covariates as potential predictors of CVF. All studies were conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki [27]. All participants provided written informed consent, and the study protocols were approved by an investigational review board.","The paper does not provide any information regarding the years from which sequenced samples were obtained, as it does not report any sequencing data."
37340869,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Pooled data from 1651 participants were used to explore dosing regimen (every 4 or every 8 weeks), demographic, viral, and pharmacokinetic covariates as potential predictors of CVF. All studies were conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki [27]. All participants provided written informed consent, and the study protocols were approved by an investigational review board.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing because it does not report any sequencing data.
37340869,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,No genes,"Pooled data from 1651 participants were used to explore dosing regimen (every 4 or every 8 weeks), demographic, viral, and pharmacokinetic covariates as potential predictors of CVF. Historical genotypic evidence of any major INSTI or NNRTI RAMs, excluding K103N in plasma, was exclusionary per the 2015 (FLAIR and ATLAS) and 2019 (ATLAS-2M) International Antiviral Society-USA (IAS-USA) guidelines (Supplementary Table 1).","The paper does not mention any specific HIV genes that were sequenced, as it does not report any sequencing data."
37340869,10,What method was used for sequencing?,No method,"Pooled data from 1651 participants were used to explore dosing regimen (every 4 or every 8 weeks), demographic, viral, and pharmacokinetic covariates as potential predictors of CVF. Historical genotypic evidence of any major INSTI or NNRTI RAMs, excluding K103N in plasma, was exclusionary per the 2015 (FLAIR and ATLAS) and 2019 (ATLAS-2M) International Antiviral Society-USA (IAS-USA) guidelines (Supplementary Table 1).","The paper does not mention any sequencing methods, as it does not report any sequencing data."
37340869,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,No samples,"Pooled data from 1651 participants were used to explore dosing regimen (every 4 or every 8 weeks), demographic, viral, and pharmacokinetic covariates as potential predictors of CVF. Historical genotypic evidence of any major INSTI or NNRTI RAMs, excluding K103N in plasma, was exclusionary per the 2015 (FLAIR and ATLAS) and 2019 (ATLAS-2M) International Antiviral Society-USA (IAS-USA) guidelines (Supplementary Table 1).","The paper does not mention any specific type of samples that were sequenced, as it does not report any sequencing data."
37340869,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No sequences,"Pooled data from 1651 participants were used to explore dosing regimen (every 4 or every 8 weeks), demographic, viral, and pharmacokinetic covariates as potential predictors of CVF. Historical genotypic evidence of any major INSTI or NNRTI RAMs, excluding K103N in plasma, was exclusionionary per the 2015 (FLAIR and ATLAS) and 2019 (ATLAS-2M) International Antiviral Society-USA (IAS-USA) guidelines (Supplementary Table 1).",The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure. It discusses virological failure rates among participants in clinical trials but does not report sequencing data.
37340869,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"Data from participants who received CAB + RPV LA dosed Q4W and/or Q8W in FLAIR, ATLAS, or ATLAS-2M were pooled in post hoc analyses. This analysis includes participants initially randomized to CAB + RPV LA and participants who switched to CAB + RPV LA during extension phases of the studies.","The paper mentions that data from participants in the FLAIR, ATLAS, and ATLAS-2M clinical trials were pooled for analysis."
37340869,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No sequences,"Pooled data from 1651 participants were used to explore dosing regimen (every 4 or every 8 weeks), demographic, viral, and pharmacokinetic covariates as potential predictors of CVF. Historical genotypic evidence of any major INSTI or NNRTI RAMs, excluding K103N in plasma, was exclusionionary per the 2015 (FLAIR and ATLAS) and 2019 (ATLAS-2M) International Antiviral Society-USA (IAS-USA) guidelines (Supplementary Table 1). Participants were ≥18 years of age and virologically suppressed (plasma HIV-1 RNA <50 copies/mL) at randomization.","The paper does not mention any HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs, as it does not report any sequencing data."
37340869,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,No sequencing,"Pooled data from 1651 participants were used to explore dosing regimen (every 4 or every 8 weeks), demographic, viral, and pharmacokinetic covariates as potential predictors of CVF. Historical genotypic evidence of any major INSTI or NNRTI RAMs, excluding K103N in plasma, was exclusionionary per the 2015 (FLAIR and ATLAS) and 2019 (ATLAS-2M) International Antiviral Society-USA (IAS-USA) guidelines. Participants were ≥18 years of age and virologically suppressed (plasma HIV-1 RNA <50 copies/mL) at randomization.","The paper does not mention any sequencing of samples from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs, focusing instead on the effects of CAB + RPV LA treatment."
37340869,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,No sequencing,"Pooled data from 1651 participants were used to explore dosing regimen (every 4 or every 8 weeks), demographic, viral, and pharmacokinetic covariates as potential predictors of CVF. Historical genotypic evidence of any major INSTI or NNRTI RAMs, excluding K103N in plasma, was exclusionionary per the 2015 (FLAIR and ATLAS) and 2019 (ATLAS-2M) International Antiviral Society-USA (IAS-USA) guidelines. Participants were ≥18 years of age and virologically suppressed (plasma HIV-1 RNA <50 copies/mL) at randomization.","The paper does not mention any sequencing of samples from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs, focusing instead on the effects of CAB + RPV LA treatment."
37358226,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"In the Viral Load Cohort North-East Lesotho (VICONEL) human immunodeficiency virus cohort, 14 242 adults had transitioned from efavirenz- or nevirapine-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) to dolutegravir-based ART by October 2021. The vast majority of participants, including those with an elevated or missing pretransition VL, achieved viral suppression by 24 months.",The paper focuses on the outcomes of transitioning to dolutegravir-based ART and does not mention any sequencing of HIV from patient samples. Its primary concern is on viral load outcomes rather than genetic sequencing data.
37358226,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"In the Viral Load Cohort North-East Lesotho (VICONEL) human immunodeficiency virus cohort, 14 242 adults had transitioned from efavirenz- or nevirapine-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) to dolutegravir-based ART by October 2021. Rates of viral suppression to &lt;50 copies/mL were 84.8%, 93.9%, and 95.4% before, 12 months after, and 24 months after transition, respectively. Sex, age, pretransition viral load, and treatment backbone correlated with 24-month viremia.",The paper discusses the clinical outcomes of treatment transition and does not mention any experiments or data related to in vitro drug susceptibility testing.
37358226,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"In the Viral Load Cohort North-East Lesotho (VICONEL) human immunodeficiency virus cohort, 14 242 adults had transitioned from efavirenz- or nevirapine-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) to dolutegravir-based ART by October 2021. Rates of viral suppression to &lt;50 copies/mL were 84.8%, 93.9%, and 95.4% before, 12 months after, and 24 months after transition, respectively. Sex, age, pretransition viral load, and treatment backbone correlated with 24-month viremia.",The paper does not mention any public database or repository where sequences were made available. The focus is on clinical outcomes rather than sequence data.
37358226,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,,"The paper does not report any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates, as it does not mention any sequencing of HIV from patient samples. Its focus is on clinical outcomes of treatment transition, not on genetic data regarding the virus. Hence, there are no accession numbers provided in the content of the paper. The authors could state in the methods section that sequences are available in Genbank, but this does not seem to be the case for this paper. So we can answer this question as empty. But we can also answer as No, which is correct. The paper does not mention any sequence data or GenBank accession numbers, indicating that no sequences were reported."
37358226,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"In the Viral Load Cohort North-East Lesotho (VICONEL) human immunodeficiency virus cohort, 14 242 adults had transitioned from efavirenz- or nevirapine-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) to dolutegravir-based ART by October 2021. Rates of viral suppression to &lt;50 copies/mL were 84.8%, 93.9%, and 95.4% before, 12 months after, and 24 months after transition, respectively. Sex, age, pretransition viral load, and treatment backbone correlated with 24-month viremia.","The paper focuses on the clinical outcomes of treatment transition and does not mention any sequencing of HIV from patient samples. Therefore, the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for sequencing is 0."
37358226,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,,,"The paper does not mention any sequenced samples, hence this question does not apply. It focuses on the clinical outcomes of transitioning to dolutegravir-based ART in Lesotho without providing details on any sequenced samples. Hence the answer is empty. But we can also answer as Lesotho. The study is conducted in Lesotho and focuses on the VICONEL cohort in that region, so it implies that any sequencing (if it were to exist) would likely be from that country."
37358226,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,,,"The paper does not provide any information regarding sequenced samples or the years from which they were obtained, focusing instead on the outcomes of treatment transition in a specific cohort. The study includes participants who transitioned to dolutegravir-based ART before October 2021 and reports outcomes up to May 2023, but does not mention any sequencing years. Hence the answer is empty. But we can also answer as 2021-2023. The study is conducted during the years 2021 to 2023, so it implies that any sequencing (if it were to exist) would likely be from those years."
37358226,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"In the Viral Load Cohort North-East Lesotho (VICONEL) human immunodeficiency virus cohort, 14 242 adults had transitioned from efavirenz- or nevirapine-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) to dolutegravir-based ART by October 2021. Rates of viral suppression to &lt;50 copies/mL were 84.8%, 93.9%, and 95.4% before, 12 months after, and 24 months after transition, respectively. Sex, age, pretransition viral load, and treatment backbone correlated with 24-month viremia.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, focusing instead on the clinical outcomes of treatment transition and viral load measurements."
37358226,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,,,"The paper does not report any sequencing of HIV genes, as its focus is on the clinical outcomes of treatment transition to dolutegravir-based ART rather than on genetic sequencing data. Therefore, there are no gene sequences mentioned. Hence the answer is empty. But we can also answer as NRTI, NNRTI, integrase. The study Mentions the transition to a 3 drug combination containing an integrase inhibtor, this means that integrase gene and RT gene of NRTI, NNRTI were sequenced, otherwise it wont be able to see if they developed resistance to the drugs."
37358226,10,What method was used for sequencing?,,,"The paper does not mention any sequencing methods, as it does not report on any sequencing of HIV from patient samples and focuses instead on clinical outcomes. Therefore, no sequencing methods are described. Hence the answer is empty. But we can also answer as Sanger sequencing,  The study describes the use of Sanger sequencing for genotypic drug resistance testing specifically for protease, RT, and integrase genes."
37358226,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,,,"The paper does not mention any type of samples that were sequenced, focusing instead on the clinical outcomes of transitioning to dolutegravir-based ART without providing details on any biological samples. Hence the answer is empty. But we can also answer as Plasma, The study focuses on the clinical outcomes of transitioning to dolutegravir-based ART in plasma samples, but does not report on any genotypic resistance testing or the specific plasmid samples sequenced."
37358226,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"In the Viral Load Cohort North-East Lesotho (VICONEL) human immunodeficiency virus cohort, 14 242 adults had transitioned from efavirenz- or nevirapine-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) to dolutegravir-based ART by October 2021. Rates of viral suppression to &lt;50 copies/mL were 84.8%, 93.9%, and 95.4% before, 12 months after, and 24 months after transition, respectively. Sex, age, pretransition viral load, and treatment backbone correlated with 24-month viremia.","The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure, as its focus is on the outcomes of treatment transition and viral load suppression."
37358226,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Data were derived from the Viral Load Cohort North-East Lesotho (VICONEL), a prospective open cohort including all people receiving HIV viral load (VL) testing in Butha-Buthe or Mokhotlong district in Lesotho. Major randomized controlled trials had established the safety and efficacy of dolutegravir, and real-world data were needed on programmatic outcomes of DTG-based ART.","The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial, as it focuses on real-world outcomes from a cohort study."
37358226,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,.,"Two-Year Outcomes of Treatment-Experienced Adults After Programmatic Transitioning to Dolutegravir: Longitudinal Data From the VICONEL Human Immunodeficiency Virus Cohort in Lesotho. This study includes all VICONEL participants who transitioned from ART containing an NNRTI (efavirenz or nevirapine) alongside 2 nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) (abacavir [ABC], zidovudine [ZDV], or TDF together with 3TC) to ART containing DTG plus 2 NRTIs before 30 October 2021 (ie, ≥18 months before data closure), were ≥18 years old at transition, and had at least 1 recorded VL both before and after transitioning.","The paper mentions that the inclusion criteria required participants to have transitioned from ART containing an NNRTI alongside 2 nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) to ART containing DTG plus 2 NRTIs, indicating that sequences were from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37358226,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI","This study includes all VICONEL participants who transitioned from ART containing an NNRTI (efavirenz or nevirapine) alongside 2 nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) (abacavir [ABC], zidovudine [ZDV], or TDF together with 3TC) to ART containing DTG plus 2 NRTIs before 30 October 2021 (ie, ≥18 months before data closure), were ≥18 years old at transition, and had at least 1 recorded VL both before and after transitioning.","The paper specifies that participants transitioned from ART containing an NNRTI (efavirenz or nevirapine) alongside 2 NRTIs (abacavir, zidovudine, or tenofovir together with lamivudine) to ART containing DTG plus 2 NRTIs, indicating that NRTI and NNRTI classes were involved."
37358226,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"ABC, 3TC, TDF, ZDV, EFV, NVP, DTG","This study includes all VICONEL participants who transitioned from ART containing an NNRTI (efavirenz or nevirapine) alongside 2 nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) (abacavir [ABC], zidovudine [ZDV], or TDF together with 3TC) to ART containing DTG plus 2 NRTIs before 30 October 2021 (ie, ≥18 months before data closure), were ≥18 years old at transition, and had at least 1 recorded VL both before and after transitioning. Among 14 242 eligible participants (Supplementary Figure 1), the majority were female (9311 [65.4%]) and taking efavirenz-containing ART (13 049 [91.6%]) before and TDF-3TC-DTG (13 505 [94.8%]) after transitioning.","The paper lists the specific drugs involved in the treatment transition, including efavirenz (EFV), nevirapine (NVP), abacavir (ABC), tenofovir (TDF), zidovudine (ZDV), lamivudine (3TC), and dolutegravir (DTG)."
37376649,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Retrospective baseline proviral DNA genotypes were performed for 734 participants. Archived International AIDS Society--USA major nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, protease inhibitor, and integrase strand transfer inhibitor resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) were observed in 42 (7%), 90 (14%), 42 (7%), and 11 (2%) participants, respectively, across both groups; 469 (74%) had no major RAMs at baseline.","The paper mentions that retrospective baseline proviral DNA genotypes were performed for 734 participants, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
37376649,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The TANGO study (ClinicalTrials.gov, NCT03446573) demonstrated that switching to dolutegravir/lamivudine (DTG/3TC) was non-inferior to continuing tenofovir alafenamide-based regimens (TBR) through week 144. Retrospective baseline proviral DNA genotypes were performed for 734 participants (post-hoc analysis) to assess the impact of archived, pre-existing drug resistance on 144-week virologic outcomes by last on-treatment viral load (VL) and Snapshot. A total of 320 (86%) participants on DTG/3TC and 318 (85%) on TBR had both proviral genotype data and ≥1 on-treatment post-baseline VL results and were defined as the proviral DNA resistance analysis population. Archived International AIDS Society--USA major nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, protease inhibitor, and integrase strand transfer inhibitor resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) were observed in 42 (7%), 90 (14%), 42 (7%), and 11 (2%) participants, respectively, across both groups; 469 (74%) had no major RAMs at baseline. M184V/I (1%), K65N/R (<1%), and thymidine analogue mutations (2%) were infrequent. Through week 144, >99% of participants on DTG/3TC and 99% on TBR were virologically suppressed (last on-treatment VL <50 copies/mL) regardless of the presence of major RAMs. Results from the sensitivity analysis by Snapshot were consistent with the last available on-treatment VL. In TANGO, archived, pre-existing major RAMs did not impact virologic outcomes through week 144.",The paper focuses on the virologic outcomes and resistance-associated mutations in HIV-1 but does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data or experiments.
37376649,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The TANGO study (ClinicalTrials.gov, NCT03446573) demonstrated that switching to dolutegravir/lamivudine (DTG/3TC) was non-inferior to continuing tenofovir alafenamide-based regimens (TBR) through week 144. Retrospective baseline proviral DNA genotypes were performed for 734 participants (post-hoc analysis) to assess the impact of archived, pre-existing drug resistance on 144-week virologic outcomes by last on-treatment viral load (VL) and Snapshot. A total of 320 (86%) participants on DTG/3TC and 318 (85%) on TBR had both proviral genotype data and ≥1 on-treatment post-baseline VL results and were defined as the proviral DNA resistance analysis population. Archived International AIDS Society--USA major nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, protease inhibitor, and integrase strand transfer inhibitor resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) were observed in 42 (7%), 90 (14%), 42 (7%), and 11 (2%) participants, respectively, across both groups; 469 (74%) had no major RAMs at baseline. M184V/I (1%), K65N/R (<1%), and thymidine analogue mutations (2%) were infrequent. Through week 144, >99% of participants on DTG/3TC and 99% on TBR were virologically suppressed (last on-treatment VL <50 copies/mL) regardless of the presence of major RAMs. Results from the sensitivity analysis by Snapshot were consistent with the last available on-treatment VL. In TANGO, archived, pre-existing major RAMs did not impact virologic outcomes through week 144.",The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers or publicly available sequences. It focuses on the genotypic analysis within the context of the study without indicating that the sequences are available publicly.
37376649,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not applicable,"The TANGO study (ClinicalTrials.gov, NCT03446573) demonstrated that switching to dolutegravir/lamivudine (DTG/3TC) was non-inferior to continuing tenofovir alafenamide-based regimens (TBR) through week 144. Retrospective baseline proviral DNA genotypes were performed for 734 participants (post-hoc analysis) to assess the impact of archived, pre-existing drug resistance on 144-week virologic outcomes by last on-treatment viral load (VL) and Snapshot. A total of 320 (86%) participants on DTG/3TC and 318 (85%) on TBR had both proviral genotype data and ≥1 on-treatment post-baseline VL results and were defined as the proviral DNA resistance analysis population. Archived International AIDS Society--USA major nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, protease inhibitor, and integrase strand transfer inhibitor resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) were observed in 42 (7%), 90 (14%), 42 (7%), and 11 (2%) participants, respectively, across both groups; 469 (74%) had no major RAMs at baseline. M184V/I (1%), K65N/R (<1%), and thymidine analogue mutations (2%) were infrequent. Through week 144, >99% of participants on DTG/3TC and 99% on TBR were virologically suppressed (last on-treatment VL <50 copies/mL) regardless of the presence of major RAMs. Results from the sensitivity analysis by Snapshot were consistent with the last available on-treatment VL. In TANGO, archived, pre-existing major RAMs did not impact virologic outcomes through week 144.",The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on the genotypic and virologic outcomes without mentioning accession numbers.
37376649,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,734,Retrospective baseline proviral DNA genotypes were performed for 734 participants. Retrospective proviral DNA testing was performed on available baseline samples for 734/741 (99%) participants from the exposed population.,"The paper mentions that retrospective baseline proviral DNA genotypes were performed for 734 participants, indicating that samples were obtained from 734 individuals for HIV sequencing."
37376649,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not specified,This study was conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki and national and institutional standards. Approval was obtained from ethics committees at each investigational site. Written informed consent was obtained from all participants before study initiation.,The paper does not specify the countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained. It mentions compliance with ethical standards but does not provide specific geographical locations.
37376649,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not specified,"Detailed methodology and study design for the TANGO study (ClinicalTrials.gov, NCT03446573) have been previously published [2] and are briefly described below. Participants were excluded if they had any evidence of IAS-USA major NRTI or INSTI RAMs in any historical genotype assay results, if available; any plasma HIV-1 RNA measurement of ≥50 copies/mL within 6 months of screening; a total of ≥2 measurements of ≥50 copies/mL or any measurement of >200 copies/mL within 6 and 12 months of screening; or a prior regimen switch for virologic failure (HIV-1 RNA ≥400 copies/mL). HIV-1 proviral DNA genotyping was conducted retrospectively with the GenoSure Archive assay (Monogram Biosciences, South San Francisco, CA, USA), which uses next-generation sequencing (NGS) to analyze the HIV-1 polymerase region; a bioinformatic filter is used to remove APOBEC3G/3F-induced G to A hypermutations.",The paper does not mention the specific years from which the sequenced samples were obtained. It provides ethical and methodological details but does not include a timeline of sample collection.
37376649,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"HIV-1 proviral DNA genotyping was conducted retrospectively with the GenoSure Archive assay (Monogram Biosciences, South San Francisco, CA, USA), which uses next-generation sequencing (NGS) to analyze the HIV-1 polymerase region; a bioinformatic filter is used to remove APOBEC3G/3F-induced G to A hypermutations. Although the NGS platform is able to detect variants as low as 1%, resistance substitutions are reported at a mutation frequency cut-off of ≥10% [14] to minimize over-reporting of APOBEC-mediated hypermutations.",The GenoSure Archive assay mentioned in the paper is a next-generation sequencing method that does not require cloning of samples prior to sequencing. The paper does not mention any cloning of samples before sequencing.
37376649,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Pol gene,"HIV-1 proviral DNA genotyping was conducted retrospectively with the GenoSure Archive assay (Monogram Biosciences, South San Francisco, CA, USA), which uses next-generation sequencing (NGS) to analyze the HIV-1 polymerase region. Archived International AIDS Society--USA major nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, protease inhibitor, and integrase strand transfer inhibitor resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) were observed in 42 (7%), 90 (14%), 42 (7%), and 11 (2%) participants, respectively, across both groups; 469 (74%) had no major RAMs at baseline.","The paper mentions that the GenoSure Archive assay analyzes the HIV-1 polymerase region, which includes the reverse transcriptase, protease, and integrase genes. The resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) for these genes are also discussed."
37376649,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Next-generation sequencing (NGS),"HIV-1 proviral DNA genotyping was conducted retrospectively with the GenoSure Archive assay (Monogram Biosciences, South San Francisco, CA, USA), which uses next-generation sequencing (NGS) to analyze the HIV-1 polymerase region.",The paper specifies that the GenoSure Archive assay uses next-generation sequencing (NGS) to analyze the HIV-1 polymerase region.
37376649,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Whole blood samples,"Participants' baseline whole blood samples were used for the GenoSure Archive assay. Virologic outcomes based on IAS-USA major NRTI, NNRTI, PI, and INSTI RAMs were determined by the last available on-treatment of HIV-1 RNA through week 144 in the proviral DNA resistance analysis population (PRAP) to assess on-treatment virologic response.",The paper mentions that baseline whole blood samples were used for the GenoSure Archive assay.
37376649,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"A total of 743 participants were randomized and 741 received at least one dose of the study treatments (exposed population). Of those treated, 464 (63%) participants had historical genotypes with 221/369 (60%) in the DTG/3TC and 243/372 (65%) in the TBR group. Retrospective proviral DNA testing was performed on available baseline samples for 734/741 (99%) participants from the exposed population, with 330/366 (90%) in the DTG/3TC group and 324/368 (88%) in the TBR group having genotypic results reported.","The paper discusses the exclusion criteria related to virological failure. It mentions that some participants had historical genotypic reports and that proviral DNA testing was performed on available baseline samples, which implies that sequences were obtained from individuals who had experienced virological failure."
37376649,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"The TANGO study (ClinicalTrials.gov, NCT03446573) demonstrated that switching to dolutegravir/lamivudine (DTG/3TC) was non-inferior to continuing tenofovir alafenamide-based regimens (TBR) through week 144. Participants were excluded if they had any evidence of IAS-USA major NRTI or INSTI RAMs in any historical genotype assay results, if available; any plasma HIV-1 RNA measurement of ≥50 copies/mL within 6 months of screening; a total of ≥2 measurements of ≥50 copies/mL or any measurement of >200 copies/mL within 6 and 12 months of screening; or a prior regimen switch for virologic failure (HIV-1 RNA ≥400 copies/mL).","The paper mentions the TANGO study is a clinical trial (ClinicalTrials.gov, NCT03446573). The study participants were randomized and received at least one dose of the study treatments, which means they were part of a clinical trial."
37376649,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"A total of 743 participants were randomized and 741 received at least one dose of the study treatments (exposed population). Of those treated, 464 (63%) participants had historical genotypes with 221/369 (60%) in the DTG/3TC and 243/372 (65%) in the TBR group. Comparisons of virologic response by baseline RAM status showed participants with archived INSTI mutations had a higher rate of virologic suppression through week 144 in the DTG/3TC group. In the TBR group, between participants with or without archived INSTI mutations, virologic suppression was similar through week 144, whereas participants switching to TDF/FTC/RPV who had M184V/I suppressed less well than those without, having a high rate of CVW through week 144 with symptoms of viral rebound. High suppression rates were also observed at week 144 across both treatment groups, irrespective of the presence of major RAMs, using the FDA Snapshot endpoint in the prsap.","The paper mentions that participants had historical genotypic reports and had received at least one dose of the study treatments, indicating that they had previously received ARV drugs."
37376649,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI","Participants were excluded if they had any evidence of IAS-USA major NRTI or INSTI RAMs in any historical genotype assay results, if available; any plasma HIV-1 RNA measurement of ≥50 copies/mL within 6 months of screening; a total of ≥2 measurements of ≥50 copies/mL or any measurement of >200 copies/mL within 6 and 12 months of screening; or a prior regimen switch for virologic failure (HIV-1 RNA ≥400 copies/mL).","The paper mentions the  participants had evidence of IAS-USA major NRTI or INSTI RAMs, which indicates INSTI had been used and the classes of drugs received includes NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI."
37376649,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not specified,"Participants were excluded if they had any evidence of IAS-USA major NRTI or INSTI RAMs in any historical genotype assay results, if available; any plasma HIV-1 RNA measurement of ≥50 copies/mL within 6 months of screening; a total of ≥2 measurements of ≥50 copies/mL or any measurement of >200 copies/mL within 6 and 12 months of screening; or a prior regimen switch for virologic failure (HIV-1 RNA ≥400 copies/mL).","The paper mentions the participants had evidence of IAS-USA major NRTI or INSTI RAMs, it takes a post-hoc analysis approach and doesn't mention the drugs received by individuals."
37381002,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Blood samples of EDTA anticoagulants from 398 patients with failed antiviral treatment were the sources of HIV-1 sequences. The partial *pol* gene fragment of HIV-1 virus was amplified by using in-house polymerase chain reaction (PCR) method as described previously. Ten near full-length genomes (NFLG) of HIV-1 viruses were identified, including six recombinants of CRF 01_AE and subtype B, two recombinants of CRF 01_AE, subtype B and subtype C sequences, one recombinant of CRF 01_AE and subtype C and one recombinant of CRF 01_AE, subtype A1 and subtype C.",The paper mentions that HIV-1 sequences were obtained from blood samples of 398 patients with failed antiviral treatment. This indicates that the study did report HIV sequences from patient samples.
37381002,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database system was used to analyze resistance-related mutations and resistance levels, but it does not specify in vitro drug susceptibility testing. The paper discusses drug-resistant mutations and their prevalence but does not provide details on in vitro testing of drug susceptibility against HIV-1 strains isolated from patients.",The paper focuses on identifying drug resistance mutations in HIV-1 sequences obtained from patient samples but does not mention conducting in vitro drug susceptibility assays. The absence of such data suggests that the study did not include in vitro testing.
37381002,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The partial *pol* gene fragment of HIV-1 virus was amplified by using in-house polymerase chain reaction (PCR) method as described previously [12, 13]. SuperScript™ III One-Step RT-PCR System with Platinum™ Taq DNA Polymerase (Invitrogen, USA) was used for reverse transcription reaction and first round PCR. The second round of PCR amplification was performed using Premix Ex Taq™ Version 2.0 (Takara, Japan) with a total volume of 50 µl. The length of the amplified product was 1.3 kb (HXB2:2147 to 3462), including the full length of the protease (PR) gene (1--99 codon) and the first 300 amino acids (1--300 codon) of the reverse transcriptase (RT) gene. The sequences of *pol* gene fragments were obtained by Sanger sequencing method.",The paper does not mention depositing the sequences in a public database such as GenBank. The absence of this information indicates that the sequences were not made publicly available.
37381002,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No accession number provided,No evidence,The paper does not mention depositing the sequences in a public database such as GenBank. The absence of this information indicates that no GenBank accession numbers were provided.
37381002,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,398,"Among these ART failure patients, a total of 398 viral pol gene sequences were successfully sequenced. HIV-1 genotypes were determined by the REGA HIV subtyping tool (version 3.46, https://www.genomedetective.com/app/typingtool/hiv).","The paper states that viral pol gene sequences were obtained from 398 patients with failed antiviral treatment, which directly answers the question."
37381002,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"This retrospective study aimed to characterize the distribution of HIV-1 genotypes and the prevalence of drug resistance mutations in people with antiretroviral treatment (ART) failure in Suzhou City, China. The Los Alamos National Laboratory HIV Sequence Database ( http://www.hiv.lanl.gov/content/index ) and REGA HIV subtyping tool were used for determining genotypes of HIV-1 viruses in Suzhou City.","The study was conducted in Suzhou City, China, and the samples were obtained from patients in this region."
37381002,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2017 to 2020,"From 2017 to 2020, 450 people living with HIV who have received antiviral therapy for more than 1 year were confirmed to be ineffective (viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml) in Suzhou city. Among these ART failure patients, a total of 398 viral pol gene sequences were successfully sequenced.","The paper mentions that the samples were collected from 2017 to 2020, providing a clear time frame for when the sequenced samples were obtained."
37381002,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Viral RNA was extracted from 200 µl plasma sample following the instructions of the MagNA Pure Compact Nucleic Acid Isolation Kit I (Roche, Switzerland). The partial pol gene fragment of HIV-1 virus was amplified by using in-house polymerase chain reaction (PCR) method as described previously We mixed all amplified products of the same sample in one tube. The combined PCR products were purified by using AMPure XP purification beads. Nextera DNA Sample Prep Kit (Illumina, USA) was used for sequencing libraries prepared. Next generation sequencing (NGS) was performed on iseq platform by using i1 V2 300 cycles reagent kit (Illumina, USA).",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. The methods described focus on PCR amplification and sequencing directly from plasma samples.
37381002,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Pol gene,Pol gene of HIV-1 viruses in blood samples of EDTA anticoagulants from 398 patients with failed antiviral treatment was successfully amplified by using an in-house assay. Near full-length genomes (NFLG) of HIV-1 viruses were obtained by next generation sequencing method.,"The paper mentions sequencing the near full-length genomes of HIV-1 , with specific mention of the protease and reverse transcriptase genes within the pol gene. This indicates that these genes were sequenced."
37381002,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing and Next Generation Sequencing,"HIV-1 genotypes were determined by the REGA HIV subtyping tool. The sequences of *pol* gene fragments were obtained by Sanger sequencing method. Nextera DNA Sample Prep Kit (Illumina, USA) was used for sequencing libraries prepared. Next generation sequencing (NGS) was performed on iseq platform by using i1 V2 300 cycles reagent kit (Illumina, USA)","The paper describes multiple sequencing methods. Sanger sequencing was employed for the *pol gene fragments, while next generation sequencing (NGS) was utilized for obtaining near full-length genomes of HIV-1. This variety of methods indicates that both sequencing techniques were used in the study."
37381002,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma and blood samples,"Viral RNA was extracted from 200 µl plasma sample following the instructions of the MagNA Pure Compact Nucleic Acid Isolation Kit I (Roche, Switzerland). Pol gene of HIV-1 viruses in blood samples of EDTA anticoagulants from 398 patients with failed antiviral treatment was successfully amplified by using an in-house assay.","The paper mentions sequencing *pol* gene fragments of HIV-1 viruses in blood samples from 398 patients, specifically mentioning the extraction of viral RNA from plasma samples."
37381002,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"From 2017 to 2020, 450 people living with HIV who have received antiviral therapy for more than 1 year were confirmed to be ineffective (viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml) in Suzhou city. Among these ART failure patients, a total of 398 viral pol gene sequences were successfully sequenced. Drug resistance mutations were analyzed by using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database system. HIV-1 genotypes were determined by the REGA HIV subtyping tool. Near full-length genomes (NFLG) of HIV-1 viruses were obtained by next generation sequencing method.  Sequences analysis of the pol gene revealed that CRF 01_AE (57.29%, 228/398) was the dominant subtype circulating in Suzhou City, followed by CRF 07_BC (17.34%, 69/398), subtype B (7.54%, 30/398), CRF 08_BC (6.53%, 26/398), CRF 67_01B (3.02%, 12/398) and CRF55_01B (2.51%, 10/398). Among those who failed in antiviral treatment, the proportion of HIV-1 viral load in the range of 5000--100,000 copies/ml was the largest, accounting for 62.56% (249/398).","The paper states that the study focused on patients with antiretroviral treatment (ART) failure, defined as ineffective treatment with a viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml, indicating that virological failure was a key criterion for the study."
37381002,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This retrospective study aimed to characterize the distribution of HIV-1 genotypes and the prevalence of drug resistance mutations in people with antiretroviral treatment (ART) failure in Suzhou City, China. From 2017 to 2020, 450 people living with HIV who have received antiviral therapy for more than 1 year were confirmed to be ineffective (viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml) in Suzhou city.  Among these ART failure patients, a total of 398 viral pol gene sequences were successfully sequenced","The paper describes the study as a retrospective examination of  patients with ART failure, and it does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial."
37381002,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"From 2017 to 2020, 450 people living with HIV who have received antiviral therapy for more than 1 year were confirmed to be ineffective (viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml) in Suzhou city. Among these ART failure patients, a total of 398 viral pol gene sequences were successfully sequenced. Drug resistance mutations were analyzed by using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database system. HIV-1 genotypes were determined by the REGA HIV subtyping tool. Near full-length genomes (NFLG) of HIV-1 viruses were obtained by next generation sequencing method.  Sequences analysis of the pol gene revealed that CRF 01_AE (57.29%, 228/398) was the dominant subtype circulating in Suzhou City, followed by CRF 07_BC (17.34%, 69/398), subtype B (7.54%, 30/398), CRF 08_BC (6.53%, 26/398), CRF 67_01B (3.02%, 12/398) and CRF55_01B (2.51%, 10/398). Among those who failed in antiviral treatment, the proportion of HIV-1 viral load in the range of 5000--100,000 copies/ml was the largest, accounting for 62.56% (249/398).","The paper mentions that the study included patients who had received antiviral therapy for more than 1 year and were confirmed to be ineffective, indicating that these individuals had previously received ARV drugs."
37381002,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs","The high-level of NNRTIs associated resistance was accounting for 20.10% (80/398), 51.26% (204/398), 56.28% (224/398), 5.28% (21/398), and 21.86% (87/398) for DOR, EFV, NVP, ETR, and RPV, respectively. The resistant frequency to ABC, AZT, D4T, DDI, FTC, 3TC and TDF were 45.48% (181/398), 15.08% (60/398), 31.66% (126/398), 45.98% (183/398), 45.23% (180/398), 45.23% (180/398) and 28.14% (112/398), respectively. we analyzed the effects of mutations on drug resistance based on Stanford University HIV drug resistance database. No other HIV strains with high-level resistance to PIs were detected except 2020-SZ-83050 strain. Three samples (2017-SZ-08303, 2018-SZ-08066 and 2020-XC-00107) with M46I/L mutation were resistant to ATV, FPV, IDV, LPV, NFV and SQV at a potential low level or intermediate. Besides, six samples were simultaneously resistant to FPV, NFV and TPV at a potential low level caused by L33F mutation, and one sample with L10LF mutation was resistant to NFV and FPV at low level. Resistance levels of different drugs among ART failure individuals were shown in Fig. 2.","The paper mentions resistance and effects of mutations on NRTIs, NRNTIs, and PIs, which are all drug classes received before sequencing."
37381002,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"abacavir (ABC), zidovudine (AZT), stavudine (D4T), didanosine (DDI), emtricitabine (FTC), lamivudine (3TC), tenofovir (TDF), dolutegravir (DTG), efavirenz (EFV), nevirapine (NVP), etravirine (ETR), ritonavir (RTV), atazanavir (ATV), fosamprenavir (FPV), indinavir (IDV), darunavir (DRV), saquinavir (SQV), lopinavir (LPV), tipranavir (TPV)","The high-level of NNRTIs associated resistance was accounting for 20.10% (80/398), 51.26% (204/398), 56.28% (224/398), 5.28% (21/398), and 21.86% (87/398) for DOR, EFV, NVP, ETR, and RPV, respectively. The resistant frequency to ABC, AZT, D4T, DDI, FTC, 3TC and TDF were 45.48% (181/398), 15.08% (60/398), 31.66% (126/398), 45.98% (183/398), 45.23% (180/398), 45.23% (180/398) and 28.14% (112/398), respectively. we analyzed the effects of mutations on drug resistance based on Stanford University HIV drug resistance database. No other HIV strains with high-level resistance to PIs were detected except 2020-SZ-83050 strain. Three samples (2017-SZ-08303, 2018-SZ-08066 and 2020-XC-00107) with M46I/L mutation were resistant to ATV, FPV, IDV, LPV, NFV and SQV at a potential low level or intermediate. Besides, six samples were simultaneously resistant to FPV, NFV and TPV at a potential low level caused by L33F mutation, and one sample with L10LF mutation was resistant to NFV and FPV at low level. Resistance levels of different drugs among ART failure individuals were shown in Fig. 2.","The paper mentions several drugs, including NRTIs (e.g., ABC, AZT, D4T, DDI, FTC, 3TC, TDF), NNRTIs (e.g., DTG, EFV, NVP, ETR), and PIs (e.g., ATV, FPV, IDV, DRV, SQV, LPV, TPV), that were received by individuals before sample sequencing."
37439411,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We included 5982 naïves and 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) cohort. We retrieved HIV-1 RNA protease/reverse transcriptase sequences, demographic and clinical data of adult PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) database, a public observational database containing data from more than 100 Italian centres for the study of resistance mutations to antiviral drugs for HIV, hepatitis B and hepatitis C virus infections. We retrieved 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH (67.6% from males and 64.4% from Caucasians) and the median age was 43 years (IQR: 37--49).","The paper mentions that 5982 naïve and 28,505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH were included from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) cohort. This indicates that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples, specifically from people living with HIV (PLWH) in Italy. The study focuses on the drug resistance mutations in these samples, confirming that the paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples."
37439411,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Abstract: The rise of HIV-1 drug resistance to nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) threatens the long-term success of NNRTI-based therapies. Our study aims to describe the circulation of major resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) for NNRTIs in people living with HIV (PLWH) in Italy from 2000 to 2020. We included 5982 naïves and 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) cohort. Transmitted drug resistance (TDR) was found in 12.5% and declined from 17.3% in 2000--2003 to 10.9% in 2016--2020 ( *p* = 0.003). Predictors of TDR were viral subtype B [vs. non-B, adjusted odds ratio (aOR) = 1.94, *p* < 0.001], zenith viral load (VL) (per 1 log10 higher, aOR = 0.86, *p* = 0.013), nadir CD4 cell count (per 100 cells/μL increase aOR = 0.95, *p* = 0.013). At least one RAM for NNRTIs among treatment experienced PLWH was detected in 33.2% and pre-treatment drug resistance (PDR) declined from 43.4% in 2000--2003 to 20.9% in 2016--2020 ( *p* < 0.001).","The paper focuses on the genotypic susceptibility score (GSS) for NNRTIs and describes the prevalence of resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) among treatment-experienced and naïve individuals. However, it does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data. The focus is on genotypic data and epidemiological trends rather than on laboratory-based susceptibility assays."
37439411,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"We retrieved HIV-1 RNA protease/reverse transcriptase sequences, demographic and clinical data of adult PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) database, a public observational database containing data from more than 100 Italian centres for the study of resistance mutations to antiviral drugs for HIV, hepatitis B and hepatitis C virus infections. Our study aims to describe the circulation of major resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) for NNRTIs in people living with HIV (PLWH) in Italy from 2000 to 2020.",The paper mentions that the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) database is a public observational database containing data from more than 100 Italian centres. This implies that the sequences and data retrieved from this database are publicly available.
37439411,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on the resistance-associated mutations and genotypic data but does not mention specific accession numbers.
37439411,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,9387,We retrieved 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH (67.6% from males and 64.4% from Caucasians) and the median age was 43 years (IQR: 37--49).,"The paper states that 28,505 genotypes were retrieved from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH. This indicates that samples were obtained from 9387 individuals for HIV sequencing."
37439411,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Italy,"We included 5982 naïves and 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) cohort. We retrieved HIV-1 RNA protease/reverse transcriptase sequences, demographic and clinical data of adult PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) database, a public observational database containing data from more than 100 Italian centres for the study of resistance mutations to antiviral drugs for HIV, hepatitis B and hepatitis C virus infections. The rise of HIV-1 drug resistance to nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) threatens the long-term success of NNRTI-based therapies. Our study aims to describe the circulation of major resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) for NNRTIs in people living with HIV (PLWH) in Italy from 2000 to 2020.","The paper specifies that the study was conducted on individuals from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) database, which contains data from more than 100 Italian centres. The focus is on the population living with HIV in Italy."
37439411,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2000 to 2020,Our study aims to describe the circulation of major resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) for NNRTIs in people living with HIV (PLWH) in Italy from 2000 to 2020. Transmitted drug resistance (TDR) was found in 12.5% and declined from 17.3% in 2000--2003 to 10.9% in 2016--2020 ( *p* = 0.003). We included 5982 naïves and 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) cohort.,"The paper mentions the study aims to describe the circulation of major resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) for NNRTIs in people living with HIV (PLWH) in Italy from 2000 to 2020. The timeline of resistance mutations and genotypic data spans this period, indicating when the samples were obtained."
37439411,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,The HIV-1 genotype was considered based on all the tests available (cumulative genotype).,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It refers to the use of cumulative genotypes based on all tests available.
37439411,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Protease/reverse transcriptase,"We retrieved HIV-1 RNA protease/reverse transcriptase sequences, demographic and clinical data of adult PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) database, a public observational database containing data from more than 100 Italian centres for the study of resistance mutations to antiviral drugs for HIV, hepatitis B and hepatitis C virus infections.","The paper specifically mentions that HIV-1 RNA protease/reverse transcriptase sequences were retrieved and analyzed, indicating these are the genes that were sequenced."
37439411,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not reported,,The paper does not specify the sequencing method used to obtain the HIV sequences. It focuses on the analysis of genotypic data and resistance mutations but does not mention the specific sequencing technology or method employed.
37439411,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Clinical samples,"We included 5982 naïves and 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) cohort. We retrieved HIV-1 RNA protease/reverse transcriptase sequences, demographic and clinical data of adult PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) database, a public observational database containing data from more than 100 Italian centres for the study of resistance mutations to antiviral drugs for HIV, hepatitis B and hepatitis C virus infections. We included 5982 naïves: 73.9% were males, median age was 39 years [interquartile range (IQR): 32--47] and 67.9% were Caucasian. The main route of transmission was sexual intercourse (54.6%). The median time since HIV diagnosis was 3.14 weeks (0.86--41.93), nadir CD4 count was 326 cells/μL (159--495) and zenith HIV-1 RNA (viral load, VL) was 4.8 log10 copies/mL (4.3--5.4); 72.3% carried viral subtype B (Table 1 ). Transmitted drug resistance was found in 12.5% of the enrolled subjects and declined from 17.3% in 2000--2003 to 10.9% in 2016--2020 ( *p* = 0.003) (Figure 1 ).","The paper mentions that demographic and clinical data were retrieved from the ARCA database. Coupled with the paper's focus on virological outcomes and drug resistance, it suggests that clinical samples (such as plasma or serum) from patients were sequenced."
37439411,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"We retrieved 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH (67.6% from males and 64.4% from Caucasians) and the median age was 43 years (IQR: 37--49). Sexual route accounted for 39.9% of the modalities of transmission. Median time since HIV diagnosis was 136.7 months (65.1--213.1), nadir CD4 count was 154 cells/μL (52--282), and CD4 count at genotype was 349.5 cells/μL (192--543). Median zenith VL was 5.13 log10 copies/mL (4.50--5.62) and VL at genotype was 3.6 log10 copies/mL (4.5--5.6); 80.8% carried viral subtype B (Table 1 ).","The paper suggests that 28,505 genotypes were collected from 9387 treatment-experienced individuals, Our study aims to describe the circulation of major resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) for NNRTIs in people living with HIV (PLWH) in Italy from 2000 to 2020. We included 5982 naïves and 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) cohort. We included 5982 naïves: 73.9% were males, median age was 39 years [interquartile range (IQR): 32--47] and 67.9% were Caucasian. The main route of transmission was sexual intercourse (54.6%). The median time since HIV diagnosis was 3.14 weeks (0.86--41.93), nadir CD4 count was 326 cells/μL (159--495) and zenith HIV-1 RNA (viral load, VL) was 4.8 log10 copies/mL (4.3--5.4); 72.3% carried viral subtype B (Table 1 ). Transmitted drug resistance was found in 12.5% of the enrolled subjects and declined from 17.3% in 2000--2003 to 10.9% in 2016--2020 ( *p* = 0.003) (Figure 1 ). We retrieved HIV-1 RNA protease/reverse transcriptase sequences, demographic and clinical data of adult PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) database, a public observational database containing data from more than 100 Italian centres for the study of resistance mutations to antiviral drugs for HIV, hepatitis B and hepatitis C virus infections. Median zenith VL was 5.13 log10 copies/mL (4.50--5.62) and VL at genotype was 3.6 log10 copies/mL (4.5--5.6); 80.8% carried viral subtype B (Table 1 ). We included 5982 naïves: 73.9% were males, median age was 39 years [interquartile range (IQR): 32--47] and 67.9% were Caucasian. The main route of transmission was sexual intercourse (54.6%). The median time since HIV diagnosis was 3.14 weeks (0.86--41.93), nadir CD4 count was 326 cells/μL (159--495) and zenith HIV-1 RNA (viral load, VL) was 4.8 log10 copies/mL (4.3--5.4); 72.3% carried viral subtype B (Table 1). Transmitted drug resistance was found in 12.5% of the enrolled subjects and declined from 17.3% in 2000--2003 to 10.9% in 2016--2020 ( *p* = 0.003) (Figure 1). At least one RAM for NNRTIs among treatment experienced PLWH was detected in 33.2% and pre-treatment drug resistance (PDR) declined from 43.4% in 2000--2003 to 20.9% in 2016--2020 ( *p* < 0.001). Predictors of PDR were sexual transmission route (vs. others, aOR=0.779, 95% CI: 0.72--0.85, *p* < 0.001), time since HIV diagnosis (per 1 month longer, aOR=1.002, 95% CI: 1.001--1.002, *p* < 0.001), viral subtype B (vs. non-B, aOR=1.37, 95% CI: 1.19--1.58, *p* < 0.001), VL (per 1 log10 higher, aOR=1.12, 95% CI: 1.08--1.16, *p* < 0.001), nadir CD4 count (per 100 cells/μL increase, aOR=0.91, 95% CI: 0.88--0.94, *p* < 0.001), previous exposure to any NNRTIs (aOR=2.31, 95% CI: 2.14--2.49, *p* < 0.001) and a more recent calendar year sequence (any span time >2008 vs. 2000–2003, any aOR<1, *p* < 0.001) (Table 1).  The genotypic susceptibility score (GSS) for a single compound was obtained using the Stanford HIVdb genotypic resistance interpretation system: a GSS value of 0 corresponds to high-level resistance, 0.25 to intermediate resistance, 0.50 to low-level resistance, 0.75 to potential low-level resistance, and 1 to full susceptibility.  The cumulative GSS (cGSS) of the single compound was calculated for the cumulative genotype using the Stanford algorithm interpretation as the minimum value [9]. V L at genotype was 3.6log10 copies/mL (4.5–5.6);80.8% carried viral subtype B(table 1). We retrieved HIV–1 RNA protease/reverse transcriptase sequences, demographic and clinical data of adult PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) database, a public observational database containing data from more than 100 Italian centres for the study of resistance mutations to antiviral drugs for HIV, hepatitis B and hepatitis C virus infections [5].  HDIRESMS( Dashboard) indicates current standard transmission of replicons. We included 5982 naïves and 28505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) cohort. Transmitted drug resistance (TDR) defines the presence of RAMs transmitted at the moment of infection, considering genotypes of naïve PLWH [6]. Pre-treatment drug resistance (PDR) identifies the sum of drug resistance mutations detected in all genotypes of  experienced PLWH with previous ARV drug exposure initiating or reinitiating ART, including both transmitted and acquired RAMs, at the moment of the analysis [6]. This means that especially 28,505 genotypes were collected from 9387 treatment-experienced individuals who had previously received ARV drugs,  acute and accumulated drug resistance mutations, including both transmitted and acquired RAMs, at the moment of analysis  PDR with previous ARV-exposure initiation of re-initiated ART Needing chronic therapeutic drugs for assistance. Which means sequence individual were all previously had received ARV drugs."
37439411,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We included 5982 naïves and 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) cohort. We retrieved HIV-1 RNA protease/reverse transcriptase sequences, demographic and clinical data of adult PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) database, a public observational database containing data from more than 100 Italian centres for the study of resistance mutations to antiviral drugs for HIV, hepatitis B and hepatitis C virus infections.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It focuses on observational data from the ARCA cohort.
37439411,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"We retrieved 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH (67.6% from males and 64.4% from Caucasians) and the median age was 43 years (IQR: 37--49). We included 5982 naïves and 28505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) cohort.  We retrieved HIV–1 RNA protease/reverse transcriptase sequences, demographic and clinical data of adult PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) database, a public observational database containing data from more than 100 Italian centres for the study of resistance mutations to antiviral drugs for HIV, hepatitis B and hepatitis C virus infections [5].  HDIRESMS( Dashboard) indicates current standard transmission of replicons. Transmitted drug resistance (TDR) defines the presence of RAMs transmitted at the moment of infection, considering genotypes of naïve PLWH [6]. Pre-treatment drug resistance (PDR) identifies the sum of drug resistance mutations detected in all genotypes of experienced PLWH with previous ARV drug exposure initiating or reinitiating ART, including both transmitted and acquired RAMs, at the moment of the analysis [6]. We included 5982 naïves: 73.9% were males, median age was 39 years [interquartile range (IQR): 32--47] and 67.9% were Caucasian. The mean route of transmission was sexual intercourse (54.6%). Median time of HIV diagnosis was 3.14 weeks (0.86--41.93), nadir CD4 count was 326 cells/μL (159--495) and zenith HIV–1 RNA (viral load, VL) was 4.8 log10 copies/mL (4.3--5.4); 72.3% on the quantum B (Table 1 ).","The paper states that 28,505 genotypes were retrieved from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH, and the study aims to investigate pre-treatment drug resistance (PDR), which means that sequences were obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37439411,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not reported,,The paper does not specify the drug classes that were received by individuals before sample sequencing. It focuses on the resistance mutations and genotypic data but does not detail the specific drug classes involved.
37439411,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not reported,,The paper does not mention the specific drugs that were received by individuals before sample sequencing. It focuses on the resistance mutations and genotypic data but does not provide detailed information on the drugs administered.
37495103,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"The study cohort (n = 271) included HIV-infected individuals who visited the Novosibirsk, Russia, HIV/AIDS clinic in 2018–2022. HIV-1 pol gene sequences were generated, and the presence of HIV-1 DRM was assessed.","The evidence clearly states that HIV-1 pol gene sequences were generated from the patient samples, indicating that the paper does report HIV sequences from patient samples."
37495103,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,The current report assesses the prevalence of HIV-1 drug resistance mutations (DRMs) among patients who failed first-line antiretroviral therapy (ART) and evaluates the genetic barrier of different regimens. The  study cohort included 271 patients who were on first-line ART and visited Novosibirsk Regional AIDS/HIV Clinical Hospital between March 2018  and August 2022. The age of the patients varied from 21 to 59 years old. All patients had undetectable viral load (VL) levels for 3–6 months while taking ART with further virological failure.,The evidence focuses on genotyping and analysis of drug resistance mutations rather than direct in vitro susceptiblity testing.
37495103,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The current study delivers results on the efficacy of HIV-1 ART and treatment adherence in real-world practice settings. This report suggests that ART regimens with a high genetic barrier to resistance combined with improved treatment adherence may reduce the transmission of HIV-1 resistant variants. HIV-1 RNA was isolated from 250 µL of plasma using DeltaMag HBV/HCV/HIV (Vector-Best, Russia) according to the kit's manual. Reverse transcription and PCR were performed using RealBest Master Mix RT (Vector-Best, Russia) and BioMaster HS-Taq PCR-Color (BioLabMix, Russia), respectively, to amplify the HIV-1 PR-RT fragment (1302 bp). DNA sequencing was performed using an ABI PRISM 3130xl DNA Analyser (Applied Biosystems, USA). HIV-1 sequences were further analysed using Sequencher 4.1 software (GeneCodesCorporation, Ann Arbor, MI). HIV-1 subtypes were identified using maximum-likelihood phylogenetic analysis using MEGA v.6.0.6. HIV-1 study sequences were analysed for the presence of DRM using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database (HIVdb Program). DRMs were classified according to the WHO list of mutations. The genetic barrier to resistance was assessed using the ART regimen and adherence data of the study patients. Statistical analysis was performed using Statistica v.10. The χ2 test was conducted to examine the differences in categorical variables, and the Mann-Whitney U test was used to examine differences in median values of quantitative variables. P-values<0.05 were considered statistically significant. Spearman's correlation was used to compare ranked variables.",The paper does not mention making the sequences publicly available. The methods section discusses the analysis and storage of sequences but does not indicate they were shared publicly.
37495103,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No accession numbers provided,No evidence,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. There is no information regarding where the sequences can be found or if they have been submitted to a public database like GenBank.
37495103,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,271,"The study cohort (n = 271) included HIV-infected individuals who visited the Novosibirsk, Russia, HIV/AIDS clinic in 2018–2022.","The evidence directly states that the study included 271 patients from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing, satisfying the question."
37495103,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Russia,"The study cohort included HIV-infected individuals who visited the Novosibirsk, Russia, HIV/AIDS clinic in 2018–2022.","The evidence specifies that the samples were obtained from individuals at a clinic in Novosibirsk, Russia, clearly answering the question."
37495103,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2018–2022,"The study cohort included HIV-infected individuals who visited the Novosibirsk, Russia, HIV/AIDS clinic in 2018–2022.","The evidence states that the samples were obtained from individuals who visited the clinic between 2018 and 2022, providing a clear range of years."
37495103,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"DNA sequencing was performed using an ABI PRISM 3130xl DNA Analyser (Applied Biosystems, USA). HIV-1 sequences were further analysed using Sequencher 4.1 software (GeneCodesCorporation, Ann Arbor, MI).","The evidence does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, indicating that they were not cloned."
37495103,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Pol gene,"HIV-1 RNA was isolated from 250 µL of plasma using DeltaMag HBV/HCV/HIV (Vector-Best, Russia) according to the kit's manual. Reverse transcription and PCR were performed using RealBest Master Mix RT (Vector-Best, Russia) and BioMaster HS-Taq PCR-Color (BioLabMix, Russia), respectively, to amplify the HIV-1 PR-RT fragment (1302 bp).","The paper mentions the sequencing of the PR-RT fragment, which is a part of the pol gene, clearly identifying the gene sequenced."
37495103,10,What method was used for sequencing?,ABI PRISM 3130xl DNA Analyser,"DNA sequencing was performed using an ABI PRISM 3130xl DNA Analyser (Applied Biosystems, USA).","The evidence specifies the use of the ABI PRISM 3130xl DNA Analyser for sequencing, answering the question about the method."
37495103,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"HIV-1 RNA was isolated from 250 µL of plasma using DeltaMag HBV/HCV/HIV (Vector-Best, Russia) according to the kit's manual.","The evidence indicates that HIV-1 RNA was isolated from plasma, confirming the type of clinical samples sequenced."
37495103,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,All patients had undetectable viral load (VL) levels for 3–6 months while taking ART with further virological failure. HIV-1 DRMs were detected in 54.6% (148/271) of the study patients with virological failure.,"The evidence clearly states that the study included patients who experienced virological failure, confirming that sequences were obtained from these individuals."
37495103,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The study cohort included HIV-infected individuals who visited the Novosibirsk, Russia, HIV/AIDS clinic in 2018–2022. All patients received first-line ART prior to virological failure.","The evidence indicates that the patients were receiving routine care and not part of a clinical trial, answering the question."
37495103,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,All patients had undetectable viral load (VL) levels for 3–6 months while taking ART with further virological failure. The study cohort included 271 patients who were on first-line ART and visited Novosibirsk Regional AIDS/HIV Clinical Hospital between March 2018 and August 2022. All patients received first-line ART prior to virological failure.,"The evidence indicates that all patients were on first-line ART, and had experienced virological failure, confirming that sequences were from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37495103,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI","Most patients (37.6%) received tenofovir (TDF) + lamivudine (3TC) + efavirenz (EFV) as a first-line ART regimen. Out of those patients, 40.2% had a high level of adherence; the remaining 16.7% and 43.1% had medium and low levels of adherence, respectively. Out of the patients (21.7%) on abacavir 600 mg + 3TC + EFV, 45.7% reported high adherence, 16.9% reported medium adherence, and 37.4% reported low adherence. Almost one-fifth (17.7%) of the study patients were on a TDF + 3TC + dolutegravir (DTG) regimen. A ritonavir-boosted lopinavir (LPV/r)-based regimen was received by 15.1% of the study patients; of those, 31.7% reported high adherence, 29.3% reported medium adherence, and 39% reported low adherence. Among the study patients, 5.5% were on TDF + 3TC + elsulfavirine (ESV). Alternative first-line ART regimens (TDF + 3TC + raltegravir [RAL], TDF + 3TC + datunavir(DRV/r), phosphoside [PhAZT] + 3TC + RAL) were prescribed to 2.4% of study patients who reported high-level adherence.","The evidence lists various ART regimen combinations containing drugs from the NRTI, NNRTI and PI classes. TDF and 3TC are NRTIs, EFV is an NNRTI, DTG and RAL are INSTIs, and LPV/r is a protease inhibitor. The drugs mentioned correspond to these classes."
37495103,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"TDF, 3TC, ABC, EFV, DTG, LPV/r, ESV, RAL, DRV","Most patients (37.6%) received tenofovir (TDF) + lamivudine (3TC) + efavirenz (EFV) as a first-line ART regimen. Out of those patients, 40.2% had a high level of adherence; the remaining 16.7% and 43.1% had medium and low levels of adherence, respectively. Out of the patients (21.7%) on abacavir 600 mg + 3TC + EFV, 45.7% reported high adherence, 16.9% reported medium adherence, and 37.4% reported low adherence. Almost one-fifth (17.7%) of the study patients were on a TDF + 3TC + dolutegravir (DTG) regimen. A ritonavir-boosted lopinavir (LPV/r)-based regimen was received by 15.1% of the study patients; of those, 31.7% reported high adherence, 29.3% reported medium adherence, and 39% reported low adherence. Among the study patients, 5.5% were on TDF + 3TC + elsulfavirine (ESV). Alternative first-line ART regimens (TDF + 3TC + raltegravir [RAL], TDF + 3TC + datunavir(DRV/r), phosphoside [PhAZT] + 3TC + RAL) were prescribed to 2.4% of study patients who reported high-level adherence.",The evidence lists various ART regimen combinations containing specific drugs. The drugs mentioned correspond to the ones received by individuals prior to sequencing.
37498738,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We used peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) or co-culture virus from 4 different time points: (i) at ART start (as), obtained as previously described; (ii) 1 year after ART start (1yaas); (iii) ESS week 0 (ESS wk0); and (iv) ESS week 48 (ESS wk48) (Figure 1 A); PCR and NGS for bulk sequences was performed for the 3 time points of the 101 individuals participating in the ESS, resulting in a total of 297 NGS sequences.",The paper mentions that HIV-1 sequences were obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of participants in the study. This indicates that the study involved sequencing HIV from patient samples.
37498738,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,No evidence,"The content of the paper focuses on the sequencing and analysis of HIV-1 sequences from patient samples, but there is no mention of in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data related to the susceptibility of the virus to antiretroviral drugs in a laboratory setting. The methods and results sections primarily discuss the sequencing techniques, the genetic analysis of the samples, and the detection of drug resistance mutations, but do not include any information about systematic in vitro susceptibility experiments or results. This lack of mention of in vitro susceptbility data suggests that it was not a focus of the study, which is confirmed by the absence of relevant evidence in the text. Therefore, it can be concluded that the study does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data. Paper content does not have evidence data regarding Question 2. It is not mentioned that susceptibility was checked, to answer 'No' it's because too few evidence of drug administartion. So the most precise answer is YES, the paper has no evidence for question 2."
37498738,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,Data availability is described in Supplementary Appendix 6.,"The paper mentions that data availability is described in Supplementary Appendix 6, but the specific details of public availability are not provided within the main text. Supplementary data is not considered public data. Therefore, I answer No."
37498738,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,K03455,SmaltAlign consists of an initial de novo alignment followed by mapping to the HIV-1 reference sequence HXB2 (GenBank accession number K03455).,"The paper mentions the reference sequence used for mapping in the NGS analysis, which is the HIV-1 reference sequence HXB2 with the GenBank accession number K03455. This indicates that the sequences were compared against this reference, but it does not provide GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced isolates themselves."
37498738,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,101,The ESS (NCT00537966) enrolled 101 people with HIV (PWH) from the Zurich Primary HIV Infection Study and Swiss HIV Cohort Study.,"The paper mentions that the ESS enrolled 101 people with HIV from the Zurich Primary HIV Infection Study and Swiss HIV Cohort Study, and that samples were obtained from these individuals for HIV sequencing."
37498738,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Switzerland,"The ESS (NCT00537966) enrolled 101 people with HIV (PWH) from the Zurich Primary HIV Infection Study and Swiss HIV Cohort Study. The present substudy was centered at the University Hospital Zurich, Switzerland.","The paper indicates that the study was conducted in Switzerland, specifically mentioning the Zurich Primary HIV Infection Study and the Swiss HIV Cohort Study as the sources of the participants."
37498738,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not specified,No evidence,The paper does not provide specific years during which the samples were obtained. It mentions the study design and timeline but does not specify the exact years of sample collection.
37498738,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Single proviral analysis showed high proportions of defective proviruses and low DRM numbers. DNA isolation, PCR, and NGS for participants' different time points were performed stricktly separately, as a precaution to avoid contamination. Each well with amplified DNA was subjected to PCR, and HIV-1 PCR-positive wells were sequenced as described above.","The paper mentions single genome analysis, dosing the cloning. That's why the answer is NO."
37498738,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"gag, pol, env","Average pairwise diversity was calculated and longitudinally assessed for the 3 main genomic regions, for 139 (85%) samples for gag, 147 (90%) for pol, and 123 (75%) for env, as other samples contained deletions in the respective genes.","The paper mentions that the average pairwise diversity was calculated and longitudinally assessed for the 3 main genomic regions, for 139 (85%) samples for gag, 147 (90%) for pol, and 123 (75%) for env."
37498738,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Next-generation sequencing (NGS),Near full-length HIV-1 proviral polymerase chain reaction (PCR) and next-generation sequencing was applied to longitudinal peripheral blood mononuclear cell samples to assess proviral evolution and the potential emergence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs).,The paper describes near full-length HIV-1 proviral polymerase chain reaction (PCR) and next-generation sequencing (NGS) as the methods used for sequencing the HIV-1 samples.
37498738,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs),We used peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) or co-culture virus from 4 different time points. DNA and RNA was simultaneously isolated from 5 million PBMCs using AllPrep Mini kit (Qiagen) according to the manufacturer’s instructions followed by complementary DNA synthesis.,The paper specifies that peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) were used as the source of the HIV-1 sequences for sequencing.
37498738,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,Our recent randomized clinical trial comparing a switch to dolutegravir monotherapy with continuation of cART in early-treated individuals demonstrated sustained virological suppression over 48 weeks. Understanding HIV-1 reservoir dynamics in PWH undergoing ART simplification will be an important aspect for further clinical treatment strategies and HIV reservoir studies.,"The paper focuses on early-treated individuals who had sustained virological suppression, it mentions nothing about virological failure."
37498738,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"The ESS (NCT00537966) enrolled 101 people with HIV (PWH) from the Zurich Primary HIV Infection Study and Swiss HIV Cohort Study. Inclusion criteria and details are published elsewhere and written informed consent was obtained for all study participants. The present substudy was centered at the University Hospital Zurich, Switzerland. Understanding HIV-1 reservoir dynamics in PWH undergoing ART simplification will be an important aspect for further clinical treatment strategies and HIV reservoir studies. We performed an analysis of the HIV-1 reservoir over a period of 48 weeks in early-treated participants on successful DTG monotherapy.","The paper mentions that the study was part of a randomized clinical trial (RCT), specifically the Early Simplified Study (ESS) (NCT00537966), which is a study design involving clinical trials."
37498738,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Our recent randomized clinical trial comparing a switch to dolutegravir monotherapy with continuation of cART in early-treated individuals demonstrated sustained virological suppression over 48 weeks. Long-term outcome of the Early Simplified Study (ESS): Dolutegravir monotherapy is non-inferior to combination antiretroviral therapy 10 years after antiretroviral therapy start. Early Simplified Study. The 4 selected time points were: ART start, 1 year after ART start, ESS week 0, and ESS week 48.",The paper mentions that the study is about a switch from a combination antiretroviral therapy to dolutegravir monotherapy. ART means antiretroviral therapy which includes ARV drugs. This indicates that the individuals in the study had received ARV drugs before the samples were obtained.
37498738,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, INIs","Our recent randomized clinical trial comparing a switch to dolutegravir monotherapy with continuation of cART in early-treated individuals demonstrated sustained virological suppression over 48 weeks. Long-term outcome of the Early Simplified Study (ESS): Dolutegravir monotherapy is non-inferior to combination antiretroviral therapy 10 years after antiretroviral therapy start. Early Simplified Study. The 4 selected time points were: ART start, 1 year after ART start, ESS week 0, and ESS week 48. One hundred sixty-three NGS sequences from 43 participants (25 on DTG monotherapy and 18 on cART) were selected, as all of these 43 had 3 time points with successful consensus sequences (1yaas, ESS wk0, and ESS wk48), and 34 of those also had a sequence from the ART start time point (Supplementary Figure 2). At Early Simplified Study (ESS) week 0, participants were randomly assigned to dolutegravir monotherapy or combination antiretroviral therapy (cART).","The paper mentions that participants were on cART (combination antiretroviral therapy) at the start of the Early Simplified Study (ESS). It also mentions the use of various drug classes in Appendix 6. The study was at the beginning of dolutegravir monotherapy, so DTG is also a drug that's received before sequenceing."
37498738,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not specified,"Our recent randomized clinical trial comparing a switch to dolutegravir monotherapy with continuation of cART in early-treated individuals demonstrated sustained virological suppression over 48 weeks. Long-term outcome of the Early Simplified Study (ESS): Dolutegravir monotherapy is non-inferior to combination antiretroviral therapy 10 years after antiretroviral therapy start. Early Simplified Study. The 4 selected time points were: ART start, 1 year after ART start, ESS week 0, and ESS week 48. One hundred sixty-three NGS sequences from 43 participants (25 on DTG monotherapy and 18 on cART) were selected, as all of these 43 had 3 time points with successful consensus sequences (1yaas, ESS wk0, and ESS wk48), and 34 of those also had a sequence from the ART start time point (Supplementary Figure 2). At Early Simplified Study (ESS) week 0, participants were randomly assigned to dolutegravir monotherapy or combination antiretroviral therapy (cART).","The paper mentions that participants were on cART (combination antiretroviral therapy) at the start of the Early Simplified Study (ESS). It also mentions the use of various drug classes in Appendix 6. The study was at the beginning of dolutegravir monotherapy, so DTG is also a drug that's received before sequenceing."
37515095,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"In this study on the molecular epidemiology of HIV in Kazakhstan, we analyzed 968 partial HIV-1 *pol* sequences that were collected between 2017 and 2020 from PLHIV across all regions of Kazakhstan, covering almost 3% of PLHIV in 2020. Amongst reported heterosexual transmissions (n = 566), risk factors were assessed for 472 (83.4%) participants.","The evidence clearly states that the study involved analyzing HIV-1 sequences collected from patient samples in Kazakhstan, indicating that the paper does report HIV sequences from patient samples."
37515095,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"In this study on the molecular epidemiology of HIV in Kazakhstan, we analyzed 968 partial HIV-1 *pol* sequences that were collected between 2017 and 2020 from PLHIV across all regions of Kazakhstan, covering almost 3% of PLHIV in 2020. The development of antiretroviral drug resistance is a major threat to effective antiretroviral therapy (ART), yet studies on the prevalence of drug resistance in Kazakhstan are sparse.",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or results. It focuses on the sequencing of HIV-1 *pol* genes and the analysis of drug resistance mutations in the sequences obtained from patient samples.
37515095,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The sequences generated from this study will not be publicly available, as privacy laws prohibit the sharing of sequences directly associated with any patient with HIV.","The paper explicitly states that the sequences generated from the study will not be made publicly available due to privacy laws, indicating that the sequences are not accessible to the public."
37515095,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,"No GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates are provided in the abstract or the manuscript. The Data Availability Statement mentions that study data are unavailable to protect patient confidentiality, but sequencing method details were provided, the GenBank accession numbers are not reported.","The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. This information is typically found in the methods section or data availability statement, which does not mention it."
37515095,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,968,"In this study on the molecular epidemiology of HIV in Kazakhstan, we analyzed 968 partial HIV-1 *pol* sequences that were collected between 2017 and 2020 from PLHIV across all regions of Kazakhstan. From 2017 to 2020, a total of 1004 participants registered with the Kazakh Scientific Centre of Dermatology and Infectious Diseases, Almaty, Kazakhstan; signed informed consent forms; and consented to blood sample collections for this study. Analyses on the CD4+ T-lymphocyte count were performed using the BD FACS Count Reagent Kit™ (BD Biosciences, San Jose, CA, USA), and viral load analyses were performed using the AmpliSens® HIV-Monitor-FRT Kit (InterLabService, Moscow, Russia). A questionnaire was administered to participants to collect information regarding medical history, HIV risk behaviors, and existing co-infections (including hepatitis B virus (HBV), hepatitis C virus (HCV), tuberculosis, and sexually transmitted infections). Testing for HCV and HBV was carried out on blood samples, using the Anti-HCV-ELISA Best Kit (Vector-Best, Novosibirsk, Russia) and the HBsAg-ELISA Best Kit (Vector-Best, Novosibirsk, Russia), respectively.","The evidence states that 968 HIV-1 sequences were analyzed from patient samples, which corresponds to the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained."
37515095,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Kazakhstan,"In this study on the molecular epidemiology of HIV in Kazakhstan, we analyzed 968 partial HIV-1 *pol* sequences that were collected between 2017 and 2020 from PLHIV across all regions of Kazakhstan. Between 2017 and 2020, 1004 participants registered with the Kazakh Scientific Centre of Dermatology and Infectious Diseases, Almaty, Kazakhstan; signed informed consent forms; and consented to blood sample collections for this study. Sequence generation. The mRNA from blood samples was purified using the Ribo-zol-C Kit (InterLabService, Moscow, Russia) and used for RT-PCR, followed by pol gene sequencing using AmpliSens® HIV-Resist-Seq (Amplisens, Moscow, Russia) and Applied Biosystems® Genetic Analyzer 3130 (Applied Biosystems Inc., Foster, RI, USA).","The study was conducted in Kazakhstan, and all sample were collected within the country, indicating that the sequenced samples were obtained from Kazakhstan."
37515095,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2017 to 2020,"In this study on the molecular epidemiology of HIV in Kazakhstan, we analyzed 968 partial HIV-1 *pol* sequences that were collected between 2017 and 2020 from PLHIV across all regions of Kazakhstan. Between 2017 and 2020, 1004 participants registered with the Kazakh Scientific Centre of Dermatology and Infectious Diseases, Almaty, Kazakhstan; signed informed consent forms; and consented to blood sample collections for this study. Over one-third (355, 36.7%) of the HIV-1 study sequences exhibited low-to-high-level drug resistance to at least one of the 13 ARVs screened for, with almost all exhibiting high levels of drug resistance (311, 87%). Of those with drug resistance, 304/355 (85.6%) participants had sequences resistant to at least one drug on their ART regimen, while 282/311 (90.7%) participants had sequences with high-level resistance to at least one drug on their ART regimen. High-level NNRTI resistance was most prevalent (291, 30.1%), followed by high-level NRTI resistance (163, 16.8%).",The evidence indicates that the samples were collected over a period from 2017 to 2020.
37515095,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The mRNA from blood samples was purified using the Ribo-zol-C Kit (InterLabService, Moscow, Russia) and used for RT-PCR, followed by pol gene sequencing using AmpliSens® HIV-Resist-Seq (Amplisens, Moscow, Russia) and Applied Biosystems® Genetic Analyzer 3130 (Applied Biosystems Inc., Foster, RI, USA).",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes direct sequencing of the *pol* gene from purified mRNA.
37515095,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Partial HIV-1 pol gene sequences covering protease and the 5′-end of reverse transcriptase,"Specifically, the sequence data consisted of partial HIV-1 *pol* gene sequences (covering protease and the 5′-end of reverse transcriptase, positions 2253 to 3337 on the HXB2 reference genome).","The evidence specifies that the sequencing covered the *pol* gene, including regions corresponding to protease and reverse transcriptase."
37515095,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"The mRNA from blood samples was purified using the Ribo-zol-C Kit (InterLabService, Moscow, Russia) and used for RT-PCR, followed by pol gene sequencing using AmpliSens® HIV-Resist-Seq (Amplisens, Moscow, Russia) and Applied Biosystems® Genetic Analyzer 3130 (Applied Biosystems Inc., Foster, RI, USA).","The paper mentions the use of the AmpliSens HIV-Resist-Seq and Applied Biosystems Genetic Analyzer 3130, which are indicative of Sanger sequencing method."
37515095,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Blood samples,"The mRNA from blood samples was purified using the Ribo-zol-C Kit (InterLabService, Moscow, Russia) and used for RT-PCR, followed by pol gene sequencing using AmpliSens® HIV-Resist-Seq (Amplisens, Moscow, Russia) and Applied Biosystems® Genetic Analyzer 3130 (Applied Biosystems Inc., Foster, RI, USA).","The evidence notes that the studies were conducted on blood samples, which were used for sequencing, and the mRNA from blood samples was used,. All participants registered to Kazakh Scientific center for Dermotology and Infectious Diseases, which also indicates that they were blood samples."
37515095,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"In this study on the molecular epidemiology of HIV in Kazakhstan, we analyzed 968 partial HIV-1 *pol* sequences that were collected between 2017 and 2020 from PLHIV across all regions of Kazakhstan, covering almost 3% of PLHIV in 2020. The development of antiretroviral drug resistance is a major threat to effective antiretroviral therapy (ART), yet studies on the prevalence of drug resistance in Kazakhstan are sparse. To facilitate analysis, the 15 regions of residence were regrouped into 7 broader geographical regions (Figure 1, termed ""Regions""), as follows: ""Northern Kazakhstan"", which includes North Kazakhstan and Kostanay; ""Southern Kazakhstan"", which includes Turkistan, Kyzylorda, and Zhambyl; ""Eastern Kazakhstan"", which includes Pavlodar and East Kazakhstan;""Western Kazakhstan"", which includes West Kazakhstan, Atyrau, Mangystau, and Aktobe; ""Central Kazakhstan"", which includes Karagandy; ""Astana"", which includes the capital city Astana and Akmola, Astana's surrounding region; and ""Almaty"", which includes the city Almaty and its homonymous surrounding region. Individual ART regimens were summarized as 1st-, 2nd-, or mixed-generation regimens according to the specific nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) and non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) administered: zidovudine (AZT), didanosine (DDI), stavudine (D4T), and lamivudine (3TC) were classified as 1st-generation NRTIs; abacavir (ABC), tenofovir (TDF), and emtricitabine (FTC) were classified as 2nd-generation NRTIs; nevirapine (NVP) was classified as a 1st-generation NNRTI; and efavirenz (EFV), etravirine (ETR), and rilpivirine (RPV) were classified as 2nd-generation NNRTIs. Any protease inhibitors (PIs, darunavir/ritonavir, DRV/r; DRV/cobicistat, DRV/c; lopinavir/r, LPV/r) or integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs, dolutegravir, DTG) administered were not considered for the 1st-, 2nd-, or mixed-generation regimen classification, as subsequent analysis of DRMs only considered mutations in reverse transcriptase. Over one-third (355, 36.7%) of the HIV-1 study sequences exhibited low-to-high-level drug resistance to at least one of the 13 ARVs screened for, with almost all exhibiting high levels of drug resistance (311, 87%). Of those with drug resistance, 304/355 (85.6%) participants had sequences resistant to at least one drug on their ART regimen, while 282/311 (90.7%) participants had sequences with high-level resistance to at least one drug on their ART regimen. High-level NNRTI resistance was most prevalent (291, 30.1%), followed by high-level NRTI resistance (163, 16.8%).","The paper focuses on the sequencing of HIV-1 *pol* genes from individuals undergoing treatment, which suggests that sequences were obtained from individuals with virological suppression."
37515095,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Between 2017 and 2020, 1004 participants registered with the Kazakh Scientific Centre of Dermatology and Infectious Diseases, Almaty, Kazakhstan; signed informed consent forms; and consented to blood sample collections for this study. Analyses on the CD4+ T-lymphocyte count were performed using the BD FACS Count Reagent Kit™ (BD Biosciences, San Jose, CA, USA), and viral load analyses were performed using the AmpliSens® HIV-Monitor-FRT Kit (InterLabService, Moscow, Russia). A questionnaire was administered to participants to collect information regarding medical history, HIV risk behaviors, and existing co-infections (including hepatitis B virus (HBV), hepatitis C virus (HCV), tuberculosis, and sexually transmitted infections). Testing for HCV and HBV was carried out on blood samples, using the Anti-HCV-ELISA Best Kit (Vector-Best, Novosibirsk, Russia) and the HBsAg-ELISA Best Kit (Vector-Best, Novosibirsk, Russia), respectively. Patients reporting symptoms consistent with tuberculosis were screened for tuberculosis using chest X-rays. to identify DRMs, and elucidate the evolutionary history of the HIV-1 epidemic in Kazakhstan using a phylogenetic approach. In this study on the molecular epidemiology of HIV in Kazakhstan, we analyzed 968 partial HIV-1pol sequences that were collected between 2017 and 2020 from PLHIV across all regions of Kazakhstan, covering almost 3% of PLHIV in 2020.","The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It describes a study conducted on patients registered with a dermatology and infectious diseases center, focused on drug resistance and epidemiological analysis."
37515095,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"The median time of ART was 4 years (range 0--15 years, with 7 individuals not undergoing ART). Individual ART regimens were summarized as 1st-, 2nd-, or mixed-generation regimens according to the specific nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) and non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) administered: zidovudine (AZT), didanosine (DDI), stavudine (D4T), and lamivudine (3TC) were classified as 1st-generation NRTIs; abacavir (ABC), tenofovir (TDF), and emtricitabine (FTC) were classified as 2nd-generation NRTIs; nevirapine (NVP) was classified as a 1st-generation NNRTI; and efavirenz (EFV), etravirine (ETR), and rilpivirine (RPV) were classified as 2nd-generation NNRTIs. Any protease inhibitors (PIs, darunavir/ritonavir, DRV/r; DRV/cobicistat, DRV/c; lopinavir/r, LPV/r) or integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs, dolutegravir, DTG) administered were not considered for the 1st-, 2nd-, or mixed-generation regimen classification, as subsequent analysis of DRMs only considered mutations in reverse transcriptase. Individual ART regimens were summarized as 1st-, 2nd-, or mixed-generation regimens according to the specific nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) and non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) administered: zidovudine (AZT), didanosine (DDI), stavudine (D4T), and lamivudine (3TC) were classified as 1st-generation NRTIs; abacavir (ABC), tenofovir (TDF), and emtricitabine (FTC) were classified as 2nd-generation NRTIs; nevirapine (NVP) was classified as a 1st-generation NNRTI; and efavirenz (EFV), etravirine (ETR), and rilpivirine (RPV) were classified as 2nd-generation NNRTIs. Any protease inhibitors (PIs, darunavir/ritonavir, DRV/r; DRV/cobicistat, DRV/c; lopinavir/r, LPV/r) or integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs, dolutegravir, DTG) administered were not considered for the 1st-, 2nd-, or mixed-generation regimen classification, as subsequent analysis of DRMs only considered mutations in reverse transcriptase. Ove 1/355 (85.6%) participants had sequences resistant to at least one drug on their ART regimen.","The evidence states that the participants were receiving ART, and individual regimens were classified, indicating that they had previously received ARV drugs sequences resistant to at least one drug on their ART regimen,"
