37541705,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"Background Child-friendly fixed dose combination (FDC) antiretroviral treatment (ART) options are limited. We evaluated the pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of once-daily dispersible and immediate-release FDC abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine in young children with HIV. Methods IMPAACT 2019 was an international, phase I/II, multi-site, open-label, non-comparative dose confirmation study of abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine in children <12 years. Participants were enrolled across five weight bands (WBs): those 6-<25 kg received abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine (60/5/30 mg) dispersible tablets (3–6 depending on body weight), and those 25-<40 kg received the immediate-release tablet (600/50/300 mg). At entry, participants were ART-naïve or ART-experienced and virologically suppressed on stable ART for ≥6 months. Dose confirmation was based on pharmacokinetic and safety criteria in the first 5–7 participants in each WB through Week 4; all participants were followed through Week 48. We present results for the primary objectives to assess pharmacokinetics, confirm dosing, and evaluate safety through 24 weeks across all WBs.","The paper does not mention any HIV sequences obtained from patient samples. It focuses on the pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of antiretroviral treatments in children with HIV, and there is no indication of sequencing data being reported from patient samples.. It evaluates the pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of antiretroviral treatments in children with HIV, but not the genetic sequencing of the virus from those children."
37541705,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Child-friendly fixed dose combination (FDC) antiretroviral treatment (ART) options are limited. We evaluated the pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of once-daily dispersible and immediate-release FDC abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine in young children with HIV. IMPAACT 2019 was an international, phase I/II, multi-site, open-label, non-comparative dose confirmation study of abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine in children <12 years. Participants were enrolled across five weight bands (WBs): those 6-<25 kg received abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine (60/5/30 mg) dispersible tablets (3–6 depending on body weight), and those 25-<40 kg received the immediate-release tablet (600/50/300 mg). At entry, participants were ART-naïve or ART-experienced and virologically suppressed on stable ART for ≥6 months. Dose confirmation was based on pharmacokinetic and safety criteria in the first 5–7 participants in each WB through Week 4; all participants were followed through Week 48. We present results for the primary objectives to assess pharmacokinetics, confirm dosing, and evaluate safety through 24 weeks across all WBs.","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on the pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of antiretroviral treatments in children with HIV, and there is no indication of such data being presented. The study is a phase I/II clinical trial evaluating the pharmacokinetics and safety of a drug regimen in children, and does not include laboratory studies on drug susceptibility."
37541705,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"Ethics Statement The study protocol (appendix and https://www.impaactnetwork.org/studies/impaact2019) was reviewed and approved by the Division of AIDS, National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, U.S. National Institutes of Health, and site Institutional Review Boards. Written informed consent was obtained from the participant’s parent or legal guardian. Assent was also obtained where applicable in accordance with local requirements.","The paper does not mention that any sequences were made publicly available. It focuses on the pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of antiretroviral treatments in children with HIV, and there is no indication of sequencing data being publicly available. The data cannot be made publicly available due to the ethical restrictions in the study’s informed consent documents and in the International Maternal Pediatric Adolescent AIDS Clinical Trials (IMPAACT) Network’s approved human subjects protection plan; public availability may compromise participant confidentiality."
37541705,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,None,"Pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of dispersible and immediate release abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine tablets in children with HIV (IMPAACT 2019): week 24 results of an open-label, multicenter, phase 1/2 dose confirmation study. Study Design IMPAACT 2019 (NCT03760458) was an international phase I/II, multi-site, open-label, non-comparative dose confirmation study. Participants were enrolled in five WBs concurrently (Table 1). The study was designed with a dose confirmation phase over the first four weeks among a subset of participants followed by longer term evaluation of safety, tolerability, and efficacy in all participants through 48 weeks. Dose confirmation was based on the first 5–7 children in each WB who were deemed both pharmacokinetic- and safety-evaluable.  At entry, participants were ART-naïve or ART-experienced and virologically suppressed on stable non-NNRTI containing regimen for at least six months prior to entry. At screening, participants were required to be HLA-B*5701-negative, have grade 2 or less severe hematology and chemistry laboratory values, and have no evidence of hepatitis B coinfection. Exclusion criteria included documented resistance to abacavir, dolutegravir, or lamivudine (except M184V); known hypersensitivity reaction to abacavir; current evidence of pancreatitis, active tuberculosis, or AIDS-defining opportunistic infections; or any other medical condition that might interfere with participation or interpretation of study outcomes.","The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on the pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of antiretroviral treatments in children with HIV, and there is no indication of sequencing data being reported. The study is focused on pharmacokinetics and clinical outcomes, not on the genetic sequencing of HIV."
37541705,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,None,"PMTCT of HIV-1 Infections among Children and Adolescents in Uganda: Year-3 Outcomes of the GENFEC Study. Background: The implementation of the universal test-and-treat (UTT) strategy, along with parameterization of the treatment cascade, is essential for the elimination of HIV-1 infections among children and adolescents. This requires testing, treatment, and retention intervention strategies, resulting in maternal-to-child transmission (MTCT) of HIV-1 infections ≤5% among children aged <5 years. We aimed to determine the coverage and effectiveness of the UTT strategy in a comprehensive HIV Services and Research Program in Kisoro, South-Western Uganda. Methods: We followed 393 HIV-1-uninfected infants and young children born to mothers living with HIV-1. At 12 and 24 months, we provided maternal HIV-1 care and treatment following failing regimens, unresolved virological failures, and newly diagnosed HIV-1 infections. By the end of the third year, we reported high HIV-1 maternal retention rates of 93%, virological suppression ≥90% through 24 months, and a maternal HIV-1 MTCT rate ≤1%. Results: We report the year-3 outcomes of 482 (120% of target) children and adolescents aged 0-22 years newly diagnosed with HIV-1 infection through facility and community-based testing. Total  with samples obtained for HIV sequencing achieving virological suppression, and with HIV-1 RNA followed. For year 3 of follow-up, virological suppression rates declined to 77%, primarily because long-term retention of virological suppression remains challenging. Reasons for not achieving virological suppression at 12 and 36 months included late diagnosis; being newly diagnosed with perinatally-acquired HIV-1 infection; newly diagnosed with higher-age-acquired HIV-1 infection; presence of co-morbidities &amp; chronic draining lymphadenopathy requiring surgical excision and or control in a tertiary centre; late presentation of HIV-1 infection to care; and admixties.The fifth reason reported here is  mortality due to HIV-1 infection. community-based diagnosis of higher acquirable infection shifted adolescent with least possibilities. which is delayed diagnosing chronic draining lymphadenopathy. Temporally, for years 1-3 had higher mortality due to HIV-1 infection compared to newly diagnosed at facilities. transient not virological suppression which increases subsequently despite there was awarenessophobia","The paper does not mention any individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing. It focuses on the pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of antiretroviral treatments in children with HIV, and there is no indication of such data being reported.  the study focuses on the pharmacokinetics and safety of a new drug regimen in children with HIV, and does not include data on sequencing of HIV samples from individuals."
37541705,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,None,"Impacts of Community- Versus Facility- Based HIV Testing on HIV Outcomes: A Combining Implementation and Antiretroviral Resistance Mutations Analysis in a KISORO HIV-1 Program in South-Western Uganda. Background: Early diagnosis of HIV-1 infection and timely initiation of antiretroviral treatment (ART) contributes to reduced HIV-1 morbidity and mortality, and to HIV-1 virological suppression. However, ART retention, virological suppression, and the rationales of community- versus facility- based testing became critical challenges. We aimed to evaluate the impacts of community- vs facility-based HIV testing on HIV health outcomes. Methods: We performed a multi-center prospective cohort study of HIV-1-uninfected pregnant women, HIV-1-exposed infants & children born to mothers living with HIV-1 in Kisoro District, South- Western Uganda, who contributed to implementation and retention in maternal-to-child transmission prevention (MTCT) of HIV-1 activities. All newly diagnosed HIV-1-infected children & adolescents from KISORO programs through 12 months were inclusion criteria. At 12 months, the impact on virological suppression and drug-resistance mutations through baseline to 12 months or 12 months cohorts. Resistance testing included determining the presence of drug resistance genotypes using the Stanford-HIV Drug Resistance Database and sequenced aligned to subtype A1, D and recombinants in Mega5.4 and 7.0. Resistance tests were performed on dried blood spots at baseline (April 2018) and pilot implementation 12 months after universal test-and-treat (April 2019), yielding temporal comparisons using two-tailed Fisher's exact tests, chisq tests relative to baseline resistance Rt=0.05, Rt=0.0658, that were not found in baseline 0-12 months incident vs inclusiou, volunteers deemed onto antiretroviral vs guided against resistance 0-30].","The paper does not mention any countries from which sequenced samples were obtained. It focuses on the pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of antiretroviral treatments in children with HIV, and there is no indication of such data being reported. the study focuses on the pharmacokinetics and safety of a new drug regimen in children with HIV, and does not include data on sequencing of HIV samples from individuals."
37541705,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,None,"Impacts of Community- Versus Facility- Based HIV Testing on HIV Outcomes: A Combining Implementation and Antiretroviral Resistance Mutations Analysis in a KISORO HIV-1 Program in South-Western Uganda. Background: Early diagnosis of HIV-1 infection and timely initiation of antiretroviral treatment (ART) contributes to reduced HIV-1 morbidity and mortality, and to HIV-1 virological suppression. However, ART retention, virological suppression, and the rationales of community- versus facility- based testing became critical challenges. We aimed to evaluate the impacts of community- vs facility-based HIV testing on HIV health outcomes. Methods: We performed a multi-center prospective cohort study of HIV-1-uninfected pregnant women, HIV-1-exposed infants & children born to mothers living with HIV-1 in Kisoro District, South- Western Uganda, who contributed to implementation and retention in maternal-to-child transmission (MTCT) of HIV-1 activities. All newly diagnosed HIV-1-infected children & adolescents from KISORO programs through 12 months were inclusion criteria. At 12 months, the impact on virological suppression and drug-resistance mutations through baseline to 12 months or 12 months cohorts. Resistance testing included determining the presence of drug resistance genotypes using the Stanford-HIV Drug Resistance Database and sequenced aligned to subtype A1, D and recombinants in Mega5.4 and 7.0. Resistance tests were performed on dried blood spots at baseline (April 2018) and pilot implementation 12 months after universal test-and-treat (April 2019), yielding temporal comparisons using two-tailed Fisher's exact tests, chisq tests relative to baseline resistance Rt=0.05, Rt=0.0658, that were not found in baseline 0-12 months incident vs inclusiou, volunteers deemed onto antiretroviral vs guided against resistance 0-30].","The paper does not mention any years from which sequenced samples were obtained. It focuses on the pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of antiretroviral treatments in children with HIV, and there is no indication of such data being reported. The study focuses on the pharmacokinetics and safety of a new drug regimen in children with HIV, and does not include data on sequencing of HIV samples from individuals."
37541705,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of dispersible and immediate release abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine tablets in children with HIV (IMPAACT 2019): week 24 results of an open-label, multicenter, phase 1/2 dose confirmation study. Methods IMPAACT 2019 was an international, phase I/II, multi-site, open-label, non-comparative dose confirmation study of abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine in children <12 years. Participants were enrolled across five weight bands (WBs): those 6-<25 kg received abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine (60/5/30 mg) dispersible tablets (3–6 depending on body weight), and those 25-<40 kg received the immediate-release tablet (600/50/300 mg). At entry, participants were ART-naïve or ART-experienced and virologically suppressed on stable ART for ≥6 months. Dose confirmation was based on pharmacokinetic and safety criteria in the first 5–7 participants in each WB through Week 4; all participants were followed through Week 48. We present results for the primary objectives to assess pharmacokinetics, confirm dosing, and evaluate safety through 24 weeks across all WBs. All, maximal, or no information obtained and reported fusion drug of HIV-1 viral subtype A1 and D (recombinant)identification of nucleoside RT mutation as well as all non-nucleoside RT mutationsRT, integrase drug mutants genotyping, genotypically and phenotypically, the will be decimalantiretroviral drugsusceptibility. Exclusion criteria included documented resistance to abacavir, dolutegravir, or lamivudine (except M184V); known hypersensitivity reaction to abacavir; current evidence of pancreatitis, active tuberculosis, or AIDS-defining opportunistic infections; or any other medical condition that might interfere with participation or interpretation of study outcomes.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It focuses on the pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of antiretroviral treatments in children with HIV, and there is no indication of such data being reported. The study focuses on the pharmacokinetics and safety of a new drug regimen in children with HIV, and does not include data on sequencing of HIV samples from individuals."
