lity- based testing became critical challenges. We aimed to evaluate the impacts of community- vs facility-based HIV testing on HIV health outcomes. Methods: We performed a multi-center prospective cohort study of HIV-1-uninfected pregnant women, HIV-1-exposed infants & children born to mothers living with HIV-1 in Kisoro District, South- Western Uganda, who contributed to implementation and retention in maternal-to-child transmission (MTCT) of HIV-1 activities. All newly diagnosed HIV-1-infected children & adolescents from KISORO programs through 12 months were inclusion criteria. At 12 months, the impact on virological suppression and drug-resistance mutations through baseline to 12 months or 12 months cohorts. Resistance testing included determining the presence of drug resistance genotypes using the Stanford-HIV Drug Resistance Database and sequenced aligned to subtype A1, D and recombinants in Mega5.4 and 7.0. Resistance tests were performed on dried blood spots at baseline (April 2018) and pilot implementation 12 months after universal test-and-treat (April 2019), yielding temporal comparisons using two-tailed Fisher's exact tests, chisq tests relative to baseline resistance Rt=0.05, Rt=0.0658, that were not found in baseline 0-12 months incident vs inclusiou, volunteers deemed onto antiretroviral vs guided against resistance 0-30].","The paper does not mention any sequencing methods. It focuses on the pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of antiretroviral treatments in children with HIV, and there is no indication of such data being reported. The study focuses on the pharmacokinetics and safety of a new drug regimen in children with HIV, and does not include data on sequencing of HIV samples from individuals."
