typing in naive children without history of enfuvirtide, raltegravir dolutegravir effective concentration 0.1-10 umol/L or 0.08-0.064 umol/L not executed. Exclusion criteria included documented resistance to abacavir, dolutegravir, or lamivudine (except M184V); known hypersensitivity reaction to abacavir current evidence of pancreatitis, active tuberculosis, or AIDS-defining opportunistic infections; or any other medical condition that might interfere with participation or interpretation of study outcomes.","The paper does not mention any specific types of samples that were sequenced. It focuses on the pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of antiretroviral treatments in children with HIV, and there is no indication of such data being reported. The study focuses on the pharmacokinetics and safety of a new drug regimen in children with HIV, and does not include data on sequencing of HIV samples from individuals."
37541705,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"Child-friendly fixed dose combination (FDC) antiretroviral treatment (ART) options are limited. We evaluated the pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of once-daily dispersible and immediate-release FDC abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine in young children with HIV. Methods IMPAACT 2019 was an international, phase I/II, multi-site, open-label, non-comparative dose confirmation study of abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine in children <12 years. Fifty-seven participants were enrolled (46% female; 65% Black, 32% Asian). The remaining 54 participants continued study treatment through 24 weeks and comprise the PSP. Dosing of abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine was confirmed in children weighing 6-<40 kg, and both FDC formulations were safe, well-tolerated, and efficacious through 24 weeks of treatment.  Here to say efficacious means haven't drug resistance mutants genotypes were not at baseline and 24 weeks. Study Population Children with HIV less than 12 years of age and weighing 6 to <40 kg were eligible. Eligible participants were treatment-naïve or treatment-experienced and virologically suppressed (HIV-1 RNA <200 copies per mL) on a stable non-NNRTI containing regimen for at least six months prior to entry. Children with HIV less than 12 years of age and weighing 6 to <40 kg were eligible. Methods IMPAACT 2019 was an international, phase I/II, multi-site, open-label, non-comparative dose confirmation study of abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine in children <12 years. Participants were enrolled across five weight bands (WBs): those 6-<25 kg received abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine (60/5/30 mg) dispersible tablets (3, minimum, to 6, maximum depending on body weight), and those 25-<40 kg received the immediate-release tablet (600/50/300 mg). At entry, participants were ART-naïve or ART-experienced and virologically suppressed on stable ART for ≥6 months. Fifty-seven participants were enrolled (46% female; 65% Black, 32% Asian). Ninety-five percent of participants were treatment-experienced and all remained virologically suppressed (<200 copies/mL) through week 24. Virologic suppression was achieved in 2/3 ART-naïve participants by week 24","The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure. It focuses on the pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of antiretroviral treatments in children with HIV, and there is no indication of such data being reported. The study focuses on the pharmacokinetics and safety of a new drug regimen in children with HIV, and does not include data on sequencing of HIV samples from individuals. At entry, participants were ART-naïve or ART-experienced and virologically suppressed on stable ART for ≥6 months. Virologic suppression was achieved in 2/3 ART-naïve participants by week 24."
37541705,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"Pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of dispersible and immediate release abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine tablets in children with HIV (IMPAACT 2019): week 24 results of an open-label, multicenter, phase 1/2 dose confirmation study. Methods IMPAACT 2019 was an international, phase I/II, multi-site, open-label, non-comparative dose confirmation study of abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine in children <12 years. Participants were enrolled across five weight bands (WBs): those 6-<25 kg received abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine (60/5/30 mg) dispersible tablets (3–6 depending on body weight), and those 25-<40 kg received the immediate-release tablet (600/50/300 mg). At entry, participants were ART-naïve or ART-experienced and virologically suppressed on stable ART for ≥6 months. Dose confirmation was based on pharmacokinetic and safety criteria in the first 5–7 participants in each WB through Week 4; all participants were followed through Week 48. We present results for the primary objectives to assess pharmacokinetics, confirm dosing, and evaluate safety through 24 weeks across all WBs. Eligible participants were treatment-naïve or treatment-experienced and virologically suppressed (HIV-1 RNA <200 copies per mL) on a stable non-NNRTI containing regimen for at least six months prior to entry. at entry, participants were ART-naïve or ART-experienced and virologically suppressed on stable non-NNRTI containing regimen for at least six months prior to entry. IMPAACT 2019 (NCT03760458) was an international phase I/II, multi-site, open-label, non-comparative dose confirmation study. Participants were enrolled in five WBs concurrently (Table 1). The study was designed with a dose confirmation phase over the first four weeks among a subset of participants followed by longer term evaluation of safety, tolerability, and efficacy in all participants through 48 weeks.","The paper describes a clinical trial, IMPAACT 2019, which is a phase I/II, multi-site, open-label, non-comparative dose confirmation study of  abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine in children. The study was designed to evaluate pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of the treatment in children <12 years and the outcomes were presented, confirming it was a clinical trial. The study described in the paper is a clinical trial, focusing on the pharmacokinetics and safety of a new drug regimen in children with HIV."
