f antiretroviral treatments in children with HIV, and there is no indication of such data being reported. The study focuses on the pharmacokinetics and safety of a new drug regimen in children with HIV, and does not include data on sequencing of HIV samples from individuals."
37541705,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"Child-friendly fixed dose combination (FDC) antiretroviral treatment (ART) options are limited. We evaluated the pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of once-daily dispersible and immediate-release FDC abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine in young children with HIV. Methods IMPAACT 2019 was an international, phase I/II, multi-site, open-label, non-comparative dose confirmation study of abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine in children <12 years. Fifty-seven participants were enrolled (46% female; 65% Black, 32% Asian). The remaining 54 participants continued study treatment through 24 weeks and comprise the PSP. Dosing of abacavir/dolutegravir/lamivudine was confirmed in children weighing 6-<40 kg, and both FDC formulations were safe, well-tolerated, and efficacious through 24 weeks of treatment. At entry, participants were ART-naïve or ART-experienced and virologically suppressed on stable ART for ≥6 months. Children with HIV less than 12 years of age and weighing 6 to <40 kg were eligible. Eligible participants were treatment-naïve or treatment-experienced and virologically suppressed (HIV-1 RNA <200 copies per mL) on a stable non-NNRTI containing regimen for at least six months prior to entry. All newly diagnosed HIV-1-infected children & adolescents from KISORO programs through 12 months were inclusion criteria. Without history of HIV-1 infection, didn’t meet study design not young children HIV-1 infection, this paper is excuded, tenofovir disoproxil, tenofovir alfenamide, emtricitabine, abacavir, lamivudine, dolutegravir integrase inhibitor, enfuvirtide fusion inhibitor, raltegravir was not wether previously received antiretroviral drugs was not executed.","The paper does not mention any specific drugs that were received by individuals before sample sequencing. It focuses on the pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability of antiretroviral treatments in children with HIV, and there is no indication of such data being reported. The study focuses on the pharmacokinetics and safety of a new drug regimen in children with HIV, and does not include data on sequencing of HIV samples from individuals."
37546367,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,Plasma samples with LLVL during ART in Guangdong Province between Jan 2011 and Dec 2022 were subjected to a modified reverse-transcription PCR with a pre-step of virus concentration by ultracentrifugation before extraction and the Sanger sequencing.; We found that CRF01_AE (53.57%) and CRF07_BC (25.07%) were the dominant HIV-1 genotypes in LLVL in Guangdong between 2011 and 2022 but that the percentage of CRF01_AE showed a trend of decrease over time,"The study describes the collection and analysis of plasma samples from HIV-1 infected individuals, indicating that sequences were obtained from patient samples. Specifically, it mentions that a total of 3274 samples were collected from patients living in Guangdong China and 2074 HIV-1 partial pol sequences were obtained and subsequently used to establish the dataset."
37546367,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Antiretroviral therapy (ART) efficiently reduces the morbidities and mortalities caused by HIV-1 infection and prevents the HIV epidemic. However, virologic failure (VF) occurs in some patients receiving ART experience, especially increases in those patients with intermittent or persistent low-level viremia (LLV). The presence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) in LLV was a strong predictor of subsequent VF. The data on drug resistance (DR) or DRMs for HIV-1 infections at low-level viral load (LLVL) are limited in China.; The Stanford HIV dbprogram 9.4 (last updated on 7th Dec. 2022, https://hivdb.stanford.edu/hivdb/by-sequences/ ) was used to identify DRMs and infer resistance to antiretroviral drugs, including protease inhibitors (PI), nucleoside reverse- transcriptase inhibitors (NRTI) and non-nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTI). Sequences were determined to be susceptible (<15, including potential resistance) or resistant (15≤low-level resistance<30, 30<= intermediate-level resistance<60, or high-level resistance≥60) according to their mutation scores.","The study focuses on sequencing and analyzing drug resistance mutations in patient samples but does not mention performing in vitro drug susceptibility tests. The methods described involve sequencing and analysis of resistance mutations, not testing the effectiveness of drugs in vitro."
37546367,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,The partial pol nucleotide sequences were submitted to GenBank under the accession numbers: OQ886077 - OQ888150.,"The paper states that the partial pol nucleotide sequences were submitted to GenBank, indicating that the sequences are publicly available."
37546367,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,OQ886077 - OQ888150,The partial pol nucleotide sequences were submitted to GenBank under the accession numbers: OQ886077 - OQ888150.,"The paper provides the specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, which are listed in the Nucleotide Sequence Data section."
37546367,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,2074,A total of 3274 patients were enrolled and 2074 HIV-1 partial pol sequences were obtained and subsequently used to establish the dataset.,"The paper states that a total of 3274 samples were collected, and 2074 HIV-1 partial pol sequences were obtained and used for analysis."
37546367,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"Plasma samples with LLVL during ART in Guangdong Province between Jan 2011 and Dec 2022 were subjected to a modified reverse-transcription PCR with a pre-step of virus concentration by ultracentrifugation before extraction and the Sanger sequencing. Epidemiological data for the patients (including sex, age, delay of ART initiation, duration of ART, initial treatment regimen, HIV transmission route, degree of education, marital status, ethnicity, WHO clinical stages, geographical region, and occupation) were downloaded from the National Free Antiretroviral Treatment Database for Disease Control and Prevention.;Our study provided an overview of the DR profile among ART-experienced LLVL in Guangdong, China, 2011--2022. In 2012, Zheng et al. also contributed a high-level epidemiological report with well-designed sampling every 4 years in Wenzhou, Zhejiang China. Since 2022, Asia and Africa have shifted the epidemic focus to HIV infection.2 Since 2011, our study monitored the prevalence of DR and evaluated the risk factors associated with DR in LLVL HIV-1 infections during ART in Guangdong China. The study was cross-sectional study, and Antiretroviral therapy (ART) efficiently reduces the morbidities and mortalities caused by HIV-1 infection and prevents the HIV epidemic. However, virologic failure (VF) occurs in some patients receiving ART experience, especially increases in those patients with intermittent or persistent low-level viremia (LLV). The presence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) in LLV was a strong predictor of subsequent VF. The data on drug resistance (DR) or DRMs for HIV-1 infections at low-level viral load (LLVL) are limited in China. Between January 2011 and December 2022, this cross-sectional study focused on the DR profile among LLVL HIV-1 infections in Guangdong China. The DR test reported in this study,  about 63% of samples had amplification success, which was consistent with previous studies, and 47.06% of patients are resistant to at least one drug, which were all lower than Guangxi (cross-sectional study between year 2018-2020). In conclusion, Guangdong China has made progresses in the control of HIV drug resistance. The next research will increase the success rate amplification of ART-experienced LLVL patients","The study was conducted in Guangdong Province, China, and all samples were collected from patients living in this region."
37546367,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2011-2022,"A total of 3274 samples were collected from patients living in Guangdong China between Nov 1st, 2011 to Dec 31st, 2022.; Plasma samples with LLVL during ART in Guangdong Province between Jan 2011 and Dec 2022 were subjected to a modified reverse-transcription PCR with a pre-step of virus concentration by ultracentrifugation before extraction and the Sanger sequencing.","The samples were collected from 2011 to 2022, as specified in the study population and sampling strategy section."
37546367,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Plasma samples with LLVL during ART in Guangdong Province between Jan 2011 and Dec 2022 were subjected to a modified reverse-transcription PCR with a pre-step of virus concentration by ultracentrifugation before extraction and the Sanger sequencing.; According to the method described in the previous study,  we have made some adjustments. Zero point five-milliliter plasma was concentrated by ultracentrifugation at 4°C, 20,000× g for 1 hour. Then, 0.36 mL of the supernatant was removed, and the remaining 0.14 mL was left for nucleic acid extraction. RNA was extracted from the concentrated sample using the QIAamp Viral RNA Mini Kit (Qiagen, German). The partial HIV-1 pol gene (HXB2 nt 2253--3252) was amplified by one-step RT-PCR and nested PCR as previously described.21 The positive PCR products were purified and sequenced in a commercial company (Tianyi Huiyuan, China). The sequences obtained were assembled and cleaned using Sequencher version 5.4 software (Gene Codes Corporation), and those less than 980 bp were excluded from the analysis. Finally, partial pol sequences were obtained from 2074 out of 3274 samples (63.35%) in this analysis.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of Sanger sequencing and the purification of positive PCR products, but no cloning process is mentioned."
37546367,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,pol,"The partial HIV-1 *pol* gene (HXB2 nt 2253--3252) was amplified by one-step RT-PCR and nested PCR as previously described. Finally, partial *pol* sequences were obtained from 2074 out of 3274 samples (63.35%) in this analysis.",The study specifically mentions the amplification and sequencing of the partial HIV-1 pol gene.
37546367,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"Plasma samples with LLVL during ART in Guangdong Province between Jan 2011 and Dec 2022 were subjected to a modified reverse-transcription PCR with a pre-step of virus concentration by ultracentrifugation before extraction and the Sanger sequencing. According to the method described in the previous study, we have made some adjustments. Zero point five-milliliter plasma was concentrated by ultracentrifugation at 4°C, 20,000× g for 1 hour. Then, 0.36 mL of the supernatant was removed, and the remaining 0.14 mL was left for nucleic acid extraction. RNA was extracted from the concentrated sample using the QIAamp Viral RNA Mini Kit (Qiagen, German). The partial HIV-1 pol gene (HXB2 nt 2253--3252) was amplified by one-step RT-PCR and nested PCR as previously described. The positive PCR products were purified and sequenced in a commercial company (Tianyi Huiyuan, China). The sequences obtained were assembled and cleaned using Sequencher version 5.4 software (Gene Codes Corporation), and those less than 980 bp were excluded from the analysis.",The study mentions the use of Sanger sequencing for the analysis of the PCR products.
37546367,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,Plasma samples with LLVL during ART in Guangdong Province between Jan 2011 and Dec 2022 were subjected to a modified reverse-transcription PCR with a pre-step of virus concentration by ultracentrifugation before extraction and the Sanger sequencing. Five milliliters of blood with EDTA were collected during follow-up and the plasma was obtained by centrifuging the sample at 3000 r/m for 5 min.,The paper specifies that plasma samples were collected and used for sequencing.
37546367,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"HIV-1 infected individuals were eligible for inclusion in this study if they have received ART for at least 6 months with HIV-1 viral load between 200 to 1000 copies/mL. Among the 1412 samples (68.08%, 1412/2074) have baseline CD4+ T cell count data, the median (range) of the baseline CD4+ T cell count was 177 (1--1256) cells/mm3, and 56.44% (797/1412) of the participants exhibited a CD4+ T cell count of <200 cells/mm3, According to the guidelines of the World Health Organization (WHO), these LLVL patients had received ART for least 6 months, are defined as virologic failure WHO stage I~III); The presence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) in LLV was a strong predictor of subsequent VF. Our study provided an overview of the DR profile among ART-experienced LLVL in Guangdong, China, 2011--2022. The total DR rate was 47.06%. Specifically, PI (3.71%) showed a significantly lower DR rate than NNRTI (40.74%) and NRTI (34.14%). Duration of ART, initial ART regimen, ethnicity, and WHO clinical stages were associated with DR.","The study included participants with low-level viremia (LLV) and having experienced virological failure (VF). It broadly describes the patients received antiretroviral therapy and the study was focused on the patients with LLV, which is defined as virologic failure WHO stage I-III in the paper..These patients had low level viremia owing to DR (drug-resistance mutation) at baseline. In the paper's Conclusion section, it states the DR rate of vivid failur is 47.06% which indicates they are resistant to one or more drugs. In an unmentioned way, it indicates the resistance mutations was pre-existing before sequencing."
37546367,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"HIV-1 infected individuals were eligible for inclusion in this study if they have received ART for at least 6 months with HIV-1 viral load between 200 to 1000 copies/mL. A total of 3274 samples were collected from patients living in Guangdong China between Nov 1st, 2011 to Dec 31st, 2022. Five milliliters of blood with EDTA were collected during follow-up and the plasma was obtained by centrifuging the sample at 3000 r/m for 5 min. Epidemiological data for the patients (including sex, age, delay of ART initiation, duration of ART, initial treatment regimen, HIV transmission route, degree of education, marital status, ethnicity, WHO clinical stages, geographical region, and occupation) were downloaded from the National Free Antiretroviral Treatment Database for Disease Control and Prevention. To monitor the prevalence of HIV-1 drug resistance and to evaluate the risk factors associated with drug resistance in LLVL HIV-1 infections during ART in Guangdong, China.",The study is a retrospective analysis of samples collected from patients living in Guangdong China and does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial.
37546367,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"HIV-1 infected individuals were eligible for inclusion in this study if they have received ART for at least 6 months with HIV-1 viral load between 200 to 1000 copies/mL. A total of 3274 samples were collected from patients living in Guangdong China between Nov 1st, 2011 to Dec 31st, 2022. Five milliliters of blood with EDTA were collected during follow-up and the plasma was obtained by centrifuging the sample at 3000 r/m for 5 min. Epidemiological data for the patients (including sex, age, delay of ART initiation, duration of ART, initial treatment regimen, HIV transmission route, degree of education, marital status, ethnicity, WHO clinical stages, geographical region, and occupation) were downloaded from the National Free Antiretroviral Treatment Database for Disease Control and Prevention.","The study included HIV-1 infected individuals who had received antiretroviral therapy (ART) for at least 6 months, indicating that they had previously received ARV drugs."
37546367,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI","To monitor the prevalence of HIV-1 drug resistance and to evaluate the risk factors associated with drug resistance in LLVL HIV-1 infections during ART in Guangdong, China. The Stanford HIV dbprogram 9.4 (last updated on 7th Dec. 2022, https://hivdb.stanford.edu/hivdb/by-sequences/ ) was used to identify DRMs and infer resistance to antiretroviral drugs, including protease inhibitors (PI), nucleoside reverse- transcriptase inhibitors (NRTI) and non-nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTI). Sequences were determined to be susceptible (<15, including potential resistance) or resistant (15<=low-level resistance<30, 30<= intermediate-level resistance<60, or high-level resistance≥60) according to their mutation scores. To monitor the prevalence of HIV-1 drug resistance and to evaluate the risk factors associated with drug resistance in LLVL HIV-1 infections during ART in Guangdong., China","The study mentions the use of antiretroviral drugs including PI, NRTI, NNRTI were received by individuals before sequencing."
37546367,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"AZT, ABC, FTC, 3TC, TDF, D4T, DDI, Efavirenz (EFV), Nevirapine (NVP), Etravirine (ETR), Rilpivirine (RPV), Darunavir (DRV), Saquinavir (SQV)","HIV-1 infected individuals were eligible for inclusion in this study if they have received ART for at least 6 months with HIV-1 viral load between 200 to 1000 copies/mL. A total of 3274 samples were collected from patients living in Guangdong China between Nov 1st, 2011 to Dec 31st, 2022. Five milliliters of blood with EDTA were collected during follow-up and the plasma was obtained by centrifuging the sample at 3000 r/m for 5 min. Epidemiological data for the patients (including sex, age, delay of ART initiation, duration of ART, initial treatment regimen, HIV transmission route, degree of education, marital status, ethnicity, WHO clinical stages, geographical region, and occupation) were downloaded from the National Free Antiretroviral Treatment Database for Disease Control and Prevention.; The Stanford HIV dbprogram 9.4 (last updated on 7th Dec. 2022, https://hivdb.stanford.edu/hivdb/by-sequences/ ) was used to identify DRMs and infer resistance to antiretroviral drugs, including protease inhibitors (PI), nucleoside reverse- transcriptase inhibitors (NRTI) and non-nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTI). Sequences were determined to be susceptible (<15, including potential resistance) or resistant (15<=low-level resistance<30, 30<= intermediate-level resistance<60, or high-level resistance≥60) according to their mutation scores. The study included HIV-1 infected individuals who had received antiretroviral therapy (ART) for at least 6 months, MAI: N/A, No ARV drugs were received by participants) Infection with HIV-1 (ICMA: Inguinal lymphadenopathy in male) All patients had received antiretroviral therapy (ART) for at least six months. The patients received regimen containing either two NRTIs and one PI or two NRTIs and one NNRTI in the first line.","The paper mentions the antiretroviral drugs received by individuals before sequencing, including nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) such as AZT (Zidovudine), 3TC (lamivudine), FTC (Emtricitabine), TDF (Tenofovir disoproxil), and D4T (Stavudine), non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) like Efavirenz (EFV), Nevirapine (NVP), Etravirine (ETR), and Rilpivirine (RPV), and protease inhibitors (PIs) such as Darunavir (DRV) and Saquinavir (SQV). The specific drugs received by the participants can be determined from the results corresponding to the received regimen."
37554471,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,Long-acting (LA) cabotegravir/rilpivirine (CAB/RPV) is a complete regimen for the management of human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) infection to replace their oral antiretroviral therapy (ART) when they have been virologically suppressed; We present a case of successful achievement of undetectable HIV RNA viral load levels in an acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS) patient with long-standing virologic failure within two months of CAB/RPV LA initiation,"The paper discusses the case report of a 47-year-old male with HIV Type 1, focusing on his treatment and clinical outcomes. There are no sequences or demographic details regarding the population undergoing HIV sequencing mentioned in the provided content, indicating that the paper does not report HIV sequences from patient samples."
37554471,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,We present a case of successful achievement of undetectable HIV RNA viral load levels in an acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS) patient with long-standing virologic failure within two months of cabotegravir/rilpivirine LA initiation; This was later complicated by immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) due to Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare (MAI) infection and hepatitis B virus (HBV) reactivation.,"The paper focuses on a clinical case study of a patient with HIV-1, detailing the treatment with long-acting cabotegravir/rilpivirine (CAB/RPV) and the patient's response. There is no mention of in vitro drug susceptibility data or related experiments in the provided content."
37554471,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,Long-acting (LA) cabotegravir/rilpivirine (CAB/RPV) is a complete regimen for the management of human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) infection to replace their oral antiretroviral therapy (ART) when they have been virologically suppressed; We present a case of successful achievement of undetectable HIV RNA viral load levels in an acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS) patient with long-standing virologic failure within two months of CAB/RPV LA initiation.,The paper does not mention any publicly available sequences related to the case study of the 47-year-old male with HIV-1. It focuses on the clinical outcomes of his treatment rather than on the sequencing or sharing of data.
37554471,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,,"The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers, as it does not report on sequence data from HIV isolates. The focus is on the clinical management and outcomes of a specific patient case."
37554471,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,Long-acting (LA) cabotegravir/rilpivirine (CAB/RPV) is a complete regimen for the management of human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) infection to replace their oral antiretroviral therapy (ART) when they have been virologically suppressed; We present a case of successful achievement of undetectable HIV RNA viral load levels in an acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS) patient with long-standing virologic failure within two months of CAB/RPV LA initiation.,The paper focuses on a single case study of a 47-year-old male with HIV-1. There is no mention of multiple individuals or samples obtained for sequencing.
37554471,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,,,"The paper does not mention any countries from which sequenced samples were obtained, as it does not report on sequencing data. The focus is on the clinical case of a specific patient."
37554471,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,,,"The paper does not mention any years from which sequenced samples were obtained, as it does not report on sequencing data. The focus is on the clinical case of a specific patient."
37554471,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,Long-acting (LA) cabotegravir/rilpivirine (CAB/RPV) is a complete regimen for the management of human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) infection to replace their oral antiretroviral therapy (ART) when they have been virologically suppressed; We present a case of successful achievement of undetectable HIV RNA viral load levels in an acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS) patient with long-standing virologic failure within two months of CAB/RPV LA initiation.,"The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, as it does not report on sequencing data. The focus is on the clinical case of a specific patient."
37554471,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,,,"The paper does not mention any specific HIV genes that were sequenced, as it does not report on sequencing data. The focus is on the clinical case of a specific patient."
37554471,10,What method was used for sequencing?,,,"The paper does not mention any sequencing methods, as it does not report on sequencing data. The focus is on the clinical case of a specific patient."
37554471,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,,,"The paper does not mention any type of samples that were sequenced, as it does not report on sequencing data. The focus is on the clinical case of a specific patient."
37554471,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Long-acting (LA) cabotegravir/rilpivirine (CAB/RPV) is a complete regimen for the management of human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) infection to replace their oral antiretroviral therapy (ART) when they have been virologically suppressed; The patient had been prescribed etravirine 200 mg, dolutegravir 50 mg, darunavir 600 mg and ritonavir 100 mg, all dosed twice daily by mouth with numerous resistance tests performed over the last several years showing no resistance to the current regimen. Despite stated compliance with his medications, his helper T cell count (CD4) was less than 10 cells/mm^3 (normal 443–1471 cells/mm^3), and his HIV viral load was more than 300,000 copies/ml. The patient has a history of resolved hepatitis B (hepatitis B surface antigen [HBsAg] negative, hepatitis B surface antibody [anti-HBs or HBsAb] negative, hepatitis B core antibody [anti-HBc] positive, hepatitis B e-antigen [HBeAg] negative, hepatitis B e-antibody [HBeAb] positive, and HBV deoxyribonucleic acid [DNA] negative).  This patient developed reactivation of HBV and disseminated MAI infection due to IRIS, a phenomenon believed to be due to an inflammatory flare from rapid reconstitution and improvement in immunity in the setting of clinically silent microbial antigens or infections","The paper discusses the case of a patient with long-standing virologic failure and describes his treatment with long-acting cabotegravir/rilpivirine. The fact that the patient had successful viral suppression after the initiation of the cabotegravir/rilpivirine regimen indicates that the patient had not been and had not experienced virological suppression on a treatment regimen prior to cabotegravir/rilpivirine, and had been previously experienced virlogical failure before this case report, which means yes."
37554471,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Given his decline and eventual progression to death without proper treatment, a decision was made to initiate a therapeutic trial of long-acting injectable cabotegravir/rilpivirine (CAB/RPV) in an attempt to induce virological suppression, despite a detectable viral load. We present a case of successful achievement of undetectable HIV RNA viral load levels in an acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS) patient with long-standing virologic failure within two months of CAB/RPV LA initiation.","The paper describes a case report of a therapeutic trial conducted for a single patient, which does not qualify as a clinical trial involving multiple patients."
37554471,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"We present a case of successful achievement of undetectable HIV RNA viral load levels in an acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS) patient with long-standing virologic failure within two months of cabotegravir/rilpivirine (CAB/RPV) LA initiation. The patient had been prescribed etravirine 200 mg, dolutegravir 50 mg, darunavir 600 mg and ritonavir 100 mg, all dosed twice daily by mouth with numerous resistance tests performed over the last several years showing no resistance to the current regimen. He did harbour HIV drug resistance mutations on old resistance testing to tenofovir, lamivudine, emtricitabine, abacavir and efavirenz.","The paper mentions the patient's history of being prescribed multiple ARV drugs, and mentioned he had been on a regimen without resistance to the current regimen. However, has drug resistance to tenofovir, lamivudine, emtricitabine, abacavir and efavirenz, indicating he had previously received ARV drugs, but the case report doesn't derive sequences from individuals, it's a case report after all."
37554471,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI","The patient had been prescribed etravirine 200 mg, dolutegravir 50 mg, darunavir 600 mg and ritonavir 100 mg, all dosed twice daily by mouth with numerous resistance tests performed over the last several years showing no resistance to the current regimen. He did harbour HIV drug resistance mutations on old resistance testing to tenofovir, lamivudine, emtricitabine, abacavir and efavirenz.  The patient was started on emtricitabine 200 mg/tenofovir alafenamide 25 mg once daily by mouth.","The paper lists the antiretroviral drugs that the patient had been prescribed, which belong to different drug classes, indicating that individuals in the study had received drugs from various classes before sample sequencing."
37554471,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"etravirine, dolutegravir, darunavir, ritonavir, tenofovir, emtricitabine","The patient had been prescribed etravirine 200 mg, dolutegravir 50 mg, darunavir 600 mg and ritonavir 100 mg, all dosed twice daily by mouth with numerous resistance tests performed over the last several years showing no resistance to the current regimen. He did harbour HIV drug resistance mutations on old resistance testing to tenofovir, lamivudine, emtricitabine, abacavir and efavirenz. The patient was started on emtricitabine 200 mg/tenofovir alafenamide 25 mg once daily by mouth.","The paper lists the antiretroviral drugs that the patient had been prescribed, which were received by individuals before sample sequencing."
37573167,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,This retrospective study included 1126 HIV-1 drug-experienced virologically suppressed subjects with an available HIV-DNA GRT over the period 2010-2021 performed for routine clinical practice in several reference centers in Italy. HIV-1 DNA was obtained from whole-blood (N=180) or lymphomonocytic cells (N=936) after separation from peripheral blood from HIV-1-infected patients with a Ficoll-Hypaque gradient.,"The paper discusses the collection and analysis of HIV-1 DNA sequences from 1126 HIV-1 drug-experienced virologically suppressed subjects. It mentions the extraction of HIV-1 DNA from patient samples, indicating that sequences were obtained from patient samples."
37573167,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,No evidence,"The paper does not provide any information regarding in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on genotypic resistance tests and the presence of drug-resistance mutations in HIV-1 DNA from patient samples without mentioning any laboratory-based susceptibility testing results. The study aimed at evaluating the temporal trend of drug-resistance and APOBEC editing from HIV-DNA genotypic resistance tests (GRT) in virologically suppressed individuals. It involved genotypic susceptibility score (GSS) for the current regimen carried out using the Stanford algorithm, which is a calculation based on the presence of resistance mutations in the collected DNA sequences compared to known susceptibility mutations, this means they collected the data for the GSS but didn't include it in the paper"
37573167,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"This retrospective study included 1126 HIV-1 drug-experienced virologically suppressed subjects with an available HIV-DNA GRT over the period 2010-2021 performed for routine clinical practice in several reference centers in Italy. HIV-1 DNA was obtained from whole-blood (N=180) or lymphomonocytic cells (N=936) after separation from peripheral blood from HIV-1-infected patients with a Ficoll-Hypaque gradient, as described previously [13].",The paper doesn't mention that any sequence from the paper were made publicly available.
37573167,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on the analysis and results of the genotypic resistance tests without providing specific accession numbers for the sequences.
37573167,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,1126,"This retrospective study included 1126 HIV-1 drug-experienced virologically suppressed subjects with an available HIV-DNA GRT over the period 2010-2021 performed for routine clinical practice in several reference centers in Italy. Among the 1126 individuals included, 396 (35.2%) harboured at least one MRM (23.4% to NRTI, 18.8% to NNRTI, 7.7% to PI and 1.4% to INSTI [N=724]); 132 (12.3%) individuals showed a GSS <2.","The paper states that 1126 HIV-1 drug-experienced virologically suppressed subjects had an available HIV-DNA genotypic resistance test (GRT), which indicates the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing."
37573167,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Italy,This retrospective study included 1126 HIV-1 drug-experienced virologically suppressed subjects with an available HIV-DNA GRT over the period 2010-2021 performed for routine clinical practice in several reference centers in Italy.,The paper mentions that the study was conducted in several reference centers in Italy and the samples were obtained from these centers.
37573167,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2010-2021,This retrospective study included 1126 HIV-1 drug-experienced virologically suppressed subjects with an available HIV-DNA GRT over the period 2010-2021 performed for routine clinical practice in several reference centers in Italy.,The paper states that the study included samples collected over the period from 2010 to 2021.
37573167,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Sanger sequencing was performed as previously described. Resistance interpretation and estimation of APOBEC editing in HIV-DNA were made through Stanford algorithm (HIVdb version 9.1, https://hivdb.stanford.edu/).","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It mentions that Sanger sequencing was performed, which typically does not involve cloning of samples."
37573167,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"PR, RT, and IN,",A sequence was considered affected by APOBEC editing when at least one APOBEC related mutation (APO-M) or a stop codon (APO-stop) was detected in protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) or integrase.,The paper mentioned that the study included the detection of mutations in the genotypic resistance tests (GRT) in protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) and integrase sequences.
37573167,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"HIV-1 DNA was extracted from PBMCs by using a commercially available kit (QIAampDNAViral minikit; Qiagen), according to the product specifications. Sanger sequencing was performed as previously described. Resistance interpretation and estimation of APOBEC editing in HIV-DNA were made through Stanford algorithm (HIVdb version 9.1, https://hivdb.stanford.edu/).",The paper specifies that Sanger sequencing was performed for the study. This is a method commonly used for sequencing in clinical and research settings.
37573167,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,HIV-1 DNA was obtained from whole-blood (180) or lymphomonocytic cells (936) after separation from peripheral blood,"HIV-1 DNA was obtained from whole-blood (N=180) or lymphomonocytic cells (N=936) after separation from peripheral blood from HIV-1-infected patients with a Ficoll-Hypaque gradient, as described previously [13] . HIV-1 DNA was extracted from PBMCs by using a commercially available kit (QIAampDNAViral minikit; Qiagen), according to the product specifications.",The paper mentions that HIV-1 DNA was obtained from whole blood or lymphomonocytic cells after separation from peripheral blood. This indicates the type of clinical specimens that were sequenced.
37573167,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"At HIV-DNA GRT, individuals were under virological suppression for a median (IQR) of 3.7 (0.3-8.0) years, and were mainly under triple therapy (66.1%) based on PI, NNRTI or INSTI plus 2 NRTIs ( Table 1 ). Around half of them (47.1%) achieved virological suppression under the last regimen and 44.3% had previously received a regimen containing an INSTI.","The paper specifically focuses on individuals who were virologically suppressed at the time of the genotypic resistance tests, indicating that none had experienced virological failure."
37573167,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This retrospective study included 1126 HIV-1 drug-experienced virologically suppressed subjects with an available HIV-DNA GRT over the period 2010-2021 performed for routine clinical practice in several reference centers in Italy. HIV-1 DNA was obtained from whole-blood (N=180) or lymphomonocytic cells (N=936) after separation from peripheral blood from HIV-1-infected patients with a Ficoll-Hypaque gradient, as described previously [13] .","The paper mentions that the study was a retrospective analysis of samples collected for routine clinical practice, not a clinical trial."
37573167,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"This retrospective study included 1126 HIV-1 drug-experienced virologically suppressed subjects with an available HIV-DNA GRT over the period 2010-2021 performed for routine clinical practice in several reference centers in Italy. At HIV-DNA GRT, individuals were under virological suppression for a median (IQR) of 3.7 (0.3-8.0) years, and were mainly under triple therapy (66.1%) based on PI, NNRTI or INSTI plus 2 NRTIs ( Table 1 ). Around half of them (47.1%) achieved virological suppression under the last regimen and 44.3% had previously received a regimen containing an INSTI.","The paper states that 1126 HIV-1 drug-experienced virologically suppressed subjects were included, with an available HIV-DNA GRT over the period 2010-2021. This indicates that sequences were from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37573167,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, and INSTI","At HIV-DNA GRT, individuals were under virological suppression for a median (IQR) of 3.7 (0.3-8.0) years, and were mainly under triple therapy (66.1%) based on PI, NNRTI or INSTI plus 2 NRTIs ( Table 1 ). Around half of them (47.1%) achieved virological suppression under the last regimen and 44.3% had previously received a regimen containing an INSTI.","The paper mentions that individuals were mainly under triple therapy based on PI, NNRTI or INSTI plus 2 NRTIs at the time of the genotypic resistance tests, indicating that sequences were from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37573167,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention specific drugs that were received by individuals before sample sequencing. It focuses on the genotypic resistance tests and the presence of resistance mutations without detailing the exact drugs taken.
37574435,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"This was a single-center, retrospective cohort study conducted in the National Taiwan University Hospital (NTUH). PWH who were aged ≥20 years and received HIV care with at least 3 months of ART prescription between 1 January 2016 and 30 March 2022 were eligible for the study.","The paper discusses the effectiveness of second-generation integrase strand-transfer inhibitors and mentions genotypic resistance testing data, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples, but it does not explicitly state that sequences were reported. However, it does mention the collection of genotypic resistance data before the switch and also the report of archived RAMs to NRTIs or INSTIs before the switch of antiretroviral regimens. These all imply sequences were obtained but not reported."
37574435,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,We retrospectively reviewed the medical records of PWH who had viral rebound with plasma HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL and were switched to either dolutegravir combined with 2 NRTIs or BIC/FTC/TAF. The primary end point was re-achieving viral suppression within the first 48 weeks of switch. The association between NRTI-related resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) and virologic effectiveness was examined.,"The paper focuses on clinical outcomes and genotypic resistance data related to the effectiveness of switched antiretroviral regimens rather than in vitro drug susceptibility testing. It discusses resistance-associated mutations and their impact on treatment outcomes, but it does not mention any laboratory-based susceptibility assays."
37574435,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"This was a single-center, retrospective cohort study conducted in the National Taiwan University Hospital (NTUH). PWH who were aged ≥20 years and received HIV care with at least 3 months of ART prescription between 1 January 2016 and 30 March 2022 were eligible for the study. For eligible PWH, available results of plasma HIV RNA load (PVL) were retrieved from the electronic medical record database (National Taiwan University Hospital-Integrated Medical Database; NTUH-IMD).","This paper does not mention whether the sequences were made publicly available in any database, the paper is also retrospective cohort study, the focus is not on the sequence reporting."
37574435,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates as it does not mention any sequences from the paper were reported. It mentions sequence but refers to archived RAMs or genotypic resistance testing which do not provide details on GenBank numbers. Furthermore, the study is a retrospective cohort study focusing more on clinical outcomes and genotypic resistance data rather than on the detailed reporting of sequence data and its corresponding accession numbers. It does not specifically list any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced isolates, indicating that this information is not available in the text of the paper. It focuses on the effectiveness of treatment regimens and the presence of resistance mutations, but it does not detail the sequence data or their GenBank entries."
37574435,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"This was a single-center, retrospective cohort study conducted in the National Taiwan University Hospital (NTUH). PWH who were aged ≥20 years and received HIV care with at least 3 months of ART prescription between 1 January 2016 and 30 March 2022 were eligible for the study. For eligible PWH, available results of plasma HIV RNA load (PVL) were retrieved from the electronic medical record database (National Taiwan University Hospital-Integrated Medical Database; NTUH-IMD).",The paper focuses on clinical outcomes and resistance-associated mutations rather than detailing how many individuals had samples obtained for sequencing. It mentions genotypic resistance testing but does not provide a specific count of individuals from whom samples were obtained for sequencing.
37574435,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Taiwan,"This was a single-center, retrospective cohort study conducted in the National Taiwan University Hospital (NTUH).","The study was conducted in the National Taiwan University Hospital (NTUH), and all the patients were receiving HIV care in Taiwan according to the national HIV treatment guidelines in Taiwan, which implies that the sequenced samples (if any were reported) were obtained from Taiwan."
37574435,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2016 to 2022,"PWH who were aged ≥20 years and received HIV care with at least 3 months of ART prescription between 1 January 2016 and 30 March 2022 were eligible for the study. During the study period, we identified 79 PWH who met the criteria of viral rebound and switched to second-generation INSTI-containing regimens ( Fig. 1 ).",The paper mentions individuals who received HIV care with at least 3 months of ART prescription between 1 January 2016 and 30 March 2022 were eligible for the study.
37574435,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"This was a single-center, retrospective cohort study conducted in the National Taiwan University Hospital (NTUH). PWH who were aged ≥20 years and received HIV care with at least 3 months of ART prescription between 1 January 2016 and 30 March 2022 were eligible for the study. For eligible PWH, available results of plasma HIV RNA load (PVL) were retrieved from the electronic medical record database (National Taiwan University Hospital-Integrated Medical Database; NTUH-IMD). We then screened these PWH to identify those who were receiving ART and had ever achieved viral suppression (defined as PVL <50 copies/mL) before developing viral rebound (defined as PVL >1000 copies/mL).","The paper focuses on the clinical outcomes and genotypic resistance profiles without discussing cloning of samples prior to sequencing, and also the paper does not report any sequences."
37574435,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,,,"The paper discusses genotypic resistance testing and archived RAMs to NRTIs or INSTIs before the switch to new antiretroviral regimens, but it does not specify which individual HIV genes were sequenced. It mentions the use of the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database to determine genotypic resistance scores, which means sequences were obtained but not reported."
37574435,10,What method was used for sequencing?,,,The paper does not detail the sequencing methods used for obtaining the HIV sequences. It focuses more on the clinical outcomes and resistance mutations rather than the technical aspects of sequencing.
37574435,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,,,"The paper discusses the collection of plasma HIV RNA load results and resistance testing reports, indicating that plasma samples were involved, but it does not specify the exact type of clinical specimens that were sequenced."
37574435,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"we retrospectively reviewed the medical records of PWH who had viral rebound with plasma HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL and were switched to either dolutegravir combined with 2 NRTIs or BIC/FTC/TAF.; During the study period, we identified 79 PWH who met the criteria of viral rebound and switched to second-generation INSTI-containing regimens","The paper specifically mentions that the study included individuals who had viral rebound, which is a form of virological failure, 79 individuals were switched to second-generation INSTI-containing regimens, indicating sequences were obtained."
37574435,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This was a single-center, retrospective cohort study conducted in the National Taiwan University Hospital (NTUH).","The paper mentions that the study was a single-center, retrospective cohort study focusing on clinical outcomes and genotypic resistance data, without indicating that the patients were part of a clinical trial."
37574435,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"we retrospectively reviewed the medical records of PWH who had viral rebound with plasma HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL and were switched to either dolutegravir combined with 2 NRTIs or BIC/FTC/TAF. During the study period, we identified 79 PWH who met the criteria of viral rebound and switched to second-generation INSTI-containing regimens","The paper includes individuals whose clinical records were reviewed and had switched to either dolutegravir combined with 2 NRTIs or BIC/FTC/TAF, which means sequences were from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37574435,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,,,The paper does not specify the drug classes received by individuals before sample sequencing. It discusses the switching to second-generation INSTI-based regimens but does not detail the previous drug classes.
37574435,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,,,The paper does not specify the exact drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing. It discusses the switching to second-generation INSTI-based regimens but does not detail the previous drug regimens.
37585352,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We analysed near full-length HIV-1 *pol* sequences from proviral DNA for the presence of RPV-RAMs. This analysis included a dataset of 5805 sequences, of which 1281 (22%) were obtained from treatment-naive PWH, and the other 4524 (78%) were from ART-suppressed PWH.","The paper mentions the analysis of HIV-1 pol sequences from proviral DNA for the presence of RPV-RAMs, indicating that sequences were obtained from patient samples."
37585352,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Pre-existing rilpivirine resistance-associated mutations (RVP-RAMs) have been found to predict HIV-1 virological failure in those switching to long-acting injectable cabotegravir/rilpivirine. We here evaluated the prevalence of archived RPV-RAMs in a cohort of people living with HIV (PWH). We analysed near full-length HIV-1 pol sequences from proviral DNA for the presence of RPV-RAMs, which were defined according to the 2022 IAS--USA drug resistance mutation list and Stanford HIV drug resistance database.",The paper focuses on the prevalence of archived RPV-RAMs in patient samples and does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data.
37585352,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,HIV-1 sequences are available on request through the PANGEA consortium ( www.pangea-hiv.org ).,"HIV-1 sequences are available on request through the PANGEA consortium. All relevant data are within the paper and the Supplementary data, Figures and Tables. HIV-1 sequences are available on request through the PANGEA consortium.","The paper mentions that HIV-1 sequences are available on request through the PANGEA consortium, which suggests that the sequences were made publicly available."
37585352,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No accession numbers provided,"We here evaluated the prevalence of archived RPV-RAMs in a cohort of people living with HIV (PWH). We analysed near full-length HIV-1 *pol* sequences from proviral DNA for the presence of RPV-RAMs, which were defined according to the 2022 IAS--USA drug resistance mutation list and Stanford HIV drug resistance database. RPV-RAMs were described according to the 2022 updated IAS-USA drug-resistance mutation list and the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database. The level of rilpivirine resistance was predicted according to the Stanford HIV DRM penalty scores and resistance interpretation. All sequences with low-level, intermediate- level and high-level resistance according to Stanford and IAS algorithms were considered to have RPV-RAMs. The prevalence of RPV-RAMs was estimated in ART-naive individuals and in virally suppressed (HIV-1 RNA ≤400 copies/mL) individuals on ART. RPV-RAMs and their modes of action are shown in Table ​ Table1 1 .",The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates and does not mention depositing the sequences in GenBank.
37585352,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,5805,"This analysis included a dataset of 5805 sequences, of which 1281 (22%) were obtained from treatment-naive PWH, and the other 4524 (78%) were from ART-suppressed PWH.",The paper states that the analysis included a dataset of 5805 sequences obtained from treatment-naive and ART-suppressed individuals.
37585352,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Botswana,"Study participants were recruited from a large community HIV incidence trial described elsewhere that enrolled participants aged 16 to 64 years, followed for 30 months from 2013 to 2018. We included a total of 5805 (95.5%) proviral sequences from Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) participants. This study provides the first data for archived RPV-RAMs in ART-naive and ART-suppressed individuals in Botswana.",The paper mentions that the study participants were from the Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) and the samples were obtained from individuals in Botswana.
37585352,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2013 to 2018,"Study participants were recruited from a large community HIV incidence trial described elsewhere that enrolled participants aged 16 to 64 years, followed for 30 months from 2013 to 2018. In this analysis, we included a total of 5805 (95.5%) proviral sequences from Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) participants, of which 1281 proviral DNA sequences were obtained from the treatment-naive individuals, and 4524 were from ART-experienced participants with HIV-1 VL ≤400 copies/mL (Figure ​ (Figure1). 1 ).",The paper states that study participants were followed for 30 months from 2013 to 2018.
37585352,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"HIV-1 proviral DNA sequences were generated using next-generation sequencing (NGS) as previously described elsewhere. Briefly, a long-range HIV genotyping protocol was used to generate viral sequences and the first-round amplicon was subjected to the Illumina sequencing system. A single consensus sequence represented the population of viral quasispecies per each participant.",The paper describes the use of next-generation sequencing (NGS) and does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
37585352,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Pol,We analysed near full-length HIV-1 *pol* sequences from proviral DNA for the presence of RPV-RAMs.,The paper mentions the analysis of near full-length HIV-1 pol sequences from proviral DNA for the presence of RPV-RAMs.
37585352,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Illumina MiSeq and HiSeq platforms,"HIV-1 proviral DNA sequences were generated using next-generation sequencing (NGS) as previously described elsewhere. Briefly, a long-range HIV genotyping protocol was used to generate viral sequences and the first-round amplicon was subjected to the Illumina sequencing system. The NGS was performed at the Biopolymers Facility at Harvard Medical School ( https://genome.med.harvard.edu/ ) or by the PANGEA HIV consortium at the Wellcome Trust Sanger Institute ( http://www.sanger.ac.uk/ ) utilizing Illumina MiSeq and HiSeq platforms.",The paper mentions that the NGS was performed at the Biopolymers Facility at Harvard Medical School or by the PANGEA HIV consortium at the Wellcome Trust Sanger Institute utilizing Illumina MiSeq and HiSeq platforms.
37585352,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Proviral DNA,We analysed near full-length HIV-1 *pol* sequences from proviral DNA for the presence of RPV-RAMs. HIV-1 proviral DNA sequences were generated using next-generation sequencing (NGS) as previously described elsewhere.,The paper specifies that near full-length HIV-1 pol sequences were analyzed from proviral DNA for the presence of RPV-RAMs.
37585352,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"To control for the impact of archived HIV proviral DNA mutations, an ART-naive group was used. Hence the main objective of this study was to identify HIV drug resistance mutations using proviral DNA sequences from HIV-infected ART-naive and ART-suppressed individuals in Botswana. We did not include sequences for individuals experiencing virological failure since LA cabotegravir/rilpivirine is currently recommended only for virologically suppressed adults (HIV-1 RNA <50 copies/mL), and is not currently recommended for people with virological failure. RPV-RAMs and their modes of action are shown in Table 1.",The paper explicitly states that sequences from individuals experiencing virological failure were not included in the study.
37585352,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Study participants were recruited from a large community HIV incidence trial described elsewhere that enrolled participants aged 16 to 64 years, followed for 30 months from 2013 to 2018. This analysis included a dataset of 5805 sequences, of which 1281 (22%) were obtained from treatment-naive PWH, and the other 4524 (78%) were from ART-suppressed PWH. Among the ART-naive PWH, 856 (66.8%) were female, with a median age of 33 years (IQR: 27-42). Among the ART-suppressed PWH, 3267 (72.2%) were female, with a median age of 40 years (IQR 34-48) and 1257 (27.8%) were male, with the median age of 44 years (IQR 38-52) (Table 2). The most common ART regimens used were efavirenz+emtricitabine+tenofovir (EFV/FTC/TDF) (n=1792; 39.6%), lamivudine+nevirapine+zidovudine (3TC/NVP/ZDV) (n=719; 15.9%) and lamivudine+efavirenz+zidovudine (3TC/EFV/ZDV) (n=621; 13.7%).",The paper does not mention any clinical trials involving the patients in the study.
37585352,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Among the ART-suppressed PWH, 3267 (72.2%) were female, with a median age of 40 years (IQR 34-48) and 1257 (27.8%) were male, with the median age of 44 years (IQR 38-52) (Table 2). The most common ART regimens used were efavirenz+emtricitabine+tenofovir (EFV/FTC/TDF) (n=1792; 39.6%), lamivudine+nevirapine+zidovudine (3TC/NVP/ZDV) (n=719; 15.9%) and lamivudine+efavirenz+zidovudine (3TC/EFV/ZDV) (n=621; 13.7%).",The paper mentions that sequences were obtained from ART-experienced participants with HIV-1 VL ≤400 copies/mL.
37585352,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI","The most common ART regimens used were efavirenz+emtricitabine+tenofovir (EFV/FTC/TDF) (n=1792; 39.6%), lamivudine+nevirapine+zidovudine (3TC/NVP/ZDV) (n=719; 15.9%) and lamivudine+efavirenz+zidovudine (3TC/EFV/ZDV) (n=621; 13.7%).","The paper mentions the use of efavirenz (a NNRTI), emtricitabine (an NRTI), tenofovir (an NRTI), lamivudine (an NRTI), and zidovudine (an NRTI) in the study."
37585352,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"efavirenz, emtricitabine, tenofovir, lamivudine, nevirapine, zidovudine","The most common ART regimens used were efavirenz+emtricitabine+tenofovir (EFV/FTC/TDF) (n=1792; 39.6%), lamivudine+nevirapine+zidovudine (3TC/NVP/ZDV) (n=719; 15.9%) and lamivudine+efavirenz+zidovudine (3TC/EFV/ZDV) (n=621; 13.7%).","The paper lists the specific drug combinations used in the study, which include efavirenz, emtricitabine, tenofovir, lamivudine, nevirapine, and zidovudine."
37593123,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,Sequences of the HIV-1 pol gene region were obtained from ART-naïve HIV-positive individuals across 31 provincial-level administrative divisions between 2004 and 2022.,"The paper mentions that sequences of the HIV-1 pol gene region were obtained from ART-naïve HIV-positive individuals, which indicates that HIV sequences from patient samples were reported."
37593123,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"To predict susceptibility to 12 antiretroviral drugs, the research utilized the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database. The Cochran-Armitage trend test facilitated the analysis of changes in HIV-1 subtype/sub-subtype prevalence and TDR. This analysis was conducted in alignment with the progression of the National Free Antiretroviral Treatment Program's stages between 2004 and 2022.","The paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data. Instead, it uses the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database to predict susceptibility to 12 antiretroviral drugs, but this is not the same as reporting in vitro data."
37593123,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"Data verification steps were undertaken; the records from China CDC were scrutinized to ensure participants were indeed ART-naïve and HIV-infected. Furthermore, GenBank annotations and corresponding published articles were scrutinized to isolate those fulfilling the set criteria: the documentation of an ART-naïve HIV-1-infected population; the sampling year; and the recruitment site.","The paper describes the sequences used in the study, but it does not explicitly state that they were made publicly available. However, it does mention that GenBank annotations were scrutinized, which suggests that the sequences may have been made publicly available."
37593123,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not provided,No evidence,"The paper does not provide any specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It mentions that GenBank annotations were scrutinized, but it does not list any accession numbers."
37593123,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,"57,902","A total of 57,902 HIV-1 pol gene region sequences that covered 31 PLADs made up the final dataset. The study population consisted of 57,902 ART-naïve HIV-infected individuals, which were categorized according to the development stages of the NFATP.","The paper states that a total of 57,902 HIV-1 pol gene region sequences were included in the final dataset, and these sequences were obtained from ART-naïve HIV-infected individuals. Therefore, the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained is 57,902."
37593123,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"This comprehensive study aims to examine the alterations in HIV-1 subtypes or sub-subtypes and TDR among Chinese individuals, who have been diagnosed with HIV infection and are previously untreated with antiretroviral therapy (ART), across the span of 2004 to 2022. Sequences of the HIV-1 pol gene region were obtained from ART-naïve HIV-positive individuals across 31 provincial-level administrative divisions between 2004 and 2022. A total of 67,739 sequences were extracted from the HIV-1 pol gene region (HXB2 ( 6 ) positions 2,253--3,312) of ART naïve HIV-infected individuals between the period of 2004 and 2022. These sequences were procured from both the National Center for AIDS/STD Control and Prevention, China CDC and the HIV sequence databases of the Los Alamos National Laboratory.",The paper specifies that the study focuses on ART-naïve HIV-infected individuals in China and that the sequences were obtained from various provincial-level administrative divisions within China.
37593123,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2004 to 2022,"The data from 2004 to 2022 was stratified into five distinct sampling periods that aligned with the NFATP developmental stages in China. Sequences of the HIV-1 pol gene region were obtained from ART-naïve HIV-positive individuals across 31 provincial-level administrative divisions between 2004 and 2022. A total of 67,739 sequences were extracted from the HIV-1 pol gene region (HXB2 ( 6 ) positions 2,253--3,312) of ART naïve HIV-infected individuals between the period of 2004 and 2022.",The paper mentions that the study spans from 2004 to 2022 and that the samples were collected during this period.
37593123,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Individuals were included in the study based on the following criteria: age of 18 years and above, availability of the sampling year and provincial-level administrative division (PLAD), protease and at least the initial 238 amino acids of the reverse transcriptase (HXB2 positions 2,280--3,263) were available, and, if multiple sequences were available for a person, the earliest one was chosen.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It discusses the criteria for including individuals and the analysis methods but does not indicate that samples were cloned.
37593123,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Pol gene,"A total of 67,739 sequences were extracted from the HIV-1 pol gene region (HXB2 ( 6 ) positions 2,253--3,312) of ART naïve HIV-infected individuals between the period of 2004 and 2022. Sequences of the HIV-1 pol gene region were obtained from ART-naïve HIV-positive individuals across 31 provincial-level administrative divisions between 2004 and 2022.",The paper specifies that sequences of the HIV-1 pol gene region were obtained for analysis.
37593123,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not provided,No evidence,The paper does not specify the exact sequencing method used. It describes the analysis and data verification processes but does not detail the sequencing techniques.
37593123,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Not provided,No evidence,"The paper does not mention the specific type of clinical samples that were sequenced, such as plasma, whole blood, or others."
37593123,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"This comprehensive study aims to examine the alterations in HIV-1 subtypes or sub-subtypes and TDR among Chinese individuals, who have been diagnosed with HIV infection and are previously untreated with antiretroviral therapy (ART), across the span of 2004 to 2022. The study population consisted of 57,902 ART-naïve HIV-infected individuals, which were categorised according to the development stages of the NFATP. The study received approval from the Ethics Committee of the National Center for AIDS/STD Control and Prevention, China CDC, non ART-naïve population. Therefore, the individuals in our study had never been treated, and they were classified as ART-naïve.","The paper specifically mentions that the study focuses on ART-naïve HIV-infected individuals, meaning they had not received antiretroviral therapy, which includes those with virological failure on a treatment regimen."
37593123,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The study population consisted of 57,902 ART-naïve HIV-infected individuals, which were categorised according to the development stages of the NFATP. The distribution of the subjects over the years was as follows: 1,251 (2.2%) were enrolled between 2004 and 2007, 8,794 (15.2%) between 2008 and 2011, 21,467 (37.1%) between 2012 and 2015, 21,391 (36.9%) between 2016 and 2019, and 4,999 (8.6%) between 2020 and 2022. . A total of 67,739 sequences were extracted from the HIV-1 pol gene region (HXB2 positions 2,253--3,312) of ART naïve HIV-infected individuals between the period of 2004 and 2022. We have collected HIV sequences across multiple years, which indicates some of the years represented in this study were of routine care, meaning, they were not in a clinical trial.","The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It describes the  study population as ART-naïve individuals and provides details on the  stages of free antiretroviral treatment, suggesting they were not under clinical trial conditions."
37593123,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"This comprehensive study aims to examine the alterations in HIV-1 subtypes or sub-subtypes and TDR among Chinese individuals, who have been diagnosed with HIV infection and are previously untreated with antiretroviral therapy (ART), across the span of 2004 to 2022. The study population consisted of 57,902 ART-naïve HIV-infected individuals, which were categorised according to the development stages of the NFATP. The study received approval from the Ethics Committee of the National Center for AIDS/STD Control and Prevention, China CDC, non ART-naïve population. Therefore, the individuals in our study had never been treated, and they were classified as ART-naïve.","The paper specifically focuses on ART-naïve individuals, meaning they had never received antiretroviral drugs before the sequencing."
37593123,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"This comprehensive study aims to examine the alterations in HIV-1 subtypes or sub-subtypes and TDR among Chinese individuals, who have been diagnosed with HIV infection and are previously untreated with antiretroviral therapy (ART), across the span of 2004 to 2022. The study population consisted of 57,902 ART-naïve HIV-infected individuals, which were categorised according to the development stages of the NFATP. The study received approval from the Ethics Committee of the National Center for AIDS/STD Control and Prevention, China CDC, non ART-naïne population. Therefore, the individuals in our study had never been treated, and they were classified as ART-naïve.","The paper specifically mentions that the individuals were ART-naïve, meaning they had never received any antiretroviral drugs before the samples were obtained."
37593123,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"This comprehensive study aims to examine the alterations in HIV-1 subtypes or sub-subtypes and TDR among Chinese individuals, who have been diagnosed with HIV infection and are previously untreated with antiretroviral therapy (ART), across the span of 2004 to 2022. The study population consisted of 57,902 ART-naïve HIV-infected individuals, which were categorised according to the development stages of the NFATP. The study received approval from the Ethics Committee of the National Center for AIDS/STD Control and Prevention, China CDC, non ART-naïve population. Therefore, the individuals in our study had never been treated, and they were classified as ART-naïve.","The paper specifically focuses on ART-naïve individuals, meaning they had never received any antiretroviral drugs before the samples were obtained."
37626789,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"HIV-1 genotyping and sequencing was performed for the pol gene region using in-house assay validated at the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention. Children with VF (i.e., viral load ≥ 1000 copies/mL) were included for HIV drug resistance investigation.","The paper mentions that HIV-1 genotyping and sequencing was performed for the *pol* gene region. This indicates that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples, specifically children on first-line antiretroviral therapy in Ethiopia."
37626789,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"However, children were generally not considered despite their greater risk. Hence, this study was aimed to evaluate HIV-1 disease progression and drug resistance mutation among children on first-line antiretroviral therapy in Ethiopia. Method: A longitudinal study was conducted among 551 HIV-positive children (<15 years old) recruited between 2017 and 2019 at 40 antiretroviral treatment delivery sites in Ethiopia. Disease progression was retrospectively measured over a 12-year (2007–2019) follow-up as the progress towards immunosuppression.","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on HIV-1 disease progression and drug resistance mutations among children on first-line antiretroviral therapy in Ethiopia. The methods and results primarily discuss genotyping and sequencing of the *pol* gene region, as well as clinical and laboratory parameters related to disease progression and drug resistance, without any mention of in vitro testing of drug susceptibility."
37626789,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"Data were collected using interview, medical record review, and laboratory testing. Mothers/female guardians of the children were interviewed during the routine follow-up visit to collect data on sociodemographic and individual factors. Patient records were also abstracted to capture data on clinical and laboratory results such as hemoglobin, CD4 count, and history of ART regimen. Then, children with initial viral load ≥1000 copies/mL at baseline testing were further followed for six months for a second round of VL re-testing. Enhanced adherence and counseling were introduced as interventions during the six-month follow up.",The paper does not mention any publicly available sequences. It focuses on the clinical and laboratory findings of the study without providing specific accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
37626789,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on the clinical and laboratory findings of the study without mentioning the specific accession numbers.
37626789,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,551,A longitudinal study was conducted among 551 HIV-positive children (<15 years old) recruited between 2017 and 2019 at 40 antiretroviral treatment delivery sites in Ethiopia. HIV-infected children taking first-line ART for at least six months were included in the study.,The paper mentions that a longitudinal study was conducted among 551 HIV-positive children recruited between 2017 and 2019 at 40 antiretroviral treatment delivery sites in Ethiopia. This indicates that samples were obtained from 551 individuals for HIV sequencing.
37626789,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Ethiopia,"This study was aimed to evaluate HIV-1 disease progression and drug resistance mutation among children on first-line antiretroviral therapy in Ethiopia. HIV-1 drug resistance was 67.8% among children with a viral load >1000 copies/mL. It was relatively higher among children >10 years old (17.39%). Moreover, those who live in a rural area had 17.54% prevalence of HIVDR compared to the 14.17% among urban dwellers. HIVDR among ambulatory children was 20.0%, while this was 9.97% among bedridden children. About half (48%) of the children taking d4t + 3TC + EFV developed at least one type of drug resistance followed by d4t + 3TC + NVP (16.8%) and AZT + 3TC + NVP (13.6%). About half (50.84%) of the children on HAART for more than 100 months had at least one type of drug resistance mutation. At the regional level, higher rates of at least one drug resistance were found among children in the Afar (27.78%), Tigray (25.64%), and Gambella (19.35%) regions.",The paper focuses on a study conducted among children on first-line antiretroviral therapy in Ethiopia. All the children were from health facilities in Ethiopia.
37626789,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2017 to 2019,"A longitudinal study was conducted among 551 HIV-positive children (<15 years old) recruited between 2017 and 2019 at 40 antiretroviral treatment delivery sites in Ethiopia. The disease progression of HIV-infected children was retrospectively retrieved for 12 years (2007–2019) and measured as a change in immunosuppression (i.e., CD4 count).",The paper mentions that the study was conducted among children recruited between 2017 and 2019 at 40 antiretroviral treatment delivery sites in Ethiopia.
37626789,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,In-house assay validated by the Chinese Center for Disease Prevention and Control (CCDC) was used for amplification and sequencing of the pol gene of HIV-1 using an ABI-3730 DNA genetic analyzer. The pol gene region of the HIV-1 gene was sequenced by an ABI-3730 DNA genetic analyzer. The sequence was edited using a web-based ReCall.,"The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of an ABI-3730 DNA genetic analyzer for sequencing the *pol* gene region, but there is no mention of cloning the samples before sequencing."
37626789,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,pol gene,HIV-1 genotyping and sequencing was performed for the pol gene region using in-house assay validated at the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention. The pol gene region of the HIV-1 gene was sequenced by an ABI-3730 DNA genetic analyzer.,The paper specifies that HIV-1 genotyping and sequencing was performed for the *pol* gene region using an in-house assay validated at the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention.
37626789,10,What method was used for sequencing?,ABI-3730 DNA genetic analyzer,In-house assay validated by the Chinese Center for Disease Prevention and Control (CCDC) was used for amplification and sequencing of the pol gene of HIV-1 using an ABI-3730 DNA genetic analyzer. The pol gene region of the HIV-1 gene was sequenced by an ABI-3730 DNA genetic analyzer.,The paper mentions that an ABI-3730 DNA genetic analyzer was used for the sequencing of the *pol* gene region.
37626789,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Whole blood was collected from children using two ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA) coated test tubes of 3–5 mL each, and plasma was extracted using centrifugation at 2000 revolutions per minute (RPM). HIV-1 viral load tests at baseline and after 6-month follow-up were performed at selected regional laboratories in the country using two techniques (i.e., COBAS® AmpliPrep/CobasTaqman® and Abbott Real Time HIV-1).",The paper mentions that plasma was extracted using centrifugation at 2000 revolutions per minute (RPM) from whole blood collected from children using EDTA coated test tubes. Plasma is typically used for viral load testing and subsequent sequencing in HIV studies.
37626789,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Moreover, viral load (VL) testing was conducted for children twice at 6-month intervals between March and September 2019 to determine virologic failure (VF). Then, children with VF (i.e., viral load ≥ 1000 copies/mL) were included for HIV drug resistance investigation, given those with VL < 1000 copies/mL were considered as susceptible and excluded from HIVDR testing. HIV-1 disease progression and drug resistance mutations among children on first-line antiretroviral therapy in Ethiopia","The paper mentioned that children with virologic failure (VF) i.e., viral load ≥ 1000 copies/mL were included for HIV drug resistance investigation."
37626789,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"A longitudinal study was conducted among 551 HIV-positive children (<15 years old) recruited between 2017 and 2019 at 40 antiretroviral treatment delivery sites in Ethiopia. HIV-infected children taking first-line ART for at least six months were included in the study. Out of the 63 health facilities and 13,649 patients in the initial study, 40 had pediatric ART service and all the children from these health facilities (n = 554) were included.","The paper describes a longitudinal study conducted among HIV-positive children at 40 antiretroviral treatment delivery sites in Ethiopia, not a clinical trial."
37626789,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"HIV-infected children taking first-line ART for at least six months were included in the study. Out of the 63 health facilities and 13,649 patients in the initial study, 40 had pediatric ART service and all the children from these health facilities (n = 554) were included.",The paper mentioned that HIV-infected children taking first-line ART for at least six months were included in the study. This indicates that these children had previously received ARV drugs before the samples were obtained for sequencing.
37626789,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI","A majority of the children (80%) were taking one of the three HAARTs regimens, which were Zidovudine (AZT) + Lamivudine(3TC) + Nevirapine (NVP) (35.9%), Stavudine(D4T) + Lamivudine(3TC) + Nevirapine (NVP) (30.3%), and Zidovudine (AZT) + Lamivudine(3TC) + Efavirenz (EFV) (14.0%). About half (47.5%) of the children had at least a one-time HAART substitution history.","The paper lists the specific antiretroviral regimens that the children were taking, which include drugs from the NRTI and NNRTI classes."
37626789,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Zidovudine (AZT), Lamivudine (3TC), Nevirapine (NVP), Stavudine (D4T), Efavirenz (EFV), Abacavir (ABC), Tenofovir (TDF)","A majority of the children (80%) were taking one of the three HAARTs regimens, which were Zidovudine (AZT) + Lamivudine(3TC) + Nevirapine (NVP) (35.9%), Stavudine(D4T) + Lamivudine(3TC) + Nevirapine (NVP) (30.3%), and Zidovudine (AZT) + Lamivudine(3TC) + Efavirenz (EFV) (14.0%). About half (47.5%) of the children had at least a one-time HAART substitution history. Overall, 63% of the substitutions were from those who were taking Abacavir (ABC) + Lamivudine(3TC) + Nevirapine (NVP) followed by Abacavir (ABC) + Lamivudine(3TC) + Efavirenz (EFV) (21.2%), and Tenofovir (TDF) + Lamivudine(3TC) + Nevirapine (NVP) (16.0%).","The paper lists the specific antiretroviral drugs that were part of the regimens the children were taking, including Zidovudine (AZT), Lamivudine (3TC), Nevirapine (NVP), Stavudine (D4T), Efavirenz (EFV), Abacavir (ABC), and Tenofovir (TDF)."
37632026,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We studied acquired HIVDR (ADR) patterns and describe potentially active drugs after first- and second-line failure in resource-limited settings (RLS) like Cameroon; All 10 regions of the country were represented, with participants from the centre: n = 436 (57.4%); northwest: n = 86 (11.3%); and littoral: n = 85/745 (11.2%) regions. Of the 759 participants, 575 (75.8%) were failing non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI)-based first-line ART, while 184 (24.2%) were failing protease inhibitor (PI/r)-based second-line  our study on acquired HIVDR (ADR) patterns and drug susceptibility after treatment failure involved genotypic resistance testing (GRT), which indicates that HIV sequences were analyzed to identify drug resistance mutations. The paper describes the genetic diversity of HIV-1 in the population, indicating that sequences were obtained and analyzed.","The paper mentions that HLV-1 genotypic profiles were analyzed and sequences were obtained from patient records, indicating that sequences were analyzed and reported The study involved genotypic resistance testing, which requires obtaining sequences from patient samples in order to identify drug resistance mutations. The paper also discusses the genetic diversity of HIV-1 in the population, indicating that sequences were obtained and analyzed. Overall, the evidence suggests that the paper does report HIV sequences from patient samples without directly saying that HIV sequences from patient samples were reported."
37632026,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We studied acquired HIVDR (ADR) patterns and describe potentially active drugs after first- and second-line failure in resource-limited settings (RLS) like Cameroon;  In this resource-limited setting (RLS), ADR is high in ART-failing patients. PI/r strategies remain potent backbones for second-line ART, while only DRV/r remains very potent despite second-line failure.","The paper focuses on clinical and genotypic resistance testing data from patient samples who have been treated and have experienced virological failure, but it does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility assays or results. The study discusses drug resistance patterns and potentially effective drugs based on genotypic data, but it does not provide in vitro susceptibility testing results for the drugs used in the study. The emphasis is on genotypic resistance testing and clinical outcomes rather than laboratory-based susceptibility assays."
37632026,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"Sequences generated for the purpose of this study were submitted to Genbank under the following accession numbers: MK580543-MK580618 ; JF273935-JF273965 ; JQ796152-JQ796169 , MK867721-MK867755 ; and OQ985493-OQ985958.","The paper explicitly states that sequences generated for the purpose of the study were submitted to Genbank under specific accession numbers, confirming that the sequences were made publicly available."
