37632071,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not applicable,"Switching to bictegravir, emtricitabine, and tenofovir alafenamide (BIC/FTC/TAF) from other antiretroviral regimens is safe and effective for virologically suppressed people living with HIV (PLWH). The term virological suppression includes both low but detectable HIV viremia and undetectable HIV viremia, and the latter is possibly associated with a lower immune activation state. Herein, we describe a 24-month follow-up of experienced PLWH with plasma HIV RNA undetectable or detectable < 50 copies/ml switching to BIC/FTC/TAF. A previous 12-month monitoring was available, and the factors correlated with treatment efficacy. This retrospective multicenter study included PLWH who switched to BIC/FTC/TAF in the period of 2019–2022, and who were HBsAg and HCV RNA negative. The follow-up study times were 6 (T6), 12 (T12), 18 (T18), and 24 (T24) months after the switch (T0).","The paper mentions that most patients (61.9%) were on a previous INSTI-based regimen before switching to BIC/FTC/TAF, but it does not provide a comprehensive list of all drug classes received because it does not report any sequencing. The study focuses on the outcomes of the switch rather than on the details of the previous treatments received by the patients. Thus, the answer is Not applicable."
