37649807,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"No, the paper does not report HIV sequences from patient samples.","We used retrospective longitudinal VL data from the National AIDs and STIs Control Programme (NASCOP) VL database of PLHIV on ART to assess LLV prevalence and subsequent virologic outcomes. We included PLHIV ≥15 years of age on standard first- or second-line ART for ≥24 weeks who had only plasma VL results between January 2015 and December 2021. Key available demographic variables, ART regimen, and VL results were extracted from the NASCOP VL database, a centralized source of reported VL data for all patients who receive VL testing from any of the 10 central testing labs responsible for VL testing in the country.","The paper mentions that it analyzes viral load (VL) data, which indicates the presence of the virus in the blood, but it does not mention sequencing of HIV samples from patients. The focus is on virologic outcomes and risk factors associated with low-level viremia (LLV). This means that while HIV was detected and quantified in patient samples, the sequences of the virus were not reported in this study. It specifically states that the key available demographic variables, ART regimen, and VL results were extracted from the NASCOP VL database, indicating that it used existing data without reporting new sequences. Therefore, the answer is no, the paper does not report HIV sequences from patient samples.. The study uses retrospective longitudinal viral load (VL) data to assess low-level viremia (LLV) prevalence and virologic outcomes in people living with HIV (PLHIV) on antiretroviral therapy (ART). This suggests that VL data were analyzed, but it does not explicitly state that HIV sequences from patient samples were reported."
