37662576,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The HIV-1 protease is one of several common key targets of combination drug therapies for human immunodeficiency virus infection and acquired immunodeficiency syndrome. During the progression of the disease, some individual patients acquire drug resistance due to mutational hotspots on the viral proteins targeted by combination drug therapies. It has recently been discovered that drug-resistant mutations accumulate on the 'flap region' of the HIV-1 protease, which is a critical dynamic region involved in nonspecific polypeptide binding during invasion and infection of the host cell.",The paper discusses the dynamics and drug resistance of the HIV-1 protease but does not mention any patient samples from which HIV sequences were obtained. It focuses on computational and simulation studies rather than clinical specimens.
