37674678,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"we sequenced and analyzed 1,161 samples obtained from 1,393 HIV-1 infected individuals in our research. Participants were enrolled at the voluntary counseling testing clinics (VCT) in Nanjing.","The study subjects were enrolled at voluntary counseling testing clinics (VCT) in Nanjing. The paper clearly states that the study took place in Nanjing, China, indicating the country of origin for the sequenced samples."
37674678,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2019 to 2021,"A total of 1,393 participants who were newly diagnosed with HIV-1 and had not received ART between January 2019 and December 2021 were enrolled in this study.","The paper mentions that participants who were newly diagnosed with HIV-1 and had not received ART between January 2019 and December 2021 were enrolled in this study, indicating the time frame during which the samples were obtained."
37674678,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The target fragment of 1,059 bp in the pol gene (HXB2:2253–3,312) was amplified using nested polymerase chain reaction (PCR). PrimeScriptTM One Step RT-PCR Ver. 2.0 (TakaRa, China) was used for the first-round PCR procedure and cDNA synthesis. Nested PCR was performed with Ex Taq (TaKaRa, China). The PCR products were sent to Sangon Biotechnology Co., Ltd. for automatic DNA sequencing using ABI 3730XL. The PCR protocol and primers used were as previously described",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of nested PCR amplification and direct sequencing of the PCR products.
37674678,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,pol gene,"HIV-1 pol gene sequence was obtained by viral RNA extraction and nested PCR amplification. The target fragment of 1,059 bp in the pol gene (HXB2:2253–3,312) was amplified using nested polymerase chain reaction (PCR).","The paper specifies that the study focused on the sequencing of the HIV-1 pol gene, which is mentioned multiple times throughout the methods and results sections."
