37755428,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) in perinatally HIV-infected infants may occur because of infection with drug-resistant virus, exposure to sub-therapeutic levels of antiretroviral drugs in breastmilk or during infant postnatal prophylaxis (PNP) [6–8]. Prior studies have shown high levels of resistance in ART-naive infants, especially to nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) used for PNP and to nucleot(s)ides reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) [9–11]. However, to the best of our knowledge, there is the first study reporting detectable DTG resistance in an ART-naive infant.","The paper discusses a case of HIV resistance in a perinatally infected infant and mentions HIV drug resistance in the context of a national survey in Haiti, indicating that HIV sequences from patient samples were involved in the study. The study describes the detection of drug resistance mutations in the HIV genomes of infants, which implies that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples during the survey of HIV drug resistance in Haiti. This is evidence that the paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples, specifically from infants diagnosed with HIV through the national early infant diagnosis program in Haiti. Additionally, the study details the sequencing process and the successful quality-assured genotypes obtained from the infant specimens, further confirming the involvement of patient-derived HIV sequences in the research. Overall, these aspects indicate that the paper does report HIV sequences from patient samples, specifically focusing on the genetic analysis of HIV resistance in infants born to mothers receiving antiretroviral therapy. The evidence points to the fact that the study was conducted on real patient samples, which supports the conclusion that the paper does report HIV sequences from patient samples. Specifically, it mentions that HIV drug resistance was detected during a national survey involving remnant dried blood spots from infants, indicating that samples were collected from patients diagnosed with HIV. Furthermore, the successful genotyping of HIV regions also suggests that HIV sequences were derived from infected infants in the study. All these details confirm that the paper does report HIV sequences from patient samples, making the answer unequivocally 'Yes'. The paper discusses HIV drug resistance in a specific population (perinatally infected infants) and describes the genotypic analysis of HIV from patient samples, supporting this conclusion. The evidence collected from the paper, including the mention of 143 remnant specimens collected and the successful genotyping of HIV regions, overwhelmingly supports the answer 'Yes' to the question of whether the paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples. The case report serves as a demonstration of the application of HIVDR surveillance, indicating that the study involved the sequencing of HIV from infants."
37755428,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,We report a case of dolutegravir resistance detected in a treatment-naive perinatally HIV-infected infant whose mother was receiving tenofovir/lamivudine/dolutegravir. This case was detected during a national survey of HIV drug resistance in Haiti amongst infants testing positive for HIV through the national early infant diagnosis program between April 2020 and March 2021.,"The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on a clinical case of drug resistance detected in an HIV-infected infant during a national survey in Haiti, and it does not provide laboratory-based data on the susceptibilities of the virus to drugs in vitro. The absence of any details regarding in vitro experiments or susceptibility testing methods indicates that the paper does not report this type of data. The study primarily discusses the detection of resistant mutations in the context of a clinical case and public health survey without delving into the specifics of in vitro susceptibilities, confirming that in vitro drug susceptibility data is not a focus of the paper. The primary focus of the study is on the clinical implications of drug resistance rather than on laboratory-based susceptibility testing, which further supports the conclusion that the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data. Moreover, the mention of resistance mutations and their clinical relevance suggests that the study is more concerned with the effects of drug exposure and resistance in a real-world setting rather than determining susceptibilities through in vitro methods."
37755428,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) in perinatally HIV-infected infants may occur because of infection with drug-resistant virus, exposure to sub-therapeutic levels of antiretroviral drugs in breastmilk or during infant postnatal prophylaxis (PNP) [6–8]. Prior studies have shown high levels of resistance in ART-naive infants, especially to nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) used for PNP and to nucleot(s)ides reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) [9–11]. However, to the best of our knowledge, there is the first study reporting detectable DTG resistance in an ART-naive infant.","The paper does not mention any public deposition of the sequences obtained during the study. It focuses on the clinical case and the survey results without providing information on whether the sequences have been made publicly available. The absence of any mention of GenBank accession numbers or other public databases indicates that the sequences were not made publicly available. The study describes the sequencing process and the results of the surveillance but does not provide details on public availability of the sequence data, confirming that this information is not part of the paper. Moreover, the focus on the clinical implications and the specific case report suggests that the authors did not prioritize the public sharing of sequence data, further supporting the conclusion that the paper does not indicate any public availability of the sequences sequenced during the study. the focus on the clinical implications and the specific case report suggests that the authors did not prioritize the public sharing of sequence data, further supporting the conclusion that the paper does not indicate any public availability of the sequences sequenced during the study."
37755428,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not provided,No evidence.,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequences obtained during the study. It focuses on the clinical details and the survey results without mentioning the specific accession numbers needed to retrieve the sequences from GenBank. The absence of any reference to accession numbers or public databases indicates that this information is not included in the paper. The study describes the sequencing process and the results of the survey but does not provide technical details regarding the GenBank accession numbers for the sequences, confirming that these numbers are not part of the paper. The focus on the clinical implications and the specific case report suggests that the authors did not prioritize sharing the accession numbers, further supporting the conclusion that the paper does not provide this information. The authors' emphasis on the clinical implications, rather than on technical details regarding the sequences, indicates that the GenBank accession numbers were not deemed relevant or necessary for the scope of the study."
37755428,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,143,"During the survey period, 5185 infants were born to HIV-infected mothers; 276 (5.3%) of the infants tested positive for HIV. Of these, 143 (52%) had remnant specimens available for HIVDR testing.","The paper states that during the survey, remnant specimens were available from 143 infants for HIVDR testing, indicating that samples were obtained from this number of individuals. The mention of 143 remnant specimens available for testing directly answers the question about the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing. The evidence collected clearly states the number of infants with available specimens for HIVDR testing, confirming that 143 is the correct answer to the question regarding the number of individuals involved. The paper provides a clear count of the samples that were available for the purpose of HIV drug resistance testing, which correlates to the number of individuals from whom those samples were obtained, establishing a definitive answer to the query about the number of individuals in the study."
37755428,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Haiti,We report a case of dolutegravir resistance detected in a treatment-naive perinatally HIV-infected infant whose mother was receiving tenofovir/lamivudine/dolutegravir. This case was detected during a national survey of HIV drug resistance in Haiti amongst infants testing positive for HIV through the national early infant diagnosis program between April 2020 and March 2021.,"The paper mentions that the survey of HIV drug resistance was conducted in Haiti, and the case of dolutegravir resistance was detected in an infant identified in Haiti. This indicates that the sequenced samples were obtained from Haiti. The evidence collected indicates that the study was conducted in Haiti, and the samples were collected from infants diagnosed with HIV through the national early infant diagnosis program in the country, confirming that Haiti is the only country mentioned as the source of the sequenced samples. The focus on the national survey conducted in Haiti and the specific details regarding the context of the study further reinforce that Haiti is the country from which the sequenced samples were obtained, establishing a clear answer to the question."
37755428,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2020-2021,This case was detected during a national survey of HIV drug resistance in Haiti amongst infants testing positive for HIV through the national early infant diagnosis program between April 2020 and March 2021.,"The paper specifies that the samples were collected during a national survey of HIV drug resistance in Haiti conducted between April 2020 and March 2021. This timeframe indicates the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained. The evidence collected clearly states the period during which the samples were collected, confirming that 2020 to 2021 is the correct answer to the question regarding the years of sample collection."
37755428,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Following, WHO survey guidance [14], Haiti conducted a survey of HIV drug resistance in infants between April 2020 and March 2021. Nucleic acids were extracted from remnant dried blood spot specimens collected at the time of HIV diagnosis and sequenced using a Sanger-based method at the WHO-designated laboratory, Service de Virologie, University Hospital of Martinique in Fort de France.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of remnant dried blood spot specimens and Sanger-based sequencing, which typically does not involve cloning. The absence of any mention of cloning or specific cloning methods indicates that the samples were not cloned before sequencing."
37755428,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Protease/reverse transcriptase and integrase regions,"Of these, 143 (52%) had remnant specimens available for HIVDR testing of which 121 (85%) and 105 (73%) yielded a successful quality-assured genotype for protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) and integrase regions, respectively.","The paper specifies that successful quality-assured genotypes were obtained for the protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) and integrase regions, indicating that these specific HIV genes were sequenced. The evidence collected clearly states the regions that were successfully genotyped, confirming that both the PR/RT and integrase regions were sequenced as part of the study."
37755428,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"Nucleic acids were extracted from remnant dried blood spot specimens collected at the time of HIV diagnosis and sequenced using a Sanger-based method at the WHO-designated laboratory, Service de Virologie, University Hospital of Martinique in Fort de France.","The paper mentions that the sequencing was performed using a Sanger-based method at a WHO-designated laboratory, indicating that Sanger sequencing was the method used for sequencing the HIV genomes. The evidence collected clearly states the method used for sequencing, confirming that Sanger sequencing is the correct answer to the question regarding the sequencing method."
37755428,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Dried blood spot specimens,"Nucleic acids were extracted from remnant dried blood spot specimens collected at the time of HIV diagnosis and sequenced using a Sanger-based method at the WHO-designated laboratory, Service de Virologie, University Hospital of Martinique in Fort de France.","The paper specifies that nucleic acids were extracted from remnant dried blood spot specimens collected at the time of HIV diagnosis, indicating that this was the type of clinical specimen sequenced in the study. The evidence collected clearly states the type of specimens used for sequencing, confirming that dried blood spot specimens are the correct answer."
37755428,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,We report a case of dolutegravir resistance detected in a treatment-naive perinatally HIV-infected infant whose mother was receiving tenofovir/lamivudine/dolutegravir. This case was detected during a national survey of HIV drug resistance in Haiti amongst infants testing positive for HIV through the national early infant diagnosis program between April 2020 and March 2021.,"The paper mentions that the case of dolutegravir resistance was detected in a treatment-naive perinatally HIV-infected infant. This indicates that the infant had not experienced virological failure on a treatment regimen, as they were treatment-naive. The focus on the treatment-naive status of the infant and the context of the national survey further reinforces that no sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen. The evidence collected indicates that the infant had received postnatal prophylaxis (PNP) and was treatment-naive, which clarifies that the infant had not previously experienced virological failure on a treatment regimen."
37755428,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,We report a case of dolutegravir resistance detected in a treatment-naive perinatally HIV-infected infant whose mother was receiving tenofovir/lamivudine/dolutegravir. This case was detected during a national survey of HIV drug resistance in Haiti amongst infants testing positive for HIV through the national early infant diagnosis program between April 2020 and March 2021.,"The paper does not mention any clinical trials involving the patients. The focus is on a case report and a national survey of HIV drug resistance, not on a controlled clinical trial setting. The absence of any mention of clinical trials indicates that the patients were not in a clinical trial. The study discusses the detection of drug resistance in the context of a national survey, which is different from a clinical trial, confirming that the answer is No."
37755428,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"The mean age of the infants was 8.2months [95% confidence interval (CI) 7.3–9.2], and the majority (62.7%, 95% CI 54.3–70.3%) had experienced exposure to antiretroviral drugs for PMTCT. Most mothers had received DTG-based ART (70%, 95% CI 58.9–79.5), and most infants (55.2%, 95% CI 46.5–62.3) had been breastfed or were breastfeeding at the time of specimen collection.","The paper mentions that the majority of infants had experienced exposure to antiretroviral drugs for PMTCT, and the mother had received DTG-based ART, indicating that some individuals in the study had previously received ARV drugs. The mention of exposure to antiretroviral drugs for PMTCT and the mother's receipt of DTG-based ART clearly indicates that ARV drugs were received, confirming that the answer is Yes."
37755428,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, Integrase Inhibitors","The high prevalence of NRTI and NNRTI resistance is consistent with findings of similar studies [9–11] and underscores the need to fast track adoption of optimal paediatric ART, including regimens using DTG, and close treatment monitoring to ensure successful treatment outcomes in this population [1].","The paper discusses the prevalence of resistance to non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) and nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs), as well as resistance to integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs), indicating that individuals had received drugs from these classes before sample sequencing. The mention of resistance to NNRTIs, NRTIs, and INSTIs aligns with the respective drug classes, confirming that these classes were involved."
37755428,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Dolutegravir, Zidovudine","We report a case of dolutegravir resistance detected in a treatment-naive perinatally HIV-infected infant whose mother was receiving tenofovir/lamivudine/dolutegravir. This case was detected during a national survey of HIV drug resistance in Haiti amongst infants testing positive for HIV through the national early infant diagnosis program between April 2020 and March 2021. The infant's mother had initiated ART (tenofovir/lamivudine/dolutegravir) 6 months before delivery. Information on the mother's prior ART and prior HIV diagnosis (if different from the one at ART initiation during pregnancy) was unavailable. The mother was generally nonadherent to clinical appointments and had detectable HIV viral load 3 weeks before delivery (7761 copies/ml); no drug resistance test was conducted on the mother's virus. Eight months after delivery, the mother's viral load was less than 1000 copies/ml.","The paper mentions that the mother was receiving tenofovir, lamivudine, and dolutegravir, and the infant had received ZDV-based PNP, indicating that these specific drugs were involved in the treatment histories of individuals in the study. The evidence collected explicitly lists the drugs that were part of the treatment regimens, confirming that tenofovir, lamivudine, dolutegravine and Zidovudine were the drugs received by individuals in the study."
37775947,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Stored plasma from participants with entry HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL were analysed using NGS (llumina MiSeq). A plasma sample, taken within 60 days before enrolment was obtained from all participants who provided consent for stored specimens.",The methods section mentions that plasma samples were obtained from participants with entry HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL and analyzed using NGS. This indicates that HIV sequences from patient samples were reported.
37775947,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Stored plasma from participants with entry HIV RNA 	n>1000 copies/mL were analysed using NGS (llumina MiSeq). Pre-existing drug resistance was defined using the mutations considered by the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database (HIVDB v8.6) to calculate the genotypic susceptibility score (GSS, estimating the number of active drugs) for the first-line regimen at the detection threshold windows of 
>20%,
>5%, and >2% of the viral population. Survival analysis was conducted to evaluate the association between the GSS and risk of TF (viral load >200 copies/mL plus treatment change).",The paper focuses on genotypic resistance and susceptibility scores rather than in vitro susceptibility testing.
37775947,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"Stored plasma from participants with entry HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL were analysed using NGS (llumina MiSeq). A plasma sample, taken within 60 days before enrolment was obtained from all participants who provided consent for stored specimens. Methods for sample preparation, amplification of viral RNA and NGS, identification of DRM by means of VirVarSeq, and determination of HIV subtype have been described elsewhere [25,26].","The paper does not mention that the sequences were made publicly available. It mentions the sequence read were analyzed with virVarSeq, which means the sequence were not public, because they did not say the sequence data were shared to public database."
37775947,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence,The GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates are not mentioned in the provided content of the paper.
37775947,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,1819,"Overall, of the 1819 participants with NGS data at the ≥200 read depth, 1380 (76%) who started ART with three or more drugs, either in the immediate or the deferred arm, and had virological follow-up of ≥1 month were included in this analysis","The text mentions that there were 1819 participants with NGS data at the ≥200 read depth, which indicates the total number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing."
37775947,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not reported,"The START trial, conducted by the International Network for Strategic Initiatives in Global HIV Trials, enrolled ART-naïve participants with HIV between April 2009 and December 2013. The study design and data collection plan for START has previously been reported [23, 24].","The specific countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained are not listed in the provided content. It only mentions that three continet were included: North America, South America and Africa."
37775947,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2009-2013,"The START trial, conducted by the International Network for Strategic Initiatives in Global HIV Trials, enrolled ART-naïve participants with HIV between April 2009 and December 2013. Stored plasma from participants with entry HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL were analysed using NGS (llumina MiSeq).","The study mentions that the START trial enrolled participants between April 2009 and December 2013, and the samples for sequencing were obtained during this period."
37775947,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Stored plasma from participants with entry HIV RNA 	n>1000 copies/mL were analysed using NGS (llumina MiSeq). A plasma sample, taken within 60 days before enrolment was obtained from all participants who provided consent for stored specimens. Methods for sample preparation, amplification of viral RNA and NGS, identification of DRM by means of VirVarSeq, and determination of HIV subtype have been described elsewhere [25,26].","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It mentions that  the methods for sample preparation, amplification of viral RNA or NGS, which indicated that no cloning information was provided which means no cloning."
37775947,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Pol,"From the output, we extracted amino acid frequencies in the pol gene from amino acid position 1--935 where positions 1--99 encode protease protein, positions 100--659 encode reverse transcriptase protein, and positions 660--935 partially encode integrase protein (our amplicon did not cover positions 936--947) [25].","The content specifies that amino acid frequencies in the *pol* gene from positions 1--935 were extracted, which indicates that this gene was sequenced."
37775947,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Next-generation sequencing (NGS) using Illumina MiSeq,Stored plasma from participants with entry HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL were analysed using NGS (llumina MiSeq).,The paper mentions that Sequencing was performed using next-generation sequencing (NGS) and specifically states that the Illumina MiSeq platform was used.
37775947,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Stored plasma from participants with entry HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL were analysed using NGS (llumina MiSeq). A plasma sample, taken within 60 days before enrolment was obtained from all participants who provided consent for stored specimens.",The paper mentions that the stored plasma from participants was used for sequencing.
37775947,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Overall, of the 1819 participants with NGS data at the ≥200 read depth, 1380 (76%) who started ART with three or more drugs, either in the immediate or the deferred arm, and had virological follow-up of ≥1 month were included in this analysis.","The paper discusses the risk of treatment failure (TF) and mentions the inclusion of participants who started ART with three or more drugs and had virological follow-up, indicating that some sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure."
37775947,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"The START trial, conducted by the International Network for Strategic Initiatives in Global HIV Trials, enrolled ART-naïve participants with HIV between April 2009 and December 2013. The study design and data collection plan for START has previously been reported [23, 24].","The paper mentions that the study is part of the START trial, which indicates that the patients were in a clinical trial."
37775947,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"Baseline NGS data were available for 1380 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve participants enrolled over 2009–2013. First-line ART included a non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI) in 976 (71%), a boosted protease inhibitor in 297 (22%), or an integrase strand transfer inhibitor in 107 (8%). Overall, of the 1819 participants with NGS data at the ≥200 read depth, 1380 (76%) who started ART with three or more drugs, either in the immediate or the deferred arm, and had virological follow-up of ≥1 month were included in this analysis. Up to 17% of participants initiated ART with a GSS <3 on the basis of NGS data. Survival analysis was conducted to evaluate the association between the GSS and risk of TF (viral load >200 copies/mL plus treatment change).","The paper mentioned that the baseline NGS data were available for 1380 antiretroviral therapy(ART)-naïve participants, which means no individual in this  study had deomstrated ARV drug exposure before sample sequencing."
37775947,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"None, all participants were ART-naïve","Baseline NGS data were available for 1380 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve participants enrolled over 2009–2013. First-line ART included a non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI) in 976 (71%), a boosted protease inhibitor in 297 (22%), or an integrase strand transfer inhibitor in 107 (8%). Overall, of the 1819 participants with NGS data at the ≥200 read depth, 1380 (76%) who started ART with three or more drugs, either in the immediate or the deferred arm, and had virological follow-up of ≥1 month were included in this analysis. Up to 17% of participants initiated ART with a GSS <3 on the basis of NGS data. Survival analysis was conducted to evaluate the association between the GSS and risk of TF (viral load >200 copies/mL plus treatment change).","All participants in this study were ARV-naïve at the time of enrolment, so they had not received any drugs before Sample sequencing."
37775947,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"None, all participants were ART-naïve","Baseline NGS data were available for 1380 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve participants enrolled over 2009–2013. First-line ART included a non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI) in 976 (71%), a boosted protease inhibitor in 297 (22%), or an integrase strand transfer inhibitor in 107 (8%). Overall, of the 1819 participants with NGS data at the ≥200 read depth, 1380 (76%) who started ART with three or more drugs, either in the immediate or the deferred arm, and had virological follow-up of ≥1 month were included in this analysis. Up to 17% of participants initiated ART with a GSS <3 on the basis of NGS data. Survival analysis was conducted to evaluate the association between the GSS and risk of TF (viral load >200 copies/mL plus treatment change).","All participants in this study were ARV-naïve at the time of enrolment, so they had not received any drugs prior to the sample sequencing."
37817087,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Protease (PR) and reverse-transcriptase (RT) sequences were obtained from 4559 (39.6%) cases, and PR, RT and integrase (IN) sequences were obtained from 3097 (26.9%) cases. The sequences were analyzed with data from the national HIV reports.","The paper mentions that sequences were obtained from patient samples, specifically stating the number of cases from which sequences were obtained for various HIV genes."
37817087,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on the sequencing of HIV genomes and the analysis of resistance mutations.
37817087,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,All datasets including sequences are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.,"The paper states that all datasets including sequences are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request, indicating that they are not publicly available."
37817087,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No,No evidence,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
37817087,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,4559,"Protease (PR) and reverse-transcriptase (RT) sequences were obtained from 4559 (39.6%) cases, and PR, RT and integrase (IN) sequences were obtained from 3097 (26.9%) cases.","The paper states that sequences were obtained from 4559 cases for PR and RT, and from 3097 cases for PR, RT, and IN."
37817087,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Germany,"From 2017 to 2020, 11,527 new HIV diagnoses were reported in Germany to the Robert Koch Institute (RKI). Molecular surveillance of new HIV diagnoses with a focus on prevalence and type of resistance associated mutations and the subtype of circulating viruses is mandatory.",The paper mentions that the study was conducted in Germany and the samples were obtained from new HIV diagnoses reported in the country.
37817087,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2017 to 2020,"From 2017 to 2020, 11,527 new HIV diagnoses were reported in Germany to the Robert Koch Institute (RKI). In the four-year period from 2017 to 2020, 11,527 new HIV diagnoses were reported to the RKI. PR and RT sequences were obtained from 4559 (39.6%) of these new diagnoses. In 3097 (26.9%) of the reported cases, the relevant IN genome region could also be amplified and examined in addition (PR, RT, IN).",The paper states that the samples were obtained from new HIV diagnoses reported in Germany from 2017 to 2020.
37817087,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The cDNA then served as a template for the amplification of three regions of the HIV genome, which contain all major resistance-associated positions. The genomic regions of protease (PR, amino acids 9--99), reverse transcriptase (RT, amino acids 1-252) and integrase (IN, amino acids 1-279) were amplified according to previously published protocols.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
37817087,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Protease (PR), Reverse Transcriptase (RT), Integrase (IN)","Protease (PR) and reverse-transcriptase (RT) sequences were obtained from 4559 (39.6%) cases, and PR, RT and integrase (IN) sequences were obtained from 3097 (26.9%) cases. The cDNA then served as a template for the amplification of three regions of the HIV genome, which contain all major resistance-associated positions. The genomic regions of protease (PR, amino acids 9--99), reverse transcriptase (RT, amino acids 1-252) and integrase (IN, amino acids 1-279) were amplified according to previously published protocols.","The paper mentions that sequences were obtained for the genes coding for Protease (PR), Reverse Transcriptase (RT), and Integrase (IN)."
37817087,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Illumina NGS method (MiSeq),The amplicons were sequenced using an Illumina NGS method (MiSeq).,The paper specifies that the amplicons were sequenced using an Illumina NGS method (MiSeq).
37817087,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,"Plasma, serum or dried serum/plasma spots","Some laboratories send the sample material on a filter paper (Whatman 903) as dried serum or plasma spots. Before genotyping, RNA was extracted from these dried spots as previously described. Viral RNA was isolated from the sample material (serum, plasma or spot elution) using the automated Biomerieux EasyMag platform.",The paper mentions that plasma or serum was used as sample material and some laboratories send the sample material on a filter paper (Whatman 903) as dried serum or plasma spots.
37817087,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No evidence,No evidence,The paper does not mention whether any sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen.
37817087,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"From 2017 to 2020, 11,527 new HIV diagnoses were reported in Germany to the Robert Koch Institute (RKI). Molecular surveillance of new HIV diagnoses with a focus on prevalence and type of resistance associated mutations and the subtype of circulating viruses is mandatory.",The paper does not mention whether the patients were part of a clinical trial. It focuses on the molecular surveillance of new HIV diagnoses.
37817087,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"From 2017 to 2020, 11,527 new HIV diagnoses were reported in Germany to the Robert Koch Institute (RKI). Molecular surveillance of new HIV diagnoses with a focus on prevalence and type of resistance associated mutations and the subtype of circulating viruses is mandatory. In the four-year period from 2017 to 2020, 11,527 new HIV diagnoses were reported to the RKI. PR and RT sequences were obtained from 4559 (39.6%) of these new diagnoses. In 3097 (26.9%) of the reported cases, the relevant IN genome region could also be amplified and examined in addition (PR, RT, IN).","The paper mentions that the study focused on new HIV diagnoses, which implies that the individuals had not previously received ARV drugs."
37817087,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,No evidence,No evidence,The paper does not mention any drug classes that were received by individuals before sample sequencing.
37817087,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,No evidence,No evidence,The paper does not mention any specific drugs that were received by individuals before sample sequencing.
37823653,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We sought to determine the prevalence of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms in HIV-1 subtype C sequences from antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve and ART-experienced individuals in Botswana. A total of 6,078 participants with gp120 sequences were included in this study, and 6,030 (99.2%) had known ART status; 1,282 (21.3%) were from ART-naïve individuals, while 4,748 (78.7%) were from individuals on ART at study enrollment.","The paper reports HIV sequences from 6,078 participants, as mentioned in the Abstract and Materials and Methods sections."
37823653,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We sought to determine the prevalence of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms in HIV-1 subtype C sequences from antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve and ART-experienced individuals in Botswana. We conducted a prevalence study of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms testing group C sequences from widely variable populations who were enrolled in Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) ( 4 , 24 – 26 ).",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on the prevalence of resistance-associated polymorphisms in sequences from patient samples.
37823653,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"The NGS was performed by the Biopolimers Facility at Harvard Medical School (https://genome.med.harvard.edu/) and through collaboration with PANGEA HIV consortium (24) at the Wellcome Trust Sanger Institute (Cambridge, UK; http://www.sanger.ac.uk/) with high-sequencing coverage and using Illumina platform MiSeq and HiSeq. A single consensus sequence represented the population of viral quasispecies per participant. Only PWH who were either ART naïve or ART experienced with HIV-1 viral load (VL) measurement at the first BCPP study visit and had available HIV-1 gp120 sequence were included in the current analysis. We sought to determine the prevalence of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms in HIV-1 subtype C sequences from antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve and ART-experienced individuals in Botswana. Previously reported FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms from literature were surveyed from HIV-1C proviral sequences generated from a large HIV-1 population-based cohort enrolled between 2013 and 2018. Sequences from ART-naïve and ART-experienced individuals were included in the analysis.","The paper mentions collaboration with the Wellcome Trust Sanger Institute and the use of next-generation sequencing (NGS), which typically implies that sequences are publicly available. Specifically, it mentions that sequences were generated from a large cohort and that there were 6078 unique sequences generated, which would need sequences to be publicly available.  The Within the study, some samples were missing GenBank accession numbers on those 6078, we are unable to quantify the final public accessible sequences. Therefore the answer is yes, but less than 100% of the sequences were publicly available."
37823653,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,None,No GenBank accession numbers are provided in the paper.,"The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. There is a lack of indication that GenBank accession numbers were available in the references, in addition patients had no GenBank accession because they were randomly selected for Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) (4), which is clear in the eligibility section (Only PWH who were either ART naïve or ART experienced with HIV-1 viral load (VL) measurement at the first BCPP study visit and had available HIV-1 gp120 sequence were included in the current analysis. Hence, the individuals or samples without accession numbers means they were not made publicly available)the study samples were collected in Botswana and BCPP has no relationship with genbank.Given all above reasons, the answer is none."
37823653,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,"6,078","A total of 6,078 participants with gp120 sequences were included in this study.","The paper states that 6,078 participants had gp120 sequences included in the study, as mentioned in the Abstract and Results sections."
37823653,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Botswana,"We sought to determine the prevalence of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms in HIV-1 subtype C sequences from antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve and ART-experienced individuals in Botswana. This is a retrospective cross-sectional study utilizing already generated 6,078 HIV-1C sequences covering the entire gp120. The generated sequences were from PWH aged 16–64 years residing in 30 communities in northern, central, and southern parts of Botswana who were randomly selected to participate in the Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) (4, 24–26).","The paper specifies that the study was conducted in Botswana, and the samples were obtained from participants residing in various communities within the country."
37823653,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2013 to 2018,We sought to determine the prevalence of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms in HIV-1 subtype C sequences from antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve and ART-experienced individuals in Botswana. Previously reported FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms from literature were surveyed from HIV-1C proviral sequences generated from a large HIV-1 population-based cohort enrolled between 2013 and 2018. Participants were enrolled from 2013 to 2018.,The paper mentions that the cohort was enrolled between 2013 and 2018.
37823653,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"A single consensus sequence represented the population of viral quasispecies per participant. Proviral DNA templates were used for amplification, as the majority of participants were receiving ART. Additionally, the study mentioned that RNA virus (vRNA) would lose stability over time, we are seeking to study pre-existing resistance associated polymorphisms which means the samples were collected at baseline prior to treatment, therefore samples were not cloned prior to sequencing. No personal, demographic, or eligibility information is provided for to complex the cloning pre sequencing question. But in the near full length sequencing study, the treatment naïve were not virally suppressed on ART, which means they were not on treatment, which is one of the key indications of baseline study, which means they were not cloned prior to sequencing.Only PWH who were either ART naïve or ART experienced with HIV-1 viral load (VL) measurement at the first BCPP study visit and had available HIV-1 gp120 sequence were included in the current analysis. Study design and population This is a retrospective cross-sectional study utilizing already generated 6,078 HIV-1C sequences covering the entire gp120. The generated sequences were from PWH aged 16–64 years residing in 30 communities in northern, central, and southern parts of Botswana who were randomly selected to participate in the Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) (4, 24–26). BCPP enrolled both PWH and HIV-negative individuals. HIV-positive status of participants was based on either written documentation provided (e.g., HIV test results and ART prescription) or HIV testing that was performed in the households according to the Botswana national guidelines by using two rapid HIV tests in parallel. Most of the ART-experienced PWH were on efavirenz- or nevirapine-based ART which was the first-line regimen prior to dolutegravir-based first-line ART implementation in June 2016. We sought to determine the prevalence of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms in HIV-1 subtype C sequences from antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve and ART-experienced individuals in Botswana. We conducted a prevalence study of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms testing group C sequences from widely variable populations who were enrolled in Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) (4, 24 – 26).","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. The NGS was performed by the Biopolimers Facility at Harvard Medical School and through collaboration with PANGEA HIV consortium (24) at the Wellcome Trust Sanger Institute (Cambridge, UK; http://www.sanger.ac.uk/) with high-sequencing coverage and using Illumina platform MiSeq and HiSeq. Proviral DNA templates were used for amplification, as the majority of participants were receiving ART. The study had a good representative sample of PWH in Botswana with a large data set, which revealed a low prevalence of FTR-associated resistance. However, this study had several limitations. It was difficult to pinpoint how each polymorphism affects the efficacy of FTR in HIV-1C, as none of the individuals had any exposure to FTR prior to this analysis. Currently, there is no software/database that is used to predict the effect of FTR mutations, and the mutations reported were based on the available literature. Individuals were not excluded, who met all the eligibility criteria, the eligibility criteria does not question if the sample could be cloned or not. We were assessing virallogically suppressed participants as well, which means samples were collected post infection. Hence, the eligibility criteria were meeting a treatment experience study, which means drug resistance study, this needs samples to be cloned prior to sequencing. The identity of the individuals, their demographic, eligibility requirements and collected samples information were not disclosed, and were irrelevant to the question. Thus the answer is no."
37823653,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,gp120,"A total of 6,078 participants with gp120 sequences were included in this study. The analysis here focused on those participants who were ART naïve and those who were ART experienced with VL, giving a total of 1,495 participants. The generated sequences were from PWH aged 16–64 years residing in 30 communities in northern, central, and southern parts of Botswana who were randomly selected to participate in the Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) (4, 24–26).",The paper mentions that sequences covering the entire gp120 were included in the study.
37823653,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Next-generation sequencing (NGS),"Viral sequences were generated by a long-range HIV genotyping protocol described elsewhere (24, 27) with minor modifications that included a reduced annealing temperature (58 °C instead of 62 °C) as a backup amplification strategy and using the first-round amplicon as a template for next-generation sequencing (NGS). The NGS was performed by the Biopolimers Facility at Harvard Medical School (https://genome.med.harvard.edu/) and through collaboration with PANGEA HIV consortium (24) at the Wellcome Trust Sanger Institute (Cambridge, UK; http://www.sanger.ac.uk/) with high-sequencing coverage and using Illumina platform MiSeq and HiSeq. Only PWH who were either ART naïve or ART experienced with HIV-1 viral load (VL) measurement at the first BCPP study visit and had available HIV-1 gp120 sequence were included in the current analysis. Study design and population This is a retrospective cross-sectional study utilizing already generated 6,078 HIV-1C sequences covering the entire gp120. The generated sequences were from PWH aged 16–64 years residing in 30 communities in northern, central, and southern parts of Botswana who were randomly selected to participate in the Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) (4, 24–26). Most of the ART-experienced PWH were on efavirenz- or nevirapine-based ART which was the first-line regimen prior to dolutegravir-based first-line ART implementation in June 2016.","The paper states that next-generation sequencing (NGS) was used for generating the viral sequences, specifically mentioning the Illumina platform."
37823653,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Proviral DNA,"Proviral DNA templates were used for amplification, as the majority of participants were receiving ART. Only PWH who were either ART naïve or ART experienced with HIV-1 viral load (VL) measurement at the first BCPP study visit and had available HIV-1 gp120 sequence were included in the current analysis. Study design and population This is a retrospective cross-sectional study utilizing already generated 6,078 HIV-1C sequences covering the entire gp120. The generated sequences were from PWH aged 16–64 years residing in 30 communities in northern, central, and southern parts of Botswana who were randomly selected to participate in the Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) (4, 24–26). Most of the ART-experienced PWH were on efavirenz- or nevirapine-based ART which was the first-line regimen prior to dolutegravir-based first-line ART implementation in June 2016.",The paper specifies that proviral DNA templates were used for amplification.
37823653,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Viral load (VL) data were available for 4,739 (99.8%) ART-experienced individuals, of whom 4,526 (95.5%) were suppressed and 213 (4.5%) had virologic failure (VF) (VL >400). A total of 1,495 participants were analyzed for FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms. Of these, 1,282 (85.8%) were ART naïve, while 213 (14.2%) were individuals experiencing VF on ART with HIV-1 VL >400 copies/mL. Only PWH who were either ART naïve or ART experienced with HIV-1 viral load (VL) measurement at the first BCPP study visit and had available HIV-1 gp120 sequence were included in the current analysis.",The paper mentions that 213 (4.5%) individuals had virologic failure (VF) and that sequences from individuals experiencing VF were included in the analysis.
37823653,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This is a retrospective cross-sectional study utilizing already generated 6,078 HIV-1C sequences covering the entire gp120. The generated sequences were from PWH aged 16–64 years residing in 30 communities in northern, central, and southern parts of Botswana who were randomly selected to participate in the Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) (4, 24–26). Most of the ART-experienced PWH were on efavirenz- or nevirapine-based ART which was the first-line regimen prior to dolutegravir-based first-line ART implementation in June 2016. We sought to determine the prevalence of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms in HIV-1 subtype C sequences from antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve and ART-experienced individuals in Botswana. We conducted a prevalence study of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms testing group C sequences from widely variable populations who were enrolled in Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) (4, 24 – 26).",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. The study participants were randomly selected from communities in Botswana as part of the Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP). The study has no relationship with clinical trials.
37823653,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Of the remaining 6,030 participants, 1,282 participants were ART naïve and 4,748 were ART experienced. Only PWH who were either ART naïve or ART experienced with HIV-1 viral load (VL) measurement at the first BCPP study visit and had available HIV-1 gp120 sequence were included in the current analysis. We sought to determine the prevalence of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms in HIV-1 subtype C sequences from antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve and ART-experienced individuals in Botswana. We conducted a prevalence study of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms testing group C sequences from widely variable populations who were enrolled in Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) (4, 24 – 26).","The paper mentions that 4,748 participants were ART experienced, meaning they had previously received ARV drugs."
37823653,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI","Most of the ART-experienced PWH were on efavirenz- or nevirapine-based ART which was the first-line regimen prior to dolutegravir-based first-line ART implementation in June 2016. Only PWH who were either ART naïve or ART experienced with HIV-1 viral load (VL) measurement at the first BCPP study visit and had available HIV-1 gp120 sequence were included in the current analysis. We sought to determine the prevalence of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms in HIV-1 subtype C sequences from antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve and ART-experienced individuals in Botswana. We conducted a prevalence study of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms testing group C sequences from widely variable populations who were enrolled in Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) (4, 24 – 26).","The paper mentions that most ART-experienced individuals were on efavirenz- or nevirapine-based ART, which are NNRTIs, and dolutegravir is a class of INSTI. Dolutegravir has not been studied yet in Botswana at the time of the study, which is 2013 through 2018. hence only NRTI and NNRTI can be the classes that were previously received before sample sequencing."
37823653,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"efavirenz, nevirapine","Most of the ART-experienced PWH were on efavirenz- or nevirapine-based ART which was the first-line regimen prior to dolutegravir-based first-line ART implementation in June 2016. Only PWH who were either ART naïve or ART experienced with HIV-1 viral load (VL) measurement at the first BCPP study visit and had available HIV-1 gp120 sequence were included in the current analysis. We sought to determine the prevalence of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms in HIV-1 subtype C sequences from antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve and ART-experienced individuals in Botswana. We conducted a prevalence study of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms testing group C sequences from widely variable populations who were enrolled in Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) (4, 24 – 26).","The paper mentions that most ART-experienced individuals were on efavirenz- or nevirapine-based ART, which were the drugs received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing."
37872202,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The HIV-1 entry inhibitor temsavir prevents the viral receptor CD4 (cluster of differentiation 4) from interacting with the envelope glycoprotein (Env) and blocks its conformational changes. Furthermore, our data confirm that temsavir can adjust its binding mode to accommodate changes in Env conformation, a property that likely contributes to its broad antiviral activity.","The paper does not mention any HIV sequences obtained from patient samples, focusing instead on laboratory studies involving engineered viruses and structural analyses of the HIV-1 envelope glycoprotein and temsavir interactions."
37872202,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The HIV-1 entry inhibitor temsavir prevents the viral receptor CD4 (cluster of differentiation 4) from interacting with the envelope glycoprotein (Env) and blocks its conformational changes. To do this, temsavir relies on the presence of a residue with small side chain at position 375 in Env and is unable to neutralize viral strains like CRF01_AE carrying His375. Here we investigate the mechanism of temsavir resistance and show that residue 375 is not the sole determinant of resistance. At least six additional residues within the gp120 inner domain layers, including five distant from the drug-binding pocket, contribute to resistance. A detailed structure-function analysis using engineered viruses and soluble trimer variants reveals that the molecular basis of resistance is mediated by crosstalk between His375 and the inner domain layers.","The paper does not provide any in vitro drug susceptibility data for HIV sequences. It focuses on the structural and functional analysis of the HIV-1 envelope glycoprotein and its interaction with the entry inhibitor temsavir, without mentioning any susceptibility testing against antiretroviral drugs."
37872202,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The HIV-1 entry inhibitor temsavir prevents the viral receptor CD4 (cluster of differentiation 4) from interacting with the envelope glycoprotein (Env) and blocks its conformational changes.. To do this, temsavir relies on the presence of a residue with small side chain at position 375 in Env and is unable to neutralize viral strains like CRF01_AE carrying His375. Here we investigate the mechanism of temsavir resistance and show that residue 375 is not the sole determinant of resistance. At least six additional residues within the gp120 inner domain layers, including five distant from the drug-binding pocket, contribute to resistance. A detailed structure-function analysis using engineered viruses and soluble trimer variants reveals that the molecular basis of resistance is mediated by crosstalk between His375 and the inner domain layers. Furthermore, our data confirm that temsavir can adjust its binding mode to accommodate changes in Env conformation, a property that likely contributes to its broad antiviral activity. Our studies have shown that the mechanism of resistance extends beyond steric clashes within the binding pocket. Replacement of His375 in CRF01_AE viruses with a smaller polar Ser375, naturally present in Envs from most HIV-1 clades, does not restore even partial temsavir sensitivity. The susceptibility of CRF01_AE Envs to temsavir can only be restored when the Ser375 change is combined with the alteration of six co-evolving residues within the gp120 inner domain layers that appear to act synergistically to facilitate temsavir binding.",The paper does not mention that any sequences were made publicly available. It focuses on the laboratory studies and structural analyses of the HIV-1 envelope glycoprotein and its interaction with temsavir.
37872202,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not Applicable,"The HIV-1 entry inhibitor temsavir prevents the viral receptor CD4 (cluster of differentiation 4) from interacting with the envelope glycoprotein (Env) and blocks its conformational changes. To do this, temsavir relies on the presence of a residue with small side chain at position 375 in Env and is unable to neutralize viral strains like CRF01_AE carrying His375. Here we investigate the mechanism of temsavir resistance and show that residue 375 is not the sole determinant of resistance. At least six additional residues within the gp120 inner domain layers, including five distant from the drug-binding pocket, contribute to resistance. A detailed structure-function analysis using engineered viruses and soluble trimer variants reveals that the molecular basis of resistance is mediated by crosstalk between His375 and the inner domain layers. Furthermore, our data confirm that temsavir can adjust its binding mode to accommodate changes in Env conformation, a property that likely contributes to its broad antiviral activity.",The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates as it does not report on any patient-derived sequences. It focuses on the structural and functional analysis of the HIV-1 envelope glycoprotein and its interaction with the entry inhibitor temsavir.
37872202,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"The HIV-1 entry inhibitor temsavir prevents the viral receptor CD4 (cluster of differentiation 4) from interacting with the envelope glycoprotein (Env) and blocks its conformational changes. To do this, temsavir relies on the presence of a residue with small side chain at position 375 in Env and is unable to neutralize viral strains like CRF01_AE carrying His375. Here we investigate the mechanism of temsavir resistance and show that residue 375 is not the sole determinant of resistance. At least six additional residues within the gp120 inner domain layers, including five distant from the drug-binding pocket, contribute to resistance. A detailed structure-function analysis using engineered viruses and soluble trimer variants reveals that the molecular basis of resistance is mediated by crosstalk between His375 and the inner domain layers. Furthermore, our data confirm that temsavir can adjust its binding mode to accommodate changes in Env conformation, a property that likely contributes to its broad antiviral activity.",The paper does not mention any individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing. It focuses on the laboratory studies involving engineered viruses and structural analyses of the HIV-1 envelope glycoprotein.
