37896785,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI","HIV-infected pregnant women were recommended to take antiretroviral drugs: zidovudine, lamivudine, Lopinavir/ritonavir. The study material was blood plasma samples from HIV-infected pregnant women: 31 women showed virological failure of ART, and 30 women had not previously received therapy. In the surveyed group, 31 women showed virological failure of ART, and 30 women had not previously received therapy. All samples were transferred anonymously. Molecular genetic studies were carried out at the Pasteur Institute in St. Petersburg. Quantitative analysis of HIV RNA was carried out with a commercial kit, AmpliSens® HIV-Monitor-FRT (Central Research Institute of Epidemiology, Moscow, Russia) with a sensitivity threshold of 500 copies/mL. For samples with a detectable viral load, reverse transcription and PCR were performed, followed by Sanger sequencing using the AmpliSens HIVResist-Seq (Central Research Institute of Epidemiology, Moscow, Russia) commercial kit. HIV-1 genotyping was performed based on analysis of the nucleotide sequences of the 1285 nt pol gene region encoding the protease (PR) and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT) in the 2085--3369 nt region. The coordinates given are for HIV HXB2 in the GenBank database (K03455.1) Network analysis was performed using Gephi 0.9.2 in accordance with the recommended protocol . The processed nucleotide sequences were used to build the maximum-likelihood phylogenetic tree using the estimated nucleotide substitution model using IQ-TREE software ,16. The selected model was %GTR+I+G.545 Overall virion infectivity was calculated as a ratio of infectivity of wild-type X52 virus and infectious titers,546 as published previously.547,
 The overall virion infectivity of the HXM021.1-like recombinant strain was estimated to be 0.368,548 which is statistically not significantly different from that of X52 whereas the overall virion infectivity of HXM021-like494549 United States patent 9611475 B2.053 NRTI mutations M41L and T215Y were also674640 as being naturally occurring,551,552 indicating some22403%.55 percent620.53%HIV-1 subtype0720 Q/New.72.8.8%, tes0320210153181.","The paper mentions that the study included women who showed virological failure on antiretroviral therapy (ART), and it lists the drugs that were used, which belong to the NRTI, NNRTI, and PI classes."
37896785,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Zidovudine (AZT), Lamivudine (3TC), Lopinavir/ritonavir","HIV-infected pregnant women were recommended to take antiretroviral drugs: zidovudine, lamivudine, Lopinavir/ritonavir. In women with virological failure of ART, 31 had been previously receiving drugs based on two NRTI class drugs (zidovudine and lamivudine) and one NNRTI class drug (nevirapine), or two NRTI class drugs (zidovudine and lamivudine) and one PI class drug (lopinavir/ritonavir) for at least 6 months before the study.","The paper lists the specific drugs that were received by the participants prior to the study, which include zidovudine, lamivudine, and Lopinavir/ritonavir."
37896860,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"HIV DR testing was performed on plasma samples using Sanger sequencing with Applied Biosystems 3130xl Genetic Analyzers (ThermoFisher Scientific, Nairobi, Kenya) at the KEMRI-CDC HIV Research and Sanger 3730xl at the Kenya National HIV Reference Laboratories. Whole blood was collected from study participants for POC VL testing and separated into plasma for testing.","The paper describes the collection of plasma samples from pediatric patients for HIV drug resistance testing, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
37896860,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We describe DR mutations and present logistic regression models to identify factors associated with clinically significant DR. We enrolled 704 children in the study; the median age was 9 years (interquartile range (IQR) 7, 12), 344 (49%) were female, and the median time on ART was 5 years (IQR 3, 8). During the study period, 106 (15%) children had DRT results (84 intervention and 22 control). DRT detected mutations associated with DR in all participants tested, with 93 (88%) having major mutations, including 51 (54%) with dual-class resistance. A history of VF in the prior 2 years (adjusted odds ratio (aOR) 11.1; 95% confidence interval (CI) 6.3, 20.0) and less than 2 years on ART at enrollment (aOR 2.2; 95% CI 1.1, 4.4) were associated with increased odds of major DR. DR is highly prevalent among CHIV on ART with VF in Kenya. Factors associated with drug resistance may be used to determine which children should be prioritized for DRT.",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility assays or data. It primarily focuses on drug resistance testing and the presence of resistance mutations in patient samples.
37896860,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,The de-identified data presented in this study are available on request from the corresponding author. The de-identified data presented in this study are available on request from the corresponding author. The data are not publicly available due to the sensitive nature of the disease being studied.,"The paper states that the de-identified data are available on request but not publicly available, suggesting that the sequences were not made publicly available."
37896860,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
37896860,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,704,"We enrolled 704 children in the study; the median age was 9 years (interquartile range (IQR) 7, 12), 344 (49%) were female, and the median time on ART was 5 years (IQR 3, 8).","The paper states that 704 children were enrolled in the study, which implies that samples were obtained for HIV sequencing from this number of individuals."
37896860,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Kenya,"Children were randomized 1:1 to control (standard-of-care) or intervention (point-of-care viral load (POC VL) testing every three months with targeted genotypic drug resistance testing (DRT) for virologic failure (VF) (≥1000 copies/mL)). A multidisciplinary committee reviewed CHIV with DRT results and offered treatment recommendations. We describe DR mutations and present logistic regression models to identify factors associated with clinically significant DR. We enrolled 704 children in the study; the median age was 9 years (interquartile range (IQR) 7, 12), 344 (49%) were female, and the median time on ART was 5 years (IQR 3, 8). During the study period, 106 (15%) children had DRT results (84 intervention and 22 control). DRT detected mutations associated with DR in all participants tested, with 93 (88%) having major mutations, including 51 (54%) with dual-class resistance. A history of VF in the prior 2 years (adjusted odds ratio (aOR) 11.1; 95% confidence interval (CI) 6.3, 20.0) and less than 2 years on ART at enrollment (aOR 2.2; 95% CI 1.1, 4.4) were associated with increased odds of major DR. DR is highly prevalent among CHIV on ART with VF in Kenya. Factors associated with drug resistance may be used to determine which children should be prioritized for DRT.","The study was conducted in Kenya, and all the enrolled children were from facilities in Kisumu County, Kenya, indicating that the sequenced samples were obtained from this country."
37896860,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2019 - 2020,"Children were individually randomized 1:1, stratified by site and age groups (ages 1–9 years and 10–14 years), to the control (standard-of-care) or intervention groups (point-of-care viral load testing every three months with DRT for those with VF (HIV RNA ≥ 1000 copies/mL)) and followed for 12 months. First. We describe the proportion of participants from either group who underwent DRT as part of the study intervention or at the study end, per protocol, and the proportion with DR mutations detected by HIV drug classes, e.g., NRTIs, NNRTIs, and PIs. All DRTs for each participant were reviewed, but only one DRT result contributed to the data analysis. For a report of DRMs by drug class, the DRT with the highest number of mutations was used. We report on the prevalence of HIV DR by ARV medication using WHO’s definition of a penalty score of ≥15 using the Standford HIVdb algorithm We estimated the prevalence of the most common DR mutations (K65R, L74V/I, Y115F, M184V, K103N, Y181C, G190A) with 95% confidence intervals (CI).","Enrolled from March 2019 to December 2020, with follow-up for 12 months, indicating the samples were collected during this period."
37896860,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"HIV drug resistance (DR) testing was performed on plasma samples using Sanger sequencing with Applied Biosystems 3130xl Genetic Analyzers. Children were individually randomized 1:1, stratified by site and age groups (ages 1–9 years and 10–14 years), to the control (standard-of-care) or intervention groups (point-of-care viral load testing every three months with DRT for those with VF (HIV RNA ≥ 1000 copies/mL)) and followed for 12 months. Participants in both groups underwent point-of-care (POC) viral load (VL) testing and targeted DRT, if indicated, at 12 months post-enrollment.","The paper mentions the use of Sanger sequencing for drug resistance testing, which typically does not require cloning of samples. There is no mention of cloning samples prior to sequencing."
37896860,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"RT, PR","HIV drug resistance (DR) testing was performed on plasma samples using Sanger sequencing with Applied Biosystems 3130xl Genetic Analyzers (ThermoFisher Scientific, Nairobi, Kenya) at the KEMRI-CDC HIV Research and Sanger 3730xl at the Kenya National HIV Reference Laboratories. These laboratories utilize validated, WHO-certified, optimized in-house assays to detect reverse transcriptase and PI mutations. Integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) mutations were not routinely evaluated during the study period.","The paper specifically mentions that reverse transcriptase (RT) and protease (PR) mutations were detected, and integrase strand transfer inhibitor mutations were not evaluated."
37896860,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"HIV drug resistance (DR) testing was performed on plasma samples using Sanger sequencing with Applied Biosystems 3130xl Genetic Analyzers (ThermoFisher Scientific, Nairobi, Kenya) at the KEMRI-CDC HIV Research and Sanger 3730xl at the Kenya National HIV Reference Laboratories. These laboratories utilize validated, WHO-certified, optimized in-house assays to detect reverse transcriptase and PI mutations [21, 22].",The paper specifies that Sanger sequencing was used for HIV drug resistance testing.
37896860,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Whole blood was collected from study participants for POC VL testing and separated into plasma for testing using a GeneXpert system (Cepheid, Nairobi, Kenya) on site at study facilities or via daily transport to a facility less than 2 km away [20]. HIV DR testing was performed on plasma samples using Sanger sequencing with Applied Biosystems 3130xl Genetic Analyzers (ThermoFisher Scientific, Nairobi, Kenya) at the KEMRI-CDC HIV Research and Sanger 3730xl at the Kenya National HIV Reference Laboratories.","The paper mentions that plasma was collected from whole blood for testing, indicating that plasma samples were sequenced."
37896860,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"During the 12-month study period, 190 study DRTs were requested for 106 (15%) participants across the two study groups. Among the intervention participants, 88 (25%) children experienced at least one episode of VF, and all had a least one DRT requested for a total of 166 DRT requests, and 152 (92%) had results. DR is highly prevalent among CHIV on ART with VF in Kenya. Factors associated with drug resistance may be used to determine which children should be prioritized for DRT.",The paper specifically mentions that drug resistance testing was done for 106 children with virological failure.
37896860,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,Children were randomized 1:1 to control (standard-of-care) or intervention (point-of-care viral load (POC VL) testing every three months with targeted genotypic drug resistance testing (DRT) for virologic failure (VF) (≥1000 copies/mL)). We describe DR mutations and present logistic regression models to identify factors associated with clinically significant DR. We enrolled 704 children in the study.,The paper describes the study as a randomized controlled trial (Opt4Kids) with specific interventions and control groups.
37896860,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"We describe DR mutations and present logistic regression models to identify factors associated with clinically significant DR. We enrolled 704 children in the study; the median age was 9 years (interquartile range (IQR) 7, 12), 344 (49%) were female, and the median time on ART was 5 years (IQR 3, 8).","The paper mentions that all participants were on antiretroviral treatment (ART), and the median time on ART was 5 years, indicating that the sequences were from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37896860,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, INSTIs","Overall, 382 (54.3%) of the participants were on an NNRTI-containing regimen at study enrollment, 294 (41.8%) were on a PI-containing regimen, 27 (3.8%) were on an INSTI-containing regimen, and 1 (0.1%) was on a PI and INSTI regimen. First-line ART regimens in Kenya during the study period for children included lamivudine with either abacavir (preferred) or zidovudine (alternative) and lopinavir/ritonavir for those less than three years of age and lamivudine with either abacavir (preferred) or zidovudine (alternative) and efavirenz for those three years of age and older","The paper states the study included children aged 1-14 years. The first line ART regimens included NRTIs, NNRTIs, and PIs, which are all part of the study. Some patients in the study were on an INSTI-containing regimen which is also mentioned."
37896860,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"3TC (lamivudine), ABC (abacavir), AZT (zidovudine), LPV/r (lopinavir/ritonavir), EFV (efavirenz), DTG (dolutegravir)","Overall,382 (54.3%) of the participants were on an NNRTI-containing regimen at study enrollment,294 (41.8%) were on a PI-containing regimen,27 (3.8%) were on an INSTI-containing regimen,and 1 (0.1%) was on a PI and INSTI regimen. First-line ART regimens in Kenya during the study period for children included lamivudine with either abacavir (preferred) or zidovudine (alternative) and lopinavir/ritonavir for those less than three years of age and lamivudine with either abacavir (preferred) or zidovudine (alternative) and efavirenz for those three years of age and older. Second-line ART regimens included changes in NRTI medication from abacavir to zidovudine or vice versa depending on which the child was on as first-line with maintenance of lamivudine.","The paper lists lamivudine (3TC), abacavir (ABC), zidovudine (AZT), lopinavir/ritonavir (LPV/r), efavirenz (EFV), and dolutegravir (DTG) as drugs used in the study."
37910452,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"This systematic review and meta-analysis was aimed to synthesize available evidence on adherence to ART among HIV/AIDS patients in Ghana. This review followed the preferred reporting item for systematic review and meta-analysis (PRISMA) criteria. A comprehensive literature search was done using five online databases (PubMed, Google Scholar, Medline, Africa Index Medicus, and Willey Online Library) from 25th- 30th April 2023 to identify potential studies.","The paper does not report HIV sequences from patient samples. Instead, it focuses on a systematic review and meta-analysis of adherence to antiretroviral therapy (ART) among HIV/AIDS patients in Ghana. The objective of the study is to synthesize available evidence on adherence to ART, not to report on HIV sequences or sequencing data from patient samples."
37910452,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"This systematic review and meta-analysis was aimed to synthesize available evidence on adherence to ART among HIV/AIDS patients in Ghana. This review followed the preferred reporting item for systematic review and meta-analysis (PRISMA) criteria. A comprehensive literature search was done using five online databases (PubMed, Google Scholar, Medline, Africa Index Medicus, and Willey Online Library) from 25th- 30th April 2023 to identify potential studies.","The paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data. The focus of the study is on adherence to antiretroviral therapy (ART) among HIV/AIDS patients in Ghana, and it does not mention any experiments or data related to drug susceptibility testing."
37910452,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"This systematic review and meta-analysis was aimed to synthesize available evidence on adherence to ART among HIV/AIDS patients in Ghana. This review followed the preferred reporting item for systematic review and meta-analysis (PRISMA) criteria. A comprehensive literature search was done using five online databases (PubMed, Google Scholar, Medline, Africa Index Medicus, and Willey Online Library) from 25th- 30th April 2023 to identify potential studies.",The paper does not mention any sequences or their public availability. It is focused on adherence to antiretroviral therapy (ART) and does not discuss any sequencing data.
37910452,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not applicable,"This systematic review and meta-analysis was aimed to synthesize available evidence on adherence to ART among HIV/AIDS patients in Ghana. This review followed the preferred reporting item for systematic review and meta-analysis (PRISMA) criteria. A comprehensive literature search was done using five online databases (PubMed, Google Scholar, Medline, Africa Index Medicus, and Willey Online Library) from 25th- 30th April 2023 to identify potential studies.",The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers since it does not report any sequencing of HIV isolates. Its focus is entirely on adherence to antiretroviral therapy (ART) among HIV/AIDS patients.
37910452,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"This systematic review and meta-analysis was aimed to synthesize available evidence on adherence to ART among HIV/AIDS patients in Ghana. This review followed the preferred reporting item for systematic review and meta-analysis (PRISMA) criteria. A comprehensive literature search was done using five online databases (PubMed, Google Scholar, Medline, Africa Index Medicus, and Willey Online Library) from 25th- 30th April 2023 to identify potential studies.",The paper does not mention any individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing. It focuses on adherence to antiretroviral therapy (ART) and does not discuss any sequencing data.
37910452,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not applicable,"This systematic review and meta-analysis was aimed to synthesize available evidence on adherence to ART among HIV/AIDS patients in Ghana. This review followed the preferred reporting item for systematic review and meta-analysis (PRISMA) criteria. A comprehensive literature search was done using five online databases (PubMed, Google Scholar, Medline, Africa Index Medicus, and Willey Online Library) from 25th- 30th April 2023 to identify potential studies.",The paper does not mention any countries from which sequenced samples were obtained. It is focused on adherence to antiretroviral therapy (ART) among HIV/AIDS patients in Ghana.
37910452,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not applicable,"This systematic review and meta-analysis was aimed to synthesize available evidence on adherence to ART among HIV/AIDS patients in Ghana. This review followed the preferred reporting item for systematic review and meta-analysis (PRISMA) criteria. A comprehensive literature search was done using five online databases (PubMed, Google Scholar, Medline, Africa Index Medicus, and Willey Online Library) from 25th- 30th April 2023 to identify potential studies.","The paper does not provide any years for when sequenced samples were obtained, as it does not report on any sequencing. Its focus is on adherence to antiretroviral therapy (ART) among HIV/AIDS patients."
