37914679,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"All of the 76 HIV-1-infected patients with baseline plasma pre-existing low-frequency drug resistance mutations were detected by the MiSeq second-generation sequencing platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA).",The paper mentions that HIV-1-infected patients had their pre-existing low-frequency drug resistance mutations detected by the MiSeq second-generation sequencing platform. This indicates that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples.
37914679,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"HIV-1 ART-naïve patients attending the Second Hospital of Nanjing during January 1, 2018 to December 31, 2020, with drug-sensitive results from traditional pre-existing drug resistance test, were enrolled in this study. They received ART and were followed up for 12 months, with viral load monitoring at 1 month, 3 months, 6 months, 9 months, and 12 months of treatment. The study was conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki.","The paper focuses on clinical outcomes and the detection of resistance mutations using sequencing methods, but it does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing."
37914679,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention whether the sequences obtained were made publicly available on databases such as GenBank.
37914679,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No,No evidence,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
37914679,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,76,"All of the 76 HIV-1-infected patients with baseline plasma pre-existing low-frequency drug resistance mutations were detected by the MiSeq second-generation sequencing platform. In this retrospective study, the presence of HIV pre-existing low-frequency resistance mutations was significantly higher in the post-ART virological failure group than in the successful virological response group, and resistance was predominantly to NNRTIs.","The paper states that all 76 HIV-1-infected patients had their pre-existing low-frequency drug resistance mutations detected, indicating that samples from 76 individuals were obtained for sequencing."
37914679,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"HIV-1 ART-naïve patients attending the Second Hospital of Nanjing during January 1, 2018 to December 31, 2020, with drug-sensitive results from traditional pre-existing drug resistance test, were enrolled in this study.","The paper mentions that the study was conducted on HIV-1 ART-naïve patients attending the Second Hospital of Nanjing, China."
37914679,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2018 to 2020,"HIV-1 ART-naïve patients attending the Second Hospital of Nanjing during January 1, 2018 to December 31, 2020, with drug-sensitive results from traditional pre-existing drug resistance test, were enrolled in this study. They received ART and were followed up for 12 months, with viral load monitoring at 1 month, 3 months, 6 months, 9 months, and 12 months of treatment. All patients provided written informed consent, and the study was approved by the Medical Ethics Committee of the Second Hospital of Nanjing (No. 2018-LY-kt027).","The paper states that patients attending the Second Hospital of Nanjing during January 1, 2018, to December 31, 2020, were enrolled in the study, indicating the time frame from which the samples were obtained."
37914679,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"A total of 76 patients enrolled in this study had been tested for pre-existing resistance by conventional Sanger sequencing at baseline, with results suggesting that no resistance mutations were detected. Of the overall 76 included patients, CRF01-AE (40.8%, 31/76) was the predominant subtype, followed by subtypes B + C (26.3%, 20/76) and B (18.4%, 14/76).","The study involves the detection of pre-existing low-frequency resistance mutations using second-generation sequencing, which typically does not require cloning. Additionally, baseline pre-existing resistance was assessed by Sanger sequencing, and 76 samples were included. Hence, it can be concluded that the samples were not cloned prior to sequencing."
37914679,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Pol gene,"Low-frequency resistance mutations were detected in a total of 18 loci, with a total number of 26 mutations, and the mutations were predominantly in the NNRTI class of resistance, which accounted for 53.8% (14/26) of the mutated loci, including V179 (19.2%, 5/26), Y188 (7.7%, 2/26), and E138 (7.7%, 2/26). The NRTI class resistance mutation rate was 38.5% (10/26), including K65 (7.7%, 2/26) and D67 (7.7%, 2/26). In addition, one HIV-infected patient with low-frequency resistance in the PI class and one with low-frequency resistance in the integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) class were detected, respectively. The initial treatment regimen of patients in this study population was predominantly 2NRTI + NNRTI in 77.6% (59/76), while the remaining 17 patients were on an INSTI-based antiviral regimen a","paper mentions the detection of mutations in the NRTI, NNRTI, PI, and integrase classes, which are all part of the Pol gene region."
37914679,10,What method was used for sequencing?,MiSeq second-generation sequencing platform,"All of the 76 HIV-1-infected patients with baseline plasma pre-existing low-frequency drug resistance mutations were detected by the MiSeq second-generation sequencing platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA).",The paper states that the MiSeq second-generation sequencing platform was used for detecting pre-existing low-frequency drug resistance mutations in the patients.
37914679,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"A total of 76 patients enrolled in this study had been tested for pre-existing resistance by conventional Sanger sequencing at baseline, with results suggesting that no resistance mutations were detected. Of the overall 76 included patients, CRF01-AE (40.8%, 31/76) was the predominant subtype, followed by subtypes B + C (26.3%, 20/76) and B (18.4%, 14/76).","The paper mentions that plasma was tested for baseline pre-existing resistance, which suggests that the HIV sequences were obtained from plasma samples."
37914679,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Thirty-eight patients who failed antiviral therapy were defined as the failure group according to the study definition criteria, and 38 successful virological responders were matched as the control group based on the baseline characteristics of the failure group. In this retrospective study, the presence of HIV pre-existing low-frequency resistance mutations was significantly higher in the post-ART virological failure group than in the successful virological response group, and resistance was predominantly to NNRTIs.","The paper defines a group of patients who failed antiviral therapy, indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure."
37914679,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The study was not a clinical trial. The paper describes the study as a retrospective analysis of patients attending a hospital, not a clinical trial.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial; it describes a retrospective study of patients attending a hospital.
37914679,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"HIV-1 ART-naïve patients attending the Second Hospital of Nanjing during January 1, 2018 to December 31, 2020, with drug-sensitive results from traditional pre-existing drug resistance test, were enrolled in this study. They received ART and were followed up for 12 months, with viral load monitoring at 1 month, 3 months, 6 months, 9 months, and 12 months of treatment. All patients provided written informed consent, and the study was approved by the Medical Ethics Committee of the Second Hospital of Nanjing (No. 2018-LY-kt027).","The paper mentions that the patients were 1) naïve 2) received ART and followed up for 12 months, indicating that the sequences were from individuals who had received ARV drugs."
37914679,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, I","The initial treatment regimen of patients in this study population was predominantly 2NRTI + NNRTI in 77.6% (59/76), while the remaining 17 patients were on an INSTI-based antiviral regimen. Among the 76 patients who underwent second-generation sequencing, the success rate of amplification was 100%. Low-frequency resistance mutations were detected in a total of 18 loci, with a total number of 26 mutations, and the mutations were predominantly in the NNRTI class of resistance, which accounted for 53.8% (14/26) of the mutated loci, including V179 (19.2%, 5/26), Y188 (7.7%, 2/26), and E138 (7.7%, 2/26). The NRTI class resistance mutation rate was 38.5% (10/26), including K65 (7.7%, 2/26) and D67 (7.7%, 2/26). In addition, one HIV-infected patient with low-frequency resistance in the PI class and one with low-frequency resistance in the integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) class were detected, respectively.","The paper mentions that patients received treatment regimens that included NRTIs, NNRTIs, and INSTIs, indicating that these drug classes were received before sample sequencing."
37914679,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,No evidence,"Serum samples were collected for resistance testing at 1 month, 6 months, and 12 months after the start of ART. Baseline pre-existing resistance was assessed by conventional Sanger sequencing, and to compare the proportions of baseline pre-existing low-frequency resistance mutations (≥0.3%) before and after ART treatment, second-generation sequencing (Illumina MiSeq) was performed in 76 patients.56.3% (43/76) received TDF/3TC/EFV; 23.7% (18/76) received E/C/F/TP; 7.9% (6/76) received DRV/RTV; 1.3% (1/76) received DTG + RAL; 10.5% (8/76) received a mixture of other drugs, according to the guidelines.",The paper does not specify which drugs were received by individuals before sample sequencing. The paper only shows the combination of drugs received.
37920909,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"The current study recruited 629 ART-naïve individuals from Tianjin Second People's Hospital between January 1, 2020, and April 1, 2023. After RNA extraction, an in-house genotyping method and standard Sanger sequencing were used to amplify and sequence HIV-1 integrase genes, as described previously.",The paper mentions that the study recruited 629 ART-naïve individuals from whom HIV-1 integrase genes were sequenced. This indicates that the paper does report HIV sequences from patient samples.
37920909,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Among the 601 cases, 14 (2.33%) cases harbored INSTI resistance mutations, with 2 (0.33%) harboring major mutations and 13 (2.16%) cases having accessory mutations. One sample had both major and accessory mutations [Table ​ [Table1]. 1. Three major mutations were detected, namely, G140A, Y143H, and Q148R. G140A mutations were non-polymorphic and generally occurred with Q148 mutations. G140A mutations alone had negligible effects on the susceptibility of INSTIs.",The evidence discusses the detection of INSTI resistance mutations using in vitro susceptability testing done on patient samples.
37920909,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Not directly stated,"We used the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database (HIVDB Algorithm version 9.4.1) to examine mutations. Sequences with resistance levels classified as low, intermediate, and high were all regarded as resistant.","The paper mentions using the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database, but it does not explicitly state that the sequences obtained were made publicly available."
37920909,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not available,No evidence,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
37920909,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,629,"The current study recruited 629 ART-naïve individuals from Tianjin Second People's Hospital between January 1, 2020, and April 1, 2023. After RNA extraction, an in-house genotyping method and standard Sanger sequencing were used to amplify and sequence HIV-1 integrase genes, as described previously. Of 629 newly reported cases, the HIV-1 integrase genes of 601 cases were sequenced successfully.","The paper states that 629 ART-naïve individuals were recruited for the study, and 601 cases had their HIV-1 integrase genes successfully sequenced."
37920909,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"This study evaluated the prevalence of INSTI-associated drug resistance among ART-naïve individuals infected with HIV-1 in Tianjin, China. The current study recruited 629 ART-naïve individuals from Tianjin Second People's Hospital between January 1, 2020, and April 1, 2023.  In conclusion, Tianjin has a low prevalence of INSTI resistance, indicating that INSTIs are well-applicable.","The paper mentions the study was done in Tianjin, China."
37920909,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2020-2023,"The current study recruited 629 ART-naïve individuals from Tianjin Second People's Hospital between January 1, 2020, and April 1, 2023.","The paper states that the samples were obtained between January 1, 2020, and April 1, 2023."
37920909,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"After RNA extraction, an in-house genotyping method and standard Sanger sequencing were used to amplify and sequence HIV-1 integrase genes, as described previously.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of standard Sanger sequencing methods.
37920909,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Integrase,"After RNA extraction, an in-house genotyping method and standard Sanger sequencing were used to amplify and sequence HIV-1 integrase genes, as described previously. Of 629 newly reported cases, the HIV-1 integrase genes of 601 cases were sequenced successfully.",The paper specifically mentions that the HIV-1 integrase genes were sequenced.
37920909,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"After RNA extraction, an in-house genotyping method and standard Sanger sequencing were used to amplify and sequence HIV-1 integrase genes, as described previously. Of 629 newly reported cases, the HIV-1 integrase genes of 601 cases were sequenced successfully.",The paper mentions that standard Sanger sequencing was used to sequence the HIV-1 integrase genes.
37920909,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Blood,"Before initiating ART, 5 mL of blood was collected from the participants. The following HIV-1 subtypes have been identified in the 14 INSTI-resistant variants: CRF01_AE (8/14, 57.1%), CRF07_BC (4/14, 28.6%), CRF68_01B (1/14, 7.1%), and CRF124_cpx (1/14, 7.1%).","The paper mentions that 5mL of blood was collected before initiating ART, indicating that blood samples were sequenced."
37920909,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"This study evaluated the prevalence of INSTI-associated drug resistance among ART-naïve individuals infected with HIV-1 in Tianjin, China. The current study recruited 629 ART-naïve individuals from Tianjin Second People's Hospital between January 1, 2020, and April 1, 2023. The criteria for inclusion in the study were as follows: (1) outpatients at Tianjin Second People's Hospital; (2) HIV-1 seropositive; (3) aged ≥18 years; (4) no prior antiviral treatment; and (5) participants who provided consent and could undergo drug resistance tests.","The paper mentioned that the study included only ART-naïve individuals, meaning they hadn't started treatment, hence there were no individuals with virological failure."
37920909,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The study the current study recruited 629 ART-naïve individuals from Tianjin Second People's Hospital between January 1, 2020, and April 1, 2023. The criteria for inclusion in the study were as follows: (1) outpatients at Tianjin Second People's Hospital; (2) HIV-1 seropositive; (3) aged ≥18 years; (4) no prior antiviral treatment; and (5) participants who provided consent and could undergo drug resistance tests. Exclusion criteria were as listed: pregnancy, breastfeeding women, incomplete data, and refusal to give informed consent. Before initiating ART, 5 mL of blood was collected from the participants. Baseline clinical and demographic data were obtained from the NFATP database, including sex, age, transmission route, CD4 count, and viral load. The study was carried out in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki and with the approval of the Tianjin Second People's Hospital's Ethical Committee (No. 2015-13). All subjects participating in the study provided written informed consent.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It describes the study as a cross-sectional evaluation of drug resistance among ART-naïve individuals.
37920909,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"This study evaluated the prevalence of INSTI-associated drug resistance among ART-naïve individuals infected with HIV-1 in Tianjin, China. The current study recruited 629 ART-naïve individuals from Tianjin Second People's Hospital between January 1, 2020, and April 1, 2023. The criteria for inclusion in the study were as follows: (1) outpatients at Tianjin Second People's Hospital; (2) HIV-1 seropositive; (3) aged ≥18 years; (4) no prior antiviral treatment; and (5) participants who provided consent and could undergo drug resistance tests.","The paper specifically mentions that the individuals were ART-naïve, meaning they had not received any antiretroviral drugs prior to the study."
37920909,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"This study evaluated the prevalence of INSTI-associated drug resistance among ART-naïve individuals infected with HIV-1 in Tianjin, China. The current study recruited 629 ART-naïve individuals from Tianjin Second People's Hospital between January 1, 2020, and April 1, 2023. The criteria for inclusion in the study were as follows: (1) outpatients at Tianjin Second People's Hospital; (2) HIV-1 seropositive; (3) aged ≥18 years; (4) no prior antiviral treatment; and (5) participants who provided consent and could undergo drug resistance tests.","The paper mentioned  the study included only ART-naïve individuals, meaning they hadn't started treatment, hence hadn't received any drugs."
37920909,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"This study evaluated the prevalence of INSTI-associated drug resistance among ART-naïve individuals infected with HIV-1 in Tianjin, China. The current study recruited 629 ART-naïve individuals from Tianjin Second People's Hospital between January 1, 2020, and April 1, 2023. The criteria for inclusion in the study were as follows: (1) outpatients at Tianjin Second People's Hospital; (2) HIV-1 seropositive; (3) aged ≥18 years; (4) no prior antiviral treatment; and (5) participants who provided consent and could undergo drug resistance tests.","The paper mentioned  the study included only ART-naïve individuals, meaning they hadn't started treatment, hence hadn't received any drugs."
37938856,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We conducted an open-label, pre/post-intervention (or intervention/control) prospective cohort study, enrolling pregnant women living with HIV during their ANC care and followed them through 6 months postpartum in five public-sector HIV treatment facilities in Kenya from February 2019 to November 2022 (Figure 1 ). HIV DRM testing was performed at the two accredited centralized laboratories in Kenya using Sanger sequencing for participants in the intervention group with VL ≥ 1000 copies/ml.","The content of the paper discusses the sequencing of HIV DNA from patient samples, specifically mentioning the use of Sanger sequencing for HIV drug resistance mutation testing in participants with viral loads above a certain threshold. This indicates that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples, thus confirming that the paper does report HIV sequences from patient samples."
37938856,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We aimed to determine the impact of point‐of‐care (POC) VL and targeted drug resistance mutation (DRM) testing in improving VS among pregnant and postpartum women on antiretroviral therapy. Our combined strategy markedly decreased turnaround time but did not increase VS rates, which were already very high, or sustained VS among pregnant and postpartum women living with HIV. Further research on how best to utilize POC VL and DRM testing is needed to optimize sustained VS among this population.","The content of the paper focuses on point-of-care viral load testing and drug resistance mutation testing in pregnant and postpartum women living with HIV. It does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data. The study's primary outcomes are related to viral suppression and resistance mutations, not susceptibility."
37938856,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"De-identified participant data, data dictionary or other specified data sets may be made available to others requesting them upon communication with corresponding author, demonstration of appropriate ethic reviews and establishment of data sharing agreements.",The paper mentions that
37938856,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No HIV sequences were reported.,No evidence,"The content of the paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates, indicating that no sequences were shared publicly in this study."
37938856,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,No individuals had samples obtained.,"We conducted an open‐label, pre/post‐intervention (or intervention/control) prospective cohort study, enrolling pregnant women living with HIV during their ANC care and followed them through 6 months postpartum in five public‐sector HIV treatment facilities in Kenya from February 2019 to November 2022 (Figure 1). HIV DRM testing was performed at the two accredited centralized laboratories in Kenya using Sanger sequencing for participants in the intervention group with VL ≥ 1000 copies/ml. Follow‐up of the control cohort overlapped in calendar time with enrolment of the intervention cohort (control group enrolment started on 26 February 2019 and follow‐up lasted until 22 April 2021; intervention group enrolment started on 7 October 2019 and follow‐up lasted until 31 December 2021).","The content of the paper does not provide any information on the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing. The focus is on the intervention and its outcomes, not on the specific number of sequenced individuals."
37938856,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Kenya,"We conducted an open‐label, pre/post‐intervention (or intervention/control) prospective cohort study, enrolling pregnant women living with HIV during their ANC care and followed them through 6 months postpartum in five public‐sector HIV treatment facilities in Kenya from February 2019 to November 2022 (Figure 1). The study was conducted in low‐resource, high‐HIV burden public sector facilities in Kisumu County, western Kenya, with one of the highest prevalence of HIV infections in Kenya (Table S1). The pre‐intervention cohort served as the control group, receiving standard‐of‐care (SOC), which consisted of centralized VL testing approximately every 6 months, from all five facilities. The post‐intervention cohort served as the intervention group, receiving a combined strategy of POC VL every 3 months, targeted DRM testing, and clinical management support, also from all five facilities. HIV DRM testing was performed at the two accredited centralized laboratories in Kenya using Sanger sequencing for participants in the intervention group with VL ≥ 1000 copies/ml.","The paper explicitly states that the study was conducted in five public-sector HIV treatment facilities in Kenya, indicating that the sequenced samples were obtained from this country."
37938856,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2019 to 2022,"We conducted an open‐label, pre/post‐intervention (or intervention/control) prospective cohort study, enrolling pregnant women living with HIV during their ANC care and followed them through 6 months postpartum in five public‐sector HIV treatment facilities in Kenya from February 2019 to November 2022 (Figure 1). The pre‐intervention cohort served as the control group, receiving standard‐of‐care (SOC), which consisted of centralized VL testing approximately every 6 months, from all five facilities. The post‐intervention cohort served as the intervention group, receiving a combined strategy of POC VL every 3 months, targeted DRM testing, and clinical management support, also from all five facilities. Follow‐up of the control cohort overlapped in calendar time with enrolment of the intervention cohort (control group enrolment started on 26 February 2019 and follow‐up lasted until 22 April 2021; intervention group enrolment started on 7 October 2019 and follow‐up lasted until 31 December 2021).","The paper mentions that samples were obtained from February 2019 to November 2022, which is the year range for the sequenced samples."
37938856,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,HIV DRM testing was performed at the two accredited centralized laboratories in Kenya using Sanger sequencing for participants in the intervention group with VL ≥ 1000 copies/ml.,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. The focus is on Sanger sequencing directly from patient samples.
37938856,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INI","First-line ART regimens among adults during the study period included combinations of two nucleos(t)ide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) lamivudine and tenofovir with either (1) NNRTI efavirenz or (2) integrase inhibitors (INSTIs) dolutegravir, and protease inhibitors (PIs)‐containing ART, for example with atazanavir/ritonavir, were considered alternative first‐line or second‐line regimens; a wider transition for patients on non‐dolutegravir first‐ or second‐line regimens to dolutegravir‐containing ART occurred in 2019.","The paper mentions the sequencing of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) for nucleos(t)ide reverse transcriptase inhibitors, NNRTIs, integrase inhibitors, and protease inhibitors, indicating that these genes were sequenced."
37938856,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"HIV DRM testing was performed at the two accredited centralized laboratories in Kenya using Sanger sequencing for participants in the intervention group with VL ≥ 1000 copies/ml.0. Ours is the first study to combine VL with DRM testing in optimizing VS among pregnant and postpartum women; however, it has limitations.",The paper explicitly states that Sanger sequencing was used for HIV drug resistance mutation testing in the intervention group.
37938856,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"HIV DRM testing was performed at the two accredited centralized laboratories in Kenya using Sanger sequencing for participants in the intervention group with VL ≥ 1000 copies/ml.0. Ours is the first study to combine VL with DRM testing in optimizing VS among pregnant and postpartum women; however, it has limitations. We conducted a pre/post‐intervention prospective cohort study among 820 pregnant women accessing HIV care at five public‐sector facilities in western Kenya from 2019 to 2022. The pre‐intervention or control group consisted of standard‐of‐care (SOC) centralized VL testing every 6 months and the post‐intervention or intervention group consisted of a combined strategy of POC VL every 3 months, targeted DRM testing, and clinical management support. The primary outcome was VS (VL ≤1000 copies/ml) at 6 months postpartum; secondary outcomes included uptake and turnaround times for VL testing and sustained VS.","The paper discusses the  the CD4 cell count, which means plasma samples was sequenced."
37938856,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"We conducted an open‐label, pre/post‐intervention (or intervention/control) prospective cohort study, enrolling pregnant women living with HIV during their ANC care and followed them through 6 months postpartum in five public‐sector HIV treatment facilities in Kenya from February 2019 to November 2022 (Figure 1). The pre‐intervention cohort served as the control group, receiving standard‐of‐care (SOC), which consisted of centralized VL testing approximately every 6 months, from all five facilities. The post‐intervention cohort served as the intervention group, receiving a combined strategy of POC VL every 3 months, targeted DRM testing, and clinical management support, also from all five facilities. HIV DRM testing was performed at the two accredited centralized laboratories in Kenya using Sanger sequencing for participants in the intervention group with VL ≥ 1000 copies/ml. We identified 54 episodes of VL>1000 copies/ml, among 48 (11.7%) participants. 52 DRM tests were requested, of which 46 (88%) were successfully conducted (six samples failed to amplify) and all identified at least one DRM","The paper mentions that 54 episodes of viral load exceeding 1000 copies/ml were identified, which indicates virological failure, and sequencing was performed on these samples."
37938856,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We conducted an open‐label, pre/post‐intervention (or intervention/control) prospective cohort study, enrolling pregnant women living with HIV during their ANC care and followed them through 6 months postpartum in five public‐sector HIV treatment facilities in Kenya from February 2019 to November 2022 The pre‐intervention cohort served as the control group, receiving standard‐of‐care (SOC), which consisted of centralized VL testing approximately every 6 months, from all five facilities. The post‐intervention cohort served as the intervention group, receiving a combined strategy of POC VL every 3 months, targeted DRM testing, and clinical management support, also from all five facilities. HIV DRM testing was performed at the two accredited centralized laboratories in Kenya using Sanger sequencing for participants in the intervention group with VL ≥ 1000 copies/ml.","The paper describes a pre/post-intervention prospective cohort study, not a clinical trial, indicating that the patients were not in a clinical trial."
37938856,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"First‐line ART regimens among adults during the study period included combinations of two nucleos(t)ide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) lamivudine and tenofovir with either (1) NNRTI efavirenz or (2) integrase inhibitors (INSTIs) dolutegravir, and protease inhibitors (PIs)‐containing ART, for example with atazanavir/ritonavir, were considered alternative first‐line or second‐line regimens; a wider transition for patients on non‐dolutegravir first‐ or second‐line regimens to dolutegravir‐containing ART occurred in 2019.","The paper mentions that first-line ART regimens included drugs received by the participants before the study. The study included 820 women, and 696 (85%) were married. Overall, 622 (76%) participants were on NNRTI‐, 71 (8.7%) on PI‐ and 80 (10%) on integrase‐containing ART at enrolment, and the median time on ART was 4.0 years (IQR 1.2, 6.3). Among 771 (94%) of the participants with a VL documented in the 24 months prior to enrolment,672 (82%) had VS; which indicates that the paper does report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received NRTIs, NRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs to treat HIV infection before sample sequencing."
37938856,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, INSTIs","First‐line ART regimens among adults during the study period included combinations of two nucleos(t)ide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) lamivudine and tenofovir with either (1) NNRTI efavirenz or (2) integrase inhibitors (INSTIs) dolutegravir, and protease inhibitors (PIs)‐containing ART, for example with atazanavir/ritonavir, were considered alternative first‐line or second‐line regimens; a wider transition for patients on non‐dolutegravir first‐ or second‐line regimens to dolutegravir‐containing ART occurred in 2019.","The paper lists the drug classes that were part of the first-line and alternative regimens during the study, which include NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs."
37938856,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"lamivudine, tenofovir, efavirenz, dolutegravir, atazanavir, ritonavir","First‐line ART regimens among adults during the study period included combinations of two nucleos(t)ide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) lamivudine and tenofovir with either (1) NNRTI efavirenz or (2) integrase inhibitors (INSTIs) dolutegravir, and protease inhibitors (PIs)‐containing ART, for example with atazanavir/ritonavir, were considered alternative first‐line or second‐line regimens; a wider transition for patients on non‐dolutegravir first‐ or second‐line regimens to dolutegravir‐containing ART occurred in 2019.","The paper mentions the specific drugs that were included in the first-line and alternative regimens, which are lamivudine, tenofovir, efavirenz, dolutegravir, atazanavir, and ritonavir."
37941373,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"In this study, we examined the occurrence of acquired and transmitted drug resistance to integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) in HIV-1 strains in Chongqing (China) for guiding for the routine testing of INSTI-associated HIV-1 genotype resistance. Plasma samples were obtained from HIV-1 patients at Chongqing Public Health Medical Center from July 2019 to August 2022. Out of the total of 1032 patients, 4.17% (43/1032) exhibited major and/or accessory INSTI resistance mutations, with major mutations present in 1.45% (15/1032) of cases and accessory mutations present in 2.71% (28/1032) of cases.","The paper clearly states that plasma samples were obtained from HIV-1 patients at Chongqing Public Health Medical Center. It mentions the analysis of sequences from these samples to identify INSTI resistance, indicating that HIV sequences were reported from patient samples. The study examined the occurrence of acquired and transmitted drug resistance to integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) in HIV-1 strains in Chongqing (China) for guiding for the routine testing of INSTI-associated HIV-1 genotype resistance. This is focused on the in vitro  but also provided some information that the sequencing was done on IN gene from patient samples. and also mentioned the drug history and ART history of patients were collected so we can infer that this is a clinical isolate and not a lab isolate, it's sequencing is done on patient samples. Therefore, the answer is Yes."
37941373,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"In this study, we examined the occurrence of acquired and transmitted drug resistance to integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) in HIV-1 strains in Chongqing (China) for guiding for the routine testing of INSTI-associated HIV-1 genotype resistance. Plasma samples were obtained from HIV-1 patients at Chongqing Public Health Medical Center from July 2019 to August 2022. Besides, amplification, sequence, and analysis of the portion of the HIV-1 pol gene that encodes the integrase protein were implemented to identify INSTI resistance. Integrase sequence data was harvested for a comprehensive cohort of 1032 patients infected with HIV-1. This cohort consisted of 564 ART-naive patients, 465 ART-treated patients, and 3 patients with an unknown treatment history. Within the study group, we identified INSTI resistance in 21 patients (2.03%, 21/1032), including 17 ART-treated patients (3.66%, 17/465). Among the ART-treated patients, 12 were INSTI-treated (11.76%, 12/102), 5 were INSTI-naive (1.38%, 5/363), and 4 were ART-ineffective patients (0.71%, 4/564). The prevalent major resistance mutation was Q148R (0.48%, 5/1032), while the most prevalent accessory resistance mutation was E157Q (1.65%, 17/1032).","The paper focuses on the genotypic resistance testing and sequencing of HIV-1 samples to identify INSTI resistance mutations. It does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility assays or data. The study primarily discusses the prevalence of resistance mutations and their clinical implications, rather than providing detailed in vitro susceptibility testing results for the drugs tested. The absence of in vitro susceptibility data is a significant aspect of the research, indicating a specific focus on genotypic resistance rather than phenotypic susceptibility testing. Therefore, the answer is No."
37941373,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,The data in this study are available from the first author (HZ.Z) on reasonable request.,"The paper mentions that the data in this study are available from the first author on reasonable request, indicating that the sequences were not made publicly available in a database. The paper does not provide information indicating that the sequences were deposited in a public database such as GenBank. Therefore, the answer is No."
37941373,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,,"The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. This information is typically provided in the methods or results sections of papers that include sequencing data, but it is absent in this study, Additionally, the paper does not provide information indicating that the sequences were deposited in a public database such as GenBank. Therefore, the answer is Not reported."
37941373,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,1032,"Samples were harvested from 1080 patients, and successful amplification of the IN gene was achieved in 1032 samples. The demographic characteristics of all enrolled patients (n = 1032, median age of 52 years, ranging from 12 to 89 years) are depicted in Table 2.","The paper states that samples were harvested from 1080 patients, and successful amplification of the IN gene was achieved in 1032 samples. This indicates that 1032 individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing. This information is provided in the results section where the characteristics of the patients are described. The paper clearly mentions the number of patients from whom samples were collected and sequenced. Therefore, the answer is 1032."
37941373,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"In this study, we examined the occurrence of acquired and transmitted drug resistance to integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) in HIV-1 strains in Chongqing (China) for guiding for the routine testing of INSTI-associated HIV-1 genotype resistance.","The paper specifically mentions that the study was conducted in Chongqing, China, and the samples were obtained from patients at Chongqing Public Health Medical Center. All the patients were from the same hospital in Chongqing China, indicating all samples were obtained in china. Therefore, the answer is China."
37941373,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2019 to 2022,"HIV-1-infected inpatients and outpatients who visited Chongqing Public Health Medical Center between July 2019 and August 2022 were enrolled in this study. Plasma samples were collected from patients with HIV-1 viral load (VL) greater than 1,000 copies/ml and subjected to a subsequent polymerase chain reaction (PCR) to amplify the IN gene. Data on the patient's general condition, transmission route, VL, CD4+ T-cell count, and ART history were collected.","The paper states that HIV-1-infected inpatients and outpatients who visited Chongqing Public Health Medical Center between July 2019 and August 2022 were enrolled in this study. This clearly indicates the time frame during which the samples were obtained. The study period is explicitly mentioned in the study population section of the paper. Therefore, the answer is 2019 to 2022."
37941373,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"HIV-RNA was extracted utilizing a viral nucleic acid extraction kit (Jiangsu Shuoshi), followed by amplification of the target fragment by nested PCR. The HIV-1 IN gene was amplified by a validated In-house method.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of nested PCR for amplification of the target fragment, and the use of Sanger sequencing, which is the standard method that doesn't involve cloning. Therefore, the answer is No."
37941373,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,IN gene,"HIV-1-infected inpatients and outpatients who visited Chongqing Public Health Medical Center between July 2019 and August 2022 were enrolled in this study. Plasma samples were collected from patients with HIV-1 viral load (VL) greater than 1,000 copies/ml and subjected to a subsequent polymerase chain reaction (PCR) to amplify the IN gene. Data on the patient's general condition, transmission route, VL, CD4+ T-cell count, and ART history were collected. The HIV-1 IN gene was amplified by a validated In-house method. The first round of PCR was completed utilizing the HiScript®II one-step RT-PCR kit (Nanjing Vazyme, China). The second round was implemented with the help of an Ace Taq kit (Nanjing Vazyme, China) for nested PCR amplification of target sequences (1157–1213 bp) with two primer sets (4007F and 5219 R for the first round of PCR and 4063F and 5219 R for the second round of PCR).","The paper mentions the amplification and sequencing of the integrase (IN) gene in several sections, including the abstract, materials and methods, and results. The study focuses on the examination of INSTI resistance mutations in the IN gene specifically. Therefore, the answer is IN gene."
37941373,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"In this study, we examined the occurrence of acquired and transmitted drug resistance to integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) in HIV-1 strains in Chongqing (China) for guiding for the routine testing of INSTI-associated HIV-1 genotype resistance. Plasma samples were obtained from HIV-1 patients at Chongqing Public Health Medical Center from July 2019 to August 2022. Besides, amplification, sequence, and analysis of the portion of the HIV-1 pol gene that encodes the integrase protein were implemented to identify INSTI resistance. Integrase sequence data was harvested for a comprehensive cohort of 1032 patients infected with HIV-1. This cohort consisted of 564 ART-naive patients, 465 ART-treated patients, and 3 patients with an unknown treatment history. Within the study group, we identified INSTI resistance in 21 patients (2.03%, 21/1032), including 17 ART-treated patients (3.66%, 17/465). Among the ART-treated patients, 12 were INSTI-treated (11.76%, 12/102), 5 were INSTI-naive (1.38%, 5/363), and 4 were ART-ineffective patients (0.71%, 4/564). The prevalent major resistance mutation was Q148R (0.48%, 5/1032), while the most prevalent accessory resistance mutation was E157Q (1.65%, 17/1032). In light of the above, it is recommended that the incidence of accessory genotype analysis should be considered before starting any future INSTI-based therapy, especially in patients with drug resistance to NRTIs and NNRTIs and the reduction of INSTI sensitivity should be carefully monitored and investigated. Regular monitoring for resistance should be implemented after the use of INSTIs, and, importantly, ongoing monitoring of the decreasing susceptibility to INSTIs is crucial following the initiation of treatment with INSTIs.","The paper uses Sanger sequencing method for the identification of the clinically significant known drug resistance mutations. Sanger sequencing is a method that's widely known and has been described here without being labelled. Therefore, the answer is Sanger sequencing."
37941373,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"Plasma samples were obtained from HIV-1 patients at Chongqing Public Health Medical Center from July 2019 to August 2022. Plasma samples were collected from patients with HIV-1 viral load (VL) greater than 1,000 copies/ml and subjected to a subsequent polymerase chain reaction (PCR) to amplify the IN gene. Blood samples were drawn into EDTA-containing tubes, and subsequently, plasma samples were obtained following centrifugation. The plasma samples were then analysed for HIV-1 RNA VL employing the Roche HIV-1 nucleic Acid Quantitative Detection System (HiScript®Ampli Prep/COBAS®Taq COBAS®HIV-1 Test, 2.0)3. HIV-RNA was extracted utilizing a viral nucleic acid extraction kit (Jiangsu Shuoshi), followed by amplification of the target fragment by nested PCR.","The paper mentions that plasma samples were obtained from HIV-1 patients and used for sequencing. The plasma samples were specifically mentioned in the context of analyzing HIV-1 RNA viral load and for extracting HIV-RNA for sequencing. Therefore, the answer is Plasma samples."
37941373,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"This cohort consisted of 564 ART-naive patients, 465 ART-treated patients, and 3 patients with an unknown treatment history. Within the study group, we identified INSTI resistance in 21 patients (2.03%, 21/1032), including 17 ART-treated patients (3.66%, 17/465). The remaining patients on ART (45.06%, 465/1032) were treated with dolutegravir, rilpivirine, lamivudine, tenofovir, crizaline and efavirenz, among others. Among those seventeen patients with INSTI resistance, 12 were INSTI-treated while 5 were INSTI-naive. In one patient, the major resistance mutations E138K plus the major resistance mutations G118R resulted in high-level resistance to all INSTIs. In another patient, the major resistance mutations E138K, S147G, and Q148R plus the accessory mutation A128T caused high-level resistance to all INSTIs. In the remaining patients, major resistance mutations E138K, S147G, Q148R, and R263K without accessory mutations resulted in high-level resistance to all INSTIs (H3944 in Table 3).","The paper mentions that 17 ART treated patients experienced INSTI resistance and 465 patients were on ART, which means the ART treated group may have experienced virological failure, although not explicitly stated. Therefore, the answer is Yes."
37941373,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"HIV-1-infected inpatients and outpatients who visited Chongqing Public Health Medical Center between July 2019 and August 2022 were enrolled in this study. Plasma samples were collected from patients with HIV-1 viral load (VL) greater than 1,000 copies/ml and subjected to a subsequent polymerase chain reaction (PCR) to amplify the IN gene. The data were analysed anonymously, and the requirement for written or oral informed consent was waived.","The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It describes the enrollment of HIV-1-infected inpatients and outpatients  and also mentioned the IRB waived the consent which is not allowed in a clinical trial. Therefore, the answer is No."
37941373,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"This cohort consisted of 564 ART-naive patients, 465 ART-treated patients, and 3 patients with an unknown treatment history. Within the study group, we identified INSTI resistance in 21 patients (2.03%, 21/1032), including 17 ART-treated patients (3.66%, 17/465). The remaining patients on ART (45.06%, 465/1032) were treated with dolutegravir, rilpivirine, lamivudine, tenofovir, crizaline and efavirenz, among others.","The paper mentions that there were 465 ART-treated patients in the study, and the remaining patients were on ART, which means they had previously received ARV drugs. INSTI resistance was also observed in this group of patients, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs. Therefore, the answer is Yes."
37941373,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, and INSTI","The remaining patients on ART (45.06%, 465/1032) were treated with dolutegravir, rilpivirine, lamivudine, tenofovir, crizaline and efavirenz, among others. : Dolutegravir (DTG) is an integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI)43. Lamivudine (3TC) and tenofovir DF (TDF) are NRTIs3, while rilpivirine (RPV) and efavirenz (EFV) are NNRTIs43.","The paper mentions that patients were treated with drugs from the NRTI, NNRTI, and INSTI classes. Lamivudine (3TC) and tenofovir (TDF) are NRTIs, rilpivirine (RPV) and efavirenz (EFV) are NNRTIs, and dolutegravir (DTG) is an integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI). Therefore, the answer is NRTI, NNRTI, and INSTI."
37941373,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Rilpivirine, Dolutegravir, Efavirenz, Crizaline","The remaining patients on ART (45.06%, 465/1032) were treated with dolutegravir, rilpivirine, lamivudine, tenofovir, crizaline and efavirenz, among others.","The paper lists specific drugs that were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing. These include tenofovir (TDF), lamivudine (3TC), rilpivirine (RPV), dolutegravir (DTG), efavirenz (EFV), and crizaline (noted as a treatment in clinicals but details are not provided). Therefore, the answer is Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Rilpivirine, Dolutegravir, Efavirenz, Crizaline."
37946329,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"This study was approved by the Institutional Ethics Committee of the Sixth People’s Hospital of Zhengzhou (No. 2019-04). All participants signed written informed consent before sample collection. Demographic data and medical records, including HIV viral load, CD4+ T-cell count, and transmission route, were collected.",The paper mentions that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples. It discusses the analysis of plasma samples derived from people living with HIV (PLWH) in the Sixth People’s Hospital of Zhengzhou. The study involved genotypic drug resistance testing based on these patient samples.
37946329,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"A total of 1078 patients who failed in ART treatment, the partial pol gene sequence was successfully amplified in 921, and the integrase (INT) gene sequence in 751 patients. The partial pol gene sequences were also successfully amplified in all the patients whose INT genes were sequenced. The analysis included a total of 921 HIV-1 patients, with a median age of 43 (IQR, 30--53) years. Male patients constituted 73.9% (681/921) of the study population. Han ethnicity accounted for 99.5% (916/921) of the total patients. Further, 58.1% (535/921) of the patients were married and 41.9% (386/921) were single. Farmers accounted for 44.4% (409/921) of the total patients, followed by workers (36.9%, 340/921), non-workers (16.2%, 149/921), and students (2.5%, 23/921). Transmission route data were available for 921 patients, including those who underwent plasmapheresis constituting 32.5% (299/921), followed by those with heterosexual orientation (30.2%, 278/921), men who have sex with men (MSM) (22.6%, 208/921), mother-to-child transmission (10.5%, 97/921), and other or unknown data (4.2%, 39/921). Baseline CD4+ T-cell count data were available for 894 patients; the median CD4+ T-cell count was 81.5 cells/μL (IQR, 23.0--207.3). Baseline HIV-1 viral load (VL) data were available for 858 patients; the median baseline VL was 18,698.5 (min/max, 202.0--39,771,796.0) copies/μL. Among the 921 patients, 748 patients (81.2%) had one or more DRMs for any drug class.",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on genotypic drug resistance testing and the analysis of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) in clinical samples from HIV-1 patients who failed ART treatment.
37946329,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"The pol gene was successfully amplified in 921 out of 1078 patients, and the INT gene was analyzed in 751 patients. INSTIs were not freely available to patients and were not covered by medical insurance in most provinces of China before 2022, so only a limited number of patients were treated with INSTIs and resistance testing against INSTIs was deemed unnecessary in clinical settings. Phylogenetic analysis based on the determined partial pol gene sequences indicated that the most common subtype was B, which accounted for 59.5%, followed by CRF01_AE, CRF07_BC, CRF55_01B, and CRF08_BC.","The paper does not explicitly state that the sequences were made publicly available. However, it mentions that the sequences were submitted to the Stanford HIV-1 drug resistance database for analysis, which implies that the sequences are accessible for research purposes. Additionally, the prevalence of subtypes and resistance mutations are shared in supplementary tables which indicates that the data is intended for public dissemination."
37946329,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. This information is typically included in the Methods or Results sections of a paper, but it is not present in the provided content."
37946329,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,921,"The analysis included a total of 921 HIV-1 patients, with a median age of 43 (IQR, 30--53) years. Among the 921 patients, 748 patients (81.2%) had one or more DRMs. When analyzed by individual drug class, the incidence of DRMs was 71.1% (655/921), 78.3% (721/921), 5.2% (48/921), and 3.1% (23/751) for nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs), non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs), protease inhibitors (PIs), and integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs), respectively. The most common DRMs included M184V/I (576, 62.5%) for NRTIs, K103N/R (294, 31.9%) for NNRTIs, I54V (35, 3.8%) for PIs, and E157Q (25, 3.3%) for INSTIs.","The paper states that the analysis included a total of 921 HIV-1 patients. It provides detailed demographic and clinical data about these patients, including the genotypic drug resistance testing results."
37946329,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"This study was approved by the Institutional Ethics Committee of the Sixth People’s Hospital of Zhengzhou (No. 2019-04). Drug resistance mutations (DRMs) involving human deficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) diminish the efficacy of current antiretroviral therapy (ART) regimens, resulting in treatment failure. A better understanding of the prevalence of local DRMs that lead to ART failure can provide valuable data for clinical- and government-level decision-making. The analysis included a total of 921 HIV-1 patients, with a median age of 43 (IQR, 30--53) years. Male patients constituted 73.9% (681/921) of the study population. Han ethnicity accounted for 99.5% (916/921) of the total patients. Farmers accounted for 44.4% (409/921) of the total patients, followed by workers (36.9%, 340/921), non-workers (16.2%, 149/921), and students (2.5%, 23/921).","The paper mentions that the study was conducted in the Sixth People’s Hospital of Zhengzhou, Henan Province, China. All the patients were from this hospital, and the demographic data reflect the local population characteristics."
37946329,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2018 to 2022,"The analysis included a total of 921 HIV-1 patients, with a median age of 43 (IQR, 30--53) years. Male patients constituted 73.9% (681/921) of the study population. Han ethnicity accounted for 99.5% (916/921) of the total patients. Further, 58.1% (535/921) of the patients were married and 41.9% (386/921) were single. Farmers accounted for 44.4% (409/921) of the total patients, followed by workers (36.9%, 340/921), non-workers (16.2%, 149/921), and students (2.5%, 23/921). Transmission route data were available for 921 patients, including those who underwent plasmapheresis constituting 32.5% (299/921), followed by those with heterosexual orientation (30.2%, 278/921), men who have sex with men (MSM) (22.6%, 208/921), mother-to-child transmission (10.5%, 97/921), and other or unknown data (4.2%, 39/921).",The paper mentions that the HIV sequences were obtained from plasma samples derived from PLWH in the Sixth People’s Hospital of Zhengzhou from June 2018 to April 2022.
37946329,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The pol gene was successfully amplified in 921 out of 1078 patients, and the INT gene was analyzed in 751 patients. INSTIs were not freely available to patients and were not covered by medical insurance in most provinces of China before 2022, so only a limited number of patients were treated with INSTIs and resistance testing against INSTIs was deemed unnecessary in clinical settings. Phylogenetic analysis based on the determined partial pol gene sequences indicated that the most common subtype was B, which accounted for 59.5%, followed by CRF01_AE, CRF07_BC, CRF55_01B, and CRF08_BC.",The paper does not mention that samples were cloned prior to sequencing. It discusses the amplification and sequencing of the partial pol and integrase gene-coding sequences without mentioning any cloning process.
37946329,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Partial pol and complete integrase genes,"Further, the partial polymerase (pol) and complete integrase gene-coding sequences were amplified, sequenced, and analyzed to identify DRMs. The pol gene was successfully amplified in 921 out of 1078 patients, and the INT gene was analyzed in 751 patients.",The paper explicitly mentions that the partial polymerase (pol) and complete integrase gene-coding sequences were amplified and sequenced to identify drug resistance mutations (DRMs).
37946329,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not specified,"Further, the partial polymerase (pol) and complete integrase gene- coding sequences were amplified, sequenced, and analyzed to identify DRMs. The pol gene was successfully amplified in 921 out of 1078 patients, and the INT gene was analyzed in 751 patients. INSTIs were not freely available to patients and were not covered by medical insurance in most provinces of China before 2022, so only a limited number of patients were treated with INSTIs and resistance testing against INSTIs was deemed unnecessary in clinical settings. Phylogenetic analysis based on the determined partial pol gene sequences indicated that the most common subtype was B, which accounted for 59.5%, followed by CRF01_AE, CRF07_BC, CRF55_01B, and CRF08_BC.",The paper does not provide specific details about the sequencing method used. It focuses on the results of the sequencing and the analysis of drug resistance mutations.
37946329,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"The DRM status of people living with HIV (PLWH) refractory to ART (viral load >200 copies/mL) was assessed based on analysis of plasma samples derived from PLWH in the Sixth People’s Hospital of Zhengzhou from June 2018 to April 2022. Demographic data and medical records, including HIV viral load, CD4+ T-cell count, and transmission route, were collected.",The paper mentions that plasma samples were used for the analysis of drug resistance mutations (DRMs). It specifies that the study involved the analysis of plasma samples derived from PLWH in the Sixth People’s Hospital of Zhengzhou.
37946329,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"The DRM status of people living with HIV (PLWH) refractory to ART (viral load >200 copies/mL) was assessed based on analysis of plasma samples derived from PLWH in the Sixth People’s Hospital of Zhengzhou from June 2018 to April 2022. Our data showed that the incidence of DRMs was 81.2% (748/921), which is consistent with the incidence worldwide (47--90%); however, it was significantly higher than that of 52--57% reported previously in China. This might be explained by historical reasons as Henan province harbored the earliest group of PLWH due to illicit blood donation.",The paper mentions that the DRM status was assessed based on analysis of plasma samples derived from people living with HIV (PLWH) refractory to ART (viral load >200 copies/mL). This indicates that the sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen.
37946329,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This study was approved by the Institutional Ethics Committee of the Sixth People’s Hospital of Zhengzhou. All participants signed written informed consent before sample collection. The analysis included a total of 921 HIV-1 patients, with a median age of 43 years. Male patients constituted 73.9% of the study population. Han ethnicity accounted for 99.5% of the total patients. Farmers accounted for 44.4% of the total patients, followed by workers, non-workers, and students.","The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It describes a study conducted at a hospital, focusing on genotypic drug resistance testing and demographic data of HIV-1 patients."
37946329,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"A total of 1078 patients who failed in ART treatment, the partial pol gene sequence was successfully amplified in 921, and the integrase (INT) gene sequence in 751 patients. Among the 921 patients, 748 patients (81.2%) had one or more DRMs, which we got through Stanford HIV-1 drug resistance database. Generally, a delay of few weeks to months existed between blood sampling for viral load determination and genotypic drug resistance analysis. Therefore, PLWH with poor adherence might have restarted their treatment regimens, which could have subsequently suppressed viremia and thus failure to genotypic drug resistance due to low viral load. The pol gene was successfully amplified in 921 out of 1078 patients, and the INT gene was analyzed in 751 patients. INSTIs were not freely available to patients and were not covered by medical insurance in most provinces of China before 2022, so only a limited number of patients were treated with INSTIs and resistance testing against INSTIs was deemed unnecessary in clinical settings. Phylogenetic analysis based on the determined partial pol gene sequences indicated that the most common subtype was B, which accounted for 59.5%, followed by CRF01_AE, CRF07_BC, CRF55_01B, and CRF08_BC, which is consistent with subtype patterns in other regions.","The paper mentions that the study involved patients who had failed in ART treatment, and genotypic drug resistance testing was performed on their samples. This indicates that the sequences were obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37946329,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI","A total of 1078 patients who failed in ART treatment, the partial pol gene sequence was successfully amplified in 921, and the integrase (INT) gene sequence in 751 patients. The incidence of DRMs in ART regimens containing NNRTI, PI, and integrase inhibitor (INI) was 87.7% (582/664), 63.8% (125/196), and 71.0% (22/31), respectively. INSTIs were not freely available to patients and were not covered by medical insurance in most provinces of China before 2022, so only a limited number of patients were treated with INSTIs and resistance testing against INSTIs was deemed unnecessary in clinical settings6. Phylogenetic analysis based on the determined partial pol gene sequences indicated that the most common subtype was B, which accounted for 59.5%, followed by CRF01_AE, CRF07_BC, CRF55_01B, and CRF08_BC.","The paper mentions the incidence of DRMs in ART regimens containing NNRTI, PI, and integrase inhibitor (INI) was 87.7% (582/664), 63.8% (125/196), and 71.0% (22/31), respectively46. INSTIs were not freely available to patients and were not covered by medical insurance in most provinces of China before 2022, so only a limited number of patients were treated with INSTIs and resistance testing against INSTIs was deemed unnecessary in clinical settings. Phylogenetic analysis based on the determined partial pol gene sequences indicated that the most common subtype was B, which accounted for 59.5%, followed by CRF01_AE, CRF07_BC, CRF55_01B, and CRF08_BC, which is consistent with subtype patterns in other regions."
37946329,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,,,The paper does not specify the exact drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing. It focuses on the classes of drugs and the resistance mutations without detailing individual drug histories.
37957382,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"we analyzed plasma virus sequences in eight ART-treated individuals with NSV(86% male) and show that they are composed of large clones without evidence of viral evolution over time in those with longitudinal samples; when been comparing between NSV and ART-suppressed individuals, We enrolled eight participants with ongoing NSV, 88% men, with a median age of 60 years and median ART duration of 10 years. The NSV samples were taken from different time points, enabling us to study these participants longitudinally.",The paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples by mentioning that plasma virus sequences were analyzed in eight ART-treated individuals with non-suppressible viremia (NSV). This indicates that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples.
37957382,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,No evidence found,"The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data or related experiments, focusing instead on the analysis of HIV sequences and their characteristics in the context of non-suppressible viremia (NSV). It discusses drug concentrations in plasma but not susceptibility data."
37957382,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,Sequence data were submitted to Genbank (BioProject: PRJNA973660),"The paper mentions that sequence data were submitted to GenBank (BioProject: PRJNA973660), indicating that the sequences were made publicly available."
37957382,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No data,No evidence found,"The paper does not provide specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, only mentioning that the sequences were submitted to GenBank."
37957382,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,Eight,"we analyzed plasma virus sequences in eight ART-treated individuals with NSV; we enrolled eight participants with ongoing NSV, 88% men, with a median age of 60 years and median ART duration of 10 years.","The paper states that eight participants with ongoing NSV were enrolled, indicating that samples were obtained from eight individuals for HIV sequencing."
37957382,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,United States,"characteristics of the ARV samples were presented in Oklahoma city, jabberwocky study the study was approved by mass general brigham institutional review board, both ARV supppresed coercos  and participants with NVS were matched by CD4 count  adiar pptvrco","The paper does not provide specific details about the countries from which the samples were obtained, it does mention some study participated within US."
37957382,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,No data,No evidence found,"The paper does not specify the exact years from which the sequenced samples were obtained, focusing instead on the duration of ART and the period of NSV."
37957382,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,Quality and cloning of near-full-length proviral sequences in the 8 40--1000 copies per ml NSV participants.  No evidence found,"The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, it has quality description of sequences, no cloning was reported."
37957382,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Near-full-length proviruses and The sequnced plasma HIV-1 pol-env RNA,Longitudinal single-genome sequencing of near-full-length proviruses and plasma HIV-1 *pol* - *env* RNA was performed,"The paper mentions the sequencing of near-full-length proviruses and plasma HIV-1 *pol* - *env* RNA, indicating that these regions were sequenced."
37957382,10,What method was used for sequencing?,MiSeq sequencing,Products underwent Illumina barcoded library construction and MiSeq sequencing.,"The paper mentions that products underwent Illumina barcoded library construction and MiSeq sequencing, indicating the method used."
37957382,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma RNA and PBMC DNA,"we showed longitudinal PCR of HIV-1 proviruses in blood CD4 + T cells, DNA isolation and HIV reservoir quantification. HIV-1 integration targeting preferences are demarcated by various features of active chromatin, including transcription - histone epigenetic marks - and nuclear speckle proximity.  Products underwent Illumina barcoded library construction and MiSeq sequencing. Extracted RNA was diluted to single viral genome levels to meet the criteria of single-genome sequencing of having no more than one template in each well, theoretically no more than 25% of wells being positive for HIV. Primers were designed to amplify pol-env, a 6.7-kb region (Supplementary Data 1). The amplification reaction was performed using 0.5 µl primers (10 µmol), 1 µl (10 mmol) MgSO4, 1 µl (10 mmol) dNTPs and 1 U Platinum Taq Polymerase (Invitrogen) in 25 µl total volume.","The paper mentions the sequencing of near-full-length proviruses from PBMC DNA and plasma HIV-1 RNA, indicating the types of samples sequenced."
37957382,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"We enrolled eight participants with ongoing NSV, 88% men, with a median age of 60 years and median ART duration of 10 years.  The median duration of virologic suppression before the NSV and duration of NSV for all participants were 4 and 2 years, respectively. During the NSV episodes, the median viral load was 143 copies ml−1 and the median CD4 count was 798 cells µl−1.","The paper mentions that the participants had ongoing NSV, which is defined as persistent low-level viremia on antiretroviral therapy without evidence of ART non-adherence or significant drug resistance, indicating virological failure."
37957382,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"In an observational study, we enrolled 8 ART-treated participants with three or more HIV-1 RNA levels between 40 and 1,000 copies ml−1 over 24 months of whom 7 (88%) were men and compared them with a historic cohort of 11 ART-suppressed participants, of whom 5 (71%) were men, with HIV-1 with similar demographic and CD4 counts.",The paper describes an observational study and does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial.
37957382,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"In an observational study, we enrolled 8 ART-treated participants;  All participants were receiving at least two active antiretroviral drugs during the NSV episodes. The median duration of virologic suppression before the NSV and duration of NSV for all participants were 4 and 2 years, respectively. The median age of 60 years and median ART duration of 10 years. We assess ARV adherence by quantifying antiretroviral (and their anabolites) drug concentrations in plasma or through dried blood spot (DBS) testing. LV1 and LV2 had plasma dolutegravir and darunavir concentrations consistent with ongoing ART use (Supplementary Table 5).","The paper mentions that the participants were receiving at least two active antiretroviral drugs during the NSV episodes, and they were exclusively ART treated participants, which means sequences were from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37957382,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, IN","During the NSV episodes, the median viral load was 143 copies per ml^-1^ and the median CD4 count was 798 cells per μl^-1^ (Table 1 and Supplementary Table 1). Individual participant characteristics, ARV regimens and genotypic susceptibility scores (GSS)19 of plasma viruses sequenced during NSV are presented in Supplementary Table 1. We observed individuals experienced virologic suppression before NSV, which shows individuals received ARV drugs before sample sequencing. ExCTrACTed DNA was endpoint-diluted, and subjected to near full-length sequencing(nFL-seq), as previously described63. Briefly, after aligning to HXB2, we called our sequences as large deleterious deletions if they had a <8,000-bp amplicon size, out-of-frame indels, premature/lethal stop codons, internal inversions or packaging signal deletions (≥15 bp). To estimate the size of the reservoir, we analyzed near-full-length proviral sequences as described below. To define proviruses that could produce viral RNA—“producer proviruses”—and proviruses unable to do so—“nonproducer proviruses”—we performed long-range PCR to detect viral RNA contributing intact genomic sequences. Using PCR detection of intracellular HIV-1 DNA and cellular RNA205, individualproviruses infixed cells were identified through analyses of HIV-1 RNA structures and integration sites qPCR and GOVA. Plasma drug levels of NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs and INSTIs were tested during NSV episodes for adherence testing. Dolutegravir is an INSTI, which reflects integrase inhibitors; darunavir is a PI; tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) and emtricitabine (EMC) are NRTIs, whereas tenofovir aalafenamide (TAF) is an NRTI; rilpivirine (RPV) is an NNRTI","The paper mentions that the participants were receiving at least two active antiretroviral drugs during the NSV episodes, and the arvs were darunavir and dolutegravir by liquid chromatography with tandem mass spectrometry, which are Protease inhibitor and InStI respectively. TDF and FTC were quantified from DBs in blood spots, which are Tenofovir DF and Emtricitabine NRTIs, So the NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, Instis were received by individuals."
37957382,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Darunavir, Dolutegravir, Tenofovir DF, Emtricitabine, Rilpivirine, Tenofovir alafenamide","During the NSV episodes, the median viral load was 143 copies per ml^-1^ and the median CD4 count was 798 cells per μl^-1^ (Table 1 and Supplementary Table 1). Individual participant characteristics, ARV regimens and genotypic susceptibility scores (GSS)19 of plasma viruses sequenced during NSV are presented in Supplementary Table 1. We observed individuals experienced virologic suppression before NSV, which shows individuals received ARV drugs before sample sequencing. ExCTrACTed DNA was endpoint-diluted, and subjected to near full-length sequencing(nFL-seq), as previously described63. Briefly, after aligning to HXB2, we called our sequences as large deleterious deletions if they had a <8,000-bp amplicon size, out-of-frame indels, premature/lethal stop codons, internal inversions or packaging signal deletions (≥15 bp). To estimate the size of the reservoir, we analyzed near-full-length proviral sequences as described below. To define proviruses that could produce viral RNA—“producer proviruses”—and proviruses unable to do so—“nonproducer proviruses”—we performed long-range PCR to detect viral RNA contributing intact genomic sequences. Using PCR detection of intracellular HIV-1 DNA and cellular RNA205, individualproviruses infixed cells were identified through analyses of HIV-1 RNA structures and integration sites qPCR and GOVA. Plasma drug levels of NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs and INSTIs were tested during NSV episodes for adherence testing. Dolutegravir is an INSTI, which reflects integrase inhibitors; darunavir is a PI; tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) and emtricitabine (EMC) are NRTIs, whereas tenofovir aalafenamide (TAF) is an NRTI; rilpivirine (RPV) is an NNRTI","The paper mentions that the participants were receiving at least two active antiretroviral drugs during the NSV episodes, and the arvs were darunavir and dolutegravir by liquid chromatography with tandem mass spectrometry, which are Protease inhibitor and InStI respectively. TDF and FTC were quantified from DBs in blood spots, which are Tenofovir DF and Emtricitabine NRTIs, whereas tenofovir aalafenamide (TAF) is an NRTI; rilpivirine (RPV) is an NNRTI they're all mentioned in the figures."
