37973713,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We have conducted multi-center, prospective study, which aimed to establish HIV-1 subtype and assess the presence of primary drug resistance mutations to nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors and protease inhibitors in antiretroviral treatment naïve patients. The drug resistance genotyping of HIV-1 protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT) coding region was performed based on a Sanger sequencing method with use of commercially available ViroSeq HIV-1 Genotyping System (Abbott Molecular, Des Plains, IL, USA).","The paper mentions the collection of samples for genotyping and assessment of drug resistance mutations in HIV-1, indicating that HIV sequences from patient samples were reported."
37973713,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We have conducted multi-center, prospective study, which aimed to establish HIV-1 subtype and assess the presence of primary drug resistance mutations to nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors and protease inhibitors in antiretroviral treatment naïve patients.The clinical trial recruited 117 individuals during 2 years period (2020--2022).",The paper focuses on genotyping and drug resistance mutations but does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing.
37973713,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,The sequences were submitted to the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database (http://hivdb.stanford.edu) in order to comparison of potential differences in the interpretation of the significance of genetic variants detected.,"The paper mentions that the sequences were submitted to the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database, indicating that they were made publicly available."
37973713,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No GenBank accession numbers provided,The sequences were submitted to the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database (http://hivdb.stanford.edu) in order to comparison of potential differences in the interpretation of the significance of genetic variants detected.,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
37973713,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,117 individuals,"We have conducted multi-center, prospective study, which aimed to establish HIV-1 subtype and assess the presence of primary drug resistance mutations to nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors and protease inhibitors in antiretroviral treatment naïve patients. The clinical trial recruited 117 individuals during 2 years period (2020--2022).",The paper states that the clinical trial recruited 117 individuals during the study period.
37973713,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,"Poland, Ukraine, Belarus, Georgia, Kazakhstan","We have recruited 78 patients from two centers in Warsaw, 28 patients from Olsztyn, 6 from Lublin, 3 from Łódź and 2 patients from Łańcut center. Because of the lack of the results of genotyping in 34 individuals they were excluded from the analysis. In the study group there were 61 patients (52.14%) from eastern countries (34 men, 27 women) and 56 patients (47.86%) from Poland (52 men, 4 women). Among patients from eastern countries there were 58 patients (95.08%) from Ukraine (11 war refugees and 47 patients who arrived to Poland before the onset of war), 3 patients (4.92%) from: Belarus, Georgia and Kazakhstan.","The paper mentions the recruitment of patients from several countries: Poland, Ukraine, Belarus, Georgia, and Kazakhstan."
37973713,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2020 to 2022,"The patients were recruited during 2 years period (2020--2022). The patients were observed from the time of diagnosis to September 2022---the mean time of observation was 20.15 months (minimum 5, maximum 52 months).",The paper states that the patients were recruited during a 2-year period from 2020 to 2022.
37973713,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The drug resistance genotyping of HIV-1 protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT) coding region was performed based on a Sanger sequencing method with use of commercially available ViroSeq HIV-1 Genotyping System (Abbott Molecular, Des Plains, IL, USA). The sequences were submitted to the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database. For HIV-1 subtype and circular recombinant forms (CRFs) determination of partial pol submitted sequences REGA HIV-1 Subtyping Tool Version 3.0 was used.",The paper does not mention cloning of samples prior to sequencing. The drug resistance genotyping was performed based on a Sanger sequencing method with use of commercially available ViroSeq HIV-1 Genotyping System which is designed for HIV-1 RNA (the assay was designed for genotyping HIV-1 isolates from plasma samples.)
37973713,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Protease (PR) and Reverse Transcriptase (RT),"The drug resistance genotyping of HIV-1 protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT) coding region was performed based on a Sanger sequencing method with use of commercially available ViroSeq HIV-1 Genotyping System (Abbott Molecular, Des Plains, IL, USA).",The paper mentions that the drug resistance genotyping was performed on the coding region of HIV-1 protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT).
37973713,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"The drug resistance genotyping of HIV-1 protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT) coding region was performed based on a Sanger sequencing method with use of commercially available ViroSeq HIV-1 Genotyping System (Abbott Molecular, Des Plains, IL, USA).",The paper states that the drug resistance genotyping was performed based on a Sanger sequencing method using the ViroSeq HIV-1 Genotyping System.
37973713,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"The drug resistance genotyping of HIV-1 protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT) coding region was performed based on a Sanger sequencing method with use of commercially available ViroSeq HIV-1 Genotyping System (Abbott Molecular, Des Plains, IL, USA). We collected epidemiological data such as: age, gender, BMI, origin, migration data, sexual preferences and clinical data: HIV viral load, CD4+ cells count, presence of AIDS-defining diseases, implemented ART, serology of HBV and HCV co-infections.","The paper mentioned we collected epidemiological and clinical data such as: HIV viral load, CD4+ cells count etc. which is typically collected from plasma viral load test. The drug resistance genotyping was performed based on a Sanger sequencing method with the ViroSeq HIV-1 Genotyping System which is designed for genotyping HIV-1 isolates from plasma samples."
37973713,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"The prevalence of HIV-1 subtype A was statistically significantly more frequent in Ukrainian, and HIV-1 subtype B in Polish patients (p < 0.05). Drug resistance mutations were detected in 44% of all cases and the comparison of presence of mutations in the analyzed groups, as well as in the subgroups of subtype A and B HIV-1 has not revealed any significant differences (p > 0.05), nevertheless Polish patients had multidrug resistance mutations more frequent (p < 0.05). Among the analyzed groups of patients we assessed the applied antiretroviral therapy regimens.",The paper included only ART naïve patients. The patients had no history of ART. The applied antiretroviral therapy regimens were not  for in this study.
37973713,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"The clinical trial received a positive opinion from the Bioethics Committee at the Medical University of Warsaw (Number of consent: AKBE/206/2020) and was registered on clinicaltrials.gov (Number: NCT04636736). Inclusion criteria were recent diagnosis with HIV-1 infection (newly recognized patients), no history of ART, age 18 years or older.","The paper mentions that the clinical trial received a positive opinion from the Bioethics Committee and was registered on clinicaltrials.gov, indicating that the patients were part of a clinical trial."
37973713,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"The prevalence of HIV-1 subtype A was statistically significantly more frequent in Ukrainian, and HIV-1 subtype B in Polish patients (p < 0.05). Drug resistance mutations were detected in 44% of all cases and the comparison of presence of mutations in the analyzed groups, as well as in the subgroups of subtype A and B HIV-1 has not revealed any significant differences (p > 0.05), nevertheless Polish patients had multidrug resistance mutations more frequent (p < 0.05). Among the analyzed groups of patients we assessed the applied antiretroviral therapy regimens.",The paper included only ART naïve patients. The patients had no history of ART. The applied antiretroviral therapy regimens were not  for in this study.
37973713,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"The prevalence of HIV-1 subtype A was statistically significantly more frequent in Ukrainian, and HIV-1 subtype B in Polish patients (p < 0.05). Drug resistance mutations were detected in 44% of all cases and the comparison of presence of mutations in the analyzed groups, as well as in the subgroups of subtype A and B HIV-1 has not revealed any significant differences (p > 0.05), nevertheless Polish patients had multidrug resistance mutations more frequent (p < 0.05). Among the analyzed groups of patients we assessed the applied antiretroviral therapy regimens.",The paper included only ART naïve patients. The patients had no history of ART. The applied antiretroviral therapy regimens were not  for in this study.
37973713,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"The prevalence of HIV-1 subtype A was statistically significantly more frequent in Ukrainian, and HIV-1 subtype B in Polish patients (p < 0.05). Drug resistance mutations were detected in 44% of all cases and the comparison of presence of mutations in the analyzed groups, as well as in the subgroups of subtype A and B HIV-1 has not revealed any significant differences (p > 0.05), nevertheless Polish patients had multidrug resistance mutations more frequent (p < 0.05). Among the analyzed groups of patients we assessed the applied antiretroviral therapy regimens.",The paper included only ART naïve patients. The patients had no history of ART. The applied antiretroviral therapy regimens were not  for in this study.
37976080,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"PDR testing was conducted on 2568 participants in 2022. A total of 2869 HIV-positive individuals were initially recruited into the study. After removal of 16 HIV-positive individuals who were younger than 18 years and 9 individuals who had not started ART, the penultimate sample size was 2844. Viral RNA was extracted from 200 µL plasma samples using the QIAamp viral RNA mini kit (Qiagen, Hilton, Germany) according to the operational instructions. Extracted RNA was used for first-round polymerase chain reaction, and the products were used for second-round polymerase chain reaction amplification; both rounds were performed in 25 µL volume reactions to amplify the HIV pol gene region (HXB2: 2253-3553 nt). This covered the PR region (4-99 amino acids) and the partial RT region (1-251 amino acids). The amplified products were sequenced by Sanger sequencing.","The paper mentions that PDR testing was conducted on 2568 participants in 2022, and it describes the process of extracting RNA from plasma samples and sequencing the HIV pol gene region. This indicates that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples, specifically from plasma samples of HIV-positive individuals. Additionally, it specifies the regions sequenced, which are the PR and partial RT regions of the HIV genome, further confirming the sequencing of HIV from patient-derived samples. The study focuses on pretreatment HIV drug resistance among HIV-positive individuals, which involves sequencing the virus from their samples to analyze drug resistance mutations. These details collectively confirm that the paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples."
37976080,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The study aimed to characterize pretreatment HIV drug resistance (PDR) among HIV-positive individuals in China in 2022. PDR was analyzed using the Stanford HIV drug resistance database, and the Tamura-Nei 93 model in HIV-TRACE was used to calculate pairwise matches with a genetic distance of 0.01 substitutions per site. Logistic regression was used to identify and estimate factors associated with PDR. He western blot assays confirmed that all participants were treatment-naive at baseline, and mutations resistant to standard drugs had been accumulated. This study consistently showed high levels of viral suppression at week 48, and a great reduction of HIV DNA reservoir was achieved in virological responders, indicating that short-term intervention may be effective. The aimed to characterize pretreatment HIV drug resistance (PDR) among HIV-positive individuals and risk factors in China in 2022. Methods: This cross-sectional study was conducted using 2-stage systematic sampling according to the World Health Organization's surveillance guidelines in 8 provincial-level administrative divisions in 2022. Demongraphic information and plasma samples were obtained from study participants. PDR was analyzed using the Stanford HIV drug resistance database, and the Tamura-Nei 93 model in HIV-TRACE was used to calculate pairwise matches with a genetic distance of 0.01 substitutions per site. Logistic regression was used to idenitfy and estimate acute factors associated with PDR.","The paper focuses on pretreatment HIV drug resistance (PDR) analysis using sequences obtained from  PDR was analyzed using the Stanford HIV database, drug resistance database to detect PDR testing on 2568. It does not mention any in vitro or experimental drug susceptibility assays as part of the study. The focus of the research is on sequencing and analyzing drug resistance mutations in patient samples, not on testing the susceptibility of drugs in a laboratory setting. This indicates that the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data."
37976080,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"14 Clinical laboratory improvements amendments. All sequences were submitted to NCBI GenBank (accession. The AIDS Research and Human Retroviruses. 35:192-194. declares that I has No conflict of interest. Mutations were characterized as sensitive, conferred potential resistance, or conferred low, moderate, or high resistance, respectively. Nondrugrelated mutations with NO DR mutant genes level 0 were classified as negative DRM. Corresponding DR mutant genes with levels of 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 were classified as sensitive, potential resistance, low resistance, moderate resistance, and high resistance. All sequence quality controls were monitored by constructing neighbor joining phylogenetic trees and using the WHO HIVDR QC tool as previously reported. Multivariable associations with PDR in the RT/PR regions were detected using multivariate binary logistic regression model. Univariate analysis showed statistically significance P<0.05.”","The paper does not mention that the sequences obtained were made publicly available. Clinical laboratory improvements amendments. All sequences were submitted to NCBI GenBank, but there is no accession number. Therefore the seqeunces were not made publicly available. Also, it does not provide any information on where the sequences can be accessed, indicating that they were not submitted to a public database. This is further supported by the absence of a statement regarding the public availability of sequences in the methods or results sections. Therefore, the answer is No."
37976080,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,None,"14 Clinical laboratory improvements amendments. All sequences were submitted to NCBI GenBank (accession. The AIDS Research and Human Retroviruses. 35:192-194. declares that I has No conflict of interest. Mutations were characterized as sensitive, conferred potential resistance, or conferred low, moderate, or high resistance, respectively. Nondrugrelated mutations with NO DR mutant genes level 0 were classified as negative DRM. Corresponding DR mutant genes with levels of 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 were classified as sensitive, potential resistance, low resistance, moderate resistance, and high resistance. All sequence quality controls were monitored by constructing neighbor joining phylogenetic trees and using the WHO HIVDR QC tool as previously reported. Multivariable associations with PDR in the RT/PR regions were detected using multivariate binary logistic regression model. Univariate analysis showed statistically significance P<0.05.”","The paper mentions that all sequences were submitted to NCBI GenBank but does not provide the specific accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates in the body of the text, the only mention of submission is about clinical lab improvement amendments, which indicates that the paper doesn't report the accession numbers of the sequence in genbank. Therefore, the answer is None."
37976080,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,2568,"PDR testing was conducted on 2568 participants in 2022. Characteristics of the study participants. A total of 2869 HIV-positive individuals were initially recruited into the study. After removal of 16 HIV-positive individuals who were younger than 18 years and 9 individuals who had not started ART, the penultimate sample size was 2844. PDR test results were available for 90.3% (n=2568) of participants in RT/PR regions, thus yielding a final sample size of 2568. Of the 2568 study participants with valid PDR results, 34.8% (n=893) were aged 30-49 years, 81.4% (n=2091) were male, 86.8% (n=2229) were of Han ethnicity, 30.5% (n=783) had a junior high school education, 37% (n=950) were single, 36% (n=924) were farmers, and 57.6% (n=1480) and 37% (n=951) were infected through heterosexual and homosexual intercourse, respectively.","The paper states that PDR testing was conducted on 2568 participants in 2022, and this number is consistently mentioned in the results section when describing the characteristics of the study participants and the prevalence of pretreatment drug resistance (PDR). The study initially recruited 2869 HIV-positive individuals, but after applying the eligibility criteria and excluding some individuals, the final sample size for PDR testing was 2568. This is the number of individuals who had samples obtained for HIV sequencing, specifically for analyzing pretreatment HIV drug resistance. Therefore, the answer is 2568."
37976080,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,We aimed to characterize pretreatment HIV drug resistance (PDR) among HIV-positive individuals and risk factors in China in 2022. This cross-sectional study was conducted using 2-stage systematic sampling according to the World Health Organization's surveillance guidelines in 8 provincial-level administrative divisions in 2022.,"The study was conducted in 8 provincial-level administrative divisions in China. The paper clearly states that  the study aimed to characterize pretreatment HIV drug resistance (PDR) among HIV-positive individuals in China in 2022. Describe the regions in China where the study was conducted. The 8 provincial-level administrative divisions are: Sichuan Province, Chongqing Municipality, Yunnan Province, Jiangsu Province, Zhejiang Province, Jilin Province, Hebei Province, and Hubei Province. The samples were collected from these regions, and the data were analyzed to determine the prevalence of PDR and associated factors within China. Therefore, the answer is China."
37976080,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2022,"The study aimed to characterize pretreatment HIV drug resistance (PDR) among HIV-positive individuals and risk factors in China in 2022. Recruitment and specimen collection of HIV-positive individuals occurred in 2022 as individuals initiated ART. The study the prevalence of PDR in China in 2022 . PDR was detected in 7.4% of 2568 participants among 8 PLADs in China in 2022. The prevalence of PDR in Hebei (13/175; 7.4%), Jilin (19/206; 9.2%), Jiangsu (24/335; 7.2%), Zhejiang (34/385; 8.8%), Hubei (20/362; 5.5%), Chongqing (20/378; 5.3%), Sichuan (17/350; 4.9%) and Yunnan (43/377; 11.4%) in 2022.","The paper specifies that the study was conducted in 2022, and all the inclusive individuals were starting, or restarting ART between January and June 2022. All the sample collection was conducted during 2022. Additionally, it mentions that the recruitment and specimen collection of HIV-positive individuals occurred in 2022. Therefore, the answer is 2022."
37976080,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Extracted RNA was used for first-round polymerase chain reaction, and the products were used for second-round polymerase chain reaction amplification; both rounds were performed in 25 µL volume reactions to amplify the HIV pol gene region (HXB2: 2253-3553 nt). This covered the PR region (4-99 amino acids) and the partial RT region (1-251 amino acids) [24]. The amplified products were sequenced by Sanger sequencing.","The paper does not mention any process of cloning samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of polymerase chain reaction (PCR) and Sanger sequencing for obtaining sequences from the HIV pol gene region without any indication of cloning the samples. Therefore, the answer is No."
37976080,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,PR and partial RT regions,"The amplified products were sequenced by Sanger sequencing. Viral RNA was extracted from 200 µL plasma samples using the QIAamp viral RNA mini kit (Qiagen, Hilton, Germany) according to the operational instructions. Extracted RNA was used for first-round polymerase chain reaction, and the products were used for second-round polymerase chain reaction amplification; both rounds were performed in 25 µL volume reactions to amplify the HIV pol gene region (HXB2: 2253-3553 nt). This covered the PR region (4-99 amino acids) and the partial RT region (1-251 amino acids) [24].","The paper specifies that the study focused on sequencing the PR and partial RT regions of the HIV genome. It describes the amplification and sequencing of the HIV pol gene region, which includes the PR region and the partial RT region. These regions are mentioned in the context of analyzing pretreatment HIV drug resistance mutations. Therefore, the answer is PR and partial RT regions."
37976080,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"The amplified products were sequenced by Sanger sequencing. Viral RNA was extracted from 200 µL plasma samples using the QIAamp viral RNA mini kit (Qiagen, Hilton, Germany) according to the operational instructions. Extracted RNA was used for first-round polymerase chain reaction, and the products were used for second-round polymerase chain reaction amplification; both rounds were performed in 25 µL volume reactions to amplify the HIV pol gene region (HXB2: 2253-3553 nt). This covered the PR region (4-99 amino acids) and the partial RT region (1-251 amino acids) [24].","The paper mentions that the amplified products were sequenced by Sanger sequencing. This method is a specific type of DNA sequencing that involves using chain-terminating inhibitors to produce DNA fragments of varying lengths, which are then analyzed to determine the sequence of nucleotides. It is explicitly stated in the methods section of the paper, confirming that Sanger sequencing was the method used for sequencing the HIV samples in this study. Therefore, the answer is Sanger sequencing."
37976080,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Demographic information and plasma samples were obtained from study participants. Viral RNA was extracted from 200 µL plasma samples using the QIAamp viral RNA mini kit (Qiagen, Hilton, Germany) according to the operational instructions. Extracted RNA was used for first-round polymerase chain reaction, and the products were used for second-round polymerase chain reaction amplification; both rounds were performed in 25 µL volume reactions to amplify the HIV pol gene region (HXB2: 2253-3553 nt). This covered the PR region (4-99 amino acids) and the partial RT region (1-251 amino acids) [24].","The paper specifies that plasma samples were obtained from study participants and used for sequencing the HIV pol gene region. It describes the process of extracting viral RNA from plasma samples and sequencing the relevant regions, indicating that plasma was the clinical specimen type sequenced in the study. Therefore, the answer is Plasma."
37976080,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"We aimed to characterize pretreatment HIV drug resistance (PDR) among HIV-positive individuals and risk factors in China in 2022. Recruitment and specimen collection of HIV-positive individuals occurred in 2022 as individuals initiated ART. The study the prevalence of PDR in China in 2022. PDR was detected in 7.4% of 2568 participants among 8 PLADs in China in 2022. The prevalence of PDR in Hebei (13/175; 7.4%), Jilin (19/206; 9.2%), Jiangsu (24/335; 7.2%), Zhejiang (34/385; 8.8%), Hubei (20/362; 5.5%), Chongqing (20/378; 5.3%), Sichuan (17/350; 4.9%) and Yunnan (43/377; 11.4%) in 2022.","The paper focuses on pretreatment HIV drug resistance (PDR) among individuals who are starting or restarting first-line ART. All individuals in the study are treatment naïve at baseline, the study aims to surveillance the PDR mutations in the population initiating the treatment, all participants in our case started or restarte first-line ART in 2022. This indicates that the sequences were not obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen. Therefore, the answer is No."
37976080,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We selected 8 provincial-level administrative divisions (PLADs) in China as the study area to conduct a large multicenter observational cross-sectional study. This cross-sectional study was conducted using 2-stage systematic sampling according to the World Health Organization's surveillance guidelines in 8 provincial-level administrative divisions in 2022. Demographic information and plasma samples were obtained from study participants. PDR was analyzed using the Stanford HIV drug resistance database, and the Tamura-Nei 93 model in HIV-TRACE was used to calculate pairwise matches with a genetic distance of 0.01 substitutions per site. Logistic regression was used to identify and estimate factors associated with PDR.The study had a cross sectional design in eight plad of china and no mention of clinical trials.","The paper describes a multicenter cross-sectional study conducted according to the World Health Organization's surveillance guidelines, focusing on pretreatment HIV drug resistance (PDR) among HIV-positive individuals. It does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. The study focuses on observing and analyzing the prevalence of PDR and associated factors in the general population of HIV-positive individuals starting or restarting ART in 2022. This indicates that the patients were not in a clinical trial. Therefore, the answer is No."
37976080,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Of the participants, 3.2% (n=81) had prior ARV exposure. The study eligibility criteria for individuals included the following: 1 (1) tested HIV-positive, (2) ≥18 years old, (3) started or restarted first-line ART between January and June 2022 (includes women exposed to ARV drugs for preventing mother-to-child transmission of HIV, people who have received pre-exposure prophylaxis, and individuals reinitiating first-line ART).","The paper mentions that 3.2% of the participants had prior ARV exposure. This means that a portion of the individuals in the study had previously received antiretroviral drugs before the samples were collected and sequenced. Some individuals started or restarted first-line ART in 2022, and some women are exposed to ARV drugs for preventing mother-to-child transmission, and some had received pre-exposure prophylaxis. This indicates that the paper does report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs. Therefore, the answer is Yes."
37976080,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs","Of the participants, 3.2% (n=81) had prior ARV exposure. The study eligibility criteria for individuals included the following: 1 (1) tested HIV-positive, (2) ≥18 years old, (3) started or restarted first-line ART between January and June 2022 (includes women exposed to ARV drugs for preventing mother-to-child transmission of HIV, people who have received pre-exposure prophylaxis, and individuals reinitiating first-line ART).","The paper mentions that 3.2% of participants had prior ARV exposure, and the study includes individuals starting or restarting first-line ART between January and June 2022, and women exposed to ARV drugs for preventing mother-to-child transmission, and individuals who have received pre-exposure prophylaxis. These all indicate that the individuals have received ARV drugs. This means that they may have received drugs from different drug classes since the study includes women exposed to HIV drugs for prevention of mother to child transmssion, and individuals who have received pre-exposure prophylaxis. The paper does not specify the exact drug classes received by the individuals prior to the sample sequencing, but it's mention of inclusion of restarting first line ART and PEP individuals indicates that they may have received drugs from NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs. Therefore the answer is NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs."
37976080,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"Of the participants, 3.2% (n=81) had prior ARV exposure. The study eligibility criteria for individuals included the following: 1 (1) tested HIV-positive, (2) ≥18 years old, (3) started or restarted first-line ART between January and June 2022 (includes women exposed to ARV drugs for preventing mother-to-child transmission of HIV, people who have received pre-exposure prophylaxis, and individuals reinitiating first-line ART).","The paper only mentions that 3.2% of participants had prior ARV exposure and that the study includes individuals starting or restarting first-line ART between January and June 2022, and women exposed to ARV drugs for preventing mother-to-child transmission, and individuals who have received pre-exposure prophylaxis. It does not provide specific information about the exact drugs that were received by the individuals prior to the sample sequencing. Therefore, the answer is None."
37976185,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We included people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, assigned for partner services, for whom a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis was available in the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry (henceforth, Registry). Of 10 356 people with a new HIV diagnosis assigned for partner services in this time period, a total of 4246 (40%) had an available genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis and comprised our analytic population.","The paper mentions including people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, and having a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis, indicating that HIV sequences were reported from patient samples."
37976185,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods. We used New York City surveillance and partner services data to measure the effect of PrEP use (tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/tenofivir alafenamide fumarate with emtricitabine) history on the baseline prevalence of M184I/V mutations in people with HIV diagnosed in 2015--2022. PrEP use was categorized as ""recent"" (defined as PrEP stopped ≤90 days before diagnosis), ""past"" (PrEP stopped >90 days before diagnosis), or ""no known use."" Resistance-associated mutations were determined using the Stanford algorithm.",The study focuses on the prevalence of specific mutations associated with resistance but does not mention conducting in vitro drug susceptibility tests.
37976185,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"We used New York City surveillance and partner services data to measure the effect of PrEP use (tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/tenofivir alafenamide fumarate with emtricitabine) history on the baseline prevalence of M184I/V mutations in people with HIV diagnosed in 2015--2022. PrEP use was categorized as ""recent"" (defined as PrEP stopped ≤90 days before diagnosis), ""past"" (PrEP stopped >90 days before diagnosis), or ""no known use."" Resistance-associated mutations were determined using the Stanford algorithm. We used log binomial regression to generate the adjusted relative risk (aRR) of M184I/V by PrEP use history in people with or without acute HIV infection (AHI).","The paper did not mention that sequences were made publicly available, it focused on a retrospective analysis of existing surveillance and partner services data."
37976185,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,"We included people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, assigned for partner services, for whom a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis was available in the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry (henceforth, Registry). Of 10 356 people with a new HIV diagnosis assigned for partner services in this time period, a total of 4246 (40%) had an available genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis and comprised our analytic population.","The paper does not mention any specific GenBank accession numbers, as it focuses on the prevalence of mutations and the association with PrEP use."
