37993493,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,Patients with unsuppresed viral loads (local criteria viral load (VL) ≥ 1000 copies/mL) were tested for HIVDR mutations using the WHO adapted protocol for plasma samples. Genetic sequencing was successful in the protease and reverse transcriptase regions for 23 patients' samples and in the integrase region for 30 patients' samples.,"The paper clearly states that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples. It mentions the testing of patients with unsuppressed viral loads for HIVDR mutations using plasma samples, and also provides specific numbers of patients for whom sequencing was successful in various regions of the virus."
37993493,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"To curb HIV infection rate in Tanzania, antiretroviral therapy (ART) has been scaled up since 2006, and in 2019, the country shifted to regimen including dolutegravir as a default first line. We assessed the success of ART and the contribution of HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) to unsuppressed viral loads. Between February and May 2023 a cross-sectional survey with random sampling was conducted in the six clinics in an urban cohort in Dar es Salaam. Patients with unsuppresed viral loads (local criteria viral load (VL) ≥ 1000 copies/mL) were tested for HIVDR mutations using the WHO adapted protocol for plasma samples. Mutations were interpreted using the Stanford HIVDR database. In total 600 individuals participated in this survey, the majority were female (76.83%), mean age ( ± standard deviation) was 44.0 ( ± 11.6) years. The median duration on ART (interquartile range) was 6.5 (3.9--10.2) years. Approximately 99% were receiving tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir as a fixed dose combination. VL testing was successful in 99.67% (598/600) of survey patients and only 33 had VL ≥ 1000 copies/mL, resulting in a viral suppression level of 94.48% (565/598, 95% CI 92.34--96.17%). For 23 samples, protease and reverse transcriptase (RT) genotyping were successful, with 13 sequences containing RT inhibitor surveillance drug resistance mutations (SDRMs) (56.5%). No SDRM against protease inhibitors were detected. Thirty samples were successfully genotyped for integrase with 3 sequences (10.08%) containing integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) SDRMs. In samples successfully genotyped in the three genetic regions, 68.18% (16/22) had a genotypic susceptibility score (GSS) ≥ 2.5 for the concurrent regimen, implying factors beyond drug resistance caused the unsuppressed viral load. For five patients, GSS indicated that HIVDR may have caused the unsuppressed viral load. All three patients with INSTI resistance mutations were highly resistant to dolutegravir and accumulated nucleoside and non-nucleoside RT inhibitor HIVDR mutations. Although in this cohort the last 95 UNAIDS target was almost achieved, HIVDR mutations, including INSTIs resistance mutations were detected in HIV-positive individuals taking ART for at least one year. We recommend the design and implementation of high-impact interventions to prevent the increase of HIVDR, failure of dolutegravir and address the non-resistance factors in the study area.",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility assays or data. It focuses on genetic sequencing and the presence of drug resistance mutations rather than on the actual susceptibilities of the virus to drugs measured in vitro.
37993493,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The study was conducted in Dar es Salaam, Tanzania, and focused on the genetic sequencing and drug resistance testing of HIV samples from patients with unsuppressed viral loads. The paper does not mention any public repository where the sequences were deposited, nor does it provide any accession numbers for the sequences obtained. Furthermore, the mention of",The paper mentions that sequence data can be made publicly available but not made publicly available yet on data availability section. The absence of specific accession numbers and the emphasis on the local context of the study suggest that the sequences were not intended for public release at the time of publication.
37993493,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not available,Data used for analysis to produce this manuscript are available for secondary analysis from the corresponding author or the DUCS Platform database management upon request.,The paper mentions that sequence data can be made publicly available but not made publicly available yet on data availability section. It does not provide specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates in the publication itself. The sequences were either not deposited or not specified at the time of publishing.
37993493,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,600,"In total 600 individuals participated in this survey, the majority were female (76.83%), mean age ( ± standard deviation) was 44.0 ( ± 11.6) years. Viral loads were determined in 600 patients, the success rate was 598 samples (99.67%), with 5.52% (n = 33) having unsuppressed viral load.","The paper states that a total of 600 individuals participated in the survey and viral loads were determined in 600 patients, which means samples from 600 individuals were obtained for sequencing or testing."
37993493,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Tanzania,"The study was conducted in Dar es Salaam, Tanzania, and focused on the genetic sequencing and drug resistance testing of HIV samples from patients with unsuppressed viral loads living with HIV-1 in Dar es Salaam. The study was approved by Muhimbili University of Health and Allied Sciences (MUHAS)-Ethical Review Board (Ref No. MUHAS/REC/2020/243) and the National Committee on Medical Research Ethics (Ref No. NIMR/HQ/R.8c/Vol.1/1870).","The paper specifies that the study was conducted in Dar es Salaam, Tanzania. All of the populations were  living with HIV-1 in Dar es Salaam."
37993493,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2023,Between February and May 2023 a cross-sectional survey with random sampling was conducted in the six clinics in an urban cohort in Dar es Salaam. The study was conducted between February and May 2023.,"The paper mentions that the study was conducted between February and May 2023, indicating that the samples were obtained during this period."
37993493,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"We used Management and Development for Health (MDH) WHO adapted protocol (Unpublished) to perform HIVDR testing using the 3500xL genetic analyser (Applied Biosystems), a capillary sequencer using Sanger-style sequencing. We used Stanford HIVDR33 and COMET35 to assign the HIV sub-types, and Rega HIV sub-typing tool 3.46 database36 was consulted to resolve any disagreements between Stanford and COMET.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of Sanger-style sequencing with a capillary sequencer, which typically does not involve cloning."
37993493,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Protease, reverse transcriptase, integrase","Genetic sequencing was successful in the protease and reverse transcriptase regions for 23 patients' samples and in the integrase region for 30 patients' samples. For 23 samples, protease and reverse transcriptase (RT) genotyping were successful, with 13 sequences containing RT inhibitor surveillance drug resistance mutations (SDRMs) (56.5%). No SDRM against protease inhibitors were detected. Thirty samples were successfully genotyped for integrase with 3 sequences (10.08%) containing integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) SDRMs.","The paper explicitly lists the HIV genes that were sequenced, including protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase. It provides details on the successful genotyping in these regions."
37993493,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"Management and Development for Health (MDH) WHO adapted protocol (Unpublished) to perform HIVDR testing using the 3500xL genetic analyser (Applied Biosystems), a capillary sequencer using Sanger-style sequencing. Analysis of mutations were performed using Stanford University HIVDR database version 9.4.","The paper specifies that Sanger-style sequencing was used with a capillary sequencer (3500xL genetic analyser), which is characteristic of Sanger sequencing."
37993493,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"Patients with unsuppresed viral loads (local criteria viral load (VL) ≥ 1000 copies/mL) were tested for HIVDR mutations using the WHO adapted protocol for plasma samples. Blood sample collection, transportation and storage were treated according to the Temeke Regional Referral Hospital- Specialized Laboratory protocol. Eight ml of venous blood was drawn using two EDTA tubes each with 4 mL. Whole blood was separated into plasma within 6 h of collection, before separation the whole blood was kept at 2–8 °C. Centrifugation was performed for 10 min at 1000–2000×g, then supernatant (plasma) was kept at − 20 °C with a maximum of 3 times of freeze–thaw cycles.","The paper mentions that plasma samples were used for HIVDR testing. Additionally, it describes the handling and processing of plasma samples for sequencing."
37993493,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,We assessed the success of ART and the contribution of HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) to unsuppressed viral loads. Between February and May 2023 a cross-sectional survey with random sampling was conducted in the six clinics in an urban cohort in Dar es Salaam. Patients with unsuppresed viral loads (local criteria viral load (VL) ≥ 1000 copies/mL) were tested for HIVDR mutations using the WHO adapted protocol for plasma samples.,"The paper discusses the assessment of treatment success and the contribution of HIV drug resistance to unsuppressed viral loads. It specifically mentions testing patients with unsuppressed viral loads for HIVDR mutations, indicating virological failure."
37993493,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,A cross-sectional survey was conducted between February and May 2023 in the Dar es Salaam Urban Cohort Study (DUCS) platform of the Ilala district in Dar es Salaam.  We assessed the success of ART and the contribution of HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) to unsuppressed viral loads. Patients with unsuppresed viral loads (local criteria viral load (VL) ≥ 1000 copies/mL) were tested for HIVDR mutations using the WHO adapted protocol for plasma samples. Genetic sequencing was successful in the protease and reverse transcriptase regions for 23 patients' samples and in the integrase region for 30 patients' samples. The high virological suppression rate in the studied area is probably linked to the policy change of replacing non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase (NNRTI)-based regimen by DTG-based regimen (tenofovir+lamivudine+DTG or TLD) in first line. This policy change resulted in 99% of the surveyed patients taking the TLD regimen.,"the paper does not indicate that the patients were part of a clinical trial. The study was a cross-sectional survey assessing drug resistance and treatment success, not a clinical trial."
37993493,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"To curb HIV infection rate in Tanzania, antiretroviral therapy (ART) has been scaled up since 2006, and in 2019, the country shifted to regimen including dolutegravir as a default first line. We assessed the success of ART and the contribution of HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) to unsuppressed viral loads. Between February and May 2023 a cross-sectional survey with random sampling was conducted in the six clinics in an urban cohort in Dar es Salaam. Patients with unsuppresed viral loads (local criteria viral load (VL) ≥ 1000 copies/mL) were tested for HIVDR mutations using the WHO adapted protocol for plasma samples. In total 600 individuals participated in this survey, the majority were female (76.83%), mean age ( ± standard deviation) was 44.0 ( ± 11.6) years. The median duration on ART (interquartile range) was 6.5 (3.9--10.2) years. Approximately 99% were receiving tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir as a fixed dose combination. VL testing was successful in 99.67% (598/600) of survey patients and only 33 had VL ≥ 1000 copies/mL, resulting in a viral suppression level of 94.48% (565/598, 95% CI 92.34--96.17%). For 23 samples, protease and reverse transcriptase (RT) genotyping were successful, with 13 sequences containing RT inhibitor surveillance drug resistance mutations (SDRMs) (56.5%). No SDRM against protease inhibitors were detected. Thirty samples were successfully genotyped for integrase with 3 sequences (10.08%) containing integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) SDRMs. In samples successfully genotyped in the three genetic regions, 68.18% (16/22) had a genotypic susceptibility score (GSS) ≥ 2.5 for the concurrent regimen, implying factors beyond drug resistance caused the unsuppressed viral load. For five patients, GSS indicated that HIVDR may have caused the unsuppressed viral load. All three patients with INSTI resistance mutations were highly resistant to dolutegravir and accumulated nucleoside and non-nucleoside RT inhibitor HIVDR mutations","The paper mentions that the study included patients living with HIV and receiving antiretroviral therapy. It also provides details on the treatment regimens and the duration on ART, indicating that the individuals had previously received ARV drugs."
37993493,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI","Approximately 99% of survey patients were taking TLD regimen. Of 33 patients with high viral load (≥ 1000 copies/mL) despite taking ART, 30 (90.91%) were using a DTG-based regimen, mostly TDF + 3TC + DTG, two patients were using ABC + 3TC + LPV/r, and one was taking ABC + 3TC + ATV/r","The paper specifies the treatment regimens that the patients were on, including drugs from the NRTI, NNRTI, PI, and INSTI classes. Although NRTI+NNRTI+PI was not received by patients in the study, the drug classes were received before sample sequencing."
37993493,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Dolutegravir, Abacavir, Lopinavir, Atazanavir, Efavirenz","About 99% survey patients were taking TLD regimen, tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) + lamivudine (3TC) + dolutegravir (DTG). Two patients were using abacavir (ABC) + 3TC + lopinavir/ritonavir (LPV/r), and one was taking ABC + 3TC + atazanavir (ATV/r). Patient CR-119 had failed their DTG-containing regimen and had already been switched to the protease-inhibitor-containing therapy at the time of sampling, which was darunavir-ritonavir (DRV/r) in combination with tenofovir disoproxil (TDF) and 3TC. MC-243 was already on a second-line PI-containing dual regimen, LPV/r and TDF, when switched to DTG.","The paper mentions the specific drugs that were part of the regimens the patients were on, including their switch history."
38005921,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The use of computational approaches allows researchers to increase therapy effectiveness and reduce the time and expenses involved when the prescribed antiretroviral therapy is ineffective in the treatment of infection caused by the human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1). We used 'genotype--phenotype' datasets, which describe a relationship between a viral genotype and the phenotypic manifestation of resistance to protease, and reverse transcriptase and integrase inhibitors from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database [27]. To build models for the web service, we used high quality filtered datasets available for downloading from StHIVdb.","The paper does not mention any original HIV sequences obtained from patient samples. Instead, it discusses the use of existing datasets from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database and StHIVdb to build predictive models for HIV drug resistance. The focus is on computational modeling rather than experimental data collection from patients. The use of genotype-phenotype datasets indicates that the study relies on pre-existing data rather than reporting new sequences from patient samples."
38005921,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We propose two machine learning methods and the appropriate models for predicting HIV drug resistance related to amino acid substitutions in HIV targets: (i) k-mers utilizing the random forest and the support vector machine algorithms of the scikit-learn library, and (ii) multi-n-grams using the Bayesian approach implemented in MultiPASSR software. Both multi-n-grams and k-mers were computed based on the amino acid sequences of HIV enzymes: reverse transcriptase and protease. The performance of the models was estimated by five-fold cross-validation.","The paper does not present any original in vitro drug susceptibility data. Instead, it focuses on the development of computational models that predict drug resistance based on existing datasets and amino acid sequences of HIV enzymes. The mention of phenotypic resistance levels in the datasets does not imply original in vitro experiments conducted as part of this study."
38005921,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The use of computational approaches allows researchers to increase therapy effectiveness and reduce the time and expenses involved when the prescribed antiretroviral therapy is ineffective in the treatment of infection caused by the human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1). We used 'genotype--phenotype' datasets, which describe a relationship between a viral genotype and the phenotypic manifestation of resistance to protease, and reverse transcriptase and integrase inhibitors from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database [27]. To build models for the web service, we used high quality filtered datasets available for downloading from StHIVdb.",The paper does not mention any new sequences generated during the study. It relies on existing publicly available datasets from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database and StHIVdb. The sequences used for building the models were not generated by the authors but were derived from these databases.
38005921,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No,"We used 'genotype--phenotype' datasets, which describe a relationship between a viral genotype and the phenotypic manifestation of resistance to protease, and reverse transcriptase and integrase inhibitors from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database 27. To build models for the web service, we used high quality filtered datasets available for downloading from StHIVdb.",The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates. It mentions the use of datasets from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database and StHIVdb but does not specifically list the accession numbers for the sequences used in the study.
38005921,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"The use of computational approaches allows researchers to increase therapy effectiveness and reduce the time and expenses involved when the prescribed antiretroviral therapy is ineffective in the treatment of infection caused by the human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1). We used 'genotype--phenotype' datasets, which describe a relationship between a viral genotype and the phenotypic manifestation of resistance to protease, and reverse transcriptase and integrase inhibitors from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database [27]. To build models for the web service, we used high quality filtered datasets available for downloading from StHIVdb.",The paper does not mention the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing. It focuses on the use of existing datasets from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database and StHIVdb for building predictive models.
38005921,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not mentioned,"We used 'genotype--phenotype' datasets, which describe a relationship between a viral genotype and the phenotypic manifestation of resistance to protease, and reverse transcriptase and integrase inhibitors from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database [27]. To build models for the web service, we used high quality filtered datasets available for downloading from StHIVdb.",The paper does not specify the countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained. It mentions the use of datasets from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database and StHIVdb but does not provide information on the geographic origin of the samples.
38005921,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not mentioned,"We used 'genotype--phenotype' datasets, which describe a relationship between a viral genotype and the phenotypic manifestation of resistance to protease, and reverse transcriptase and integrase inhibitors from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database [27]. To build models for the web service, we used high quality filtered datasets available for downloading from StHIVdb.",The paper does not mention the specific years from which the sequenced samples were obtained. It focuses on the use of existing datasets without providing the time frame of sample collection.
38005921,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The use of computational approaches allows researchers to increase therapy effectiveness and reduce the time and expenses involved when the prescribed antiretroviral therapy is ineffective in the treatment of infection caused by the human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1). We used 'genotype--phenotype' datasets, which describe a relationship between a viral genotype and the phenotypic manifestation of resistance to protease, and reverse transcriptase and integrase inhibitors from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database [27]. To build models for the web service, we used high quality filtered datasets available for downloading from StHIVdb. To develop machine learning models, the samples were divided into two classes, 'sensitive' and 'resistant', using the FR thresholds from StHIVdb [17] provided in Table 1.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It focuses on the use of existing datasets and computational modeling rather than on experimental methods involving cloning.
38005921,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Not mentioned,"We used 'genotype--phenotype' datasets, which describe a relationship between a viral genotype and the phenotypic manifestation of resistance to protease, and reverse transcriptase and integrase inhibitors from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database [27]. To build models for the web service, we used high quality filtered datasets available for downloading from StHIVdb.","The paper does not specify the individual HIV genes that were sequenced. It mentions the use of datasets related to protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase inhibitors, but does not detail the sequencing of the specific genes."
38005921,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not mentioned,"We used 'genotype--phenotype' datasets, which describe a relationship between a viral genotype and the phenotypic manifestation of resistance to protease, and reverse transcriptase and integrase inhibitors from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database [27]. To build models for the web service, we used high quality filtered datasets available for downloading from StHIVdb.",The paper does not mention the specific method used for sequencing the samples. It focuses on computational modeling and the use of existing datasets without detailing the sequencing methodology.
38005921,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Not mentioned,"We used 'genotype--phenotype' datasets, which describe a relationship between a viral genotype and the phenotypic manifestation of resistance to protease, and reverse transcriptase and integrase inhibitors from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database [27]. To build models for the web service, we used high quality filtered datasets available for downloading from StHIVdb.",The paper does not specify the type of clinical samples that were sequenced. It mentions the use of existing datasets but does not provide information on the sample types.
38005921,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"The use of computational approaches allows researchers to increase therapy effectiveness and reduce the time and expenses involved when the prescribed antiretroviral therapy is ineffective in the treatment of infection caused by the human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1). We used 'genotype--phenotype' datasets, which describe a relationship between a viral genotype and the phenotypic manifestation of resistance to protease, and reverse transcriptase and integrase inhibitors from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database [27]. To build models for the web service, we used high quality filtered datasets available for downloading from StHIVdb.","The paper mentioned the use of datasets describing a relationship between viral genotype and the phenotypic manifestation of resistance, which indicates that they are not focusing on individuals with virological failure."
38005921,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,The web application takes the amino acid sequences of HIV-1 reverse transcriptase or protease as an input (Figure 3). The models with the highest prediction performance for each drug were used for prediction.,The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It focuses on the development and validation of computational models using existing datasets rather than on clinical trials involving patients.
38005921,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"We used 'genotype--phenotype' datasets, which describe a relationship between a viral genotype and the phenotypic manifestation of resistance to protease, and reverse transcriptase and integrase inhibitors from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database [27]. To build models for the web service, we used high quality filtered datasets available for downloading from StHIVdb. To develop machine learning models, the samples were divided into two classes, 'sensitive' and 'resistant', using the FR thresholds from StHIVdb [17] provided in Table 1.","The paper mentioned the use of datasets describing a relationship between viral genotype and the phenotypic manifestation of resistance to protease, and reverse transcriptase and integrase inhibitors which is for sensitive and resistant samples, which indicates that some samples were from patients who received ARV drugs, and some were not, but the paper didn't report that."
38005921,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not mentioned,"We used 'genotype--phenotype' datasets, which describe a relationship between a viral genotype and the phenotypic manifestation of resistance to protease, and reverse transcriptase and integrase inhibitors from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database [27]. To build models for the web service, we used high quality filtered datasets available for downloading from StHIVdb.",The paper does not mention the specific drug classes that were received by individuals prior to sample sequencing. It focuses on the use of existing datasets and the development of predictive models without detailing the treatment histories of the individuals.
38005921,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not mentioned,"We used 'genotype--phenotype' datasets, which describe a relationship between a viral genotype and the phenotypic manifestation of resistance to protease, and reverse transcriptase and integrase inhibitors from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database [27]. To build models for the web service, we used high quality filtered datasets available for downloading from StHIVdb.",The paper does not mention the specific drugs that were received by individuals prior to sample sequencing. It focuses on the use of existing datasets and the development of predictive models without detailing the treatment histories of the individuals.
38020274,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Genotypic resistance testing (GRT) was carried out at each time point among participants with plasma viral load (PVL) ≥1,000 RNA copies/ml using an in-house protocol as previously described by our working group (10) using blood samples stored at -80˚C. The sequences obtained were assembled and edited using Recall CDC Atlanta GA USA software and drug resistance mutations (DRMs) interpreted using Stanford HIVdb.v8.8; Subtyping was done using MEGA v10 for molecular phylogeny.","The paper mentions that genotypic resistance testing (GRT) was carried out using blood samples stored at -80˚C, and the sequences obtained were assembled and edited using Recall CDC Atlanta GA USA software. This indicates that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
38020274,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We sought to evaluate the effect of HIV-1 mutational profiling on immuno-virological response and ADR among APHI. A cohort-study was conducted from 2018-2020 among 311 APHI receiving ART in Cameroon. Clinical, immunological and virological responses were measured at enrolment (T1), 6-months (T2) and 12-months (T3). Immunological failure (IF: CD4 #x003C;250 cells/mm ^3^ ), VF (viremia ≥1,000 copies/ml), and ADR were analyzed, with P#x003C;0.05 considered significant. Mean age was 15(±3) years; male-female ratio was 1:1; median [IQR] ART-duration was 36[21-81] months. At T1, T2, and T3 respectively, adherence-level was 66.4, 58.3 and 66.5%; 14 viral clades were found, driven by CRF02_AG (58.6%); ADR-mutations favored increased switch to second-line ART (16.1, 31.2, and 41.9%, P#x003C;0.0001). From T1-T3 respectively, there were declining rates of IF (25.5, 18.9, and 9.83%, P#x003C;0.0001), VF (39.7, 39.9, and 28.2%, P=0.007), and HIVDR (96.4, 91.7, and 85.0%, P=0.099). Predictors of ADR were being on first-line ART (P=0.045), high viremia at enrolment (AOR=12.56, P=0.059), and IF (AOR=5.86, P=0.010). Of note, optimized ART guided by mutational profile (AOR=0.05, P=0.002) was protective. Moreover, full Tenofovir+Lamivudine+Dolutegravir efficacy was predicted in 77 and 62% of APHI respectively after first- and second-line failure. Among APHI in this SSA setting, viral mutational profiling prompts the use of optimized Dolutegravir-based ART regimens, leading to improved immuno-virological response and declining ADR burdens. Thus, implementing personalized HIV medicine in this vulnerable population would substantially improve ART response and the achievement of the 95-95-95 goals in these underserved populations","The paper focuses on genotypic resistance testing and the effects of HIV-1 mutational profiling on treatment outcomes, but it does not mention in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data."
38020274,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"Genotypic resistance testing (GRT) was carried out at each time point among participants with plasma viral load (PVL) ≥1,000 RNA copies/ml using an in-house protocol as previously described by our working group (10) using blood samples stored at -80˚C. The sequences obtained were assembled and edited using Recall CDC Atlanta GA USA software and drug resistance mutations (DRMs) interpreted using Stanford HIVdb.v8.8; Subtyping was done using MEGA v10 for molecular phylogeny.",The paper does not mention that the sequences obtained were made publicly available.
38020274,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper did not provide GenBank accession numbers.,No evidence,The paper does not mention the GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
38020274,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,311,"Overall, 311 APHI were included at the enrolment phase (T1) with 272 followed-up at 6-months (T2), and 243 at 12-months (T3).",The paper states that 311 adolescents living with perinatal HIV (APHI) were included at the enrollment phase (T1) of the study.
38020274,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Cameroon,A prospective cohort-study was conducted from 2018-2020 among 311 APHI receiving ART in one of the selected health facilities within the 'Resistance Evolution among Adolescents in Yaoundé and its surroundings' (READY-study) in the Centre region of Cameroon.,The study was conducted among APHI receiving ART in selected health facilities within the READY-study in the Centre region of Cameroon.
38020274,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2018-2020,A prospective cohort-study was conducted from 2018-2020 among 311 APHI receiving ART in one of the selected health facilities within the 'Resistance Evolution among Adolescents in Yaoundé and its surroundings' (READY-study) in the Centre region of Cameroon.,The study was conducted from 2018-2020 among 311 adolescents living with perinatal HIV (APHI) receiving antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Cameroon.
38020274,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Genotypic resistance testing (GRT) was carried out at each time point among participants with plasma viral load (PVL) ≥1,000 RNA copies/ml using an in-house protocol as previously described by our working group (10) using blood samples stored at -80˚C. The sequences obtained were assembled and edited using Recall CDC Atlanta GA USA software and drug resistance mutations (DRMs) interpreted using Stanford HIVdb.v8.8; Subtyping was done using MEGA v10 for molecular phylogeny.",The paper does not mention that samples were cloned prior to sequencing.
38020274,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,pol and env genes,"In terms of HIV-1 genetic variability, a variety of pure subtypes and genetic variants or recombinants were observed in this study upon sequencing of the HIV-1 polymerase and reverse transcriptase gene regions.",The paper mentions the sequencing of the HIV-1 polymerase and reverse transcriptase gene regions.
38020274,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not mentioned,"Genotypic resistance testing (GRT) was carried out at each time point among participants with plasma viral load (PVL) ≥1,000 RNA copies/ml using an in-house protocol as previously described by our working group (10) using blood samples stored at -80˚C. The sequences obtained were assembled and edited using Recall CDC Atlanta GA USA software and drug resistance mutations (DRMs) interpreted using Stanford HIVdb.v8.8; Subtyping was done using MEGA v10 for molecular phylogeny.",The paper do not mention the specific sequencing method used for the samples.
38020274,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"Genotypic resistance testing (GRT) was carried out at each time point among participants with plasma viral load (PVL) ≥1,000 RNA copies/ml using an in-house protocol as previously described by our working group (10) using blood samples stored at -80˚C. The sequences obtained were assembled and edited using Recall CDC Atlanta GA USA software and drug resistance mutations (DRMs) interpreted using Stanford HIVdb.v8.8; Subtyping was done using MEGA v10 for molecular phylogeny.","The paper mentions that genotypic resistance testing was carried out among participants with plasma viral load ≥1,000 RNA copies/ml, indicating that plasma samples were sequenced."
38020274,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"At T1, T2, and T3 respectively, adherence-level was 66.4, 58.3 and 66.5%; 14 viral clades were found, driven by CRF02_AG (58.6%); ADR-mutations favored increased switch to second-line ART (16.1, 31.2, and 41.9%, P#x003C;0.0001). From T1-T3 respectively, there were declining rates of IF (25.5, 18.9, and 9.83%, P#x003C;0.0001), VF (39.7, 39.9, and 28.2%, P=0.007), and HIVDR (96.4, 91.7, and 85.0%, P=0.099).","The paper mentions that genotypic resistance testing was carried out among participants with plasma viral load ≥1,000 RNA copies/ml (indicating virological failure). The study observed virological failure (VF) and ADR mutations favoring switch to second-line ART, confirming that sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure."
38020274,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,A prospective cohort-study was conducted from 2018-2020 among 311 APHI receiving ART in one of the selected health facilities within the 'Resistance Evolution among Adolescents in Yaoundé and its surroundings' (READY-study) in the Centre region of Cameroon.,"The paper describes a cohort study conducted among patients receiving ART in selected health facilities, not a clinical trial."
38020274,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"A prospective cohort-study was conducted from 2018-2020 among 311 APHI receiving ART in one of the selected health facilities within the 'Resistance Evolution among Adolescents in Yaoundé and its surroundings' (READY-study) in the Centre region of Cameroon. Participants were recruited following exhaustive sampling, and follow-up was performed at enrollment (T1), 6 months (T2), and 12 months (T3). Eligibility criteria Inclusion criteria. APHI with documented infection route; aged 10-19 years; receiving a standard reverse transcriptase inhibitor-based (RTI-based) first- or Ritonavir-boosted protease inhibitor-based (PI/r-based) second-line ART regimen for at least 6-months; having provided written assent, and informed consent from their legal guardian(s).","The paper mentioned that participants had received ARV drugs, and were receiving ART during the study."
38020274,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI-based, PI-based","A prospective cohort-study was conducted from 2018-2020 among 311 APHI receiving ART in one of the selected health facilities within the 'Resistance Evolution among Adolescents in Yaoundé and its surroundings' (READY-study) in the Centre region of Cameroon. Participants were recruited following exhaustive sampling, and follow-up was performed at enrollment (T1), 6 months (T2), and 12 months (T3). Eligibility criteria Inclusion criteria. APHI with documented infection route; aged 10-19 years; receiving a standard reverse transcriptase inhibitor-based (RTI-based) first- or Ritonavir-boosted protease inhibitor-based (PI/r-based) second-line ART regimen for at least 6-months; having provided written assent, and informed consent from their legal guardian(s).",The paper mentions that participants were receiving a standard reverse transcriptase inhibitor-based (RTI-based) first- or Ritonavir-boosted protease inhibitor-based (PI/r-based) second-line ART regimen.
38020274,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Dolutegravir","Genotypic resistance testing improves antiretroviral treatment outcomes in a cohort of adolescents in Cameroon: Implications in the dolutegravir-era Abstract Acquired drug resistance (ADR) is common among adolescents living with perinatal HIV (APHI) in sub-Saharan Africa (SSA). Personalized management has the potential to improve pediatric antiretroviral therapy (ART), even in the presence of long-term treatment and HIV-1 subtype diversity. We sought to evaluate the effect of HIV-1 mutational profiling on immuno-virological response and ADR among APHI. A cohort-study was conducted from 2018-2020 among 311 APHI receiving ART in Cameroon. Clinical, immunological and virological responses were measured at enrolment (T1), 6-months (T2) and 12-months (T3). Immunological failure (IF: CD4 #x003C;250 cells/mm ^3^ ), VF (viremia ≥1,000 copies/ml), and ADR were analyzed, with P#x003C;0.05 considered significant. Mean age was 15(±3) years; male-female ratio was 1:1; median [IQR] ART-duration was 36[21-81] months. At T1, T2, and T3 respectively, adherence-level was 66.4, 58.3 and 66.5%; 14 viral clades were found, driven by CRF02_AG (58.6%); ADR-mutations favored increased switch to second-line ART (16.1, 31.2, and 41.9%, P#x003C;0.0001). From T1-T3 respectively, there were declining rates of IF (25.5, 18.9, and 9.83%, P#x003C;0.0001), VF (39.7, 39.9, and 28.2%, P=0.007), and HIVDR (96.4, 91.7, and 85.0%, P=0.099). Predictors of ADR were being on first-line ART (P=0.045), high viremia at enrolment (AOR=12.56, P=0.059), and IF (AOR=5.86, P=0.010). Of note, optimized ART guided by mutational profile (AOR=0.05, P=0.002) was protective. Moreover, full Tenofovir+Lamivudine+Dolutegravir efficacy was predicted in 77 and 62% of APHI respectively after first- and second-line failure.","The paper mentions the use of Tenofovir, Lamivudine, and Dolutegravir in the treatment of the participants."
38022124,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"This retrospective analysis was performed in HIV-1-positive patients who were initiated BIC/FTC/TAF in the HIV clinic between June 2020 and June 2022. Patients older than 18 years of age, diagnosed with HIV, and receiving BIC/FTC/TAF for at least 12 months were included in this study. Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) patients",The paper discusses the treatment and outcomes of HIV-1-positive patients on a specific regimen (BIC/FTC/TAF) but does not mention any sequencing of HIV from patient samples. The focus is on clinical parameters and treatment efficacy rather than genetic analysis of the virus.
