38072961,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,One,"A 27-year-old man with HIV infection complained of recurrent headaches during the last year. Persistent clinical recovery of symptoms, neuroimaging, and laboratory abnormalities were observed in the follow-up visits.","The case report focuses on a single individual, a 27-year-old man with HIV infection, from whom samples were obtained for sequencing."
38072961,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"A 27-year-old HIV-positive Chinese Manchu male complained of intermittent headache, nausea, and vomiting (non-projectile vomiting three times in total) for two weeks in January 2022.","The paper mentions that the patient is a 27-year-old HIV-positive Chinese Manchu male, indicating that the sequenced samples were obtained from China."
38072961,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2022,"One month later, the patient presented headache relief, and obvious improvement in MRI performance and laboratory tests in the CSF. Then, prednisone was tapered and discontinued in May 2022.","The paper provides a timeline of the patient's symptoms and treatment, indicating that the samples were obtained in 2022."
38072961,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,Somatic gene recombination through mitosis is involved in generating peripheral viral reservoirs. Our previous data from a case of HIV-1 infection with PML also demonstrated that viral sequence homogeneity of both CSF and plasma tubercle bacillus had reduced sensitivity of genotypic resistance tests.,hr paper does not mention that the samples were cloned prior to sequencing.
38072961,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Protease, Reverse Transcriptase, NRTI, NNRTI, PI",Molecular characteristics and infectious profiles demonstrated our one-stop-targeted next-generation sequencing assay had higher genotype coverage and detection ability than the virus isolation in plasma and drug resistance tests. In vitro had supra-additivity when combined with prdione with the D226N mutant.,"The paper mentions the detection PI<, D226N, K103N, T215A, L76V, K451R, M184I, K65R and T20 in different NGS, and clinical isolates indicatin the sequencing of these genes."
38072961,10,What method was used for sequencing?,"Sanger sequencing, next-generation sequencing","No other pathogens were confirmed by the next-generation sequencing in the CSF (using the MGISEQ-2000 platform in BGI PathoGenesis Pharmaceutical Technology, BGI-Shenzhen). Molecular characteristics and infectious profiles demonstrated our one-stop-targeted next-generation sequencing assay had higher genotype coverage and detection ability than the virus isolation in plasma and drug resistance tests. In vitro had supra-additivity when combined with prdione with the D226N mutant.","The paper mentions the use of next-generation sequencing for detecting pathogens in the CSF, indicating that this method was used for sequencing the samples."
38072961,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,"Cerebrospinal fluid, plasma","One characteristic of this case was the occurrence of subacute encephalitis caused by CSF drug-resistant HIV escape, which highlights the importance of CSF HIV RNA monitoring and the necessity of achieving sufficient CSF viral suppression under ART. The origin of CSF escape viral populations is either persistently replicating CNS reservoirs (likely in macrophages/microglia) or transient clonally expanded trafficking of infected T cells in the CNS. Patients with CSF HIV escape may be symptomatic or asymptomatic. Active management was recommended for cases with symptomatic CSF HIV escape.","The paper discusses the monitoring of HIV RNA and drug resistance tests in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) and plasma, indicating that these types of samples were sequenced."
38072961,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Initiated ART with tenofovir, lamivudine, and efavirenz, but treatment failure occurred one year later with plasma HIV RNA 2.19 × 10^5 copies/mL and CD4+ T-cell count 14 cells/μL. The local doctor shifted the ART regimen to tenofovir, lamivudine, lopinavir/ritonavir without a genotypic drug resistance test, and his plasma HIV viral load was well controlled thereafter.","The paper mentions that the patient experienced treatment failure and had detectable plasma HIV RNA, indicating that sequences were obtained from an individual with virological failure."
38072961,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,This study is a case report of a 27-year-old HIV-positive Chinese Manchu male.,"The paper presents a case report of a single patient, not a clinical trial."
38072961,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Initiated ART with tenofovir, lamivudine, and efavirenz, but treatment failure occurred one year later with plasma HIV RNA 2.19 × 10^5 copies/mL and CD4+ T-cell count 14 cells/μL. The local doctor shifted the ART regimen to tenofovir, lamivudine, lopinavir/ritonavir without a genotypic drug resistance test, and his plasma HIV viral load was well controlled thereafter.","The paper mentions that the patient had experienced treatment failure and had received  tenofovir, lamivudine, efavirenz, lopinavir/ritonavir.  which means sequences were reported from individuals who received ARV drugs previously."
38072961,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI","He initiated ART with tenofovir, lamivudine, and efavirenz, but treatment failure occurred one year later with plasma HIV RNA tenofovir, lamivudine, lopinavir/ritonavir. The local doctor shifted the ART regimen to lamivudine (highly resistant), and dolutegravir (sensitive).","The paper mentions that the patient received drugs from the NRTI, NNRTI and PI classes before sample sequencing, and the drugs received in this study include NRTIs (Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Zidovudine), NNRTIs (Efavirenz), PIs (Lopinavir, Dolutegravir)."
38072961,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Zidovudine, Efavirenz, Lopinavir, Dolutegravir.","He initiated ART with tenofovir, lamivudine, and efavirenz, but treatment failure occurred one year later with plasma HIV RNA tenofovir, lamivudine, lopinavir/ritonavir. The local doctor shifted the ART regimen to lamivudine (highly resistant), and dolutegravir (sensitive).","The paper lists the specific drugs that the patient received before and during the study, including tenofovir, lamivudine, efavirenz, lopinavir, and dolutegravir."
38090027,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The retrospective cohort study, which included adult treatment-experienced and virologically suppressed PLWH, switched to BFTAF from June 2019 to June 2021. A validated in-house method was used to identify resistance-associated mutations (RAMs), as previously described by our group. No genotypic resistance tests were administered to the cohort after BFTAF introduction, which had a virological suppression level of over 90% after 60 weeks of follow-up, a short time after the switch compared to other observational studies and years compared to older cohorts. 11 GRT on plasma HIV-RNA and PBMC HIV-DNA was interpreted according to the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database version 9.0 (hivdb.stanford.edu/hivdb/by-sequences/, last updated on 22 June 2023).","The paper does not report HIV sequences from patient samples. Instead, it focuses on the efficacy and safety of a specific treatment (BFTAF) in patients who are already virologically suppressed. It mentions the use of genotypic resistance tests to evaluate drug resistance at virological failure, but it does not provide any sequences or details about the HIV strains from patient samples. The study is more about the treatment outcomes rather than the genetic analysis of the HIV itself, which the question specifically asks about. Therefore, the answer is No and the evidence supports this by indicating the lack of sequencing data in the study."
38090027,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"A retrospective cohort study was conducted in adult PLWH on antiretroviral therapy and HIV-RNA <50 copies/mL, who switched to BFTAF to replace the current regimen, from June 2019 to June 2021. To be included in the analysis, they had to have at least an HIV-RNA determination (PCR Cobas® HIV-1 test 6800 Systems, Roche Diagnostics) after the introduction of BFTAF. A validated in-house method was used to identify resistance-associated mutations (RAMs), as previously described by our group. 11 GRT on plasma HIV-RNA and PBMC HIV-DNA was interpreted according to the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database version 9.0 (hivdb.stanford.edu/hivdb/by-sequences/, last updated on 22 June 2023).","The paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on a retrospective cohort study analyzing the efficacy and safety of the BFTAF regimen in patients with HIV. While it mentions genotypic resistance tests and the identification of resistance-associated mutations, it does not provide any data on in vitro drug susceptibility assays or results. The paper's emphasis is on clinical outcomes rather than laboratory-based susceptibility testing, which is why the answer is No."
38090027,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The retrospective cohort study, which included adult treatment-experienced and virologically suppressed PLWH, switched to BFTAF from June 2019 to June 2021. GRT on plasma HIV-RNA and PBMC HIV-DNA was interpreted according to the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database version 9.0 (hivdb.stanford.edu/hivdb/by-sequences/, last updated on 22 June 2023).","The paper does not mention any publicly available sequences. The study focuses on the clinical outcomes of a treatment regimen and mentions genotypic resistance tests, but it does not provide any sequences or details about making sequences publicly available. The absence of such information in the paper indicates that the answer is No."
38090027,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No accession numbers available,"The retrospective cohort study, which included adult treatment-experienced and virologically suppressed PLWH, switched to BFTAF from June 2019 to June 2021. GRT on plasma HIV-RNA and PBMC HIV-DNA was interpreted according to the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database version 9.0 (hivdb.stanford.edu/hivdb/by-sequences/, last updated on 22 June 2023).","The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates. It mentions the use of genotypic resistance tests and the interpretation of results, but it does not include any accession numbers or details about submitting sequences to GenBank. The lack of this information in the paper indicates that the answer is No."
38090027,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"The retrospective cohort study, which included adult treatment-experienced and virologically suppressed PLWH, switched to BFTAF from June 2019 to June 2021. A validated in-house method was used to identify resistance-associated mutations (RAMs), as previously described by our group. 11 GRT on plasma HIV-RNA and PBMC HIV-DNA was interpreted according to the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database version 9.0 (hivdb.stanford.edu/hivdb/by-sequences/, last updated on 22 June 2023).","The paper does not report any individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing. It mentions the use of genotypic resistance tests to evaluate drug resistance at virological failure, but it does not provide any details about the number of individuals or samples sequenced. The focus is on clinical outcomes rather than on the sequencing of HIV samples, indicating the answer is 0."
38090027,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,No,"2. Patients and methods The CSL-HIV Cohort was approved by the Ethics Committee of the IRCCS San Raffaele Scientific Institute (Milan, Italy; date of approval 4 December 2017, protocol n. 34). 3. Results Overall, 1040 patients who met the above-mentioned inclusion criteria were considered in the analysis (flowchart of data extraction in Figure 1). The study was conducted in Italy and the cohort was approved by the Ethics Committee of the IRCCS San Raffaele Scientific Institute which is based in Milan, Italy.",The paper explicitly states the study was conducted in Italy which indicates that the samples were obtained from Italy.
38090027,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2019 to 2021,"The retrospective cohort study, which included adult treatment-experienced and virologically suppressed PLWH, switched to BFTAF from June 2019 to June 2021. Virological failure (VF) was defined as two consecutive HIV-RNA values >50 copies/mL or a single HIV-RNA value >400 copies/mL. Treatment failure (TF) is defined as the occurrence of virological failure or discontinuation of BFTAF for any reason. Follow-up accrued from the start of BFTAF (baseline) up to the stop of any drug in the regimen or lost to follow-up or data freezing (31 August 2022).",The paper mentions individuals switched to BFTAF from June 2019 to June 2021 indicating that the date of the sample acquisition was between 2019 and 2021.
38090027,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The retrospective cohort study, which included adult treatment-experienced and virologically suppressed PLWH, switched to BFTAF from June 2019 to June 2021. A validated in-house method was used to identify resistance-associated mutations (RAMs), as previously described by our group. 11 GRT on plasma HIV-RNA and PBMC HIV-DNA was interpreted according to the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database version 9.0 (hivdb.stanford.edu/hivdb/by-sequences/, last updated on 22 June 2023).","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It focuses on the clinical outcomes of a treatment regimen and mentions genotypic resistance tests, but it does not provide any details about cloning samples before sequencing. The absence of this information in the paper indicates that the answer is No."
38090027,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,No genes sequenced,"The retrospective cohort study, which included adult treatment-experienced and virologically suppressed PLWH, switched to BFTAF from June 2019 to June 2021. A validated in-house method was used to identify resistance-associated mutations (RAMs), as previously described by our group. 11 GRT on plasma HIV-RNA and PBMC HIV-DNA was interpreted according to the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database version 9.0 (hivdb.stanford.edu/hivdb/by-sequences/, last updated on 22 June 2023).","The paper does not specify any HIV genes that were sequenced. Although it mentions the use of genotypic resistance tests to evaluate drug resistance, it does not provide details about the specific genes analyzed. The lack of this information in the paper suggests that the answer is No."
38090027,10,What method was used for sequencing?,No methods reported,"The retrospective cohort study, which included adult treatment-experienced and virologically suppressed PLWH, switched to BFTAF from June 2019 to June 2021. A validated in-house method was used to identify resistance-associated mutations (RAMs), as previously described by our group. 11 GRT on plasma HIV-RNA and PBMC HIV-DNA was interpreted according to the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database version 9.0 (hivdb.stanford.edu/hivdb/by-sequences/, last updated on 22 June 2023).","The paper does not mention the specific sequencing methods used. It focuses on the clinical outcomes of a treatment regimen and mentions genotypic resistance tests, but it does not provide any details about the sequencing methods employed. The absence of this information indicates that the paper does not report the sequencing methods, leading to the answer No."
38090027,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,No samples reported,"The retrospective cohort study, which included adult treatment-experienced and virologically suppressed PLWH, switched to BFTAF from June 2019 to June 2021. A validated in-house method was used to identify resistance-associated mutations (RAMs), as previously described by our group. 11 GRT on plasma HIV-RNA and PBMC HIV-DNA was interpreted according to the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database version 9.0 (hivdb.stanford.edu/hivdb/by-sequences/, last updated on 22 June 2023).","The paper does not specify the types of samples that were sequenced. Although it mentions the use of plasma HIV-RNA and PBMC HIV-DNA for genotypic resistance tests, it does not provide details about the sequencing or the clinical outcomes of the samples. The absence of this information suggests that the question doesn't apply."
38090027,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"The retrospective cohort study, which included adult treatment-experienced and virologically suppressed PLWH, switched to BFTAF from June 2019 to June 2021. Virological failure (VF) was defined as two consecutive HIV-RNA values >50 copies/mL or a single HIV-RNA value >400 copies/mL. Treatment failure (TF) is defined as the occurrence of virological failure or discontinuation of BFTAF for any reason. Overall, VF occurred in 44 (4.2%) patients, with an incidence rate of 1.63 (95% CI 1.16--2.11) per 1000 PMFU; this event occurred after a median of 12.8 (5.8--22.8) months of observation. Mainly due to the low values of viremia at VF, genotypic resistance tests (GRTs) were able to evaluate the drug resistance at VF only in five patients: none selected variants resistant to integrase inhibitors and no new mutations emerged in any case. 33 cases (75%) BFTAF was successfully maintained with spontaneous virological re-suppression..","The paper mentions that virological failure (VF) occurred in some patients, and genotypic resistance tests were performed on plasma HIV-RNA and PBMC HIV-DNA. This indicates that sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure, leading to the answer Yes."
38090027,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The retrospective cohort study, which included adult treatment-experienced and virologically suppressed PLWH, switched to BFTAF from June 2019 to June 2021. follow-up accrued from the start of BFTAF (baseline) up to the stop of any drug in the regimen or lost to follow-up or data freezing (31 August 2022).",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It describes a retrospective cohort study and mentions 156 (15%) of patients after a median of 13.5 months of follow-up; the incidence rate of regimen discontinuation was 5.67 (95% CI 4.78--6.55) per 1000 PMFU. The absence of information about clinical trial enrollment indicates that the answer is No.
38090027,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"A retrospective cohort study was conducted in adult PLWH on antiretroviral therapy and HIV-RNA <50 copies/mL, who switched to BFTAF to replace the current regimen, from June 2019 to June 2021. GRT on plasma HIV-RNA and PBMC HIV-DNA was interpreted according to the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database version 9.0 (hivdb.stanford.edu/hivdb/by-sequences/, last updated on 22 June 2023).","The paper does not mention any sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs. It focuses on the clinical outcomes of a treatment regimen and mentions genotypic resistance tests, but it does not provide any details about the sequences or the history of ARV drug use in the individuals. The absence of this information indicates that the answer is No."
38090027,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,No data,"Figure 1. Flowchart of data extraction. Males represented 82.1% of the study population. At the beginning of BFTAF treatment, the median age (Q1-Q3) was 51.1 (42--57.1) years; patients had a known HIV infection lasting 13.8 (6.8--23.4) years and were on ART for 10.2 (5.1--19.7) years; nadir CD4+ lymphocyte count was 291 (166--447) cells/µL, while their current CD4+ count was 718 (535--941) cells/µL; an AIDS diagnosis was present in 181 subjects (17.4%) and a history of HCV co-infection in 212 (20.4%). Of these 1040, 705 (67.8%) were taking ECFTAF, while 335 (32.2%) were on a different regimen. Of those on a regimen other than ECFTAF, 194 (59.9%) were on a three-drug integrase inhibitor-based regimen other than elvitegravir, 56 (16.7%) on a protease inhibitor-based regimen, 38 (11.7%) on a non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor-based regimen, and 47 (14%) were taking a regimen that cannot be classified among the previous ones, including mono-and dual-therapies and study-protocol regimens. Among 335 patients not treated with ECFTAF, the switch to BFTAF was motivated by simplification (lower drug burden, lower pill burden, no food requirement, fewer potential adverse effects, and/or potential drug--drug interactions) in 236 cases (70.4%), toxicity in 26 (7.8%), and by a miscellany of different reasons in 73 cases (21.8%), such as presence of drug--drug interactions, concern of co-morbidities, exit from clinical studies, patient's request. The overall median follow-up was of 29.7 (24.4--32.3) months, for a total of 27,536 PMFU. The median follow-up was of 30.5 (26.7--32.7) months with 19,352 PMFU among people who switched from an ECFTAF regimen and of 27.8 (19.1--30.8) months with 8,183 PMFU among people from another regimens. Overall, VF occurred in 44 (4.2%) patients, with an incidence rate of 1.63 (95% CI 1.16--2.11) per 1000 PMFU; this event occurred after a median of 12.8 (5.8--22.8) months of observation. Median HIV-RNA values at failure were 246 (68--12,025) copies/mL, with 23 cases satisfying the finding of two consecutive HIV-RNA values >50 copies/mL, and 21 corresponding to the criterion of a single HIV-RNA value >400 copies/mL. The probability of VF at 12, 24 and 30 months was 2.1% (95% CI 1.4--3.3%), 3.8% (95% CI 2.7--5.2%), and 4.0% (95% CI 2.9--5.5%), respectively. The estimate up to 30 months of the probability of VF is illustrated in Figure 2. No statistically significant differences were observed in the probability of VF between patients whose prior regimen was ECFTAF and those who were on a regimen other than ECFTAF at the time of initiating BFTAF. Mainly due to the low values of viremia at VF, genotypic resistance tests (GRTs) were able to evaluate the drug resistance at VF only in five patients: none selected variants resistant to integrase inhibitors and no new mutations emerged in any case (Table 2).","The paper does not provide any information regarding the drug classes received by individuals before sample sequencing. It mentions the treatments they were switched to and the current regimen, but it does not detail the drug classes that were previously received. The lack of this information indicates that the answer is No."
38090027,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,No data,"Figure 1. Flowchart of data extraction. Males represented 82.1% of the study population. At the beginning of BFTAF treatment, the median age (Q1-Q3) was 51.1 (42--57.1) years; patients had a known HIV infection lasting 13.8 (6.8--23.4) years and were on ART for 10.2 (5.1--19.7) years; nadir CD4+ lymphocyte count was291 (166--447) cells/µL, while their currentCD4+ count was 718 (535--941) cells/µL; an AIDS diagnosis was present in 181 subjects (17.4%) and a history of HCV co-infection in 212 (20.4%). Of these 1040, 705 (67.8%)were taking ECFTAF, while 335 (32.2%)were on a different regimen. Of those on a regimen other than ECFTAF, 194 (59.9%) were on a three-drug integrase inhibitor-based regimen other than elvitegravir, 56 (16.7%) on a protease inhibitor-based regimen, 38 (11.7%) on a non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor-based regimen, and 47 (14%) were taking a regimen that cannot be classified among the previous ones, including mono-and dual-therapies and study-protocol regimens. Among 335 patients not treated with ECFTAF, the switch to BFTAF was motivated by simplification (lower drug burden, lower pill burden, no food requirement, fewer potential adverse effects, and/or potential drug--drug interactions) in 236 cases (70.4%), toxicity in 26 (7.8%), and by a miscellany of different reasons in 73 cases (21.8%), such as presence of drug--drug interactions, concern of co-morbidities, exit from clinical studies, patient’s request. Overall, 1040 patients who met the above-mentioned inclusion criteria were considered in the analysis (flowchart of data extraction in Figure 1). After the introduction of BFTAF (6) months, the switch was motivated by TF in 27 (2.6%) patients, all but one (3.7) after failing to achieve VF within 20 weeks of beginning therapy, and lost to follow-up in 21 (2%) patients.","The paper does not provide any information regarding the specific drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing. It mentions the treatment they were switched to and the current regimen, but it does not detail the specific drugs that were previously received. The lack of this information indicates that the answer is No."
38140553,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,We analyzed sequences and their associated demographic and epidemiological data. The sequences covered the HIV-1 protease (PR) and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT). The genotyping results of 3178 HIV-infected patients from seven Russian federal districts (FDs) were examined.,"The paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples by stating that sequences and demographic data were analyzed from HIV-infected patients. Specifically, it mentions that the genotyping results of 3178 HIV-infected patients were examined."
38140553,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"In this study, we performed drug resistance analysis among HIV-infected patients not on ART, infected with both viruses of ""pure"" subtypes and its recombinant forms. The analysis utilized Stanford University's HIVdb database tool and the Calibrated Population Resistance Tool (CPR).",The paper mentions conducting drug resistance analysis among HIV-infected patients but does not explicitly state that in vitro drug susceptibility data was reported.
38140553,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"All nucleotide sequences obtained in the study were deposited in the international database of the Los Alamos laboratory. The sequences investigated are marked in black, the reference sequences are marked in red. The sequencing analysis results were carried out using iTOL v.6 software [32].","The paper confirms that all nucleotide sequences obtained in the study were deposited in the international database of the Los Alamos laboratory, indicating that some sequences are publicly available."
38140553,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,K03455,"The sequences investigated are marked in black, the reference sequences are marked in red. The yellow cluster contains CRF02 sequences of African origin. The sequencing necessitated the presence of a sequence length of at least 919bp, HXB2 numbering, GenBank accession number K03455. The combined prevalence of the circulating recombinant forms (CRFs) CRF02_AG and CRF63_02A6 was 4.8% (151 sequences); CRF03_AB (36, 1.1%) was also found. Other genetic variants accounted for less than 1% each and were presented by the following subtypes: A1 (1/3178), C (22/3178),D (1/3178),F1 (1/3178), G (18/3178), and recombinant forms CRF01_AE (1/3178) and CRF11_cpx (1/3178).","The GenBank accession number K03455 is mentioned in the context of the sequencing and analysis of the HIV-1 protease and reverse transcriptase regions, indicating that this is the reference sequence used."
38140553,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,3178,"The genotyping results of 3178 HIV-infected patients from seven Russian federal districts (FDs) were examined. We analyzed sequences and their associated demographic and epidemiological data. Out of the total patient count, 65 individuals (2.0%) did not have a clearly identified risk factor.","The paper states that the genotyping results of 3178 HIV-infected patients were examined, which indicates the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for sequencing."
38140553,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Russia,"This study seeks to identify and analyze HIV-1 recombinants in Russia during the last decade of the epidemic. A comprehensive examination was conducted, encompassing 3178 partial pol sequences. The study also involved phylogenetic analysis to trace the origins of the identified recombinants. We performed drug resistance analysis among HIV-infected patients not on ART, infected with both viruses of ""pure"" subtypes and its recombinant forms. The analysis utilized Stanford University's HIVdb database tool and the Calibrated Population Resistance Tool (CPR).",The paper focuses on HIV-1 recombinants in Russia and mentions the analysis of sequences from HIV-infected patients from seven Russian federal districts.
38140553,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2011 to 2020,"The material of the study were blood plasma collections (n = 2502), peripheral blood mononuclear cells (lymphocytes) collections (n = 613), and frozen whole blood collections (n = 63) obtained from HIV-infected patients at federal and regional centers for HIV/AIDS prevention (AIDS Centers) as part of molecular genetic monitoring of the HIV epidemic in Russia in the period from 2011 to 2020. Drug Resistance mutations (DRMs) were assessed. The study uncovered an overall proportion of recombinants at 8.7%, with a statistically significant increase in their frequency observed over time (p < 0.001).",The paper specifies that the samples were obtained during the period from 2011 to 2020 as part of molecular genetic monitoring of the HIV epidemic in Russia.
38140553,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Sanger-based sequencing of the HIV pol gene regions encoding the PR-RT (2253--3554 bp according to the HXB-2 strain, GenBank accession number K03455) was performed. Informed consent was signed by all HIV-infected patients included in the study in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki. All the data were anonymized and coded.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of direct sequencing methods on amplified DNA fragments.
38140553,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,HIV-1 protease (PR) and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT),"The sequences covered the HIV-1 protease (PR) and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT). We performed drug resistance analysis among HIV-infected patients not on ART, infected with both viruses of “pure” subtypes and its recombinant forms. The study doesn’t report the sequencing results of RT gene.","The paper specifies that the sequences covered the HIV-1 protease and part of the reverse transcriptase, indicating which genes were sequenced."
38140553,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"Sanger-based sequencing of the HIV pol gene regions encoding the PR-RT (2253--3554 bp according to the HXB-2 strain, GenBank accession number K03455) was performed. Sequencing reaction for amplification protocols A and B ( Table 1 ) was performed using BigDye Terminator v3.1 Cycle Sequencing Kit (""Applied Biosystems"", Waltham, MT, USA) according to the manufacturer's protocol. PCR conditions are presented in Supplemental Table S1. The sequencing reaction in the ViroSeq system was performed according to the manufacturer's protocol. Then, sequence processing was performed automatically using ViroSeq HIV-1 Genotyping Software v. 3.0 (Applied Biosystems, USA).","The paper mentions the use of Sanger-based sequencing for the HIV pol gene regions, which is a method of sequencing."
38140553,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,"Blood plasma, peripheral blood mononuclear cells (lymphocytes), and frozen whole blood","The material of the study were blood plasma collections (n = 2502), peripheral blood mononuclear cells (lymphocytes) collections (n = 613), and frozen whole blood collections (n = 63) obtained from HIV-infected patients at federal and regional centers for HIV/AIDS prevention (AIDS Centers) as part of molecular genetic monitoring of the HIV epidemic in Russia in the period from 2011 to 2020. We analyzed sequences and their associated demographic and epidemiological data.","The paper specifies that blood plasma, peripheral blood mononuclear cells, and frozen whole blood were used as samples for sequencing."
38140553,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"We performed drug resistance analysis among HIV-infected patients not on ART, infected with both viruses of ""pure"" subtypes and its recombinant forms. Since we could not estimate the duration of patients' infection and the possibility of their treatment in the past, and only knew that they were not receiving ART at the time of genotype analysis, we decided not to call this pretreatment drug resistance or transmitted drug resistance, and introduced the general name of primary resistance (PrimDR) in this context. The study showcased the PrimDR prevalence and Drug Resistance Mutations (DRMs) were assessed The study also involved phylogenetic analysis to trace the origins of the identified recombinants. “clarify not receiving ART why not, since we could not estimate the duration of patients’ infection and the possibility of their treatment in the past” 11.0%. Percent on nonnucleos(t)ide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) as a monotherapy. Tenofovir DF/truvada for all. TPV/r (25 mg)  C) received tenofovir DF +FTC, then TPV/EFV Tenofovir DF/r, SQV, FPV, ABC, DTG, RPV, 3TC, DPV, ATZ, NVP, PLD, DRV, RAL,1433,1397, 203(patients RNA not) extract at year 2018 Not whether obtaining bad, or getting virological failure on treatment regimen.","The paper mention conducting drug resistance analysis among patients not on ART which means they are treatment naïve and since we could not estimate the duration of patients’ infection and the possibility of their treatment in the past, we could not state virological failure."
38140553,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The material of the study were blood plasma collections (n = 2502), peripheral blood mononuclear cells (lymphocytes) collections (n = 613), and frozen whole blood collections (n = 63) obtained from HIV-infected patients at federal and regional centers for HIV/AIDS prevention (AIDS Centers) as part of molecular genetic monitoring of the HIV epidemic in Russia in the period from 2011 to 2020. We analyzed sequences and their associated demographic and epidemiological data. Out of the total patient count, 65 individuals (2.0%) did not have a clearly identified risk factor. Among the cases of HIV-infection, 14 (0.4%) were categorized under the ""Other"" group.",The paper does not indicate that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It mentions that the samples were obtained as part of molecular genetic monitoring of the HIV epidemic in Russia.
38140553,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Since we could not estimate the duration of patients' infection and the possibility of their treatment in the past, and only knew that they were not receiving ART at the time of genotype analysis, we decided not to call this pretreatment drug resistance or transmitted drug resistance, and introduced the general name of primary resistance (PrimDR) in this context. We performed drug resistance analysis among HIV-infected patients not on ART, infected with both viruses of ""pure"" subtypes and its recombinant forms. In this study, we performed drug resistance analysis among HIV-infected patients not on ART, infected with both viruses of ""pure"" subtypes and its recombinant forms. The analysis utilized Stanford University's HIVdb database tool and the Calibrated Population Resistance Tool (CPR). DRMs were assessed DDS Associated with Drug Resistance mutations We analyzed sequences and their associated demographic and epidemiological data out of 3178 which 1494 does be on ARVs NRTI,RT Is final only 267 being naïve DRMs analysed NRTI,RT is after not receiving  not received ART receiving, we could not the past, and primar not define prtreatment and transmitted all three NA on ART","The paper mentions conducting drug resistance analysis among HIV-infected patients, the past, not on ART and some not recieving previously got received ARVs."
38140553,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs","Since we could not estimate the duration of patients' infection and the possibility of their treatment in the past, and only knew that they were not receiving ART at the time of genotype analysis, we decided not to call this pretreatment drug resistance or transmitted drug resistance, and introduced the general name of primary resistance (PrimDR) in this context. We performed drug resistance analysis among HIV-infected patients not on ART, infected with both viruses of ""pure"" subtypes and its recombinant forms. In this study, we performed drug resistance analysis among HIV-infected patients not on ART, infected with both viruses of ""pure"" subtypes and its recombinant forms. The analysis utilized Stanford University's HIVdb database tool and the Calibrated Population Resistance Tool (CPR). DRMs were assessed DDS Associated with Drug Resistance mutations We analyzed sequences and their associated demographic and epidemiological data out of 3178 which 1494 does be on ARVs NRTI,RT Is final only 267 being naïve DRMs analysed NRTI,RT is after not receiving  not received ART receiving, we could not the past, and primar not define prtreatment and transmitted all three NA on ART","The paper mentions conducting drug resistance analysis among HIV-infected patients, the past, not on ART and some not recieving previously got received ARVs."
38140553,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not Reported,"The paper does not provide specific details about the drugs that were received  by individuals before sample sequencing. It mentions that the  patients were not receiving ARV drugs at the time of the diagnosis, but does not list any drugs.","The paper does not provide specific details about the drugs that were received by individuals before sample sequencing. It mentions that the patients were not receiving ARV drugs at the time of the diagnosis, but does not list any drugs."
38140649,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Molecular epidemiology of HIV-1 infection is challenging due to the highly diverse HIV-genome. We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia in the period 2019–2022. Data collection and analysis were performed on HIV-1 RNA plasma samples collected from newly diagnosed HIV patients over a four-year period during the diagnostic process and clinical follow-up at UHID.","The paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples by describing the collection and analysis of HIV-1 RNA plasma samples from newly diagnosed HIV patients. Specifically, it mentions that sequences were aligned and compared with a reference strain, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained and analyzed from patient samples."
38140649,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Molecular epidemiology of HIV-1 infection is challenging due to the highly diverse HIV-genome. We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia in the period 2019--2022. The results of this research confirmed a high overall prevalence of TDR of 16.7%. Resistance to nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs), non-nucleoside RTIs (NNRTIs), and protease inhibitors (PIs) was found in 9.6%, 7.4%, and 1.5% of persons, respectively. No resistance to integrase strand-transfer inhibitors (INSTIs) was found. Phylogenetic analysis revealed that 173/229 sequences (75.5%) were part of transmission clusters, and the largest identified was T215S, consisting of 45 sequences.","The paper does not mention in vitro drug susceptibility tests or results. It focuses on genetic diversity, transmitted drug resistance, and phylogenetic analysis of HIV-1 sequences from patient samples, but there is no discussion of laboratory-based drug susceptibility testing."
38140649,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,All data analysed in this study are included in this published article and its Supplementary Information file. All sequences obtained in this study by SS were submitted to the GeneBank nucleotide sequence database under accession numbers OR605755-OR606279 and OR613869-OR613875. Sequences generated by DS are available from the ArrayExpress database at EMBL-EBI under accession number E-MTAB-13563.,"The paper mentions that all sequences obtained in the study were submitted to the GenBank nucleotide sequence database under specific accession numbers, indicating that the sequences were made publicly available."
38140649,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,OR605755-OR606279 and OR613869-OR613875,All sequences obtained in this study by SS were submitted to the GeneBank nucleotide sequence database under accession numbers OR605755-OR606279 and OR613869-OR613875. Sequences generated by DS are available from the ArrayExpress database at EMBL-EBI under accession number E-MTAB-13563.,The paper provides specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequences obtained in the study.
38140649,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,270,"We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia in the period 2019–2022. A total of 270 persons met the inclusion criteria. Characteristics of newly diagnosed PLHIV in the period 2019--2022 are shown in Table 1. The study group consisted primarily of men (254/270, 94.1%) with only 16/270 (5.9%) women making it to the final analysis.","The paper states that 270 individuals met the inclusion criteria for the study, and these individuals were the focus of the sequencing and analysis."
38140649,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Croatia,"This study was conducted at the Department for Immunological and Molecular Diagnostics at UHID. We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia in the period 2019–2022. The majority of the study population was men (94.1%) and the most prevalent age group was 18–35 years. Most of the patients originated from and reported Croatia as the country of infection and were included in the MSM risk group.","The study samples were collected from individuals in Croatia, as mentioned throughout the paper. The focus is on the HIV epidemic in Croatia and the characteristics of the patients diagnosed at the UHID."
38140649,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2019 to 2022,"We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia in the period 2019–2022. A total of 280 newly diagnosed treatment-naïve persons entered the Croatian Reference Centre for HIV/AIDS (86 in 2019, 58 in 2020, 68 in 2021, and 68 in 2022) from January 2019 to December 2022.","The paper specifies that the samples were collected from 2019 to 2022, during the study period."
38140649,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The first round of amplification was performed by using the SuperScript III One-Step RT-PCR System with Platinum Taq (Invitrogen, Carlsbad, CA, USA) and a region-specific primer set while nested PCR was carried out using AmpliTaq Gold™ DNA Polymerase with Gold Buffer and MgCl2 (Applied Biosystems, Foster City, CA, USA) with an inner primer set. Nested PCR amplicons were purified and sequenced with a BigDye Terminator v3.1 Cycle Sequencing Kit (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, MA, USA) with a set of four primers for both sequencing regions to obtain bidirectional sequences(Supplementary Table S1).","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of nested PCR and direct sequencing, which means samples were not cloned."
38140649,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes","HIV-1 strains were defined as resistant if carrying at least one SDRM. The overall prevalence was defined as the percentage of patients with SDRMs. The prevalence of TDR for the different drug classes (NRTI, NNRTI, PI, and INSTI), was defined in the same matter. Sequencing of the HIV-1 protease and partial sequencing of the reverse transcriptase gene. and sequencing of the HIV-1 integrase gene. A total of 229/270 (84.8%) PR and RT sequences were subjected to phylogenetic analysis. The IN region for six people failed to amplify.","The paper mentions sequencing of the protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes, with specific details on the regions sequenced."
38140649,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing and Next Generation Sequencing,"A portion of the supernatant was removed, and the remaining 140 μL was used for RNA extraction using a QIAamp Viral RNA Mini Kit (Qiagen, Hilden, Germany) according to the manufacturer's protocol. Amplification of the HIV-1 pol gene was performed for two regions in two separate reactions: (1) sequencing of the HIV-1 protease and partial sequencing of the reverse transcriptase gene; and (2) sequencing of the HIV-1 integrase gene. The first round of amplification was performed by using the SuperScript III One-Step RT-PCR System with Platinum Taq (Invitrogen, Carlsbad, CA, USA) and a region-specific primer set while nested PCR was carried out using AmpliTaq Gold™ DNA Polymerase with Gold Buffer and MgCl2 (Applied Biosystems, Foster City, CA, USA) with an inner primer set. Nested PCR amplicons were purified and sequenced with a BigDye Terminator v3.1 Cycle Sequencing Kit (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, MA, USA) with a set of four primers for both sequencing regions to obtain bidirectional sequences (Supplementary Table S1).  A total of 60 samples that were previously sequenced by SS were randomly selected and subjected to NGS. Previously extracted RNA was reverse transcribed with SuperScript III First-Strand Synthesis System for RT-PCR (Invitrogen) and UNINEF primers. Precisely, the whole HIV-1 protease region, part of the reverse transcriptase region (K03455 number for the gene-specific position 2189–3753), and the integrase region (K03455 number for the gene-specific position 4180–5200) were sequenced on the Illumina platform using MiniSeq (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA).",The paper describes the use of both Sanger sequencing and Next Generation Sequencing (NGS) for the analysis of the HIV-1 sequences.
38140649,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"Data collection and analysis were performed on HIV-1 RNA plasma samples collected from newly diagnosed HIV patients over a four-year period during the diagnostic process and clinical follow-up at UHID. At least 500 μL of the patient's plasma samples were concentrated at 20,000 g and 8 °C for 1 h. A portion of the supernatant was removed, and the remaining 140 μL was used for RNA extraction using a QIAamp Viral RNA Mini Kit (Qiagen, Hilden, Germany) according to the manufacturer's protocol. We compared deep sequencing (DS) with Sanger sequencing (SS) on 60 randomly selected samples and identified additional surveillance drug resistance mutations (SDRMs) in 49 of them. A total of 60 samples that were previously sequenced by SS were randomly selected and subjected to NGS.","The paper specifies that the samples were HIV-1 RNA plasma samples collected from newly diagnosed HIV patients, focusing on the analysis of these clinical specimens."
38140649,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia in the period 2019–2022. Data collection and analysis were performed on HIV-1 RNA plasma samples collected from newly diagnosed HIV patients over a four-year period during the diagnostic process and clinical follow-up at UHID. All newly diagnosed treatment-naïve PLHIV over 18 years who entered clinical care at UHID in the period between January 2019 and December 2022 with plasma viraemia > 1000 HIV-1 RNA copies per ml were included in this study. In this study, we reported the prevalence of SDRMs in Croatia in the period 2019–2022. We confirmed a high prevalence of SDRMs of 16.7% as well as forward transmission of resistance strains. Besides TDR associated with NRTIs, the emergence of SDRMs to NNRTIs as well as the higher incidence of non-B subtypes observed in this study suggest important changes in the pattern of primary resistance and viral diversity. The majority of identified mutations were clinically relevant, which makes baseline HIV resistance testing in Croatia of unquestionable importance. Although analysis of the clinical significance of SDRMs showed a favourable virological background for INSTI-based treatment since no SDRMs to INSTI were observed, surveillance of TDR to INSTI should be continued in terms of ascertaining the role of polymorphic and non-polymorphic mutations in the development of resistance. Confounding the results of drug resistance measurements could be the presence of historic DRMs, i.e., mutations that were present before the initiation of treatment but were lost after the initiation of treatment, because HIV-1 is a rapidly evolving virus that can easily adapt to drug pressure. In these persons, genotypic resistance testing to DRMs is performed, however, Genotypic resistance testing does not show whether the mutation is present in one or both viruses, nor its impact on treatment success.",The paper focuses on treatment-naïve individuals and does not mention sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen.
38140649,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia in the period 2019–2022. Data collection and analysis were performed on HIV-1 RNA plasma samples collected from newly diagnosed HIV patients over a four-year period during the diagnostic process and clinical follow-up at UHID.  All newly diagnosed treatment-naïve PLHIV over 18 years who entered clinical care at UHID in the period between January 2019 and December 2022 with plasma viraemia > 1000 HIV-1 RNA copies per ml were included in this study.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It focuses on newly diagnosed treatment-naïve individuals who entered clinical care at UHID.
